window.NOTES = [{"slug": "README", "label": "About these notes & syllabus", "tag": "", "group": "start", "title": "Sustainable Supply Chain Management — End-Sem Study Notes", "md": "# Sustainable Supply Chain Management — End-Sem Study Notes\nIn-depth notes for the **Sustainable Supply Chain Management (SSCM)** end-semester exam (MBA Operations Management, batch 2025–27), built from the course material zip. Sources: the eleven faculty lectures; the *Carbon management, carbon accounting and carbon trading* deck; the IISc TTW and WTW papers on Indian automotive fuels; Rietmann et al. on EV sales and CO₂; the HBS cases on **ITC eChoupal** and **Tesla's battery supply chain**; the **Tata Motors** and **TVS Motor** BRSR reports; and McKinsey's *Geopolitics and the geometry of global trade (2026)*. 📘 marks content from your course material. 📗 marks standard textbook knowledge or practice data added to complete an answer.\n\n## Syllabus ⭐⭐⭐\n| # | Syllabus topic | Lectures / files in the zip | Theory page | Numericals page |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **SSCM definition and business performance** | Lectures 1, 2, 3 | [SSCM & Business Performance](ch01-sscm-business-performance.md) | [Performance Measurement](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md) (Lecture 3 dashboard) |\n| 2 | **LCA and carbon footprints** | Lectures 4, 5; Carbon management deck | [LCA & Carbon Footprints](ch02-lca-carbon-footprints.md) | [Carbon Accounting](p2-carbon-accounting-numericals.md), [LCA & Energy](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md) |\n| 3 | **Sustainable procurement** | Lecture 6 | [Sustainable Procurement](ch03-sustainable-procurement.md) | [Supplier Selection](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md) |\n| 4 | **Sustainable manufacturing and productivity** | Lecture 7 | [Sustainable Manufacturing](ch04-sustainable-manufacturing.md) | [Performance Measurement](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md) (OEE, intensity) |\n| 5 | **Technologies and tools for quality improvement** | Lecture 9; Lecture 10 Section B | [Technology & Quality Tools](ch05-technology-quality-tools.md) | [Performance Measurement](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md) (TAS, NTV, Navya) |\n| 6 | **Sustainable logistics** | Lecture 8 (green and reverse logistics) | [Sustainable Logistics](ch06-sustainable-logistics.md) | [Network & Logistics](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md) |\n| 7 | **Designing the sustainable network** | Lecture 8 (network model, CE) | [Network Design](ch07-network-design.md) | [Network & Logistics](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md) |\n| 8 | **Measuring SSCM performance** | Lectures 3, 10; BRSR reports | [Performance Measurement](ch08-performance-measurement.md) | [Performance Measurement](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md) |\n| 9 | **Energy-sector supply chain** (supplies, production, end use, disposal) | IISc TTW/WTW papers; Rietmann EV paper; Tesla case | [Energy-Sector Supply Chain](ch09-energy-sector.md) | [LCA & Energy](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md) |\n| 10 | **SSCM best practices** | Lecture 11; McKinsey; ITC eChoupal | [SSCM Best Practices](ch10-sscm-best-practices.md) | [Case Studies](case-studies.md) |\n\n## What the exam asks (as announced) ⭐⭐⭐\n| CO | What to expect | Weightage | Where to practise |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **CO1** | 3-mark short theory: SSCM, TBL, strategy, business performance | Low | [Ch 1](ch01-sscm-business-performance.md), [Q&A Bank](exam-questions-qna.md) |\n| **CO2** | **Numerical: weighted scoring of suppliers** (+ scorecard, TOPSIS, risk matrix, interpretation) | Medium | [P1 Supplier Selection](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md) |\n| **CO3** | **Numerical: carbon accounting, Scope 1, 2, 3** (+ GWP, credits, net zero) | **High** | [P2 Carbon Accounting](p2-carbon-accounting-numericals.md) |\n| **CO4** | **Numerical: SSCM distribution networks, logistics cost, distance criteria** | **High** | [P3 Network & Logistics](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md) |\n| **CO5** | **Numerical: effective performance measurement systems** (KPIs, OEE, dashboards, TAS) | Medium | [P4 Performance Measurement](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md) |\n| **CO6** | 3-mark short theory: technology, quality, best practices, future transitions | Low | [Ch 5](ch05-technology-quality-tools.md), [Ch 10](ch10-sscm-best-practices.md) |\n\n- **End-sem**: 40 marks, pen and paper, whole syllabus including cases. CO2–CO5 are mainly numericals, with theory, interpretation and application sub-questions inside them.\n- **Online quiz (OLT)**: 20 marks of MCQs on **CO5 and CO6**, from **PPTs 9, 10 and 11 only** (Lecture 11) 📘. The [MCQ Bank](mcq-bank.md) has Q1–Q120 on exactly those three lectures.\n\n## About the numericals ⭐⭐⭐\nEvery numerical that appears in the slides is reproduced as the **exact problem from the zip**: the Lecture 6 weighted-score model and VoltTech scorecard, TOPSIS and the risk matrix; the carbon deck's ICEV + EV plant (Scopes 1, 2, 3); the Lecture 8 network A/B/C and the 10,000-returns allocation; the Lecture 10 FMCG distribution network; the Lecture 3 dashboard; and the Lecture 9 TAS / Navya exercise. Each comes with a full worked answer. The slides give the questions but rarely the answers. Where a slide's own arithmetic is inconsistent (the VoltTech band, the TOPSIS ranks, the carbon-credit ranges), the page says so and shows the correct working. Each page then adds **practice problems** (📗) in the same style, with answers hidden under Self-test. Where a question cannot be solved without an assumption, the assumption is written in **bold** before the working. Do the same in the exam: **state the assumption, show the formula, show the substitution, box the answer, interpret it in one line, and recommend.**\n\n## How to use this site\n- **Start** with [Rapid Revision](rapid-revision.md): every formula, list and number on one page.\n- For each CO, read the **theory page**, then attempt the **numericals page** with **Self-test** on (press `T`). Answers stay hidden until you reveal them.\n- Use the **[Exam Q&A Bank](exam-questions-qna.md)** for 3-mark and long answers, the **[MCQ Bank](mcq-bank.md)** for the quiz, the **[Case Studies](case-studies.md)** for examples to quote, and the **[Glossary](glossary.md)**, Flashcards and Quiz for terms.\n- ⭐⭐⭐ = high-yield, ⭐⭐ = important, ⭐ = good to know.\n\n## Suggested 4-day plan\n| Day | Morning | Afternoon | Evening |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ch 2 carbon + **P2 Carbon Accounting** (do the auto-plant case by hand) | Ch 6–7 logistics and network + **P3** Problems 1–5 | P3 Problems 6–11, MCQs on Lectures 4–8 |\n| 2 | Ch 3 procurement + **P1 Supplier Selection** (weighted score, VoltTech, TOPSIS) | Ch 8 + **P4 Performance Measurement** Problems 1–7 | P4 Problems 8–13 (TAS, Navya) |\n| 3 | **Quiz prep**: Lectures 9, 10, 11 (Ch 5, Ch 8, Ch 10) + MCQ Q1–Q120 | Ch 1 and Ch 4 + CO1 3-markers | Ch 9 energy + P5 LCA & Energy |\n| 4 | Redo every ⭐⭐⭐ numerical once, without looking | Case studies + Q&A bank long answers | Rapid revision, glossary flashcards |\n\n## Exam-day answer tips ⭐⭐\n- **Supplier scoring (CO2)**: table of score × weight → total → rank → *sensitivity* (does a weight change flip the winner?) → knock-out criteria (human rights, safety) → recommendation with a development plan for the runner-up.\n- **Carbon (CO3)**: classify each source by scope **first**, then activity × factor (show units, convert kg → t), then a summary table with shares, intensity per unit, and the top lever (usually Scope 3 purchased goods or use phase).\n- **Network (CO4)**: write the objective Z = C + λE + μW, compute cost *and* CO₂ for each option, find the switching λ or the minimum-cost number of DCs, then check service (OTIF) and resilience.\n- **Performance (CO5)**: formula → value → compare with target → leading or lagging → owner → corrective action. Finish with the five tests (material, balanced, actionable, comparable, credible).\n- **3-markers (CO1, CO6)**: definition + three points + one Indian example.\n- Draw: the closed-loop network (nine nodes), the R-ladder, the net-zero hierarchy, the Scope 1-2-3 boundary diagram, the technology decision matrix, the SSCM performance loop.\n"}, {"slug": "rapid-revision", "label": "Rapid Revision", "tag": "★", "group": "start", "title": "Rapid Revision — Everything on One Page", "md": "# Rapid Revision — Everything on One Page\nThe night-before sheet: every formula, list and headline number from the ten syllabus topics and the five numerical pages. Click through to the full pages when something is unclear. 📘 = course material, 📗 = textbook or practice data.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Exam pattern (Lecture 11) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Quiz (OLT), 20 marks**: MCQs on **CO5 + CO6**, from **PPTs 9, 10 and 11 only** → drill the [MCQ Bank](mcq-bank.md) Q1–Q120.\n- **End-sem, 40 marks**, pen and paper, full syllabus including cases.\n- **Weightage**: CO3 and CO4 **high**; CO2 and CO5 **medium**; CO1 and CO6 **low** (3-mark theory questions).\n- **Answer recipe for every numerical**: state assumptions → formula → substitution → boxed answer with units → one-line interpretation → one managerial recommendation.\n\n## 1. CO2 — Weighted scoring of suppliers ⭐⭐⭐ ([P1](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md))\n- **Steps**: criteria → weights summing to 100% → score each supplier (same scale) → **Σ (score × weight)** → rank → sensitivity → recommend with conditions.\n- **Gate + score**: knock-out thresholds (human rights, safety, legal compliance) *before* weighting. A 95/100 supplier with labour violations is suspended, not retained.\n- **Normalising**: benefit criterion = x ÷ max; cost criterion = min ÷ x; or min–max (x − min) ÷ (max − min).\n- **Lecture 6 example (slide 24)**: weights Price 25, Quality 20, Delivery 15, Carbon 15, Safety 10, Innovation 10, Compliance 5 → **A 8.10, B 7.70, C 8.15 → C wins** (C > A > B).\n- **VoltTech scorecard**: 0.25×88 + 0.20×90 + 0.15×85 + 0.15×80 + 0.10×92 + 0.10×78 + 0.05×95 = **86.50 → B (Good)** on the 90/75/60 bands (slide's \"86.2 A\" is inconsistent). Weak: innovation 78, carbon 80.\n- **Lecture 10 supplier scorecard** weights: cost 20, quality 20, delivery 15, environment 15, labour & HR 10, carbon 10, governance 5, innovation 5. X (labour 10) = **72.75**, Y = **81.25**.\n- **TOPSIS**: vector-normalise rᵢⱼ = xᵢⱼ ÷ √Σx² → weight → ideal A⁺ / anti-ideal A⁻ (max for benefit, min for cost) → S⁺, S⁻ (Euclidean) → **Cᵢ* = S⁻ ÷ (S⁺ + S⁻)** → rank high to low. Construction case: **C wins** (slide Cᵢ* 0.608 > A 0.382 > B 0.251 > D 0.106).\n- **Risk matrix**: Likelihood × Impact (1–5): **High 15–24 (25 extreme), Medium 6–14, Low 1–5**. Agri-food: A 4×4 = 16 high, B 3×3 = 9 medium, C 2×2 = 4 low.\n- **AHP**: pairwise 1–9 scale → normalise columns → row averages = weights → **CR = CI ÷ RI < 0.10**, CI = (λmax − n) ÷ (n − 1).\n- **Kraljic**: profit impact × supply risk → **Strategic** (partner), **Bottleneck** (secure supply), **Leverage** (compete), **Routine/non-critical** (simplify).\n- **TCO** = price + transport + duties + quality + energy/maintenance + end-of-life + risk. Lowest price ≠ lowest TCO.\n- **ESG + RTI** criteria families: Environmental, Social, Governance + Risk, Technology, Innovation.\n- **Tools**: weighted scoring, AHP, TOPSIS, DEA, LP (+ expert judgement, audits).\n\n## 2. CO3 — Carbon accounting, Scope 1, 2, 3 ⭐⭐⭐ ([P2](p2-carbon-accounting-numericals.md))\n- **Emissions = Activity data × Emission factor**; **CO₂e = gas mass × GWP**.\n- **Factors (course)**: diesel **2.68 kg/l**; natural gas **1.9 kg/Sm³**; LPG **3.0 t/t**; Indian grid **0.70 t/MWh** (= kg/kWh); petrol car 120 g/km.\n- **GWP-100**: CO₂ 1 · CH₄ 27–30 · N₂O 273 · HFCs 12–14,000 (HFC-134a ≈ 1,430) · PFCs 7,000–12,000 · SF₆ 25,200 · NF₃ 17,000. Kyoto basket = 7 gases (1997).\n- **Scope 1** \"what you burn\" (fuel, company vehicles, process, fugitive) · **Scope 2** \"what you buy\" (electricity, heat, steam, cooling) · **Scope 3** \"what you influence\" (15 categories; usually **70–90%**).\n- **Scope 3 categories**: upstream 1 purchased goods, 2 capital goods, 3 fuel & energy, 4 upstream transport, 5 waste, 6 business travel, 7 commuting, 8 upstream leased · downstream 9 downstream transport, 10 processing, 11 use of sold products, 12 end-of-life, 13 downstream leased, 14 franchises, 15 investments.\n- **5 steps**: identify sources → collect activity data → apply factors → convert to CO₂e → aggregate & report (MRV).\n- **Location-based** Scope 2 = grid average; **market-based** = contracts (PPA, RECs).\n- **Auto plant case (50,000 ICE + 20,000 EV)**: Scope 1 = 950 + 536 + 150 = **1,636 t**; Scope 2 = 25,000 × 0.70 = **17,500 t**; materials 300,000 + 200,000; use phase ICE 18 t/car → **9,00,000 t**, EV 22,500 kWh × 0.70 = 15.75 t/car → **3,15,000 t**; logistics 23,500 t. ICEV line **12,42,636 t**, EV line **5,15,000 t**, plant **17,57,636 t**. Scope 1+2 = **1.09%**, Scope 3 = **98.9%**.\n- Per vehicle (slide): ICE ~25 t; EV India grid ~28 t; EV RE grid ~13 t. Break-even grid ≈ **0.58 kg/kWh** (12 t mfg) or 0.667 (10 t).\n- **Diesel example**: 10,000 l × 2.68 = **26,800 kg**. GWP example: 10 t CH₄ = 280, 2 t N₂O = 546, 0.1 t SF₆ = 2,520 → **3,346 t CO₂e**.\n- **Practice inventory (P3)**: Scope 1 645.5, Scope 2 2,800, Scope 3 10,700 → **14,145.5 t**; shares 4.6 / 19.8 / 75.6%. Solar PPA 40% → saves 1,120 t (7.9% of total). Green steel beats everything inside the fence.\n- **Carbon credit = 1 t CO₂e**. Avoidance, reduction, removal (removal priced higher). Registries: Verra, Gold Standard, ACR, CAR. Compliance (EU ETS, India **CCTS** 2023) vs voluntary.\n- **Neutral** (offsets) vs **net zero** (cut 90–95%, neutralise residual with removals) vs carbon zero vs climate positive. **\"Reduction first, offset later.\"**\n- Standards: **GHG Protocol** (WRI + WBCSD), IPCC (GWPs), **ISO 14064** (verification), UNFCCC. Reporting: CDP, **GRI 305**, TCFD (4 pillars), IR (6 capitals), **BRSR** (9 principles, Sections A–C).\n- **CBAM**: EU levy on embedded emissions of imports (steel, aluminium, cement, fertiliser, hydrogen, electricity); verified MRV avoids punitive default values.\n\n## 3. CO4 — Distribution networks, logistics cost, distance criteria ⭐⭐⭐ ([P3](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md))\n- **Objective**: minimise **Z = C_total + λ·E_CO₂ + μ·W_waste**; C_total = facility + production + transport + inventory + collection + recovery + disposal.\n- **Variables**: xᵢⱼ flows, yⱼ open/close (0/1), rᵢ recovered quantity. **Constraints**: demand, capacity, material balance, transport, recovery, collection, recycling targets, non-negativity. Look for a **Pareto-efficient** solution.\n- **Material balance**: Returns = Reuse + Repair + Remanufacture + Recycling + Disposal.\n- **Network A/B/C (Lecture 8)**: switching λ: A = B at **λ = 0.32**, B = C at **λ = 0.35** → A if λ < 0.32, B between, C above. \"No single answer without decision-maker priorities.\"\n- **10,000 returns**: 2,000 / 2,500 / 2,000 / 3,000 / 500 → recovery **95%**, value-retaining **65%**. Practice economics: **₹96.25 lakh** net value, **228 t** CO₂ avoided.\n- **FMCG case (Lecture 10)**: 10,000 deliveries × 180 km, 62% utilisation, 18% empty → total 21.95 lakh km, 5.49 lakh l, **1,470.7 t CO₂/month**, **146 g/t-km**. At 80% utilisation and 10% empty → 1,038.5 t (**−29.4%**), 103 g/t-km, ₹1.48 cr/month saved. Grid-charged e-trucks emit *more* (390.6 vs 311.6 t).\n- **Five FMCG KPIs**: CO₂e per t-km, fuel efficiency km/l, vehicle utilisation %, empty km %, OTIF %.\n- **Transport CO₂** = t × km × factor (road 0.09, rail 0.025, water 0.015, air 0.60 kg/t-km 📗), or litres × 2.68. Intensity: **Air > Road > Rail > Water**.\n- **Centre of gravity**: Cx = Σxᵢwᵢ ÷ Σwᵢ, Cy = Σyᵢwᵢ ÷ Σwᵢ. Practice: CoG (3.11, 3.61).\n- **Load-distance** = Σ load × distance (Euclidean √(Δx² + Δy²) or rectilinear |Δx| + |Δy|). Practice: **Pune 64,629** < Hyderabad 69,998 < Nagpur 76,550 → Pune (107 t CO₂/yr less than Nagpur).\n- **Factor rating**: Σ weight × score → Y 7.30 > X 7.20 > Z 6.65.\n- **How many DCs?** Total cost U-shape: 1 → 32.0, **2 → 30.1**, 3 → 31.4, 4 → 34.4, 5 → 38.2 (₹ crore). More DCs: outbound ↓, facility + inventory ↑ (square-root law: inventory ∝ √n).\n- **Cube**: 800 → 1,000 units per truck → 25 trips saved (−20%), 8.4 t CO₂ a month.\n- **Decision levels**: strategic (where, how big), tactical (allocation, modes), operational (routing, frequency, consolidation).\n- **9 nodes of the closed loop**: suppliers → plants → DCs → customers → collection → inspection/sorting → repair/reman/recycle → recovered materials → manufacturing again.\n- **Collection networks**: centralised (scale, high-value low-volume), decentralised (local sorting, bulky high-volume), hybrid.\n- **\"The cleanest kilometre is often the kilometre eliminated.\"** Green transport ≠ sustainable logistics.\n\n## 4. CO5 — Effective performance measurement systems ⭐⭐⭐ ([P4](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md))\n- **OTIF** = orders on time *and* in full ÷ total (joint count, not OT × IF). **Fill rate** = units shipped ÷ ordered. **Perfect order** = OTIF × damage-free × correct docs (if independent).\n- Practice: OT 94.0%, IF 95.5%, **OTIF 91.5%**, fill rate **97.2%**, perfect order **89.7%**.\n- **OEE** = Availability × Performance × Quality (world class ≈ 85%). Practice: 87.5% × 89.5% × 96.0% = **75.2%**.\n- **Intensity**: t CO₂e ÷ output or ÷ ₹ crore revenue (BRSR per rupee of turnover, PPP-adjusted). Practice: 7.4 t/₹ cr, 14.8 kg/unit, 76 MJ/unit, 248 L/unit.\n- **LTIFR** = LTIs × 10⁶ ÷ person-hours → practice **0.79**.\n- **Achievement**: higher-better actual ÷ target; lower-better target ÷ actual; cap at 1.2; **composite = Σ weight × capped achievement** (Lecture 3 dashboard 1.117; BSC 1.003).\n- **Required annual improvement** to reach a benchmark in n years = 1 − (benchmark ÷ current)^(1/n) → practice 9.0%/yr.\n- **Leading** (training, RE contracts, supplier audits) vs **lagging** (injuries, actual emissions). Pair each lagging KPI with its drivers.\n- **Four KPI questions**: What happened? Why did it happen? Who owns it? What decision will change?\n- **KPI trap**: 75 + 40 + 30 indicators, no answer on whether the chain is more sustainable.\n- **Five tests**: **Material, Balanced, Actionable, Comparable, Credible**. \"If a KPI does not change a decision, question why it exists.\"\n- **SMART**: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound.\n- **Q + D + I + R + S** dimensions (Lecture 3): Quality, Delivery, Innovation, Resilience, Sustainability.\n- **Sustainability BSC**: financial, customer, internal process, learning & growth + environmental & social.\n- **SCOR + sustainability**: Plan, Source, Make, Deliver, Return, Enable, each with a green KPI.\n- **Performance loop (7)**: Identify → Measure → Target → Improve → Verify → Report → Review.\n- **TAS** = 0.25 Econ + 0.25 Env + 0.20 Social + 0.15 Resilience + 0.15 Strategic fit (1–5). **NTV** = gross benefits − adoption cost − lifecycle sustainability cost − risk cost.\n- **Navya (practice)**: gross ₹46 cr, NTV ₹28 cr, NPV ₹20.9 cr, payback 2.9 yrs, TAS 3.80 → **pilot first**.\n- **Decision matrix**: high value + high impact **Prioritise**; low value + high impact **Reconsider**; high value + low impact **Optimise**; low + low **Avoid**.\n- **Cost of poor quality**: defects → rework energy, scrap, extra trips. \"Quality failure = environmental failure.\"\n- **Company A vs B**: a good report ≠ good performance; check boundary, baseline, assurance.\n\n## 5. LCA, LCSA and energy numericals ⭐⭐ ([P5](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md))\n- **ISO 14040/44 phases**: Goal & scope (functional unit, boundary) → Inventory (LCI) → Impact assessment (LCIA) → Interpretation (iterative).\n- **Functional unit**: \"carry 20 kg of groceries a month\", not \"one bag\". Bags example: 25 vs 10 kg; reusable bag breaks even at **60 uses**.\n- **Boundaries**: cradle-to-gate, gate-to-gate, cradle-to-grave, cradle-to-cradle.\n- **LCSA = E-LCA + LCC + S-LCA**.\n- **LED vs incandescent** (practice): ₹2,050 & 157.5 kg vs ₹13,000 & 1,050 kg; payback 515 h.\n- **EV vs ICE, India WTW**: 0.146 vs **0.135 kg/km**; EV wins below a grid of about 0.63–0.73 kg/kWh.\n- **Logistic (S-curve) adoption**: EV share 12.9 / 45.9 / 83.0%; 30% reached in 8.0 yrs.\n- **Energy chain facts**: T&D losses **25.4%**; coal plant ~22% efficient; TTW 15–32%; 1.2 Mt of batteries by 2030, ~5% recycled; lithium brine ~500,000 gallons water per tonne.\n\n---\n\n## 6. CO1 — SSCM and business performance (3-markers) ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 1](ch01-sscm-business-performance.md))\n- **SSCM** = managing material, information and capital flows and co-operation along the chain while integrating **economic, environmental and social** goals from stakeholder requirements.\n- **TBL / 3P**: People, Planet, Profit. Resilience is the **fourth pillar** (Lecture 9).\n- Linear (take–make–use–dispose, cradle-to-grave) vs circular (cradle-to-cradle).\n- **5-step strategy**: assess current state → stakeholders → scenarios → design → operationalise.\n- **Risks of ignoring**: CBAM and carbon taxes, disruptions, reputation, investor pressure, customer loss.\n- **Business impact**: cost ↓ (energy, waste, logistics), revenue ↑ (green markets), risk ↓, cost of capital ↓, brand and talent, innovation.\n\n## 7. Topic 2 — LCA and carbon footprints ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 2](ch02-lca-carbon-footprints.md))\n- Carbon footprint = one impact category (climate). LCA = multi-impact (climate, ozone, smog, water, land, ecotoxicity, biodiversity).\n- **Net-zero hierarchy**: Measure → Reduce → RE transition → Sustainable supply chain → Offset (afforestation, CCUS).\n- **MRV**: Measure, Report, Verify. LCA limitations: data quality, allocation, boundary choice, cost, time.\n\n## 8. Topic 3 — Sustainable procurement ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 3](ch03-sustainable-procurement.md))\n- Procurement = **50–80% of cost** and the largest Scope 3 category.\n- **Six challenges**: higher upfront cost, supplier resistance, limited data, greenwashing, poor visibility, regulatory uncertainty.\n- Cases: **Walmart Project Gigaton**, Schneider Zero Carbon, **Nestlé** responsible sourcing, M&M supplier code (95% by value).\n\n## 9. Topic 4 — Sustainable manufacturing and productivity ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 4](ch04-sustainable-manufacturing.md))\n- **UNEP definition**: economically sound processes that minimise environmental impact, conserve energy and resources, and keep employees, communities and consumers safe.\n- **Framework**: Inputs → Process → Outputs → Recycling → Reuse → Remanufacturing → Closed loop.\n- **Principles**: lean (Toyota TPS), Six Sigma (3.4 DPMO), kaizen, circular economy (Interface, Michelin), cleaner production (3M 3P), eco-design (Philips, IKEA), life-cycle thinking (Intel, Tata Steel).\n- **TIMWOOD**: Transport, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Over-production, Over-processing, Defects (+ Skills).\n- **Productivity chain**: resource efficiency → less waste → lower cost → higher productivity → profitability → competitive advantage.\n- Takeaway: **Cleaner, Leaner, Smarter, Profitable, Resilient**.\n\n## 10. CO6 — Technology and quality tools ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 5](ch05-technology-quality-tools.md))\n- **Technology ≠ sustainability**: energy use, e-waste, capex, cyber risk; potential ≠ realised benefit.\n- **Five trade-offs**: efficiency vs footprint; traceability vs energy; visibility vs privacy; global optimisation vs local resilience; digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion.\n- **Tech-Sustain (7)**: define problem → baseline → alternatives → lifecycle impact → TBL → stress-test → pilot, measure, scale.\n- Cases: **Walmart–IBM blockchain** (mango trace 7 days → 2.2 s), Asian Paints.\n- **ISO's seven quality principles**: customer focus, leadership, engagement, process approach, improvement, evidence-based decisions, relationship management. ISO 14001 = EMS; integrated management system = 9001 + 14001 + 45001.\n\n## 11. Topic 6 — Sustainable logistics ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 6](ch06-sustainable-logistics.md))\n- **Green logistics levers**: modal shift, route optimisation, consolidation, EV/alternative fuel, green warehousing (solar, LED, natural light), right-sized returnable packaging.\n- **DHL's six levers**; Indian cases: Hindustan Zinc LNG/EV trucks, GreenLine LNG fleet.\n- **Recovery hierarchy**: reuse → repair → refurbish → remanufacture → recycle → energy recovery → disposal.\n- **Why reverse logistics is hard**: uncertain quantity, timing and condition; low density; high sorting cost; information gaps; non-standard products; dependence on consumers.\n\n## 12. Topic 7 — Designing the sustainable network ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 7](ch07-network-design.md))\n- **R-ladder**: Refuse, Reduce, Reuse, Repair, Refurbish, Remanufacture, Repurpose, Recycle. \"Recycling is usually lower in value retention.\"\n- **PaaS**: CNH harvester rental, SDLG equipment leasing, JSW MG Windsor battery-as-a-service, Philips/Signify lighting-as-a-service, Furlenco furniture. The maker keeps economic incentive + physical control + material value.\n- **Four integrated questions**: where to locate facilities, which flows to move, which transport mode, what happens to returns. Together they set **cost + carbon + circularity**.\n\n## 13. Topic 8 — Measuring SSCM performance ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 8](ch08-performance-measurement.md))\n- **GRI** (impacts, all stakeholders) · **TCFD/ISSB** (investors, climate risk) · **BRSR / BRSR Core** (SEBI, assured KPIs) · **CDP**.\n- **Greenwashing** (overclaim) vs **greenhushing** (underclaim). Credible claim = metric + boundary + method + baseline + target + evidence.\n\n## 14. Topic 9 — Energy-sector supply chain ⭐⭐ ([Ch 9](ch09-energy-sector.md))\n- **Supplies** (fuels, critical minerals: Li, Co, Ni, graphite, rare earths) → **Production** (coal-heavy grid, RE, hydrogen) → **End use** (transport, TTW vs WTW) → **Disposal** (batteries, solar panels, EPR).\n- **Critical-minerals paradox**: decarbonisation multiplies demand for minerals whose extraction carries water, biodiversity, social and geopolitical risk.\n- **Tesla EoL batteries**: reuse/second life first, then hydrometallurgical recycling; partner (Redwood, Li-Cycle) before building in-house.\n\n## 15. Topic 10 — SSCM best practices ⭐⭐⭐ ([Ch 10](ch10-sscm-best-practices.md))\n- **Polycrisis (6)**: climate, geopolitics, critical resources, regulation, social risk, technology disruption.\n- **Strategy architecture**: **D-P-R-C-M-I-S** — Diagnose, Prioritise, Redesign, Collaborate, Measure, Innovate, Scale.\n- **Five strategies**: sustainable procurement (\"do not outsource sustainability\"), product design, circular chains, sustainable logistics, digital chains (IoT, AI, digital twin, blockchain, **DPP**).\n- **JIT + JIC + JIS**; mitigation vs adaptation.\n- **Eight transitions**: fossil → renewable; virgin → circular materials; linear → circular products; concentration → diversification; opaque → traceable; ownership → access; reactive → predictive; reporting → embedded in decisions.\n- **Best-practice cases**: Apple, Walmart Gigaton, Schneider, Unilever, Nestlé, IKEA, Renault Refactory, DHL, Toyota; **ITC eChoupal**, HUL, Tata Motors Re.Wi.Re, Mahindra, Tata Steel, Asian Paints, Dalmia, Arvind, JSW Steel, Hindustan Zinc ([Case Studies](case-studies.md)).\n"}, {"slug": "p1-supplier-selection-numericals", "label": "CO2 Supplier Weighted Scoring", "tag": "P1", "group": "focus", "title": "CO2 Numericals: Supplier Weighted Scoring, Scorecards & TOPSIS", "md": "# CO2 Numericals: Supplier Weighted Scoring, Scorecards & TOPSIS\nThis page covers **CO2: weighted scoring of suppliers**, the numerical your faculty named for CO2. It starts with the **exact problems from Lecture 6** 📘 (the weighted score model, the VoltTech supplier scorecard, the TOPSIS construction example and the supplier risk matrix) and the **Lecture 10 supplier sustainability scorecard** 📘, each solved step by step. After those come practice problems built in the same style (📗 = practice data, not from the course files). Turn on **Self-test** (press `T`) to hide the answers.\n\nTheory is on [Sustainable Procurement & Supplier Selection](ch03-sustainable-procurement.md).\n\n> **Exam weightage reminder (Lecture 11, last slide 📘):** the 40-mark end-sem paper weights **CO3 and CO4 high, CO2 and CO5 medium, CO1 and CO6 low**. Expect one full CO2 numerical with theory/interpretation sub-parts.\n\n---\n\n## The method you will use every time ⭐⭐⭐\n**Step 1 — Criteria and weights.** List the criteria (price, quality, delivery, carbon, safety, innovation, compliance…). Weights must add to **100% (or 1.00)**. Convert % to decimals: 25% → 0.25.\n\n**Step 2 — Score each supplier on each criterion** on a common scale (out of 10, or out of 100). The scale must run in **one direction: higher = better**, even for \"cost-type\" criteria like price or carbon. A supplier with the lowest price gets the highest *price score*.\n\n**Step 3 — Weighted score = Score × Weight** (Lecture 6 formula: *Weighted Score = (Score out of 10) × (Weight)*).\n\n**Step 4 — Total weighted score = Σ (Score × Weight)** for each supplier.\n\n**Step 5 — Rank, recommend, and interpret.** Highest total wins, *but* always write two more lines in the exam:\n1. **Margin check** — if the gap is small (say < 0.2 on a 10-point scale), the decision is sensitive; run a sensitivity test or a pilot order.\n2. **Gate check** — weighted scoring is *compensatory* (a high score on price can hide a terrible score on labour). Non-negotiables (child labour, forced labour, serious safety or legal violations) must be **knock-out criteria applied before scoring**, not just low scores.\n\n**Lecture 6 scoring guide (out of 10):** 9–10 Excellent · 7–8 Good · 5–6 Average · 3–4 Poor · 0–2 Very poor. Tip on the slide: *customise weights to organisational priorities; base scores on data and evidence.*\n\n**Converting raw data into scores** (when the question gives ₹, ppm, kg CO₂e instead of scores):\n- *Benefit criterion* (higher is better, e.g. quality score, patents): Score = x ÷ x_max × 10 (ratio method) or 1 + 9 × (x − x_min) ÷ (x_max − x_min) (min–max method).\n- *Cost criterion* (lower is better, e.g. price, defects, CO₂e, LTIFR): Score = x_min ÷ x × 10 (ratio method) or 1 + 9 × (x_max − x) ÷ (x_max − x_min) (min–max method).\n- State which method you use. Both are accepted; ratio normalisation is gentler, min–max exaggerates gaps.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 1. Weighted Score Model — Suppliers A, B and C (Lecture 6, slide 24) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"Demonstrating Weighted Scoring for Supplier Evaluation — Easy Classroom Example.\"* Scores are out of 10.\n\n| Criteria | Weight (%) | Weight (decimal) | Supplier A score | Supplier B score | Supplier C score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Price | 25% | 0.25 | 8 | 7 | 9 |\n| Quality | 20% | 0.20 | 9 | 8 | 7 |\n| Delivery | 15% | 0.15 | 7 | 9 | 8 |\n| Carbon | 15% | 0.15 | 8 | 6 | 9 |\n| Safety | 10% | 0.10 | 9 | 8 | 7 |\n| Innovation | 10% | 0.10 | 7 | 9 | 8 |\n| Compliance | 5% | 0.05 | 9 | 8 | 9 |\n| **Total** | **100%** | **1.00** | | | |\n\n**Q1.1 Calculate the total weighted score of each supplier.**\nWeighted score = Score × Weight (decimal).\n\n| Criteria (weight) | Supplier A | Supplier B | Supplier C |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Price (0.25) | 8 × 0.25 = 2.00 | 7 × 0.25 = 1.75 | 9 × 0.25 = 2.25 |\n| Quality (0.20) | 9 × 0.20 = 1.80 | 8 × 0.20 = 1.60 | 7 × 0.20 = 1.40 |\n| Delivery (0.15) | 7 × 0.15 = 1.05 | 9 × 0.15 = 1.35 | 8 × 0.15 = 1.20 |\n| Carbon (0.15) | 8 × 0.15 = 1.20 | 6 × 0.15 = 0.90 | 9 × 0.15 = 1.35 |\n| Safety (0.10) | 9 × 0.10 = 0.90 | 8 × 0.10 = 0.80 | 7 × 0.10 = 0.70 |\n| Innovation (0.10) | 7 × 0.10 = 0.70 | 9 × 0.10 = 0.90 | 8 × 0.10 = 0.80 |\n| Compliance (0.05) | 9 × 0.05 = 0.45 | 8 × 0.05 = 0.40 | 9 × 0.05 = 0.45 |\n| **Total weighted score** | **8.10 / 10** | **7.70 / 10** | **8.15 / 10** |\n\n**Q1.2 Rank the suppliers and state the winner.**\n1st **Supplier C (8.15)**, 2nd Supplier A (8.10), 3rd Supplier B (7.70). The slide declares **Supplier C the winner** with 8.15/10.\n\n**Q1.3 Interpret the result. Is the decision robust?**\nC wins on the two \"big\" criteria for sustainability: **price (9 → 2.25)** and **carbon (9 → 1.35)**. But A is only **0.05 behind**, and A beats C on quality (9 vs 7) and safety (9 vs 7). With a 0.05 margin the decision is *not* robust: a one-point change in a single score flips it. Recommended answer: select **C as primary supplier**, keep **A as qualified second source**, and write quality and safety improvement targets into C's contract (because C scores only 7 on both). B is dragged down by the weakest carbon score (6).\n\n**Q1.4 Sensitivity: the company raises Carbon to 25% and cuts Price to 15%. Does the ranking change?**\nOnly the price and carbon rows change (−0.10 on price weight, +0.10 on carbon weight):\n- A: 8.10 − (8 × 0.10) + (8 × 0.10) = **8.10** (A scores 8 on both, so no change)\n- B: 7.70 − (7 × 0.10) + (6 × 0.10) = **7.60**\n- C: 8.15 − (9 × 0.10) + (9 × 0.10) = **8.15**\n\nRanking unchanged: **C > A > B**. B falls further because it is the highest-carbon supplier.\n\n**Q1.5 Sensitivity: management instead raises Quality to 30% and cuts Price to 15%. Does C still win?**\nPrice weight −0.10, quality weight +0.10:\n- A: 8.10 − 0.80 + 0.90 = **8.20**\n- B: 7.70 − 0.70 + 0.80 = **7.80**\n- C: 8.15 − 0.90 + 0.70 = **7.95**\n\nNow **A wins (8.20)**, then C (7.95), then B (7.80). The choice flips when the firm values quality over price. This is exactly why the slide's tip says *customise weights based on organisational priorities*.\n\n**Q1.6 What carbon score would Supplier B need to tie with Supplier C?**\nGap = 8.15 − 7.70 = 0.45. Each carbon point is worth 0.15. Points needed = 0.45 ÷ 0.15 = **3 points**, so B's carbon score would have to rise from 6 to **9**. This is a supplier-development target you could put to B (e.g. renewable power, ISO 14001).\n\n---\n\n## Problem 2. Supplier Scorecard — VoltTech Components Pvt. Ltd. (Lecture 6, slide 22) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nSupplier: VoltTech Components Pvt. Ltd. · Category: electrical components (wires & connectors) · Industry: electrical appliances · Period: Apr–Jun 2025. Scores out of 100.\n\n| Criteria | Weight | Scoring method / metric | Supplier score (/100) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Price | 25% | Delivered price vs benchmark (best price = 100) | 88 |\n| Quality | 20% | PPM defects, First Pass Yield, return rate | 90 |\n| Delivery | 15% | On-time delivery %, lead-time adherence | 85 |\n| Carbon | 15% | CO₂e emissions (t) per unit vs industry benchmark | 80 |\n| Safety | 10% | TRIR, lost-time injury, safety initiatives | 92 |\n| Innovation | 10% | New product ideas, process improvement, R&D investment | 78 |\n| Compliance | 5% | Legal, environmental, social compliance, certifications | 95 |\n\nScore range guide on the slide: **90–100 A (Excellent) · 75–89 B (Good) · 60–74 C (Average) · < 60 D (Poor)**. Trend over the last four quarters: 78.4 (Q3 FY24) → 81.2 → 83.6 → 86.2 (Q2 FY25).\n\n**Q2.1 Calculate the overall weighted score.**\n\n| Criteria | Weight × Score | Weighted score |\n|---|---|---|\n| Price | 0.25 × 88 | 22.00 |\n| Quality | 0.20 × 90 | 18.00 |\n| Delivery | 0.15 × 85 | 12.75 |\n| Carbon | 0.15 × 80 | 12.00 |\n| Safety | 0.10 × 92 | 9.20 |\n| Innovation | 0.10 × 78 | 7.80 |\n| Compliance | 0.05 × 95 | 4.75 |\n| **Total** | | **86.50 / 100** |\n\n**Q2.2 What performance rating does VoltTech get?**\nBy the slide's own range guide, **86.5 falls in 75–89 → \"B (Good)\"**. Note: the slide's headline box shows \"86.2/100, A (Excellent)\". Both numbers are inconsistent with the table: the table adds to **86.50**, and any score below 90 is a **B** on the printed guide. In the exam, show the arithmetic (86.50) and apply the guide (B, Good); mention that it is the best-ever quarter and the trend is rising.\n\n**Q2.3 Identify strengths and improvement areas, and suggest actions.**\n- *Strengths:* compliance (95), safety (92) and quality (90) — the slide lists \"strong quality performance, excellent compliance & safety\".\n- *Improvement areas:* **innovation (78)** and **carbon (80)** — the two lowest scores, exactly the slide's \"innovation capability\" and \"carbon footprint reduction\".\n- *Actions:* joint innovation/value-engineering workshops with VoltTech; a carbon-reduction plan (renewable electricity, copper scrap recovery, low-carbon PVC), with a target of carbon ≥ 88 next year; keep quarterly reviews.\n- *What the gain is worth:* raising carbon from 80 to 90 adds 0.15 × 10 = **1.5 points**; raising innovation from 78 to 88 adds 0.10 × 10 = **1.0 point**. Both together take the score to 89.0, close to the A band.\n\n**Q2.4 Quarter-on-quarter improvement?**\nFrom 78.4 to 86.2 over three quarters = +7.8 points (+9.9%). Average gain ≈ 2.6 points per quarter. Using the corrected 86.5, the gain is +8.1 points (+10.3%).\n\n---\n\n## Problem 3. TOPSIS — Supplier Selection in Construction (Lecture 6, slide 25) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*Project: residential building · Material: TMT steel bars · Requirement: 500 MT · Goal: select the best supplier.* TOPSIS = Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution: **choose the supplier closest to the ideal solution and farthest from the worst (negative-ideal) solution.**\n\n**Decision criteria:**\n\n| Criterion | Type | Weight (w) |\n|---|---|---|\n| C1 — Price (INR/MT) | Cost | 0.30 |\n| C2 — Quality (score) | Benefit | 0.25 |\n| C3 — Delivery performance (%) | Benefit | 0.20 |\n| C4 — Safety performance (score) | Benefit | 0.15 |\n| C5 — Sustainability (carbon score) | Benefit | 0.10 |\n\n**Step 1 — Decision matrix (X):**\n\n| Supplier | C1 Price | C2 Quality | C3 Delivery % | C4 Safety | C5 Sustainability |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A (BuildStrong Pvt. Ltd.) | 56,000 | 8 | 92 | 9 | 7 |\n| B (Apex Infra Materials) | 52,000 | 7 | 90 | 8 | 6 |\n| C (ConstructPro Solutions) | 54,000 | 9 | 95 | 9 | 8 |\n| D (SolidBase Industries) | 58,000 | 6 | 88 | 7 | 7 |\n\n**Q3.1 Step 2 — Normalise the decision matrix (vector normalisation).**\nr_ij = x_ij ÷ √(Σ x_ij²) for each column.\n\nColumn norms: C1 = √(56,000² + 52,000² + 54,000² + 58,000²) = **110,090.9**; C2 = √(64+49+81+36) = √230 = **15.166**; C3 = √(8,464+8,100+9,025+7,744) = √33,333 = **182.57**; C4 = √(81+64+81+49) = √275 = **16.583**; C5 = √(49+36+64+49) = √198 = **14.071**.\n\n| Supplier | C1 | C2 | C3 | C4 | C5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 56,000/110,091 = 0.509 | 8/15.166 = 0.528 | 92/182.57 = 0.504 | 9/16.583 = 0.543 | 7/14.071 = 0.497 |\n| B | 0.472 | 0.462 | 0.493 | 0.482 | 0.426 |\n| C | 0.491 | 0.593 | 0.520 | 0.543 | 0.569 |\n| D | 0.527 | 0.396 | 0.482 | 0.422 | 0.497 |\n\n**Q3.2 Step 3 — Weighted normalised matrix V = R × w.**\n\n| Supplier | C1 (×0.30) | C2 (×0.25) | C3 (×0.20) | C4 (×0.15) | C5 (×0.10) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 0.1526 | 0.1319 | 0.1008 | 0.0814 | 0.0497 |\n| B | 0.1417 | 0.1154 | 0.0986 | 0.0724 | 0.0426 |\n| C | 0.1472 | 0.1484 | 0.1041 | 0.0814 | 0.0569 |\n| D | 0.1581 | 0.0989 | 0.0964 | 0.0633 | 0.0497 |\n\n**Q3.3 Step 4 — Ideal (A⁺) and negative-ideal (A⁻) solutions.**\nRule: for a **benefit** criterion A⁺ = max, A⁻ = min; for a **cost** criterion (price) A⁺ = **min**, A⁻ = **max**.\n\n| | C1 (cost) | C2 | C3 | C4 | C5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A⁺ (ideal) | 0.1417 (B) | 0.1484 (C) | 0.1041 (C) | 0.0814 (A, C) | 0.0569 (C) |\n| A⁻ (negative-ideal) | 0.1581 (D) | 0.0989 (D) | 0.0964 (D) | 0.0633 (D) | 0.0426 (B) |\n\n**Q3.4 Step 5 — Separation measures S⁺ and S⁻.**\nS⁺ = √Σ(v_ij − A⁺_j)², S⁻ = √Σ(v_ij − A⁻_j)².\n\n*Worked for Supplier C:* differences from A⁺ are (0.1472−0.1417) = 0.0055 on C1 and 0 on C2–C5 → **S⁺ = 0.0055**. Differences from A⁻: C1 −0.0109, C2 +0.0495, C3 +0.0077, C4 +0.0181, C5 +0.0143 → squares sum = 0.000119 + 0.002450 + 0.000059 + 0.000328 + 0.000204 = 0.003160 → **S⁻ = 0.0562**.\n\n| Supplier | S⁺ (to A⁺) | S⁻ (to A⁻) |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 0.0213 | 0.0389 |\n| B | 0.0374 | 0.0250 |\n| C | 0.0055 | 0.0561 |\n| D | 0.0561 | 0.0071 |\n\n**Q3.5 Step 6 — Relative closeness Cᵢ* = S⁻ ÷ (S⁺ + S⁻) and rank.**\n\n| Supplier | Cᵢ* | Rank |\n|---|---|---|\n| A (BuildStrong) | 0.0389 ÷ 0.0602 = **0.647** | 2 |\n| B (Apex Infra) | 0.0250 ÷ 0.0624 = **0.401** | 3 |\n| C (ConstructPro) | 0.0561 ÷ 0.0616 = **0.912** | **1** |\n| D (SolidBase) | 0.0071 ÷ 0.0632 = **0.112** | 4 |\n\n**Final recommendation: select ConstructPro Solutions (Supplier C)** for the 500 MT of TMT bars. Cᵢ* close to 1 means close to the ideal. C has the best quality (9), best delivery (95%), joint-best safety (9) and best sustainability (8), at a mid-range price (₹54,000, not the cheapest). This matches the slide: *\"lowest price is not the lowest, but balanced with high performance… best overall balance across all criteria.\"*\n\n**Q3.6 How do your numbers compare with the slide?** ⭐⭐\nThe slide reaches the same winner (C, Cᵢ* = 0.608) but its intermediate numbers are illustrative (the slide's own note: *\"Numbers are illustrative for learning purpose\"*). It also prints ranks that do not follow its own Cᵢ* values (it ranks D, with 0.106, second and A, with 0.382, third). Ranking by Cᵢ* always goes from highest to lowest: on the slide's values that is **C (0.608) > A (0.382) > B (0.251) > D (0.106)**, the same order as the full recomputation above. In the exam, compute from the raw matrix as shown and rank strictly by Cᵢ*.\n\n**Q3.7 Why use TOPSIS instead of simple weighted scoring?**\n(1) It uses **raw data** in different units (₹/MT, %, scores) through normalisation, so you do not have to invent 1–10 scores. (2) It treats **cost and benefit criteria correctly** (ideal = minimum price). (3) It rewards *balance*: a supplier is judged on distance from the best **and** the worst, so one extreme weakness hurts (D is worst on four criteria and ends at 0.112). Limitations: results depend on the weights and the normalisation; adding or removing a supplier can change the ideal points (\"rank reversal\"); it still needs knock-out screening for ethics.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 4. Supplier Risk Matrix — Agri-food industry (Lecture 6, slide 26) 📘 ⭐⭐\nLikelihood scale: **Certain (> 80%), Most likely (60–80%), Possible (30–60%), Unlikely (10–30%), Rare (< 10%)**. Impact scale: **Insignificant, Minor, Moderate, Major, Severe**. Risk bands: **High 15–24 · Medium 6–14 · Low 1–5.** (Score = Likelihood 1–5 × Impact 1–5.)\n\n| Supplier | Risk | Key drivers | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | High | Drought risk, single-region sourcing | Weather monitoring, diversify sourcing |\n| B | Medium | Moderate leverage, seasonal cash flow | Financial monitoring, advance payments with controls |\n| C | Low | Strong fleet, good on-time performance | Continue KPI monitoring |\n\n**Q4.1 Assign likelihood and impact values that are consistent with the slide's classification and compute the risk score.**\nUsing Rare = 1 … Certain = 5 and Insignificant = 1 … Severe = 5:\n- **Supplier A:** drought in a single sourcing region is *Most likely* (4) and its impact is *Major* (4) because the whole category stops → **4 × 4 = 16 → High (15–24)**.\n- **Supplier B:** cash-flow stress is *Possible* (3) with *Moderate* impact (3) → **3 × 3 = 9 → Medium (6–14)**.\n- **Supplier C:** a delivery failure is *Unlikely* (2) with *Minor* impact (2) → **2 × 2 = 4 → Low (1–5)**.\n\n**Q4.2 What should the buyer do with each supplier?**\nA: treat (dual sourcing from a second agro-climatic region, weather-indexed contracts, safety stock before the monsoon, farmer support for drought-resistant practice). B: monitor (quarterly financial health review, advance payments released against milestones, escrow or bank guarantee). C: accept and monitor through the scorecard. Re-score every quarter; the matrix is a living tool.\n\n**Q4.3 Supplier A's sourcing is split across two regions so that likelihood falls to \"Possible\". What is the new score?**\n3 × 4 = **12 → Medium**. Diversification moved A from High to Medium without changing the impact. To reach Low you must also cut the impact (e.g. buffer stock reduces impact to Minor: 3 × 2 = 6, still Medium; Unlikely × Minor = 2 × 2 = 4, Low).\n\n---\n\n## Problem 5. Supplier Sustainability Scorecard — retain a 95/100 supplier with labour violations? (Lecture 10, slide 12) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 10 gives this scorecard and asks: *\"Should a supplier with the following score be retained? 95/100 on cost-quality-delivery, but serious labour violations.\"* The slide's punchline: *\"This is where sustainability becomes a management decision rather than a reporting exercise.\"*\n\n| Dimension | Weight |\n|---|---|\n| Cost competitiveness | 20 |\n| Quality | 20 |\n| Delivery | 15 |\n| Environmental performance | 15 |\n| Labour & human rights | 10 |\n| Carbon reduction | 10 |\n| Governance | 5 |\n| Innovation | 5 |\n| **Total** | **100** |\n\n**Q5.1 Using assumed scores, show why a weighted score alone can wrongly retain the supplier.**\n**Assumed scores (state them in the exam):** Supplier X scores 95 on cost, quality and delivery; environment 60; labour & human rights **10** (serious violations); carbon 50; governance 40; innovation 70. Supplier Y is a balanced alternative: 80, 85, 85, 80, 85, 75, 80, 70.\n\n| Dimension (weight) | Supplier X | Supplier Y |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost (0.20) | 95 → 19.00 | 80 → 16.00 |\n| Quality (0.20) | 95 → 19.00 | 85 → 17.00 |\n| Delivery (0.15) | 95 → 14.25 | 85 → 12.75 |\n| Environmental (0.15) | 60 → 9.00 | 80 → 12.00 |\n| Labour & human rights (0.10) | 10 → 1.00 | 85 → 8.50 |\n| Carbon reduction (0.10) | 50 → 5.00 | 75 → 7.50 |\n| Governance (0.05) | 40 → 2.00 | 80 → 4.00 |\n| Innovation (0.05) | 70 → 3.50 | 70 → 3.50 |\n| **Total** | **72.75** | **81.25** |\n\nEven with a near-zero labour score, X still totals **72.75**, which most scorecards would call \"Average/acceptable\". Labour carries only 10% weight, so the 95s on cost-quality-delivery (55% of the weight) compensate. Y wins on the weighted score anyway (81.25), but the deeper point is that X should not be \"scored\" at all.\n\n**Q5.2 So, should Supplier X be retained? Give a managerial answer.**\n**No — not on current terms.** Serious labour or human-rights violations are a **knock-out (minimum-threshold) criterion**, not a 10% trade-off. Reasons: legal exposure (EU CSDDD due-diligence rules for exporters, BRSR Principle 5 on human rights), brand and investor risk (Nike, Intel and Patagonia examples in Lecture 6), and the course principle \"*do not outsource sustainability*\" (Lecture 11). Recommended action: (1) suspend new orders; (2) demand a corrective action plan with independent audit within 30–90 days; (3) remediate workers (back pay, safe conditions); (4) develop an alternative supplier (Y) in parallel; (5) exit if the audit fails. Then redesign the scorecard as **gate + score**: suppliers must pass minimum thresholds on human rights, safety and legal compliance before any weighted score is computed.\n\n**Q5.3 Mahindra & Mahindra link (Lecture 10, slide 25).**\nM&M's supplier code of conduct is signed by **95% of suppliers by purchase value**, and environmental due diligence since FY2024 covers **68.1% by value**. The slide's question: *is supplier ESG assessment enough, or should supplier ESG performance affect purchasing decisions?* Answer: assessment is an **activity**; it only becomes performance management when the score changes volume allocation, prices, contract renewal or development support (this is the logic of Problem 5).\n\n---\n\n## Problem 6. Laptop manufacturer — evaluate three suppliers (Lecture 6 exercise, slide 32) 📘 + practice data 📗 ⭐⭐\nThe Lecture 6 exercise: *\"For a laptop manufacturing company, evaluate three suppliers using Price, Quality, Carbon, Safety, Innovation and create your own supplier scorecard.\"* The slide gives no data, so here is a fully worked version with **assumed raw data** (state it in the exam).\n\n| Supplier (battery packs) | Price (₹/pack) | Defects (ppm) | Carbon (kg CO₂e/pack) | Safety (LTIFR) | Innovation (patents, 3 yrs) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P | 42,000 | 450 | 95 | 0.8 | 6 |\n| Q | 45,000 | 200 | 70 | 0.4 | 9 |\n| R | 40,000 | 900 | 120 | 1.5 | 3 |\n\nWeights: Price 30%, Quality 25%, Carbon 20%, Safety 15%, Innovation 10%. Price, defects, carbon and LTIFR are **cost-type** (lower is better); patents are **benefit-type**.\n\n**Q6.1 Convert the raw data to scores out of 10 using ratio normalisation and compute weighted totals.**\nCost-type: score = (best ÷ actual) × 10. Benefit-type: score = (actual ÷ best) × 10.\n\n| Criterion (weight) | P | Q | R |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Price (0.30) | 40,000/42,000×10 = 9.52 → 2.86 | 40,000/45,000×10 = 8.89 → 2.67 | 10.00 → 3.00 |\n| Quality (0.25) | 200/450×10 = 4.44 → 1.11 | 10.00 → 2.50 | 200/900×10 = 2.22 → 0.56 |\n| Carbon (0.20) | 70/95×10 = 7.37 → 1.47 | 10.00 → 2.00 | 70/120×10 = 5.83 → 1.17 |\n| Safety (0.15) | 0.4/0.8×10 = 5.00 → 0.75 | 10.00 → 1.50 | 0.4/1.5×10 = 2.67 → 0.40 |\n| Innovation (0.10) | 6/9×10 = 6.67 → 0.67 | 10.00 → 1.00 | 3/9×10 = 3.33 → 0.33 |\n| **Total (/10)** | **6.86** | **9.67** | **5.46** |\n\nRanking: **Q (9.67) > P (6.86) > R (5.46).** Q is 12.5% dearer than R but best on everything else.\n\n**Q6.2 Recompute with min–max normalisation (score = 1 + 9 × relative position). Does the winner change?**\nTotals: **Q = 7.30, P = 6.28, R = 3.70**. Same ranking; min–max widens the gaps because the worst supplier on each criterion gets exactly 1.\n\n**Q6.3 Is Q's 7% higher price than P (₹3,000/pack) justified? Use a TCO view.**\nQuality: P has 250 ppm more defects; at 500,000 packs a year that is 125 more field failures. If each battery failure in warranty costs ₹25,000 (replacement + logistics + brand), that is ₹31.25 lakh. Carbon: P emits 25 kg CO₂e more per pack → 12,500 t CO₂e a year, a Scope 3 cost (at an internal carbon price of ₹2,000/t, ₹2.5 crore). The price premium is ₹3,000 × 500,000 = ₹150 crore, so on these assumptions the premium is **not** fully paid back by quality and carbon alone. The decision depends on the firm's carbon commitments and safety risk (a battery fire recall can dwarf all of these). A good answer: **award Q the majority share (e.g. 70%) and P 30% with a development plan**, and drop R (worst on quality, carbon and safety).\n\n---\n\n## Problem 7. TCO vs lowest price — \"Which supplier would you choose?\" (Lecture 6, slide 7) 📘 + practice data 📗 ⭐⭐\nLecture 6 debate: **Supplier A** — lowest cost, high emissions, child-labour allegations. **Supplier B** — 12% more expensive, renewable energy, zero human-rights issues, ISO 14001 certified. *\"Focus change: best cost → best value (economic + environmental + social).\"*\n\n**Assumed data:** A's price ₹100/unit, so B = ₹112. Annual volume 1,00,000 units. Defect rates A 3%, B 1%; each defect costs ₹200 to handle. Emissions A 2.0 kg CO₂e/unit, B 0.8 kg; carbon cost (CBAM/internal price) ₹8,000/t. Compliance/reputation risk: A has a 10% chance of a ₹50 lakh loss per year; B has 1%. Logistics ₹4/unit for both.\n\n**Q7.1 Compute the total cost of ownership per unit for each supplier.**\n\n| Cost element (₹/unit) | Supplier A | Supplier B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purchase price | 100.00 | 112.00 |\n| Quality cost = defect rate × ₹200 | 0.03 × 200 = 6.00 | 0.01 × 200 = 2.00 |\n| Carbon cost = kg ÷ 1,000 × ₹8,000 | 2.0/1,000 × 8,000 = 16.00 | 0.8/1,000 × 8,000 = 6.40 |\n| Expected risk cost = p × loss ÷ volume | 0.10 × 50,00,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = 5.00 | 0.01 × 50,00,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = 0.50 |\n| Logistics | 4.00 | 4.00 |\n| **TCO per unit** | **131.00** | **124.90** |\n\n**Q7.2 Conclusion.**\nB is **₹6.10 per unit (4.7%) cheaper on TCO** although it is 12% dearer on price; annual saving ≈ ₹6.1 lakh, before counting brand value. And A's child-labour allegation is a knock-out anyway. This is the \"lowest purchase cost → lowest total cost of ownership\" message of the Cost vs Value slide (lifecycle cost components: purchase price, maintenance, waste, energy, end-of-life).\n\n**Q7.3 At what carbon price would A and B have equal TCO (ignoring the child-labour issue)?**\nWithout the carbon line: A = 100 + 6 + 5 + 4 = 115; B = 112 + 2 + 0.5 + 4 = 118.5. B is ₹3.5 dearer. B saves 1.2 kg = 0.0012 t per unit. Break-even carbon price = 3.5 ÷ 0.0012 = **₹2,917 per t CO₂e**. Above about ₹2,900/t, B is cheaper on TCO even before ethics.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 8. AHP — deriving the weights (Lecture 6 decision tools) 📘 method + practice data 📗 ⭐\nLecture 6 lists AHP as *\"a structured technique to organise and analyse complex decisions using a hierarchy of goals, criteria and alternatives.\"* AHP gives you defensible weights instead of guessing them.\n\nA buyer compares three criteria on Saaty's 1–9 scale: Cost is **2×** as important as Quality and **3×** as important as Carbon; Quality is **2×** as important as Carbon.\n\n**Q8.1 Build the pairwise matrix and find the weights (geometric-mean method).**\n\n| | Cost | Quality | Carbon | Geometric mean | Weight |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cost | 1 | 2 | 3 | (1×2×3)^(1/3) = 1.817 | 1.817 ÷ 3.367 = **0.540** |\n| Quality | 1/2 | 1 | 2 | (0.5×1×2)^(1/3) = 1.000 | **0.297** |\n| Carbon | 1/3 | 1/2 | 1 | (0.333×0.5×1)^(1/3) = 0.550 | **0.163** |\n| | | | | Sum = 3.367 | 1.000 |\n\n**Q8.2 Check consistency.**\nA×w = (1.624, 0.894, 0.492). λ_max = average of (1.624/0.540, 0.894/0.297, 0.492/0.163) = **3.009**. CI = (λ_max − n) ÷ (n − 1) = 0.009 ÷ 2 = **0.0046**. CR = CI ÷ RI = 0.0046 ÷ 0.58 = **0.008**, well below **0.10**, so the judgements are consistent. These weights (54/30/16) then feed a weighted-scoring or TOPSIS model.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 9. Kraljic matrix — classify the purchases (Lecture 6, slide 27) 📘 ⭐⭐\nKraljic segments purchases on **supply risk** (vertical) and **profit impact** (horizontal): **Strategic** (high risk, high impact: partner and collaborate, joint innovation), **Leverage** (low risk, high impact: maximise value through negotiation and volume), **Bottleneck** (high risk, low impact: ensure supply security, build alternatives and buffers), **Routine** (low risk, low impact: automate, simplify, reduce transaction cost).\n\n**Q9.1 Classify these items for an EV two-wheeler maker: lithium-ion cells; steel tubes for the frame; a single-source special rare-earth magnet costing little per unit; office stationery; tyres from five qualified suppliers; packaging cartons.**\nLithium-ion cells → **Strategic** (40% of BOM cost, concentrated supply, conflict-mineral risk). Rare-earth magnet → **Bottleneck** (cheap but single-source, China-concentrated). Steel tubes and tyres → **Leverage** (big spend, many suppliers; use competitive tendering, but add carbon criteria, e.g. green steel). Stationery and cartons → **Routine** (e-catalogue; for cartons, specify recycled content, which costs nothing extra).\n\n**Q9.2 Supplier development tools from the same slide:** training, joint innovation, technology transfer, audits, continuous improvement, long-term partnerships. Use them mainly on strategic and bottleneck suppliers.\n\n---\n\n## Practice set (no solutions shown until you reveal) 📗\n\n**Q10. An FMCG firm scores two packaging suppliers out of 10. Weights: Cost 30%, Recycled content 25%, Quality 20%, Delivery 15%, Social compliance 10%. Supplier M: 9, 4, 8, 8, 7. Supplier N: 7, 9, 8, 7, 9. Who wins? What if Cost weight rises to 40% and Recycled content falls to 15%?**\nBase: M = 2.70 + 1.00 + 1.60 + 1.20 + 0.70 = **7.20**; N = 2.10 + 2.25 + 1.60 + 1.05 + 0.90 = **7.90** → **N wins**. Changed weights: M = 7.20 + 0.10×9 − 0.10×4 = **7.70**; N = 7.90 + 0.10×7 − 0.10×9 = **7.70** → a **tie**. The decision hinges entirely on how much the firm values recycled content; with the EPR plastic-packaging obligations in India, a defensible choice is N.\n\n**Q11. A supplier's scores out of 100 are Price 82, Quality 91, Delivery 76, Carbon 64, Safety 88, Innovation 70, Compliance 98 under the VoltTech weights (25/20/15/15/10/10/5). Compute the score and rating.**\n20.50 + 18.20 + 11.40 + 9.60 + 8.80 + 7.00 + 4.90 = **80.40 → B (Good)**. The weakest area is carbon (64). Raising carbon to 80 adds 2.4 points (82.8).\n\n**Q12. In TOPSIS, a supplier has S⁺ = 0.030 and S⁻ = 0.045. Another has S⁺ = 0.020 and S⁻ = 0.025. Which is preferred?**\nC₁* = 0.045 ÷ 0.075 = **0.600**; C₂* = 0.025 ÷ 0.045 = **0.556**. The first supplier is preferred even though it is farther from the ideal, because it is relatively much farther from the worst.\n\n**Q13. Using the Lecture 6 risk bands, classify: (i) Possible × Severe; (ii) Certain × Minor; (iii) Rare × Severe; (iv) Most likely × Moderate.**\n(i) 3 × 5 = 15 → **High**; (ii) 5 × 2 = 10 → **Medium**; (iii) 1 × 5 = 5 → **Low** (but a \"black-swan\" item: keep a contingency plan); (iv) 4 × 3 = 12 → **Medium**.\n\n**Q14. Why should \"Carbon\" be scored as a benefit criterion in the weighted score model but entered as a cost criterion in TOPSIS?**\nIn the weighted model you enter a **score** (9 = low-carbon, good), so higher is better. In TOPSIS you enter **raw emissions** (kg CO₂e/unit), where lower is better, so its ideal value is the minimum. If TOPSIS uses a \"carbon score\" (like the Lecture 6 C5), it is a benefit criterion. Always check what the number means before choosing the direction.\n\n**Q15. Write the five-step \"Recap\" of Lecture 6 and connect it to a scoring numerical.**\nStep 1 sustainable procurement → Step 2 supplier sustainability → Step 3 decision tools → Step 4 business performance → Step 5 competitive advantage. A scoring model is Step 3: it turns the sustainability criteria of Step 2 into a supplier choice whose results show up in Step 4 KPIs (OTIF, defect rate, Scope 3, cost savings).\n"}, {"slug": "p2-carbon-accounting-numericals", "label": "CO3 Carbon Accounting (Scope 1-2-3)", "tag": "P2", "group": "focus", "title": "CO3 Numericals: Carbon Accounting & Scope 1, 2, 3 Emissions", "md": "# CO3 Numericals: Carbon Accounting & Scope 1, 2, 3 Emissions\nThis page covers **CO3: carbon accounting, the calculation of Scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions**. CO3 is one of the two **high-weightage** COs in the 40-mark end-sem paper (Lecture 11, last slide 📘), so expect the biggest numerical here. It reproduces the **exact automotive plant case (ICEV + EV in the same plant)** from the deck *\"Carbon management, carbon accounting and carbon trading\"* 📘, solved line by line, plus the diesel example, GWP conversion, carbon credits, and real BRSR numbers from the **Tata Motors** and **TVS Motor** reports in your course zip 📘. Practice problems built in the same style are marked 📗. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers.\n\nTheory is on [LCA, Carbon Footprints & Carbon Management](ch02-lca-carbon-footprints.md).\n\n---\n\n## The method and the factors you must know ⭐⭐⭐\n**The basic formula (carbon deck 📘):**\n**Emissions = Activity data × Emission factor**, then **CO₂e = Emission of the gas × GWP**.\n\n**The five-step carbon accounting methodology (carbon deck 📘):**\n1. **Identify emission sources**, scope-wise (natural gas furnace, diesel generators, purchased electricity, logistics).\n2. **Collect activity data** (litres of diesel, kWh of electricity, tonnes of steel purchased).\n3. **Apply emission factors** (sources: IPCC, DEFRA, national databases). Example: 10,000 litres diesel × 2.68 kg CO₂/litre = **26,800 kg CO₂**.\n4. **Convert to CO₂e** by applying GWP multipliers.\n5. **Aggregate and report** (BRSR, CDP, GRI 305, CBAM filings), with MRV: Measure → Report → Verify.\n\n**Emission factors used in the course case 📘 (memorise):**\n\n| Fuel / energy | Emission factor |\n|---|---|\n| Diesel | **2.68 kg CO₂ per litre** |\n| Natural gas | **1.9 kg CO₂ per Sm³** |\n| LPG | **3.0 t CO₂ per tonne** |\n| Indian grid electricity | **≈ 0.70 t CO₂ per MWh** (= 0.70 kg per kWh) |\n| Petrol car tailpipe (case) | 120 g CO₂ per km |\n\n**GWP (100-year) values from the deck 📘:** CO₂ = **1**; CH₄ ≈ **27–30**; N₂O ≈ **273**; HFCs **12–14,000** (the range of the family; a common refrigerant such as HFC-134a is about 1,300–1,530); PFCs **7,000–12,000**; SF₆ ≈ **25,200**; NF₃ ≈ **17,000**. These seven are the Kyoto Protocol (1997) basket.\n\n**Scope rules (carbon deck & Lecture 10 📘):**\n- **Scope 1 — Direct, \"what you burn\":** fuel combustion (diesel in the plant generator), company vehicles (petrol in company cars), process emissions (chemical reactions in manufacturing), fugitive emissions (refrigerant leaks).\n- **Scope 2 — Energy indirect, \"what you buy\":** purchased grid electricity (e.g. MSEDCL power), purchased heating (hot water for buildings), purchased cooling, purchased gases (e.g. hydrogen for an industrial process — as listed in the course slide).\n- **Scope 3 — Value chain, \"what you influence\":** purchased goods and services, fuel- and energy-related activities not in Scope 1 & 2 (mining coal, refining petrol), product use (use of vehicles and end-of-life treatment), logistics (inbound materials and outbound products), and the rest of the 15 GHG Protocol categories. **Scope 3 typically contributes 70–90% of GHG emissions** and is the most challenging to measure.\n\n**The 15 Scope 3 categories (as listed on the case slide):** *Upstream:* 1 Purchased goods & services, 2 Capital goods, 3 Fuel & energy related, 4 Upstream transport, 5 Waste generated, 6 Business travel, 7 Employee commuting, 8 Upstream leased assets. *Downstream:* 9 Downstream transport & distribution, 10 Processing of sold products, 11 Use of sold products, 12 End-of-life treatment of sold products, 13 Downstream leased assets, 14 Franchises, 15 Investments.\n\n**Unit traps:** 1 t = 1,000 kg; 1 MWh = 1,000 kWh; kg/kWh = t/MWh; \"1.5 L km\" = 1.5 lakh km = 1,50,000 km; Indian notation 12,42,636 = 1,242,636. 1 carbon credit = 1 t CO₂e.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 1. Automotive manufacturing — ICEV + EV in the same plant (carbon deck, slides 17–19) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Context:** one plant produces **50,000 ICE cars** and **20,000 EV cars** a year.\n\n### Part A — Scope 1 (direct emissions, example data)\n\n| Source | Quantity | Emission factor |\n|---|---|---|\n| Natural gas | 500,000 Sm³ | 1.9 kg CO₂/Sm³ |\n| Diesel (DG sets) | 200,000 litres | 2.68 kg CO₂/l |\n| LPG | 50 tonnes | 3.0 t CO₂/t |\n\n**Q1.1 Calculate the plant's Scope 1 emissions.**\n- Natural gas: 500,000 Sm³ × 1.9 kg = 950,000 kg = **950 t CO₂**\n- Diesel: 200,000 l × 2.68 kg = 536,000 kg = **536 t CO₂**\n- LPG: 50 t × 3.0 t CO₂/t = **150 t CO₂**\n- **Total Scope 1 = 950 + 536 + 150 = 1,636 t CO₂e**\n\n### Part B — Scope 2 (purchased electricity)\nGrid electricity for body-shop robots, paint shop, assembly lines and the EV battery line. Annual electricity = **25,000 MWh**; Indian grid factor ≈ **0.70 t CO₂/MWh**.\n\n**Q1.2 Calculate Scope 2.**\nScope 2 = 25,000 MWh × 0.70 t CO₂/MWh = **17,500 t CO₂e**.\n\n### Part C — Scope 3 (value-chain emissions)\n*\"This is where EV vs ICEV diverge strongly. We take only key categories (not all 15).\"*\n\n| No. | Category | ICEV data | EV data |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Purchased materials | Steel, aluminium, plastics, powertrain: ≈ 6 t CO₂e per vehicle | Body + motor + electronics + lithium battery (~60 kWh): ≈ 10 t CO₂e per vehicle |\n| 4 | Upstream transport | Parts by truck & ship: total 15,000 t CO₂e | Same: 15,000 t CO₂e |\n| 6 | Business travel | Flights + hotels: total 500 t CO₂e (printed \"5,00\") | Same: 500 t CO₂e |\n| 9 | Downstream distribution | Shipping cars to dealers: total 8,000 t CO₂e | Same: 8,000 t CO₂e |\n| 11 | Use phase | Lifetime distance 1.5 lakh km, 120 g CO₂/km | Electricity 0.15 kWh/km, lifetime 22,500 kWh, grid 0.70 kg CO₂/kWh |\n\n**Q1.3 Calculate Scope 3 purchased materials (Category 1).**\n- ICEV: 50,000 × 6 t = **300,000 t CO₂e**\n- EV: 20,000 × 10 t = **200,000 t CO₂e**\n\n**Q1.4 Calculate the use-phase emissions per vehicle and for the fleet (Category 11).**\n- *ICEV per vehicle:* 1,50,000 km × 120 g/km = 18,000,000 g = **18 t CO₂e**. Fleet = 50,000 × 18 = **900,000 t CO₂e**.\n- *EV per vehicle:* electricity = 1,50,000 km × 0.15 kWh/km = **22,500 kWh**; emissions = 22,500 × 0.70 kg = 15,750 kg = **15.75 t CO₂e**. Fleet = 20,000 × 15.75 = **315,000 t CO₂e**.\n\n**Q1.5 Logistics and travel (Categories 4, 6, 9).**\n15,000 + 500 + 8,000 = **23,500 t CO₂e**. The slide books this once, against the ICEV line (\"same\" for EV), so it is not added twice.\n\n**Q1.6 Prepare the summary table and the plant total.**\n\n| Scope | ICEV (t CO₂e) | EV (t CO₂e) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope 1 | 1,636 | Included (shared plant) |\n| Scope 2 | 17,500 | Included |\n| Scope 3 — Materials | 300,000 | 200,000 |\n| Scope 3 — Logistics + travel | 23,500 | Same (counted once) |\n| Scope 3 — Use phase | 900,000 | 315,000 |\n| **Total** | **12,42,636** | **5,15,000** |\n\nICEV total = 1,636 + 17,500 + 300,000 + 23,500 + 900,000 = **12,42,636 t**. EV total = 200,000 + 315,000 = **5,15,000 t**.\n**Plant total = 12,42,636 + 5,15,000 = 17,57,636 t CO₂e.**\n\n**Q1.7 What share of the plant footprint is Scope 1 + 2 versus Scope 3? What does this mean for strategy?**\nScope 1 + 2 = 1,636 + 17,500 = 19,136 t → 19,136 ÷ 17,57,636 = **1.09%**. Scope 3 = 17,38,500 t → **98.9%**. (The slide's note: *Scope 1 + 2 < 5%, Scope 3 > 95%.*) Use phase alone = 12,15,000 t = **69.1%**; purchased materials = 5,00,000 t = **28.4%**.\n**Implication:** even a 100% renewable, zero-fuel factory would cut the footprint by only about 1%. That is why OEMs such as **Toyota and Tesla focus on supplier decarbonisation, renewable energy, battery recycling and green logistics** (slide note), and why the product's use phase (efficiency, electrification, grid mix) dominates.\n\n**Q1.8 Compare an ICE car and an EV per vehicle. Interpret the slide's \"vehicle type\" table.**\n\n| Vehicle type | Manufacturing phase | Use phase | Total (life cycle) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ICE | ~7 t | ~18 t | **~25 t** |\n| EV (India grid) | ~12 t | ~16 t | **~28 t** |\n| EV (RE grid) | ~10 t | ~3 t | **~13 t** |\n\nThe slide's four lessons: (1) Scope 1 + 2 < 5% and Scope 3 > 95%; (2) **EV is high in manufacturing because of battery materials**; (3) **ICEV is high in life usage**; (4) **EV benefit depends on grid cleanliness**. On today's coal-heavy Indian grid (0.70 kg/kWh) the EV's lifetime footprint (~28 t) can even exceed the ICE car's (~25 t); on a renewable grid it falls to about half (~13 t).\n\n**Q1.9 At what grid emission factor would the EV and the ICE car have the same life-cycle footprint?** ⭐⭐\nEV total = 12 t (manufacturing) + 22,500 kWh × g; ICE = 25 t. Set 12 + 22.5 × g = 25 (g in kg/kWh, because 22,500 kWh × g kg = 22.5 g t). g = 13 ÷ 22.5 = **0.578 kg CO₂/kWh**. Below about 0.58 kg/kWh, the EV is better over its life. India's grid has to decarbonise by about 17% from 0.70 for the EV in this case to break even. (Using the case's 10 t materials figure instead: 10 + 22.5g = 25 → g = 0.667 kg/kWh.)\n\n**Q1.10 Per-vehicle intensity: which product line is more carbon-intensive per car?**\nICEV: 12,42,636 ÷ 50,000 = **24.85 t per car**. EV: 5,15,000 ÷ 20,000 = **25.75 t per car** (no plant or logistics share allocated). If you allocate the shared Scope 1 + 2 by volume (20/70 of 19,136 = 5,467 t) and logistics (20/70 of 23,500 = 6,714 t) to the EV line, EV = (5,15,000 + 5,467 + 6,714) ÷ 20,000 = **26.36 t per car**, and the ICEV line falls to (12,42,636 − 5,467 − 6,714) ÷ 50,000 = **24.61 t**. Allocation choices change the answer, which is why the course lists \"allocation issues\" as an LCA limitation.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 2. The deck's diesel example and GWP conversion 📘 ⭐⭐\n**Q2.1 A plant uses 10,000 litres of diesel. Emission factor 2.68 kg CO₂/l. Emissions?**\n10,000 × 2.68 = **26,800 kg = 26.8 t CO₂** (Scope 1 if burnt in the company's own DG set or vehicles; Scope 3 if burnt by a hired transporter).\n\n**Q2.2 Convert to CO₂e: 10 t CH₄ from a biogas leak, 2 t N₂O from fertiliser use, 0.1 t SF₆ leaking from switchgear. Use GWPs CH₄ = 28, N₂O = 273, SF₆ = 25,200.**\nCH₄: 10 × 28 = **280 t CO₂e** · N₂O: 2 × 273 = **546 t CO₂e** · SF₆: 0.1 × 25,200 = **2,520 t CO₂e** · Total = **3,346 t CO₂e**. Notice how 100 kg of SF₆ outweighs 10 t of methane: tiny leaks of high-GWP gases matter, which is why fugitive emissions are a Scope 1 category.\n\n**Q2.3 Why convert everything to CO₂e?**\nDifferent gases trap different amounts of heat for different lengths of time. Converting with GWP (100-year) puts them on one comparable scale so that they can be added, targeted and traded (1 credit = 1 t CO₂e).\n\n---\n\n## Problem 3. Full Scope 1-2-3 inventory for a mid-size manufacturer 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\n*(Practice data in the style of the course case. Factors from the course where available; others are stated assumptions.)*\nAn auto-component plant in Pune produces **20,000 units** a year and records:\n- Diesel in DG sets and forklifts: 50,000 litres (2.68 kg CO₂/l)\n- Natural gas in heat-treatment furnaces: 200,000 Sm³ (1.9 kg CO₂/Sm³)\n- LPG in the canteen and paint oven: 20 t (3.0 t CO₂/t)\n- Refrigerant top-up (HFC-134a) of 50 kg in the AC system; **assume GWP 1,430**\n- Grid electricity: 4,000 MWh (0.70 t CO₂/MWh)\n- Purchased steel: 5,000 t; **assume 2.0 t CO₂e per t steel**\n- Inbound freight by hired trucks: 2,000,000 tonne-km; **assume 0.10 kg CO₂e per t-km**\n- Business travel 300 t CO₂e; employee commuting by contracted buses 150 t CO₂e; waste sent to landfill 50 t CO₂e (given)\n\n**Q3.1 Classify each source by scope.**\nScope 1: diesel (own DG sets/forklifts), natural gas, LPG, refrigerant leak (fugitive). Scope 2: grid electricity. Scope 3: purchased steel (Cat. 1), hired inbound trucks (Cat. 4), business travel (Cat. 6), employee commuting (Cat. 7), waste (Cat. 5).\n\n**Q3.2 Calculate each scope and the total.**\n\n| Source | Calculation | t CO₂e | Scope |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Diesel | 50,000 × 2.68 ÷ 1,000 | 134.0 | 1 |\n| Natural gas | 200,000 × 1.9 ÷ 1,000 | 380.0 | 1 |\n| LPG | 20 × 3.0 | 60.0 | 1 |\n| HFC-134a leak | 0.05 t × 1,430 | 71.5 | 1 |\n| **Scope 1** | | **645.5** | |\n| Grid electricity | 4,000 × 0.70 | **2,800.0** | **2** |\n| Purchased steel | 5,000 × 2.0 | 10,000.0 | 3 |\n| Inbound freight | 2,000,000 × 0.10 ÷ 1,000 | 200.0 | 3 |\n| Business travel | given | 300.0 | 3 |\n| Employee commuting | given | 150.0 | 3 |\n| Waste | given | 50.0 | 3 |\n| **Scope 3** | | **10,700.0** | |\n| **Grand total** | | **14,145.5** | |\n\n**Q3.3 Compute shares and intensity.**\nScope 1 = 645.5 ÷ 14,145.5 = **4.6%**; Scope 2 = **19.8%**; Scope 3 = **75.6%** (inside the course's 70–90% range). Scope 1 + 2 intensity = 3,445.5 ÷ 20,000 = **0.172 t CO₂e per unit**; total intensity = **0.707 t per unit**. Steel alone is 70.7% of the footprint, so the top lever is **green/low-carbon steel or higher recycled content**, a procurement decision.\n\n**Q3.4 The plant signs a solar PPA covering 40% of its electricity. New Scope 2 (market-based) and total reduction?**\nGrid share = 60% × 4,000 = 2,400 MWh → Scope 2 = 2,400 × 0.70 = **1,680 t**. Reduction = 2,800 − 1,680 = **1,120 t**, i.e. 32.5% of Scope 1 + 2 but only **7.9% of the total footprint**. Location-based Scope 2 (grid average) stays at 2,800 t; market-based reflects the contract. Tata Motors reports its Scope 2 market-based, after renewable certificates (iRECs).\n\n**Q3.5 Rank three projects by t CO₂e saved: (a) fix refrigerant leaks; (b) switch the furnace from natural gas to electricity bought from a renewable PPA; (c) source 50% of steel from a supplier with 1.2 t CO₂e/t.**\n(a) 71.5 t. (b) Removes 380 t of Scope 1 (assume the renewable electricity is zero-emission under market-based accounting) → **380 t**. (c) 2,500 t × (2.0 − 1.2) = **2,000 t**. Rank: **c > b > a**. Supplier decarbonisation beats everything inside the fence, the same lesson as Problem 1.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 4. Real BRSR numbers — TVS Motor Company, FY 2023-24 📘 ⭐⭐\nFrom the TVS Motor BRSR in your course zip (Principle 6):\n\n| Parameter | FY 2023-24 | FY 2022-23 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total Scope 1 emissions | 22,693.34 t CO₂e | 19,046 t CO₂e |\n| Total Scope 2 emissions | 4,437.47 t CO₂e | 9,584.32 t CO₂e |\n| Total Scope 3 emissions (in progress) | 2,27,00,763.36 t CO₂e | — |\n| Electricity from renewable sources (A) | 3,06,833.17 GJ | 2,96,430.00 GJ |\n| Electricity from non-renewable sources (D) | 22,499.84 GJ | 40,913.92 GJ |\n| Fuel from non-renewable sources (E) | 2,99,342.59 GJ | 2,90,369.94 GJ |\n| Total energy consumed | 6,28,675.60 GJ | 6,27,713.86 GJ |\n\n**Q4.1 Compute Scope 1 + 2 for both years and the change.**\nFY24: 22,693.34 + 4,437.47 = **27,130.81 t**. FY23: 19,046 + 9,584.32 = **28,630.32 t**. Change = −1,499.51 t = **−5.2%**. Scope 2 fell 53.7% (more renewable power) while Scope 1 rose 19.1% (TVS notes the boundary was widened to include the corporate office, test tracks and the Institute of Quality and Leadership, and that refrigerants and ODS are included in Scope 1).\n\n**Q4.2 What share of TVS's electricity is renewable? What share of total energy?**\nRenewable electricity share = 3,06,833.17 ÷ (3,06,833.17 + 22,499.84) = 3,06,833.17 ÷ 3,29,333.01 = **93.2%** (the report's \"93% of our energy requirements met through renewable energy\" refers to electricity). Share of *total energy* = 3,06,833.17 ÷ 6,28,675.60 = **48.8%**, because fuel (2,99,342.59 GJ) is still 100% fossil. Next lever: electrify fuel-based processes (paint ovens, heat treatment) or switch to biofuels.\n\n**Q4.3 What share of TVS's total footprint is Scope 3?**\n2,27,00,763.36 ÷ (2,27,00,763.36 + 27,130.81) = **99.88%**. Scope 3 per vehicle sold is reported as **5.41 t CO₂e**, versus Scope 1 + 2 per vehicle of about **0.01 t**. The two-wheeler's use phase and purchased parts dominate.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 5. Real BRSR numbers — Tata Motors Ltd (CV business, TML), FY 2024-25 📘 ⭐⭐\nFrom the Tata Motors integrated report BRSR (assured by KPMG):\n\n| Parameter (TML) | FY 25 | FY 24 (restated) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope 1 | 43,754 t CO₂ | 48,736 t CO₂ |\n| Scope 2 (market-based, after iRECs) | 1,31,407 t CO₂ | 1,72,409 t CO₂ |\n| Scope 1 + 2 intensity | 0.462 t CO₂ per vehicle produced | 0.552 |\n| Scope 3 Cat. 1 Purchased goods & services (spend-based) | 52,28,249 | 61,09,148 |\n| Scope 3 Cat. 11 Use of sold products | 13,95,71,557 | 15,86,21,101 |\n| Total Scope 3 | 14,49,44,801 | 16,48,85,353 |\n\n**Q5.1 Compute TML's Scope 1 + 2 and its % reduction.**\nFY25: 43,754 + 1,31,407 = **1,75,161 t**. FY24: 48,736 + 1,72,409 = **2,21,145 t**. Reduction = 45,984 t = **20.8%**.\n\n**Q5.2 Estimate the number of vehicles produced in FY25 from the intensity.**\nVehicles ≈ 1,75,161 ÷ 0.462 = **≈ 3,79,000 vehicles**.\n\n**Q5.3 What share of Scope 3 is \"use of sold products\"? What does it tell you?**\n13,95,71,557 ÷ 14,49,44,801 = **96.3%**; purchased goods = 3.6%. Scope 3 is about **827 times** Scope 1 + 2 (14,49,44,801 ÷ 1,75,161). For a truck maker, the diesel its customers burn over the trucks' life is the footprint. Strategic levers: EV/CNG/LNG/hydrogen trucks, fuel efficiency, telematics for eco-driving, and the Re.Wi.Re vehicle-scrapping network (Lecture 11). This mirrors the Hindustan Zinc–GreenLine LNG/EV truck deal in Lecture 1.\n\n**Q5.4 TML's Cat. 1 is \"spend-based\". What does that mean and what is its weakness?**\nSpend-based Scope 3 multiplies ₹ spent per category by an average emission factor per ₹. It is quick but cannot reward a supplier that actually decarbonises (spend stays the same, so the estimate stays the same). Better: supplier-specific (primary) data or average-data (t of steel × factor), moving towards product carbon footprints from suppliers.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 6. Carbon credits — how much is a project worth? (carbon deck, slide 25) 📘 ⭐⭐\n**1 carbon credit = 1 t CO₂e reduced, avoided or removed.**\n\n| Project | Credits generated | Potential financial return |\n|---|---|---|\n| 50 kW solar farm | Avoided emissions: 50–55 per year | ₹45–60 K at ₹850–1,100 per credit |\n| 50 kW wind farm | Avoided emissions: 50–80 per year (depends on wind) | ₹45–90 K at ₹850–1,100 per credit |\n| 50 acres afforestation | Removal: 40–100 per year, at 0.2–0.8 t CO₂ per acre | ₹32–300 K at ₹800–3,000 per credit |\n| 50 t methane capture plant | Emission reduction: about 1,250 per year | ~$25–30 K at $20 per credit ≈ ₹23–28 lakh |\n| 50 kW biomass plant | Avoided emissions: 200–500 per year | ₹80–625 K at ₹400–1,250 per credit |\n\n**Q6.1 Verify the solar farm's revenue range.**\nLow: 50 × ₹850 = ₹42,500; high: 55 × ₹1,100 = ₹60,500 → about **₹45–60 thousand a year** (the slide rounds the low end up).\n\n**Q6.2 Why does the methane capture plant earn so much more than the solar farm?**\nMethane has a GWP of about 27–30, so capturing 50 t of CH₄ avoids ~50 × 25 ≈ 1,250 t CO₂e (the slide's figure is consistent with a GWP of 25). 1,250 credits × $20 = **$25,000** (≈ ₹23 lakh at ~₹92/$). A 50 kW solar plant avoids only ~50 t because a small plant generates ~70 MWh a year, and 70 MWh × 0.70 t/MWh ≈ 49 t.\n\n**Q6.3 Check the biomass revenue range.**\nLow: 200 × ₹400 = **₹80,000**; high: 500 × ₹1,250 = **₹6,25,000** → ₹80–625 K, as on the slide.\n\n**Q6.4 Check the afforestation numbers. Are they consistent?**\nAt 0.2–0.8 t CO₂ per acre, 50 acres removes only 10–40 t a year. The slide's \"40–100 credits a year\" implies 0.8–2.0 t per acre, so the per-acre range and the credit range do not quite match; young plantations remove little and mature ones more. Revenue at the slide's figures: 40 × ₹800 = ₹32,000 to 100 × ₹3,000 = ₹3,00,000 → **₹32–300 K**, as printed. Removal credits command higher prices (₹800–3,000) because they are permanent removals, if they are verified.\n\n**Q6.5 Name the carbon market structures from the deck.**\nCredit life cycle: Project developer → Validation → Verification → Carbon registry → Carbon credit issued (1 credit = 1 t CO₂e) → Buyer purchases credit. Registries: **Verra (VCS), Gold Standard, American Carbon Registry, Climate Action Reserve**. **Compliance market** (mandatory: EU ETS, national schemes) vs **voluntary market** (corporate commitments, net-zero pledges). **India's CCTS** (Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, developed by BEE and the Ministry of Power, operative from 2025): India → Industry → Carbon reduction → Credits → Global market.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 7. Net-zero arithmetic and the hierarchy 📘 + 📗 ⭐⭐\n**Definitions (carbon deck 📘):** Carbon neutral = emissions balanced through offsets. **Net zero = reduce emissions deeply (90–95%) and neutralise residual emissions.** Carbon zero = near-zero emissions across operations and value chain. Climate positive = removes more carbon than emitted. **Net-zero hierarchy:** Measure (Scope 1, 2, 3, CO₂e) → Reduce (decarbonisation, energy efficiency, circularity, nature-based solutions) → RE transition (solar, wind, hydro) → Sustainable supply chain (EV fleets, circularity, biodegradable packaging) → Offset unavoidable emissions (afforestation, CCUS). **\"Reduction first, offset later.\"**\n\n**Q7.1 A company's baseline is 1,00,000 t CO₂e. To call itself net zero, how much must it reduce and how much may it neutralise?**\nReduce by 90–95% → residual 5,000–10,000 t. Only that residual may be neutralised (preferably with **removals**: afforestation, CCUS). A firm that offsets all 1,00,000 t without reducing is \"carbon neutral\", not net zero.\n\n**Q7.2 The same company plans a linear path from 2026 to 90% reduction by 2050. What is the annual reduction?**\n90,000 t over 24 years = **3,750 t a year** (3.75% of the baseline). On the course's decarbonisation pathway: 2026 measure, 2028 energy efficiency, 2030 renewable electricity, 2035 green supply chain, 2040 low-carbon products, 2050 net zero.\n\n**Q7.3 Carbon-neutral claim check: a firm emits 20,000 t, buys 20,000 avoidance credits from a solar project, and advertises its product as \"carbon neutral\". Comment.**\nTechnically \"carbon neutral\" (balanced through offsets), but it may be **greenwashing** if the claim implies reduced emissions, if the credits are low quality (non-additional, unverified), or if Scope 3 is excluded. A credible claim states the claim, metric, boundary, methodology, baseline, target and evidence (the Lecture 10 \"good reporting\" list), with MRV and third-party assurance (e.g. Bureau Veritas, TÜV, KBS).\n\n---\n\n## Practice set 📗\n\n**Q8. Classify into Scope 1, 2 or 3: (a) electricity bought from MSEDCL; (b) diesel in the company's own trucks; (c) diesel in a 3PL's trucks carrying your goods; (d) CO₂ released when limestone is calcined in your kiln; (e) R-410A leaking from your office AC; (f) steam bought from a neighbouring plant; (g) customers driving the cars you sold; (h) employees flying to a conference; (i) hydrogen bought for your hydrogenation process; (j) emissions from mining the coal burnt at your power supplier's plant.**\n(a) 2 · (b) 1 · (c) 3 (Cat. 4 or 9) · (d) 1 (process emissions) · (e) 1 (fugitive) · (f) 2 (purchased heat) · (g) 3 (Cat. 11) · (h) 3 (Cat. 6) · (i) 2 as the course slide lists purchased gases under Scope 2 (under the GHG Protocol, the production emissions of purchased hydrogen sit in Scope 3 Cat. 1 or 3; mention both if asked) · (j) 3 (Cat. 3, fuel- and energy-related activities).\n\n**Q9. A warehouse uses 1,20,000 kWh of grid electricity a month. It installs rooftop solar producing 40,000 kWh a month (Myntra, in Lecture 8, is the course example). Monthly Scope 2 before and after? Annual saving in t CO₂e?**\nBefore: 1,20,000 × 0.70 kg = 84,000 kg = **84 t/month**. After: 80,000 × 0.70 = **56 t/month**. Saving 28 t/month = **336 t/year**. The KPI to report is energy (or kg CO₂e) per unit handled (the Lecture 8 green-warehousing KPI).\n\n**Q10. A fleet of 40 company-owned diesel trucks each runs 80,000 km a year at 4 km per litre. Compute Scope 1. If they are replaced by LNG trucks emitting 15% less per km, what is the saving?**\nDiesel = 40 × 80,000 ÷ 4 = 8,00,000 l → 8,00,000 × 2.68 = 21,44,000 kg = **2,144 t CO₂**. 15% saving = **321.6 t** a year (the Hindustan Zinc–GreenLine idea).\n\n**Q11. An exporter of steel to the EU emits 2.3 t CO₂ per t of steel; the EU benchmark is 1.4 t. Assuming a CBAM price of €80/t CO₂ applied to the excess, what is the CBAM cost per tonne of steel and for 10,000 t?**\nExcess = 0.9 t CO₂/t steel → 0.9 × €80 = **€72 per t of steel**; for 10,000 t = **€7,20,000**. (Simplified; actual CBAM uses embedded emissions, free-allocation phase-out and any carbon price paid at home.) Message from the deck: carbon is becoming a **financial variable**, and MRV-quality data are needed for CBAM filings.\n\n**Q12. Why is it said that \"a company may reduce Scope 1 substantially while its total supply-chain footprint continues increasing\" (Lecture 10)? Give a numerical illustration.**\nIf a firm outsources its own fleet to a 3PL, its Scope 1 falls, but the same diesel now appears in its Scope 3 (Cat. 4/9). Example: 2,144 t moves from Scope 1 to Scope 3, and if the 3PL runs emptier trucks, the total rises. Therefore **carbon accounting must follow the value chain.**\n\n**Q13. Carbon accounting vs financial accounting: fill the table.**\nMeasure: currency vs **CO₂e**; Record: ledger vs **emission inventory**; Source: cost centres vs **emission sources**; Assessment: audit vs **verification**; Results: profitability vs **carbon intensity**. Emerging concept: the **carbon ledger**; new roles: carbon accountants, ESG controllers.\n"}, {"slug": "p3-network-logistics-numericals", "label": "CO4 Networks, Logistics Cost & Distance", "tag": "P3", "group": "focus", "title": "CO4 Numericals: Distribution Networks, Logistics Cost & Distance Criteria", "md": "# CO4 Numericals: Distribution Networks, Logistics Cost & Distance Criteria\nThis page covers **CO4: SSCM distribution networks, logistics cost and distance criteria**. CO4 is one of the two **high-weightage** COs of the end-sem paper (Lecture 11 📘). It starts with the **exact problems from Lecture 8** 📘 (the network A/B/C trade-off and objective function, the 10,000-returns allocation, and the consumer-electronics network exercise) and the **Lecture 10 FMCG distribution scenario** 📘, then the standard distance-criterion methods (load-distance, centre of gravity, factor rating), transport-emission and consolidation numericals, and the ITC eChoupal network economics 📘. Practice data are marked 📗. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers.\n\nTheory: [Sustainable Logistics](ch06-sustainable-logistics.md) and [Designing the Sustainable Network](ch07-network-design.md).\n\n---\n\n## Formulas and conventions for CO4 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Sustainable network objective (Lecture 8 📘):** Minimise **Total cost + Environmental cost + Social cost**.\n- *Total cost* = facility cost + production cost + transportation cost + inventory cost + collection cost + recovery cost + disposal cost.\n- *Environmental* = CO₂ emissions + energy consumption + waste + resource consumption.\n- Mathematical form: **Minimise Z = C_total + λ·E_CO₂ + μ·W_waste**, where λ is the price (weight) put on each unit of CO₂ and μ on each unit of waste.\n- Decision variables: **xᵢⱼ** = quantity transported from node i to node j; **yⱼ** = 1 if facility j is opened (0 otherwise); **rᵢ** = quantity recovered from returned products.\n- Constraints: demand satisfaction, facility capacity, material balance, transport capacity, recovery capacity, collection constraints, recycling targets, non-negative flow. At every recovery facility: **Returns = Reuse + Repair + Remanufacture + Recycling + Disposal**.\n- *\"The model searches for a **Pareto-efficient** solution\"* — one where you cannot reduce cost without increasing CO₂ (or vice versa).\n\n**Transport emissions:** CO₂ = tonnes × km × emission factor (kg CO₂e per tonne-km), or CO₂ = litres of fuel × 2.68 kg/l for diesel. Lecture 8's mode hierarchy: **Air > Road > Rail > Water** in emission intensity; *actual performance depends on distance, load factor, vehicle and operating conditions.*\n\nIllustrative emission factors used below (📗, state as assumptions in the exam): road truck 0.09 kg CO₂e/t-km, rail 0.025, inland/coastal water 0.015, deep-sea container 0.015, air freight 0.60.\n\n**Distance criteria:**\n- **Load-distance (ton-km) score** = Σ (load × distance). The site with the lowest score minimises transport effort, cost and (roughly) emissions.\n- **Centre of gravity:** Cx = Σ(xᵢ·Wᵢ) ÷ ΣWᵢ, Cy = Σ(yᵢ·Wᵢ) ÷ ΣWᵢ (Wᵢ = volume shipped to/from location i). It gives a *starting* location, not always the exact optimum.\n- **Euclidean distance** = √((x₁−x₂)² + (y₁−y₂)²); **rectilinear** = |x₁−x₂| + |y₁−y₂|.\n\n**Logistics KPIs (Lecture 8 & 10):** vehicle utilisation % = load carried ÷ capacity; empty km % = empty km ÷ total km; g CO₂e per tonne-km; cost per unit; return rate, recovery rate, reuse rate, remanufacture rate, recycled content %.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 1. Network A, B or C? (Lecture 8, slides 36–37) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 8's simplified sustainable network example:\n\n| Network | Cost | CO₂ | Recovery |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | ₹100 | 100 | 20% |\n| B | ₹108 | 75 | 50% |\n| C | ₹115 | 55 | 70% |\n\n*\"Question for students: for the example quoted, which network is best? Answer: there is no single answer without defining decision-maker priorities.\"* Objectives on the slide: minimise total cost, minimise CO₂ emissions, maximise recovered material, maximise social benefit.\n\n**Q1.1 Is any network dominated? Which ones are Pareto-efficient?**\nA network is **dominated** if another is at least as good on every objective and better on one. Here A is cheapest, C is lowest-CO₂ and highest-recovery, and B is in the middle on all three. No network beats another on every criterion, so **all three are Pareto-efficient**; the choice depends on priorities (weights) or on prices put on carbon and recovery.\n\n**Q1.2 Compute the trade-off ratios: how much extra cost buys each tonne of CO₂ reduction?**\n- A → B: +₹8 for −25 CO₂ → **₹0.32 per unit of CO₂ avoided**, and +30 points of recovery.\n- B → C: +₹7 for −20 CO₂ → **₹0.35 per unit of CO₂ avoided**, and +20 points of recovery.\n- A → C: +₹15 for −45 CO₂ → ₹0.333 per unit.\n\n**Q1.3 Use the objective function Z = Cost + λ·CO₂ (ignore recovery). For which carbon price λ is each network best?**\n- Z_A = 100 + 100λ; Z_B = 108 + 75λ; Z_C = 115 + 55λ.\n- A = B when 100 + 100λ = 108 + 75λ → 25λ = 8 → **λ = 0.32**.\n- B = C when 108 + 75λ = 115 + 55λ → 20λ = 7 → **λ = 0.35**.\n- **Result:** λ < 0.32 → **A** best; 0.32 < λ < 0.35 → **B** best; λ > 0.35 → **C** best. Check at λ = 0.5: A = 150, B = 145.5, C = 142.5 → C. At λ = 0.2: A = 120, B = 123, C = 126 → A.\n\n**Q1.4 Now add a value of μ = ₹0.10 per percentage point of recovery (as a benefit). Z = Cost + λ·CO₂ − μ·Recovery. With λ = 0.30, which network wins?**\n- Z_A = 100 + 30 − 2 = **128**; Z_B = 108 + 22.5 − 5 = **125.5**; Z_C = 115 + 16.5 − 7 = **124.5**.\n- **C wins**, although at λ = 0.30 alone A would have won (A 130, B 130.5, C 131.5). Valuing circularity shifts the choice towards the closed-loop network.\n\n**Q1.5 Solve with weighted scoring (min–max normalisation, 0 = worst, 1 = best) for three management priorities.**\nNormalised scores: Cost — A 1.000, B (115−108)/15 = 0.467, C 0.000. CO₂ — A 0.000, B (100−75)/45 = 0.556, C 1.000. Recovery — A 0.000, B 30/50 = 0.600, C 1.000.\n\n| Priority (weights cost/CO₂/recovery) | A | B | C | Choice |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cost-led (0.6 / 0.2 / 0.2) | 0.600 | 0.280 + 0.111 + 0.120 = **0.511** | 0.400 | **A** |\n| Balanced (⅓ each) | 0.333 | **0.541** | **0.667** | **C** |\n| Green (0.2 / 0.4 / 0.4) | 0.200 | 0.555 | **0.800** | **C** |\n\n**Q1.6 Write the managerial answer.**\n\"There is no single best network without decision-maker priorities.\" A cost-led firm picks A; any firm that prices carbon above ~₹0.35 per unit, or gives balanced weight to environment and circularity, picks C. B is a sensible **transition** network (costs only 8% more than A but cuts CO₂ by 25% and recovers 50%). The Lecture 8 conclusion: *\"The best sustainable network is not necessarily the cheapest network.\"* *\"Create the lowest-cost network that delivers the required service while minimising environmental impact and maximising resource circularity.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Problem 2. Allocating 10,000 returned units (Lecture 8, slide 37) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nAt the recovery facility, **Returns = Reuse + Repair + Remanufacture + Recycling + Disposal**:\n\n| Stream | Units |\n|---|---|\n| Reuse | 2,000 |\n| Repair | 2,500 |\n| Remanufacture | 2,000 |\n| Material recycling | 3,000 |\n| Disposal | 500 |\n| **Total** | **10,000** |\n\n*Question on the slide: where should each stream go to minimise cost and environmental impact?*\n\n**Q2.1 Verify the material balance and compute the circularity KPIs.**\n2,000 + 2,500 + 2,000 + 3,000 + 500 = **10,000** ✓.\n- **Recovery rate** = (10,000 − 500) ÷ 10,000 = **95%**.\n- **Value-retaining recovery** (reuse + repair + remanufacture, the top of the hierarchy) = 6,500 ÷ 10,000 = **65%**.\n- **Reuse rate** = 20%; **remanufacture rate** = 20%; **recycling** = 30%; **disposal to landfill** = 5%.\n\n**Q2.2 Assign each stream to a destination in the reverse network.**\nFollowing the Lecture 8 condition → destination table: Good → **resale** (reuse; route to the secondary market directly from the regional consolidation centre, no factory trip); Repairable → **repair** (regional repair hub close to customers, short turnaround); Recoverable → **remanufacture** (central remanufacturing plant, like Tata Motors Prolife or Renault Refactory, where skills and tooling are concentrated); Material recovery → **recycling** (specialised recycler such as Attero/Lohum for electronics and batteries, or Mahindra CERO for vehicles); No recovery value → **responsible disposal** (authorised TSDF/landfill). The principle: *preserve the highest possible value for the longest possible time*, and process close to the customer for streams that go back to the market, centrally for streams that need scale.\n\n**Q2.3 With the following assumed economics, compute the net value and CO₂ avoided. 📗**\n\n| Stream | Units | Processing cost (₹/unit) | Recovered value (₹/unit) | CO₂ avoided (kg/unit) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Reuse | 2,000 | 200 | 2,000 | 40 |\n| Repair | 2,500 | 800 | 1,800 | 30 |\n| Remanufacture | 2,000 | 1,500 | 3,000 | 25 |\n| Recycling | 3,000 | 300 | 500 | 8 |\n| Disposal | 500 | 150 | 0 | −2 |\n\n| Stream | Net value = units × (value − cost) | CO₂ avoided = units × kg |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reuse | 2,000 × 1,800 = ₹36,00,000 | 80,000 kg |\n| Repair | 2,500 × 1,000 = ₹25,00,000 | 75,000 kg |\n| Remanufacture | 2,000 × 1,500 = ₹30,00,000 | 50,000 kg |\n| Recycling | 3,000 × 200 = ₹6,00,000 | 24,000 kg |\n| Disposal | 500 × (−150) = −₹75,000 | −1,000 kg |\n| **Total** | **₹96,25,000** | **2,28,000 kg = 228 t** |\n\nPer returned unit: ₹962.5 net value and 22.8 kg CO₂ avoided. The 3,000 recycled units (30% of volume) earn only 6.2% of the value: moving even 500 units from recycling to remanufacture would add 500 × (1,500 − 200) = **₹6.5 lakh** and 500 × (25 − 8) = **8.5 t CO₂**. That is the Lecture 8 key principle: *\"Recycling is usually lower in the value-retention hierarchy than reuse, repair and remanufacturing.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Problem 3. FMCG distribution network — current situation and KPIs (Lecture 10, slide 12) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*Scenario: an FMCG company wants to reduce the environmental impact of its distribution network.* Current situation: **10,000 deliveries per month; average distance = 180 km; diesel trucks; 62% average vehicle utilisation; 18% empty return kilometres.* The slide's five KPIs: **CO₂e per tonne-km, fuel efficiency (km per litre), vehicle utilisation %, empty km %, OTIF %.**\n\n**Assumptions to complete the numerical (📗, state them):** 9-tonne trucks; diesel trucks average **4 km per litre**; diesel 2.68 kg CO₂ per litre; diesel ₹92 per litre; \"180 km\" is the loaded distance per delivery; \"18% empty km\" is the share of *total* km run empty.\n\n**Q3.1 Compute monthly total km, diesel, CO₂ and fuel cost.**\n- Loaded km = 10,000 × 180 = **18,00,000 km**. Since loaded km = 82% of total, total km = 18,00,000 ÷ 0.82 = **21,95,122 km**; empty km = **3,95,122 km**.\n- Diesel = 21,95,122 ÷ 4 = **5,48,780 litres**.\n- CO₂ = 5,48,780 × 2.68 = 14,70,732 kg = **1,470.7 t CO₂ per month** (≈ 17,650 t a year). This is Scope 1 if the fleet is owned, Scope 3 (Cat. 9) if hired.\n- Fuel cost = 5,48,780 × ₹92 = **₹5.05 crore per month**.\n\n**Q3.2 Compute the emission intensity in g CO₂e per tonne-km.**\nPayload per delivery = 62% × 9 t = 5.58 t. Tonne-km = 10,000 × 5.58 × 180 = **1,00,44,000 t-km**. Intensity = 14,70,732 kg ÷ 1,00,44,000 t-km = 0.1464 kg = **146 g CO₂e per t-km**.\n\n**Q3.3 The company raises utilisation to 80% (load consolidation, better routing) and cuts empty km to 10% (backhauls, freight exchange). Recompute.**\n- Same tonnage moved = 10,000 × 5.58 = 55,800 t. At 80% utilisation each truck carries 7.2 t → deliveries = 55,800 ÷ 7.2 = **7,750 trips**.\n- Loaded km = 7,750 × 180 = 13,95,000; total km = 13,95,000 ÷ 0.90 = **15,50,000 km**.\n- Diesel = 15,50,000 ÷ 4 = **3,87,500 l**; CO₂ = **1,038.5 t**; cost = **₹3.57 crore**.\n- Savings: **432 t CO₂ a month (−29.4%)** and **₹1.48 crore a month** (≈ ₹17.8 crore a year). New intensity = 10,38,500 ÷ 1,00,44,000 = **103 g CO₂e per t-km**.\n- Lesson from Lecture 11: *\"The cleanest kilometre is often the kilometre eliminated\"* — and *green transport ≠ sustainable logistics* unless packaging, delivery frequency, empty miles and returns are also fixed.\n\n**Q3.4 Write the five KPIs with formulas, current values and targets.**\n\n| KPI | Formula | Current | Target |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CO₂e per tonne-km | kg CO₂e ÷ tonne-km | 146 g | ≤ 105 g |\n| Fuel efficiency | km ÷ litres | 4.0 km/l | 4.4 km/l (eco-driving, tyre pressure, speed governors) |\n| Vehicle utilisation | load ÷ capacity | 62% | ≥ 80% |\n| Empty km % | empty km ÷ total km | 18% | ≤ 10% |\n| OTIF % | orders on time and in full ÷ total orders | (measure) | ≥ 95%, so that consolidation does not hurt service |\n\n**Q3.5 What would a switch of 30% of the km to electric trucks charged on the grid do? (Assume 1.2 kWh per km, grid 0.70 kg/kWh.)**\nAfter Q3.3, 30% of 15,50,000 km = 4,65,000 km. Diesel avoided = 4,65,000 ÷ 4 = 1,16,250 l → 311.6 t. EV emissions = 4,65,000 × 1.2 × 0.70 = 3,90,600 kg = 390.6 t. **The EVs emit more (390.6 t vs 311.6 t) on the coal-heavy grid.** EV trucks need renewable charging (solar at depots, green open-access power) to deliver a carbon benefit; with renewable power the saving is the full 311.6 t. This is the Lecture 9 point: *technology ≠ sustainability.*\n\n---\n\n## Problem 4. Road vs rail — modal shift (Lecture 8 green transportation network) 📘 concept + 📗 data ⭐⭐\nA Pune plant ships **1,000 t a month** to a Kolkata DC. Road route: 1,900 km. Rail route: 2,000 km plus 50 km of road drayage at each end. Factors: road 0.09, rail 0.025 kg CO₂e/t-km. Costs: road ₹3.2 per t-km; rail ₹1.6 per t-km + ₹400 per t for each terminal handling (two handlings).\n\n**Q4.1 Compare annual CO₂.**\nAnnual tonnage = 12,000 t. Road: 12,000 × 1,900 × 0.09 = 20,52,000 kg = **2,052 t**. Rail + drayage: 12,000 × (2,000 × 0.025 + 100 × 0.09) = 12,000 × (50 + 9) = 7,08,000 kg = **708 t**. Saving = **1,344 t (−65.5%)**.\n\n**Q4.2 Compare annual cost.**\nRoad: 12,000 × 1,900 × ₹3.2 = **₹7.30 crore**. Rail: 12,000 × [2,000 × 1.6 + 100 × 3.2 + 2 × 400] = 12,000 × [3,200 + 320 + 800] = 12,000 × 4,320 = **₹5.18 crore**. Rail is cheaper **and** cleaner (₹2.11 crore saving).\n\n**Q4.3 Why do many firms still use road?**\nTransit time and reliability (rail 5–7 days door-to-door vs road 3–4 days), extra handling and damage risk, rake availability, minimum lot sizes, and first/last-mile gaps. Answer with the Lecture 8 principle: *\"Use the lowest-impact transport mode compatible with service requirements.\"* Solution: a **sustainable multimodal network** (supplier → rail/inland waterway → regional DC → electric truck → customer), with safety stock at the DC to absorb the longer lead time.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 5. Distance criteria — load-distance and centre of gravity 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nA company serving four regional markets wants one central DC. Map grid units = 100 km.\n\n| Market | x | y | Annual volume (t) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Delhi | 2 | 8 | 4,000 |\n| Mumbai | 1 | 3 | 6,000 |\n| Kolkata | 9 | 5 | 3,000 |\n| Bengaluru | 3 | 0 | 5,000 |\n\nCandidate sites: **Nagpur (4, 5), Pune (1.5, 2.5), Hyderabad (3.5, 2).**\n\n**Q5.1 Find the centre of gravity.**\nΣW = 18,000. Cx = (2×4,000 + 1×6,000 + 9×3,000 + 3×5,000) ÷ 18,000 = (8,000 + 6,000 + 27,000 + 15,000) ÷ 18,000 = 56,000 ÷ 18,000 = **3.11**. Cy = (8×4,000 + 3×6,000 + 5×3,000 + 0×5,000) ÷ 18,000 = (32,000 + 18,000 + 15,000 + 0) ÷ 18,000 = 65,000 ÷ 18,000 = **3.61**. The CoG is at **(3.11, 3.61)**.\n\n**Q5.2 Compute the load-distance score (Euclidean) for each candidate site.**\n\n| Market (t) | Distance to Nagpur | Distance to Pune | Distance to Hyderabad |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Delhi (4,000) | √(4+9) = 3.606 → 14,422 | √(0.25+30.25) = 5.523 → 22,092 | √(2.25+36) = 6.185 → 24,739 |\n| Mumbai (6,000) | √(9+4) = 3.606 → 21,633 | √(0.25+0.25) = 0.707 → 4,243 | √(6.25+1) = 2.693 → 16,155 |\n| Kolkata (3,000) | √(25+0) = 5.000 → 15,000 | √(56.25+6.25) = 7.906 → 23,717 | √(30.25+9) = 6.265 → 18,796 |\n| Bengaluru (5,000) | √(1+25) = 5.099 → 25,495 | √(2.25+6.25) = 2.915 → 14,577 | √(0.25+4) = 2.062 → 10,308 |\n| **Total (t × grid units)** | **76,550** | **64,629** | **69,998** |\n\nLowest load-distance: **Pune (64,629)**, then Hyderabad (69,998), then Nagpur (76,550). In km, Pune ≈ 64,629 × 100 = 64.6 lakh t-km a year.\n\n**Q5.3 Why does Pune beat a site near the centre of gravity?**\nThe CoG minimises the sum of *squared*-type weighted distances, so it is a good *first approximation*, not the exact minimum of Σ(load × distance). Pune sits next to Mumbai, the heaviest market (6,000 t, one-third of volume). The CoG load-distance is 67,511 (Euclidean), worse than Pune. Always check candidate sites with the load-distance score.\n\n**Q5.4 Add the sustainability lens: convert the difference between Pune and Nagpur into CO₂ by road (0.09 kg/t-km).**\nDifference = (76,550 − 64,629) × 100 km = 11,92,100 t-km → × 0.09 = **107 t CO₂ a year** saved by Pune. But a full SSCM answer also checks rail connectivity, grid carbon intensity and renewable availability, flood/heat risk, water stress, land cost and labour — which is why we add a **factor-rating** step next. Lecture 11's new location questions: *What is the location's climate risk? Future water availability? Carbon intensity of electricity? Exposure to extreme weather? Dependence on a single port?*\n\n---\n\n## Problem 6. Factor rating for a sustainable DC location 📗 ⭐⭐\n| Factor | Weight | Site X | Site Y | Site Z |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Land & operating cost | 25 | 8 | 6 | 9 |\n| Proximity to customers (load-distance) | 25 | 6 | 9 | 7 |\n| Rail / multimodal access | 15 | 9 | 5 | 4 |\n| Renewable power availability / grid carbon | 15 | 7 | 8 | 5 |\n| Climate & flood risk (10 = lowest risk) | 10 | 6 | 8 | 4 |\n| Labour & community acceptance | 10 | 7 | 8 | 9 |\n\n**Q6.1 Rank the sites.**\nX = (25×8 + 25×6 + 15×9 + 15×7 + 10×6 + 10×7) ÷ 100 = (200 + 150 + 135 + 105 + 60 + 70) ÷ 100 = **7.20**. Y = (150 + 225 + 75 + 120 + 80 + 80) ÷ 100 = **7.30**. Z = (225 + 175 + 60 + 75 + 40 + 90) ÷ 100 = **6.65**. Ranking: **Y > X > Z**, with Y only 0.10 ahead of X.\n\n**Q6.2 Interpret.**\nZ is cheapest but has poor rail access and high flood risk (a \"cheapest location\" that the Lecture 11 climate-risk questions would reject). Y wins narrowly through customer proximity and low climate risk; X is the best multimodal site. With a margin of 0.10, check sensitivity: if the firm commits to rail-based distribution (rail weight 25, cost 15), X = 7.30 and Y = 6.90, so X wins. State the recommendation conditional on the transport strategy.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 7. How many DCs? Cost vs carbon trade-off 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\n| No. of DCs | Facility cost | Inventory cost | Inbound transport | Outbound transport | CO₂ (t/yr) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4.0 | 6.0 | 2.0 | 20.0 | 5,800 |\n| 2 | 7.0 | 7.5 | 2.6 | 13.0 | 4,500 |\n| 3 | 10.0 | 8.7 | 3.2 | 9.5 | 4,075 |\n| 4 | 13.0 | 9.8 | 3.8 | 7.8 | 4,100 |\n| 5 | 16.0 | 10.8 | 4.4 | 7.0 | 4,350 |\n\n(Costs in ₹ crore per year. CO₂ includes transport and facility energy.)\n\n**Q7.1 Find the cost-minimising number of DCs.**\nTotals: 1 DC = **32.0**; 2 DCs = **30.1**; 3 DCs = **31.4**; 4 DCs = **34.4**; 5 DCs = **38.2**. Minimum cost at **2 DCs (₹30.1 crore)**. More DCs cut outbound (last-mile) cost but raise facility, inventory (more safety stock, the square-root law) and inbound cost.\n\n**Q7.2 Find the CO₂-minimising number.**\nLowest CO₂ at **3 DCs (4,075 t)**. Beyond 3, the energy of extra buildings and the extra inbound trips outweigh the shorter last mile.\n\n**Q7.3 At what carbon price does 3 DCs become better than 2?**\nZ = cost + p × CO₂. 30.1 crore + 4,500p = 31.4 crore + 4,075p → 425p = ₹1.3 crore → p = 1,30,00,000 ÷ 425 = **₹30,588 per t CO₂**. Below ~₹30,600/t (far above today's market prices), 2 DCs stays optimal. Recommendation: **2 DCs now**, with solar roofs and EV last-mile to cut the 4,500 t, and design so that a third DC can be added if carbon prices or service needs rise.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 8. Packaging cube and milk-run consolidation (Lecture 8 green packaging & route optimisation) 📘 concept + 📗 data ⭐⭐\nLecture 8: *\"Packaging is not simply a procurement decision. It affects cube → vehicle utilisation → number of trips → fuel → CO₂.\"*\n\n**Q8.1 Right-sizing the carton raises units per truck from 800 to 1,000. Monthly volume 1,00,000 units; each trip 500 km at 4 km/l. Savings?**\nTrips: 1,00,000 ÷ 800 = 125 → 1,00,000 ÷ 1,000 = 100 → **25 trips saved (−20%)**. Diesel per trip = 500 ÷ 4 = 125 l → 25 × 125 = 3,125 l saved → **8.4 t CO₂ a month** (3,125 × 2.68) and ₹2.9 lakh of diesel at ₹92/l.\n\n**Q8.2 A depot serves three customers with separate round trips: D–A 40 km, D–B 50 km, D–C 60 km. A milk run D→A→B→C→D uses A–B 15 km and B–C 20 km. One truck can carry all three loads. Compare km and CO₂ (truck: 6 km/l).**\nSeparate: 2 × (40 + 50 + 60) = **300 km** → 50 l → 134 kg CO₂. Milk run: 40 + 15 + 20 + 60 = **135 km** → 22.5 l → 60.3 kg CO₂. Saving **165 km (55%)** and 73.7 kg CO₂ per cycle. This is the Lecture 8 network decision *\"which customers should be consolidated? — consolidate customers A, B and C for one delivery.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Problem 9. Air vs sea for an export order 📗 ⭐\nA 20 t shipment from Chennai to Rotterdam. Sea: 16,000 km, 0.015 kg/t-km, ₹3 lakh, 30 days. Air: 8,500 km, 0.60 kg/t-km, ₹40 lakh, 3 days.\n\n**Q9.1 Emissions and the cost of speed.**\nSea: 20 × 16,000 × 0.015 = **4.8 t CO₂**. Air: 20 × 8,500 × 0.60 = **102 t CO₂** (21 times more). Air costs ₹37 lakh more and emits 97.2 t more. Use air only for urgent, high-value, low-weight items (spares, pharma). Better: plan earlier (forecasting), keep sea as default, and use sea-air for partial urgency. Lecture 10 trap: *\"Fast delivery but excessive inventory/air freight\"* is green-looking efficiency that is not sustainable.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 10. Consumer-electronics network design exercise (Lecture 8, slide 42) 📘 + 📗 data ⭐⭐⭐\n*A consumer-electronics company has 3 suppliers, 2 manufacturing plants, 4 regional DCs, 10,000 customers and 20% expected product returns. It is considering one centralised recycling centre, two regional collection centres and one remanufacturing facility.* Design choices:\n\n| Decision | Option A | Option B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Collection | Centralised | Regional |\n| Transport | Road | Rail + road |\n| Recovery | Recycle | Remanufacture |\n| Warehouse | Conventional | Solar |\n| Packaging | Single-use | Returnable |\n\n*Then evaluate cost, CO₂, recovery and service. Question: should the company select the cheapest network?* The slide's three network options: Network A — low cost but high emissions; Network B — higher initial investment but lower operating emissions; Network C — higher reverse-logistics complexity but higher material recovery.\n\n**Q10.1 How many returns must the reverse network handle?**\n20% × 10,000 customers = **2,000 returned units a year** (if each customer buys one unit a year).\n\n**Q10.2 Evaluate Option A vs Option B with the assumed annual data below. 📗**\n\n| Element (₹ lakh / year) | Option A | Option B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Collection | 30 | 42 (two regional centres) |\n| Transport | 60 | 52 (rail for long-haul) |\n| Recovery processing | 10 (recycle at ₹500/unit) | 50 (remanufacture at ₹2,500/unit) |\n| Warehouse energy | 20 | 22 (14 energy + 8 annualised solar capex) |\n| Packaging | 15 | 13 (9 + 4 returnable-pool amortisation) |\n| **Gross logistics cost** | **135** | **179** |\n| Recovery revenue | 16 (scrap at ₹800/unit) | 120 (resale at ₹6,000/unit) |\n| **Net cost** | **119** | **59** |\n\n| Element (t CO₂e / year) | Option A | Option B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Transport | 400 | 260 |\n| Warehouse | 150 | 40 |\n| Packaging | 60 | 25 |\n| Credit for recovery | −16 (8 kg/unit) | −120 (60 kg/unit) |\n| **Net CO₂** | **594** | **205** |\n\nRecovery: A recovers materials only (lowest value-retention); B returns products to use (remanufacture, high value-retention). Service: return turnaround ~14 days (A, centralised) vs ~6 days (B, regional).\n\n**Q10.3 Should the company select the cheapest network?**\nOn **gross logistics cost**, A is cheapest (₹135 lakh vs ₹179 lakh). But on **net cost after recovery value** B is cheaper (₹59 lakh vs ₹119 lakh), and B cuts CO₂ by **65%** (594 → 205 t), retains more value and gives faster service. So **no**: choose B, or a hybrid (regional collection with rail-based consolidation to a central remanufacturing plant, recycling only what cannot be remanufactured). The answer depends on remanufacturable share and resale price, so test the sensitivity: if only 40% of returns are remanufacturable, B's revenue falls and the gap narrows. Key message: **design the forward and reverse network together.**\n\n---\n\n## Problem 11. ITC eChoupal — the network economics (HBS case in the course zip) 📘 ⭐⭐\nFacts from the case: a typical farmer's lot = **20 quintals (2 t)**; at the mandi he loses about **10 kg (0.5%)** in manual weighing ≈ **₹100 per lot**; farmers travelled **30–50 km** to the mandi; the eChoupal design puts a **kiosk within walking distance (< 5 km)** and a **hub within driving distance (< 30 km)** of every farmer (average 20 km, range 5–30 km); ITC saved about **$5 per tonne** on freight and the farmer gained about **$8 per tonne** on average; ITC reimbursed transport cost; the Sanchalak earned **0.5%** commission on purchases (2–3% on inputs); setup cost **₹1,70,000** per eChoupal plus **₹1,00,000** for people, travel and training; ITC had **1,695 eChoupals covering 8,400 villages** in MP, and **5 processing units + 39 warehouses = 44 points** versus 51 large soybean mandis. Exchange rate in the case: $1 = ₹45.23.\n\n**Q11.1 What does a farmer gain per lot of 2 t by selling through the eChoupal hub?**\nPrice/efficiency gain: 2 t × $8 = $16 = **₹724**; weighing loss avoided ≈ **₹100**; plus freight reimbursed and cash paid immediately (no credit period, no 2–3 day queue). Total ≈ **₹824 per lot**, against an average farmer's annual net income of ~₹20,000 from soybean and wheat (≈ 4%).\n\n**Q11.2 How many villages does one eChoupal serve? What does that say about network density?**\n8,400 ÷ 1,695 ≈ **5 villages per eChoupal**. Information nodes are dense and cheap (one PC kiosk), while physical nodes (44 hubs) are few and large. This splits the **information flow** (price discovery at village level) from the **physical flow** (bulk movement to hubs), the same physical + information + financial integration Lecture 8 asks for.\n\n**Q11.3 Using the distance criteria, why did ITC add hubs after launch?**\nTo keep every farmer within **30 km** of a hub, ITC added 3 processing hubs and 36 warehouses in MP. If the average one-way distance falls from ~40 km (mandi) to ~20 km (hub), a tractor trip saves ~40 km round-trip. At an assumed 0.25 l diesel per km, that is 10 l (≈ 27 kg CO₂) and roughly ₹900 per trip. Shorter distance, fewer intermediaries (the \"village → mandi → factory\" cycle broken) and less handling also reduce crop wastage, which is why Lecture 1 classifies it as a delivery-based SSCM initiative.\n\n**Q11.4 Break-even on one eChoupal kiosk?**\nInvestment ≈ ₹1,70,000 + ₹1,00,000 = ₹2,70,000. If a kiosk channels 1,000 t a year, ITC's freight saving alone = 1,000 × $5 × 45.23 = **₹2,26,150 a year**, so payback ≈ **1.2 years** (the case says ITC expected to recover cost within three years). The Samyojak (former commission agent) keeps a role in logistics, an example of inclusive network redesign.\n\n---\n\n## Practice set 📗\n\n**Q12. A DC handles 2,40,000 units a month using 1,80,000 kWh. After LEDs and smart HVAC (IKEA, Prologis examples) use falls to 1,35,000 kWh. Compute the KPI \"energy per unit handled\" before and after and the CO₂ saving.**\nBefore: 0.75 kWh/unit; after: 0.5625 kWh/unit (−25%). Saving 45,000 kWh × 0.70 = **31.5 t CO₂ a month**.\n\n**Q13. A reverse network collects 600 returned units a month from rural customers, 6 units per village visit, at ₹900 per visit. A drop-off point at the local kirana store (decentralised configuration) lets the truck collect 60 units per visit at ₹1,500. Compare cost per unit.**\nVillage visits: 100 visits × ₹900 = ₹90,000 → **₹150/unit**. Drop-off: 10 visits × ₹1,500 = ₹15,000 → **₹25/unit**. Low return density is a top reverse-logistics challenge (Lecture 8); aggregation points fix it.\n\n**Q14. A firm moves 5,000 t over 800 km by road (0.09 kg/t-km). Utilisation improves from 60% to 75% by consolidation. If emissions scale with vehicle-km, what is the new CO₂?**\nBase: 5,000 × 800 × 0.09 = **360 t**. Vehicle-km fall by 60/75 = 0.8, so CO₂ ≈ 360 × 0.8 = **288 t** (−20%). (Fuel per km rises slightly with load, so the true saving is a little smaller.)\n\n**Q15. Strategic, tactical or operational? (a) Location of a new recycling facility; (b) which DC serves the eastern customers; (c) collection frequency for returns; (d) capacity of a remanufacturing plant; (e) which transport mode for returns; (f) shipment consolidation.**\nStrategic: (a), (d). Tactical: (b), (e). Operational: (c), (f). Lecture 8: *\"Strategic sets the network. Tactical connects the network. Operational makes it perform.\"*\n"}, {"slug": "p4-performance-measurement-numericals", "label": "CO5 Performance Measurement", "tag": "P4", "group": "focus", "title": "CO5 Numericals: Effective Performance Measurement Systems", "md": "# CO5 Numericals: Effective Performance Measurement Systems\nThis page covers **CO5: effective performance measurement systems**. It is a **medium-weightage** CO in the end-sem paper, and it is also one of the two COs in the **20-mark online quiz** (CO5 + CO6, from PPTs 9, 10 and 11) 📘. The numericals come from **Lecture 3** (the performance dashboard and the performance management framework), **Lecture 10** (KPI architecture, SCOR + sustainability KPIs, quality as sustainability, the FMCG 5-KPI exercise, Company A vs B) and **Lecture 9** (Technology Adoption Score, Net Technology Value and the Navya exercise) 📘. Practice data are marked 📗. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers.\n\nTheory: [Measuring Sustainable SC Performance](ch08-performance-measurement.md) and [Technology & Quality Tools](ch05-technology-quality-tools.md).\n\n---\n\n## Formulas you must know for CO5 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Delivery and customer KPIs**\n- **On-time delivery %** = orders delivered on or before the promised date ÷ total orders × 100.\n- **In-full %** = orders delivered complete ÷ total orders × 100.\n- **OTIF %** = orders delivered *both* on time *and* in full ÷ total orders × 100. (OTIF is not OT% × IF% unless the two are independent; count the orders that pass both.)\n- **Fill rate (unit)** = units shipped from stock ÷ units ordered × 100.\n- **Perfect order rate** = orders that are on time, in full, damage-free and with correct documents ÷ total orders. If only component rates are given and you assume independence, perfect order ≈ OTIF × damage-free % × correct-documents %.\n- **NPS** = % promoters (9–10) − % detractors (0–6); range −100 to +100.\n\n**Operational KPIs**\n- **OEE** = Availability × Performance × Quality, where Availability = run time ÷ planned production time; Performance = (ideal cycle time × total units) ÷ run time; Quality = good units ÷ total units. World-class OEE is about 85%.\n- **First-pass yield (FPY)** = units right first time ÷ units started.\n- **Vehicle utilisation %** = load carried ÷ vehicle capacity; **empty km %** = empty km ÷ total km.\n- **Inventory turns** = COGS ÷ average inventory; **days of inventory** = 365 ÷ turns.\n\n**Environmental intensity KPIs** (always normalise; absolute numbers hide growth)\n- **Carbon intensity** = t CO₂e ÷ output (per unit, per tonne, per ₹ crore of revenue). BRSR reports intensity per rupee of turnover (and per rupee of turnover adjusted for PPP).\n- **Energy intensity** = GJ or kWh ÷ unit of output; **water intensity** = kL ÷ unit; **waste recycling rate** = waste recycled or reused ÷ total waste generated.\n- **Transport carbon intensity** = g CO₂e ÷ tonne-km.\n\n**Social KPIs**\n- **LTIFR** (lost-time injury frequency rate) = number of lost-time injuries × 1,000,000 ÷ total person-hours worked (BRSR uses per one million person-hours).\n- **Training hours per employee** = total training hours ÷ number of employees.\n- **Supplier audit coverage %** = suppliers (or spend) audited ÷ total suppliers (or spend).\n\n**Target achievement (for scorecards and composite indices)**\n- Higher-is-better KPI: achievement = actual ÷ target.\n- Lower-is-better KPI: achievement = target ÷ actual.\n- Cap the achievement (for example at 1.2 or 120%) so one over-achievement cannot hide a failure elsewhere. Composite index = Σ (weight × capped achievement).\n- **Gap to benchmark** = (your value − benchmark) ÷ benchmark. **Required annual improvement** to close a gap in n years = 1 − (benchmark ÷ current)^(1/n) for a lower-is-better KPI.\n\n**Lecture 9 technology formulas** 📘\n- **TAS (Technology Adoption Score)** = 25% Economic + 25% Environmental + 20% Social + 15% Resilience + 15% Strategic fit, each scored 1–5.\n- **Net Technology Value** = Gross benefits − Adoption cost − Lifecycle sustainability cost − Risk cost.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 1. Monthly KPI computation for a distributor 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nA distribution centre's data for one month: 2,000 orders. 1,880 were delivered on time, 1,910 in full and 1,830 both on time and in full. 1,975 arrived damage-free and 1,985 had correct invoices and documents. Customers ordered 50,000 units, of which 48,600 were shipped from stock.\n\n**Q1.1 Compute on-time %, in-full %, OTIF %, fill rate and perfect-order rate.**\n- On-time = 1,880 ÷ 2,000 = **94.0%**.\n- In-full = 1,910 ÷ 2,000 = **95.5%**.\n- OTIF = 1,830 ÷ 2,000 = **91.5%** (this is higher than the 94.0% × 95.5% = 89.8% you would get by multiplying, because late orders and short orders often overlap; always use the joint count when it is given).\n- Fill rate (units) = 48,600 ÷ 50,000 = **97.2%**.\n- Perfect order (assuming independence of damage and documents from OTIF) = 0.915 × (1,975 ÷ 2,000) × (1,985 ÷ 2,000) = 0.915 × 0.9875 × 0.9925 = **89.7%**.\n\n**Q1.2 Interpret. Why is fill rate (97.2%) so much better than perfect order (89.7%)?**\nFill rate measures only whether *units* were available; perfect order measures the *whole customer experience*, so every failure mode (late, short, damaged, wrong paperwork) multiplies down. A company that reports only fill rate looks excellent while one order in ten still fails the customer. From a sustainability view every imperfect order also creates waste: re-delivery trips, returns, damaged goods and duplicate paperwork.\n\n## Problem 2. OEE, intensity and safety KPIs for a plant 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nA plant runs one 480-minute shift. Breakdowns and changeovers take 60 minutes. The ideal cycle time is 0.8 minute per unit. The shift produced 470 units, of which 451 were good. For the year, the plant emitted 18,500 t CO₂e (Scope 1 + 2), made 1.25 million units, used 95,000 GJ of energy and 3,10,000 kL of water, on revenue of ₹2,500 crore. It had 2,400 employees working 2,100 hours each and recorded 4 lost-time injuries.\n\n**Q2.1 Compute OEE.**\n- Availability = (480 − 60) ÷ 480 = 420 ÷ 480 = **87.5%**.\n- Performance = (0.8 × 470) ÷ 420 = 376 ÷ 420 = **89.5%**.\n- Quality = 451 ÷ 470 = **96.0%**.\n- **OEE = 0.875 × 0.895 × 0.960 = 75.2%** (below the ~85% world-class benchmark; the biggest loss is availability).\n\n**Q2.2 Compute the intensity KPIs.**\n- Carbon intensity = 18,500 ÷ 2,500 = **7.4 t CO₂e per ₹ crore** of revenue, or 18,500,000 kg ÷ 1,250,000 = **14.8 kg CO₂e per unit**.\n- Energy intensity = 95,000 GJ ÷ 1.25 M units = 0.076 GJ = **76 MJ per unit**.\n- Water intensity = 3,10,000 ÷ 12,50,000 = **0.248 kL (248 litres) per unit**.\n\n**Q2.3 Compute LTIFR.**\nPerson-hours = 2,400 × 2,100 = 50,40,000. LTIFR = 4 × 10,00,000 ÷ 50,40,000 = **0.79 per million hours**.\n\n**Q2.4 How does OEE link to sustainability?**\nLost availability, slow running and defects all consume energy and materials without producing good output. Raising OEE from 75% to 85% makes about 13% more good units from the same machines, energy base-load and floor space, so energy, water and CO₂ *per good unit* fall even if absolute use stays flat. This is Lecture 7's point that lean and sustainability reinforce each other.\n\n## Problem 3. Reading the Lecture 3 performance dashboard 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 3 (slide 24) shows a \"Suggested MBA Dashboard\" with six perspectives, each with four KPIs and a \"vs target\" variance:\n\n| Perspective | KPI (actual, vs target) |\n|---|---|\n| Economic | Revenue growth 12.5% (+2.5); operating margin 15.8% (+1.8); cost per shipment $96.4 (−3.6%); ROCE 18.7% (+2.7) |\n| Environmental | CO₂ per tonne-km 62.3 (−8.7%); energy intensity 1.45 (−6.3%); water intensity 2.10 (−5.1%); waste recycling 78% (+8) |\n| Social | LTIFR 0.68 (−15%); employee training 24.6 h (+12%); diversity 36% (+6); supplier audit score 88 (+5) |\n| Risk | High-risk suppliers 6% (−2); supply disruption incidents 3 (−25%); on-time risk assessment 92% (+7); risk exposure score 2.3 (−0.4) |\n| Customer | On-time delivery 96.2% (+3.2); perfect order 93.1% (+2.1); order fill 98.4% (+1.4); NPS 68 (+8) |\n| Innovation | New products 6 (+2); process improvement projects 14 (+4); automation coverage 43% (+8); R&D spend 2.8% of revenue (+0.3) |\n\nTrend charts (Jan–Jun): operating margin rises from about 10% to 15–16%; CO₂ per tonne-km falls from about 78 to 62; LTIFR falls from about 1.4 to about 0.65; risk exposure peaks at about 3.2 in February and ends at about 2.3; on-time delivery rises from about 89% to about 96%. Risk level split: High 14%, Medium 32%, Low 54%. Innovation funnel: 48 ideas → 23 in progress → 11 testing → 6 launched. The slide's message is *\"Balanced dashboard = performance data > strategic decisions.\"*\n\n**Q3.1 Back-calculate the targets for CO₂ per tonne-km, LTIFR, on-time delivery and cost per shipment.**\n- CO₂/t-km: actual is 8.7% below target → target = 62.3 ÷ (1 − 0.087) = **68.2 g/t-km**.\n- LTIFR: actual is 15% below target → target = 0.68 ÷ 0.85 = **0.80**.\n- On-time delivery: +3.2 points → target = **93.0%**.\n- Cost per shipment: 3.6% below target → target = 96.4 ÷ 0.964 = **$100**.\n(Read \"%\" variances on a percentage KPI as percentage points, and \"%\" variances on an absolute KPI as relative change. State this assumption in the exam.)\n\n**Q3.2 Build a composite performance index with weights Economic 20%, Environmental 20%, Social 15%, Risk 15%, Customer 20%, Innovation 10%, using capped (1.2) achievement ratios averaged within each perspective.**\n\n| Perspective | Achievement ratios (capped at 1.2) | Average | Weight | Contribution |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Economic | 1.200, 1.129, 1.037, 1.169 | 1.134 | 0.20 | 0.227 |\n| Environmental | 1.095, 1.067, 1.054, 1.114 | 1.083 | 0.20 | 0.217 |\n| Social | 1.176, 1.120, 1.200, 1.060 | 1.139 | 0.15 | 0.171 |\n| Risk | 1.200, 1.200, 1.082, 1.174 | 1.164 | 0.15 | 0.175 |\n| Customer | 1.034, 1.023, 1.014, 1.133 | 1.051 | 0.20 | 0.210 |\n| Innovation | 1.200, 1.200, 1.200, 1.120 | 1.180 | 0.10 | 0.118 |\n| **Index** | | | | **1.117** |\n\n(Examples: revenue growth 12.5 ÷ 10 = 1.25 → capped 1.20; CO₂ 68.2 ÷ 62.3 = 1.095; high-risk suppliers 8 ÷ 6 = 1.33 → capped 1.20.)\n\nThe company is performing at about **112% of target** overall; customer (1.05) and environmental (1.08) are the weakest perspectives.\n\n**Q3.3 Every KPI is green. Is that good news? Give a manager's interpretation.**\nNot necessarily. Lecture 10's \"KPI trap\" warns that more KPIs do not mean better management. When all 24 KPIs beat target, the likely causes are soft targets (set from last year's actuals instead of science-based or benchmark targets), or KPIs chosen because they are easy to hit. The useful questions are: is 62.3 g/t-km good against the **benchmark** (not only against our own target)? Is the 14% high-risk share of the supply base (risk donut) consistent with only 6% \"high-risk suppliers\"? Is a 36% diversity figure measured in the same way as peers? Do the trends (CO₂ per t-km flattening after April, LTIFR rising slightly in June) need action? The next step is to raise targets to benchmark or SBTi levels and check that each KPI changes a decision.\n\n**Q3.4 Read the trends: what is the percentage improvement in CO₂ per tonne-km and in LTIFR from January to June?**\n- CO₂/t-km: (78 − 62.3) ÷ 78 ≈ **20% reduction** in six months.\n- LTIFR: (1.4 − 0.68) ÷ 1.4 ≈ **51% reduction**.\n- On-time delivery: 89% → 96.2%, a gain of about **7 percentage points**.\nBoth environmental and safety improvements came with better delivery and margin, which supports the Lecture 3 claim that sustainability and business performance can move together.\n\n## Problem 4. Sustainability balanced scorecard with weighted achievement 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nA logistics company uses a Sustainability Balanced Scorecard with these KPIs, targets and weights:\n\n| KPI | Actual | Target | Better | Weight |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cost per shipment (₹) | 9,640 | 10,000 | Lower | 15% |\n| ROCE (%) | 18.7 | 18.2 | Higher | 10% |\n| CO₂ (g per t-km) | 62.3 | 68.2 | Lower | 15% |\n| Energy intensity (kWh per unit) | 1.45 | 1.55 | Lower | 10% |\n| Waste recycling (%) | 78 | 85 | Higher | 10% |\n| LTIFR | 0.68 | 0.60 | Lower | 10% |\n| Supplier audit score | 88 | 90 | Higher | 10% |\n| OTIF (%) | 93.1 | 95 | Higher | 20% |\n\n**Q4.1 Compute the achievement ratio for each KPI and the weighted index.**\n\n| KPI | Formula | Ratio | Weighted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cost/shipment | 10,000 ÷ 9,640 | 1.037 | 0.156 |\n| ROCE | 18.7 ÷ 18.2 | 1.027 | 0.103 |\n| CO₂/t-km | 68.2 ÷ 62.3 | 1.095 | 0.164 |\n| Energy intensity | 1.55 ÷ 1.45 | 1.069 | 0.107 |\n| Waste recycling | 78 ÷ 85 | 0.918 | 0.092 |\n| LTIFR | 0.60 ÷ 0.68 | 0.882 | 0.088 |\n| Supplier audit | 88 ÷ 90 | 0.978 | 0.098 |\n| OTIF | 93.1 ÷ 95 | 0.980 | 0.196 |\n| **Index** | | | **1.003** |\n\n**Q4.2 The index is 100.3%, \"on target\". What does the manager actually learn?**\nThe headline hides that **four of eight KPIs are below target**, including the safety KPI (LTIFR 12% worse than target) and recycling (8% short). Over-achievement on cost and carbon offsets these. Two fixes are common: (a) a **knock-out or minimum threshold** for critical KPIs (for example any safety KPI below 95% of target turns the scorecard red, the same logic as Lecture 10's \"95/100 on cost-quality-delivery but serious labour violations\" supplier question); (b) report the index **by perspective** and not only as one number. A single composite is useful for ranking but dangerous for management.\n\n**Q4.3 Map these KPIs to the four Balanced Scorecard perspectives from Lecture 10.**\n- Financial: cost per shipment, ROCE (Lecture 10 examples: supply chain cost, ROIC, cost avoidance).\n- Customer: OTIF (Lecture 10: sustainable product share, customer complaints, green customer retention).\n- Internal process: CO₂/t-km, energy intensity, waste recycling, supplier audit score (Lecture 10: carbon intensity, supplier quality, circularity).\n- Learning and growth: none here. That is a gap; add ESG skills or training hours, innovation or employee engagement (Lecture 10 examples) and LTIFR could sit with people as well.\n\n## Problem 5. Five KPIs for the FMCG distribution network (Lecture 10, slide 12) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 10's exercise: *An FMCG company wants to reduce the environmental impact of its distribution network. Current situation: 10,000 deliveries/month, average distance 180 km, diesel trucks, 62% average vehicle utilisation, 18% empty return kilometres. Develop 5 KPIs.* The slide's answer: **1) Carbon → CO₂e per tonne-km; 2) Cost → fuel efficiency (km per litre); 3) Utilisation → vehicle utilisation %; 4) Empty km → empty km %; 5) Reliability → OTIF %.**\n\n**Q5.1 For each KPI write the formula, the baseline and a SMART target.**\n\n| KPI | Formula | Baseline (📗 where not given) | SMART target |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CO₂e per tonne-km | Total fleet CO₂e ÷ total tonne-km delivered | 146 g/t-km (computed in CO4 Problem 3) | Reduce to 110 g/t-km by March 2028 through consolidation and CNG/EV pilots |\n| Fuel efficiency | Total km ÷ litres of diesel | 4.0 km/l | Improve to 4.6 km/l in 12 months through driver training and route optimisation |\n| Vehicle utilisation | Load carried ÷ capacity (weight or cube) | 62% | Raise to 80% within 9 months via load planning and milk runs |\n| Empty km % | Empty km ÷ total km | 18% | Cut to 8% within 12 months through backhaul partnerships |\n| OTIF % | Orders on time and in full ÷ total orders | 92% | Hold at ≥ 95% while the changes above are made |\n\n**Q5.2 Why is OTIF on the list when the goal is environmental?**\nOTIF is the **guard-rail KPI**. Higher utilisation and fewer trips can be achieved by delaying shipments until trucks are full, which would hurt customers. Lecture 10's five tests say a KPI set must be **balanced**, so an environmental programme carries one service KPI to make sure CO₂ is not cut at the expense of reliability.\n\n**Q5.3 Classify the five KPIs as leading or lagging.**\nCO₂e per t-km, fuel efficiency and OTIF are **lagging** (they report what happened). Vehicle utilisation and empty km % are closer to **leading** for carbon, because improving them today predicts lower CO₂ next month. Lecture 10: *\"Lagging KPIs tell you what happened; leading KPIs tell you whether you are likely to improve.\"* Add a pure leading KPI such as **% of trips planned with the route-optimisation tool** or **% of drivers trained in eco-driving**.\n\n## Problem 6. Leading vs lagging and the KPI architecture (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐\nLecture 10's KPI architecture pairs each leading indicator with the lagging result it drives:\n\n| Leading indicator | Lagging indicator |\n|---|---|\n| Supplier ESG assessments | Actual supplier incidents |\n| Employee training | Injury rate |\n| Renewable-energy contract | Actual Scope 2 emissions |\n| Eco-design projects | Product carbon footprint |\n| Preventive maintenance | Equipment failure |\n| Supplier development | Defect rate |\n| Risk assessments | Disruption losses |\n\n**Q6.1 Classify each of these as leading or lagging, and as input, process, output, outcome or business value (Lecture 3 performance management framework): (a) % of suppliers trained on decarbonisation; (b) Scope 3 Category 1 emissions; (c) number of energy audits completed; (d) energy cost saved (₹ crore); (e) % of renewable electricity contracted for next year; (f) customer complaints on damaged packaging; (g) investment in solar rooftop; (h) market share of green products.**\n- (a) Leading; process.\n- (b) Lagging; outcome.\n- (c) Leading; process (activity).\n- (d) Lagging; business value.\n- (e) Leading; input.\n- (f) Lagging; outcome.\n- (g) Leading; input.\n- (h) Lagging; business value.\nThe Lecture 3 chain runs **inputs (resources, investments) → processes (activities) → outputs (immediate results) → outcomes (impact on stakeholders and the environment) → business value (profit, growth, reputation)**. A good system has KPIs at every stage, so leaders can see the problem before it reaches the lagging result.\n\n**Q6.2 Lecture 10 says every KPI must answer four questions. Apply them to \"energy per unit\".**\nThe four questions on the slide: ***What happened? Why did it happen? Who owns it? What decision will change because of it?*** For energy per unit (kWh per good unit): *what happened* — it rose from 3.0 to 3.3 kWh/unit this month; *why* — compressed-air leaks and idle running during a changeover-heavy schedule; *who owns it* — the plant head with the maintenance lead; *what decision changes* — run a leak audit, switch off idle machines and reschedule energy-intensive batches to solar hours. If no decision would change, the KPI is noise (Lecture 10's \"KPI trap\": a company may track 75 sustainability, 40 supplier and 30 logistics indicators *\"yet managers still cannot answer: are we becoming more sustainable?\"*).\n\n## Problem 7. SCOR + sustainability KPI set (Lecture 10) 📘 concept + 📗 data ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 10 adds one sustainability KPI to each SCOR process: **Plan** – carbon-aware demand planning (forecast accuracy); **Source** – supplier sustainability score; **Make** – energy per unit; **Deliver** – CO₂e per shipment; **Return** – product recovery rate; **Enable** – ESG data quality.\n\nData for one year 📗: forecast MAPE 18%; procurement spend ₹600 crore, of which ₹420 crore is with suppliers scoring ≥ 70 on the ESG scorecard; plant electricity 3,600 MWh for 1.2 million units; outbound logistics 1,850 t CO₂e over 46,000 shipments; 9,500 units returned, 8,550 recovered by reuse, refurbishment or recycling; 1,300 ESG data points reported, 1,170 of them verified against source evidence.\n\n**Q7.1 Compute the SCOR sustainability KPIs.**\n- Plan: forecast accuracy = 1 − 0.18 = **82%**. (Better forecasting reduces over-production, expediting by air and obsolete stock, so it is a carbon lever.)\n- Source: % of spend with ESG-qualified suppliers = 420 ÷ 600 = **70%**.\n- Make: energy per unit = 3,600,000 kWh ÷ 1,200,000 = **3.0 kWh per unit**.\n- Deliver: CO₂e per shipment = 1,850,000 kg ÷ 46,000 = **40.2 kg CO₂e per shipment**.\n- Return: recovery rate = 8,550 ÷ 9,500 = **90%**.\n- Enable: ESG data quality = 1,170 ÷ 1,300 = **90% verified**.\n\n**Q7.2 Which KPI should the board look at first, and why?**\n**Enable (data quality)**. With only 90% of data points verified, every other figure carries uncertainty; BRSR Core (Lecture 10) is moving companies from *\"we are committed to sustainability\"* to *\"show me the data\"*, with assurance, evidence trails and internal controls. The second priority is Source (70%), because purchased goods are usually the largest Scope 3 category.\n\n## Problem 8. Quality failure as an environmental failure (Lecture 10, Section B) 📘 concept + 📗 data ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 10: defects lead to rework, which needs extra materials and extra energy, then extra transport, then waste and more CO₂. *\"Quality failure is simultaneously an economic and environmental failure.\"*\n\nA component plant makes 5,00,000 units a year with a 4% defect rate. Of defective units, 60% are reworked (5 kWh and ₹150 each) and 40% are scrapped and remade (2.5 kg steel per unit at 2.0 kg CO₂e per kg steel, 12 kWh per remade unit, ₹900 per scrapped unit). Grid factor 0.71 kg CO₂e per kWh.\n\n**Q8.1 Compute the annual carbon and cost of poor quality.**\n- Defective units = 4% × 5,00,000 = 20,000 → reworked 12,000, scrapped 8,000.\n- Rework energy = 12,000 × 5 = 60,000 kWh → 60,000 × 0.71 = **42.6 t CO₂e**; cost 12,000 × ₹150 = ₹18 lakh.\n- Scrap material = 8,000 × 2.5 kg = 20,000 kg steel × 2.0 = **40.0 t CO₂e**.\n- Re-production energy = 8,000 × 12 = 96,000 kWh × 0.71 = **68.2 t CO₂e**.\n- Scrap cost = 8,000 × ₹900 = ₹72 lakh.\n- **Total: about 150.8 t CO₂e and ₹90 lakh a year.**\n\n**Q8.2 A Six Sigma project cuts defects to 1%. What are the savings, and what is the change in sigma level?**\nDefects fall by 75%, so savings are 0.75 × 150.8 = **113 t CO₂e** and 0.75 × ₹90 lakh = **₹67.5 lakh a year**. DPMO falls from 40,000 to 10,000, which is roughly **3.25σ → 3.8σ** (with the conventional 1.5σ shift). This is the TQM + Lean + Six Sigma + Green Supply Chain connection the slide makes; the waste type is the \"D\" (defects) of TIMWOOD.\n\n## Problem 9. Benchmarking gap and the required rate of improvement (Lecture 3) 📘 concept + 📗 data ⭐⭐\nLecture 3 defines benchmarking as *comparing performance, processes and practices with the best-performing organisations or recognised standards to identify gaps and opportunities*, against industry leaders (Amazon logistics, Toyota lean, Unilever sourcing), competitors (Tata Motors vs M&M, Asian Paints vs Berger, HUL vs ITC), global standards (ISO 9001, 14001, 45001, 27001; BRSR, GRI, CDP, SBTi) and your own history. *\"Benchmark not to imitate competition but to continuously outperform self.\"*\n\nA paint company's carbon intensity is 0.80 t CO₂e per tonne of product. The industry leader is at 0.50.\n\n**Q9.1 What is the gap?**\nGap = (0.80 − 0.50) ÷ 0.50 = **60% above the leader** (or the leader is 37.5% lower than the company).\n\n**Q9.2 What constant annual reduction closes the gap in 5 years (assuming the leader stands still)?**\nRequired rate r: 0.80 × (1 − r)⁵ = 0.50 → (1 − r) = (0.625)^(1/5) = 0.9103 → **r ≈ 9.0% a year**. (A straight-line path would be 0.06 t per year, which is 7.5% of the base in year 1 but 11% of the remaining level by year 5.)\n\n**Q9.3 If the company can manage only 5% a year, how long will it take?**\nn = ln(0.625) ÷ ln(0.95) = **9.2 years**, and the leader will have moved on. The managerial message is that benchmarking must lead to a step change (for example renewable PPAs, low-carbon raw materials) and not only to incremental improvement.\n\n## Problem 10. Company A vs Company B: report vs performance (Lecture 10) 📘 + 📗 data ⭐⭐\nLecture 10 asks *\"Which company would be more profitable after 10 years?\"* Company A has an excellent sustainability report, beautiful graphics, ambitious targets and a strong narrative. Company B has a less attractive report, but: energy ↓ 25%, CO₂ per unit ↓ 30%, water per unit ↓ 20%, supplier violations ↓ 40%, waste-to-landfill ↓ 60%. The slide's answer: *\"The report is not the performance. Measure performance first, report performance later.\"*\n\n**Q10.1 Assume both companies started with an energy bill of ₹50 crore, water cost of ₹5 crore, waste disposal of ₹4 crore and 1,00,000 t CO₂e exposed to a carbon price of ₹2,000 per tonne (for example through CBAM on exports) 📗. Estimate Company B's annual and 10-year advantage.**\n- Energy: 25% × ₹50 cr = ₹12.5 cr.\n- Water: 20% × ₹5 cr = ₹1.0 cr.\n- Waste: 60% × ₹4 cr = ₹2.4 cr.\n- Carbon cost avoided: 30% × 1,00,000 t × ₹2,000 = ₹6.0 cr.\n- **Total ≈ ₹21.9 crore a year, about ₹219 crore over 10 years** (undiscounted, and before counting lower disruption risk from a 40% fall in supplier violations).\n\n**Q10.2 What term describes Company A's risk?**\n**Greenwashing**: claims and narrative that run ahead of measured performance. Lecture 10 also names the opposite, **greenhushing**: a company doing real work but under-reporting it to avoid scrutiny (closer to Company B). BRSR Core assurance exists to close this gap.\n\n## Problem 11. Technology Adoption Score and Net Technology Value: Navya Consumer Products (Lecture 9) 📘 + 📗 assumptions ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 9 exercise: *Navya Consumer Products Ltd., an Indian FMCG company, wants to adopt PROJECT SUSTAIN_X, an AI + IoT + blockchain platform. It operates 5 plants, 20 warehouses, 1,500 suppliers, 8,000 distributors and 50,000 retail outlets. Costs: ₹80 crore initial investment, ₹12 crore per year operating cost, plus supplier training, cybersecurity, data integration and technology dependence. Expected benefits: inventory −15%, transport cost −8%, product waste −20%, forecast accuracy +15%, traceability and supplier visibility \"major improvement\". Use five criteria (financial return, environmental benefit, social impact, supply-chain resilience, technology/adoption risk). Should it ADOPT, PILOT or REJECT?*\n\nThe case gives no base values, so assume (📗, state them in the exam): average inventory ₹400 crore with a 20% annual carrying cost; annual transport spend ₹300 crore; product waste worth ₹50 crore a year; lifecycle sustainability cost (IoT device manufacture and e-waste, data-centre and blockchain energy) ₹2 crore a year; expected risk cost (cyber incident, adoption failure among small suppliers) ₹4 crore a year; discount rate 12%; 5-year horizon.\n\n**Q11.1 Compute gross annual benefits.**\n- Inventory: 15% × ₹400 cr = ₹60 cr less inventory; carrying-cost saving = ₹60 cr × 20% = **₹12 cr a year** (plus a one-time working-capital release of ₹60 cr).\n- Transport: 8% × ₹300 cr = **₹24 cr a year**.\n- Waste: 20% × ₹50 cr = **₹10 cr a year**.\n- **Gross benefits = ₹46 crore a year** (forecast accuracy and traceability benefits are mostly captured in these lines or are strategic, so do not double count them).\n\n**Q11.2 Compute Net Technology Value (Lecture 9 formula).**\n- Annual net = Gross benefits − Operating cost − Lifecycle sustainability cost − Risk cost = 46 − 12 − 2 − 4 = **₹28 crore a year**.\n- Simple 5-year NTV = 5 × 46 − 80 − 5 × (12 + 2 + 4) = 230 − 80 − 90 = **₹60 crore**.\n- NPV at 12% = 28 × 3.605 − 80 = **₹20.9 crore** (₹80.9 crore if the ₹60 crore working-capital release is counted).\n- Payback = 80 ÷ 28 = **2.9 years**.\n\n**Q11.3 Sensitivity: what if only 60% of the benefits are realised? What is the break-even realisation?**\n- At 60%: annual net = 0.6 × 46 − 18 = ₹9.6 cr → NPV = 9.6 × 3.605 − 80 = **−₹45.4 crore**.\n- Break-even: net × 3.605 = 80 → net = ₹22.2 cr → gross needed = 22.2 + 18 = ₹40.2 cr → **87% of the promised benefits**.\nThe project is positive only if almost all the promised benefits arrive.\n\n**Q11.4 Score it on TAS (1–5).**\n\n| Dimension | Weight | Score | Reason | Weighted |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Economic | 25% | 4 | NPV positive, 2.9-year payback, but sensitive | 1.00 |\n| Environmental | 25% | 4 | Less waste, fewer truck km, less over-production; but device and data-centre footprint | 1.00 |\n| Social | 20% | 3 | Traceability helps consumers; risk of excluding small suppliers and distributors who cannot adopt (Lecture 9 trade-off #5: digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion) | 0.60 |\n| Resilience | 15% | 4 | Supplier visibility and better forecasts; new cyber and vendor-dependence risk | 0.60 |\n| Strategic fit | 15% | 4 | Fits FMCG need for traceability and BRSR data | 0.60 |\n| **TAS** | | | | **3.80 / 5** |\n\n**Q11.5 Recommendation.**\n**PILOT, not full adoption and not rejection.** TAS of 3.8 and a positive NPV put the project in the **\"Prioritise\"** quadrant on paper, but the 87% break-even realisation, the complexity of 1,500 suppliers and 8,000 distributors, and Lecture 9's warning that *\"technology is an enabler, not a sustainability strategy\"* call for proof first. Pilot in one plant, about four warehouses and the top 200–300 suppliers (perhaps ₹10–15 crore) for 9–12 months, with stage-gate KPIs: inventory days, transport cost per case, waste %, forecast MAPE, supplier onboarding rate and system energy use. Fund supplier training so smaller suppliers are not excluded. Scale up only if the pilot delivers at least 85–90% of the promised benefits. *\"Don't digitise the problem — transform it sustainably.\"*\n\n## Problem 12. The technology–sustainability decision matrix (Lecture 9) 📘 + 📗 data ⭐⭐\nLecture 9's matrix: **low business value + low sustainability impact = Avoid; low business value + high sustainability impact = Reconsider; high business value + low sustainability impact = Optimise; high business value + high sustainability impact = Prioritise.**\n\nA retailer scores six technologies (1–5, cut-off 3 = \"high\"):\n\n| Technology | Business value | Sustainability impact |\n|---|---|---|\n| AI route optimisation | 5 | 4 |\n| Blockchain traceability for all SKUs | 2 | 4 |\n| Warehouse robotics | 4 | 2 |\n| IoT cold-chain sensors | 4 | 4 |\n| Metaverse showroom | 2 | 1 |\n| Solar + battery at DCs | 3 | 5 |\n\n**Q12.1 Place each in the matrix.**\n- Prioritise: AI route optimisation, IoT cold-chain sensors, solar + battery at DCs.\n- Optimise (good business case, improve its sustainability side): warehouse robotics (check its energy use and job effects).\n- Reconsider (sustainability benefit but weak business case; redesign the scope, for example only high-risk SKUs like seafood or mangoes as in the Walmart–IBM pilot, 7 days → 2.2 seconds): blockchain for all SKUs.\n- Avoid: metaverse showroom.\n\n**Q12.2 Compute TAS for AI route optimisation with scores Economic 5, Environmental 4, Social 3, Resilience 3, Strategic fit 4.**\nTAS = 0.25 × 5 + 0.25 × 4 + 0.20 × 3 + 0.15 × 3 + 0.15 × 4 = 1.25 + 1.00 + 0.60 + 0.45 + 0.60 = **3.90**.\n\n## Problem 13. Make a KPI SMART and run the five tests (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐\nLecture 10's five tests for a KPI set: **material, balanced, actionable, comparable, credible.** Its closing line: *\"If a KPI does not change a decision, question why it exists.\"*\n\n**Q13.1 Rewrite \"Be more sustainable in logistics\" as a SMART KPI.**\n\"Reduce outbound transport emissions from 146 to 110 g CO₂e per tonne-km (−25%) across all four DCs by 31 March 2028, measured monthly from fuel cards and the TMS, owned by the Head of Logistics.\" This is Specific (outbound, g/t-km), Measurable (fuel and TMS data), Achievable (consolidation and modal shift), Relevant (transport is a major Scope 1/3 source) and Time-bound (March 2028).\n\n**Q13.2 A company tracks \"number of trees planted\" as its main climate KPI. Apply the five tests.**\nIt fails **material** (it does not measure the company's own emissions hotspots), **actionable** (it does not tell operations what to change), **comparable** (peers report tCO₂e per unit, not trees) and **credible** (survival and permanence of the trees is uncertain). Replace it with Scope 1 + 2 intensity, Scope 3 Category 1 and 4 emissions and a renewable-electricity %, and keep tree planting as a separate offset activity with verified survival rates.\n\n---\n\n## Practice set 📗\n\n**Q14. A DC shipped 3,600 orders: 3,420 on time, 3,456 in full, 3,330 on time and in full; 3,564 damage-free; 3,582 with correct documents. Compute OTIF and an approximate perfect-order rate.**\nOTIF = 3,330 ÷ 3,600 = **92.5%**. Perfect order ≈ 0.925 × (3,564 ÷ 3,600) × (3,582 ÷ 3,600) = 0.925 × 0.99 × 0.995 = **91.1%**.\n\n**Q15. A line has 450 minutes planned, 45 minutes downtime, ideal cycle time 1.2 minutes, 300 units produced, 285 good. Compute OEE.**\nAvailability = 405 ÷ 450 = 90.0%; performance = (1.2 × 300) ÷ 405 = 88.9%; quality = 285 ÷ 300 = 95.0%. **OEE = 0.90 × 0.889 × 0.95 = 76.0%.**\n\n**Q16. A firm with 1,800 employees each working 2,200 hours recorded 3 lost-time injuries. Compute LTIFR. If the target is 0.50, what is the achievement ratio?**\nHours = 39,60,000. LTIFR = 3 × 10,00,000 ÷ 39,60,000 = **0.76**. Achievement (lower is better) = 0.50 ÷ 0.76 = **0.66**, which is red.\n\n**Q17. Water intensity is 3.2 kL per tonne and the benchmark is 2.0. What constant annual reduction closes the gap in 4 years?**\n(2.0 ÷ 3.2)^(1/4) = 0.625^0.25 = 0.889 → **11.1% a year.**\n\n**Q18. A technology scores Economic 3, Environmental 5, Social 4, Resilience 2, Strategic fit 3. Compute TAS. Gross benefits are ₹18 crore a year, adoption cost ₹40 crore (5-year life, straight-line ₹8 crore a year), lifecycle sustainability cost ₹1 crore a year and risk cost ₹3 crore a year. Compute annual Net Technology Value.**\nTAS = 0.75 + 1.25 + 0.80 + 0.30 + 0.45 = **3.55**. Annual NTV = 18 − 8 − 1 − 3 = **₹6 crore a year** (₹30 crore over 5 years), so it is worth adopting, with a resilience plan because resilience scored only 2.\n\n**Q19. A plant's defect rate falls from 3% to 1.5% on 8,00,000 units. Each defect costs ₹400 and 6 kg CO₂e. Compute the savings.**\nDefects avoided = (0.03 − 0.015) × 8,00,000 = 12,000. Savings = 12,000 × ₹400 = **₹48 lakh** and 12,000 × 6 = **72 t CO₂e a year**.\n\n**Q20. Revenue grew from ₹1,800 crore to ₹2,250 crore while Scope 1 + 2 emissions rose from 12,600 t to 13,500 t. Did the company improve?**\nAbsolute emissions rose 7.1%, but intensity fell from 7.0 to 6.0 t per ₹ crore, a **14.3% improvement**. The company decoupled growth from emissions *relatively*, not absolutely. A net-zero or SBTi target requires absolute reductions, so the answer is \"improved efficiency, not yet on a net-zero path\".\n"}, {"slug": "p5-lca-energy-numericals", "label": "LCA & Energy Numericals", "tag": "P5", "group": "focus", "title": "LCA, Life-Cycle Costing & Energy-Sector Numericals", "md": "# LCA, Life-Cycle Costing & Energy-Sector Numericals\nThis page supports **CO3** (carbon footprints sit inside LCA) and the syllabus topics **\"Introduction to environmental LCA and carbon footprints\"** and **\"Supply chain in the energy sector\"**. It uses the **Lecture 4** functional-unit examples, the **Lecture 5** LCSA examples (laptop E-LCA, petrol vs electric car, LED vs incandescent LCC, packaging LCSA), the two **IISc papers** on Tank-to-Wheel and Well-to-Wheel efficiency of Indian automotive fuels, the **Rietmann et al. (2020)** EV-forecast paper and the **Tesla battery supply chain** case from the course zip 📘. Practice data are marked 📗. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers.\n\nTheory: [LCA & Carbon Footprints](ch02-lca-carbon-footprints.md) and [Supply Chain in the Energy Sector](ch09-energy-sector.md).\n\n---\n\n## Formulas and conventions ⭐⭐⭐\n- **LCA (ISO 14040/14044)** has four phases: goal and scope (including the **functional unit** and system boundary), life-cycle inventory (LCI), life-cycle impact assessment (LCIA) and interpretation.\n- **Impact = Σ (inventory flow × characterisation factor).** For climate change the factor is the GWP: CO₂ = 1, CH₄ ≈ 28–30, N₂O ≈ 265–273 (AR5/AR6, 100-year).\n- **Always compare on the same functional unit.** Impact per functional unit = impact per product ÷ number of functional units the product delivers.\n- **Life-cycle stages:** raw materials → manufacturing → distribution → use → end-of-life. Boundaries: cradle-to-gate, gate-to-gate, cradle-to-grave, cradle-to-cradle.\n- **LCSA = E-LCA + S-LCA + LCC** (Klöpffer 2008, Lecture 5).\n- **Life-cycle cost (LCC / TCO)** = purchase + operating (energy) + maintenance + replacement + end-of-life cost − residual value.\n- **Use-phase electricity emissions** = power (kW) × hours × grid emission factor. Indian grid factor: about 0.71–0.79 kg CO₂ per kWh in the course sources (CEA ~0.71–0.72; Rietmann uses 0.791).\n- **Well-to-Wheel efficiency = Well-to-Tank efficiency × Tank-to-Wheel efficiency.** WTT covers recovery, refining or generation, and transport/T&D; TTW covers the vehicle.\n- **Energy at source per km** = energy needed at the wheels per km ÷ WTW efficiency.\n- **Logistic growth (Rietmann):** I(t) = L ÷ [1 + (L ÷ I(0) − 1) · e^(−k·L·t)], where L is the saturation level.\n- **Renewable generation** = capacity (MW) × capacity utilisation factor × 8,760 hours; avoided CO₂ = MWh × grid factor.\n\n---\n\n## Problem 1. The functional unit — why \"per product\" comparisons mislead (Lecture 4) 📘 concept + 📗 data ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 4 calls the functional unit *\"the most misunderstood\"* part of LCA and gives examples such as **delivering 1 litre of …**, **carrying 20 kg of groceries** and **storing 159 litres of petrol**. The functional unit describes the *function delivered*, not the product.\n\nA retailer compares carry bags. A plastic bag weighs 10 g, carries 10 kg and is used once; its footprint is 2.5 kg CO₂e per kg of plastic. A cotton bag weighs 150 g, carries 20 kg and lasts 150 trips; its footprint is 20 kg CO₂e per kg of cotton.\n\n**Q1.1 Which bag looks greener \"per bag\"?**\nPlastic: 0.010 × 2.5 = **0.025 kg CO₂e per bag**. Cotton: 0.150 × 20 = **3.0 kg CO₂e per bag**. Per bag, cotton is 120 times worse.\n\n**Q1.2 Define a functional unit and compare again.**\nFunctional unit: *carrying 20 kg of groceries home once a week (or 500 trips) over its service life.*\n- Plastic: 2 bags per trip × 500 trips = 1,000 bags × 0.025 = **25 kg CO₂e**.\n- Cotton: 500 ÷ 150 = 3.33 bags × 3.0 = **10 kg CO₂e** (12 kg if you buy 4 whole bags).\n- On the functional unit, **cotton is about 60% lower**.\n\n**Q1.3 How many uses must the cotton bag achieve to break even with plastic?**\nPlastic per trip = 2 × 0.025 = 0.05 kg. Break-even = 3.0 ÷ 0.05 = **60 uses**. If shoppers lose or discard the cotton bag before 60 trips, plastic wins on carbon (though not on litter or marine pollution, which a full LCIA would capture in other impact categories). This is why LCA conclusions depend on the functional unit, the use pattern and the impact categories chosen.\n\n## Problem 2. Laptop E-LCA: where are the hotspots? (Lecture 5) 📘 + 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 5: *\"Manufacturing 1 laptop = 320 kg CO₂e + 18,000 L water + 850 MJ energy.\"* Add (📗): the laptop draws 50 W, is used 8 hours a day for 250 days a year over 4 years; grid factor 0.71 kg/kWh; distribution and end-of-life add 20 kg CO₂e.\n\n**Q2.1 Compute the cradle-to-grave carbon footprint and the share of each stage.**\n- Use-phase electricity = 0.050 kW × 8 × 250 × 4 = **400 kWh** → 400 × 0.71 = **284 kg CO₂e**.\n- Total = 320 + 284 + 20 = **624 kg CO₂e**.\n- Shares: manufacturing **51%**, use **46%**, distribution + EoL **3%**.\n\n**Q2.2 The company extends life to 6 years through repair and a battery swap. What happens to the footprint per year of service?**\n- 6-year total = 320 + (0.050 × 8 × 250 × 6 × 0.71 = 426) + 20 = **766 kg**.\n- Per year: 4-year life = 624 ÷ 4 = **156 kg/yr**; 6-year life = 766 ÷ 6 = **127.7 kg/yr** → **18% lower** per year of service.\nThe manufacturing \"embodied\" carbon is spread over more years. This is the logic of the R-strategies (repair, refurbish) in Lecture 8 and of product-as-a-service models.\n\n**Q2.3 What would a carbon footprint study miss that a full LCA catches?**\nA carbon footprint reports only GWP (kg CO₂e). A full LCA would also report the 18,000 L of water (water scarcity), the 850 MJ of energy (resource depletion), toxicity from electronics manufacturing and e-waste, and metal depletion (cobalt, gold, rare earths). Lecture 4: *a carbon footprint is a single-issue subset of LCA.*\n\n## Problem 3. LED vs incandescent bulb — life-cycle costing (Lecture 5) 📘 + 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 5's LCC table: LED purchase ₹250, lifetime 25,000 hours, electricity very low, maintenance low; incandescent purchase ₹40, lifetime 1,000 hours, electricity high, maintenance high. Add (📗): LED 9 W, incandescent 60 W, electricity ₹8 per kWh, grid factor 0.70 kg CO₂ per kWh. Functional unit: **25,000 hours of light at equal brightness**.\n\n**Q3.1 Compute the life-cycle cost and CO₂ of each option.**\n\n| | Incandescent | LED |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bulbs needed | 25,000 ÷ 1,000 = 25 | 1 |\n| Purchase cost | 25 × ₹40 = ₹1,000 | ₹250 |\n| Energy | 0.060 × 25,000 = 1,500 kWh | 0.009 × 25,000 = 225 kWh |\n| Energy cost | 1,500 × ₹8 = ₹12,000 | 225 × ₹8 = ₹1,800 |\n| **Life-cycle cost** | **₹13,000** | **₹2,050** |\n| **Use-phase CO₂** | 1,500 × 0.70 = **1,050 kg** | 225 × 0.70 = **157.5 kg** |\n\nThe LED costs **84% less** over its life and emits **85% less CO₂**, even though its purchase price is 6.25 times higher.\n\n**Q3.2 How many hours until the LED pays back its higher purchase price?**\nExtra purchase cost = ₹250 − ₹40 = ₹210. Hourly saving = (60 − 9) W × ₹8 ÷ 1,000 = ₹0.408 per hour. Payback = 210 ÷ 0.408 ≈ **515 hours** (about two months at 8 hours a day), and that ignores the 24 replacement bulbs the incandescent option needs.\n\n**Q3.3 What is the general managerial lesson?**\nPurchase price is a poor decision criterion when most of the cost and impact sits in the use phase. This is the same TCO logic as sustainable procurement (Lecture 6): *cost vs value*.\n\n## Problem 4. Petrol car vs electric car in India (Lecture 5 + Rietmann 2020) 📘 + 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 5's qualitative E-LCA: raw materials — petrol medium, EV high (battery metals); manufacturing — petrol medium, EV high; use phase — petrol very high emissions, EV low; maintenance — petrol medium, EV low; end-of-life — petrol recycling possible, EV battery recycling critical. *\"If only manufacturing is studied, the petrol car appears greener. If the entire life cycle is studied, the electric car generally has much lower lifetime emissions.\"*\n\nRietmann et al. (2020) give India-specific data: ICE car **0.135 kg CO₂ per km**; EV consumption **0.185 kWh per km**; Indian grid **0.791 kg CO₂ per kWh**; average driving **20,800 km per year**. Add (📗): manufacturing footprint ICE 6 t, EV 10 t (the battery adds about 4 t); life 10 years.\n\n**Q4.1 Compare the use-phase emissions per km.**\n- EV = 0.185 × 0.791 = **0.146 kg CO₂ per km**. ICE = **0.135 kg per km**.\n- On India's coal-heavy grid the EV is about **8% worse** per km in the use phase. This is why Rietmann finds India's transport CO₂ still rising (+129.5% between 2018 and 2035) even with EV adoption.\n\n**Q4.2 Compute 10-year lifecycle emissions.**\n- Distance = 20,800 × 10 = 2,08,000 km.\n- ICE = 6 + 0.135 × 2,08,000 ÷ 1,000 = 6 + 28.1 = **34.1 t CO₂**.\n- EV = 10 + 0.146 × 2,08,000 ÷ 1,000 = 10 + 30.4 = **40.4 t CO₂**.\n\n**Q4.3 At what grid factor does the EV break even (a) per km and (b) over the life cycle?**\n- (a) Per km: 0.135 ÷ 0.185 = **0.730 kg/kWh**.\n- (b) Lifecycle: 10 + 0.185 × g × 208 = 34.08 → g = 24.08 ÷ 38.48 = **0.626 kg/kWh**.\n- If the grid reaches 0.45 kg/kWh (more renewables), EV lifecycle = 10 + 0.185 × 0.45 × 208 = **27.3 t**, about **20% below ICE**.\n\n**Q4.4 What does this tell a supply-chain manager electrifying a fleet?**\nElectrification only cuts emissions when paired with **clean electricity** (on-site solar, green PPAs, green tariffs). Without it, emissions shift from Scope 1 (diesel) to Scope 2 (grid power) and may even rise. It also shows why LCA boundaries matter: a tailpipe-only (TTW) view would wrongly score the EV as zero-emission.\n\n## Problem 5. Well-to-Wheel efficiency of Indian fuels (IISc papers) 📘 + 📗 ⭐⭐\nKey results from the two IISc papers: liquid petroleum fuels have **WTT efficiencies of 75–85%** (LPG best at 85%); **electricity WTT is only about 20%** because 69.9% of power comes from coal, a typical Indian coal plant runs at about 22% efficiency and **T&D losses are 25.4%**; hydrogen from natural-gas reforming has WTT of about 48% (reforming at 70%); TTW efficiencies are 15–27% for gasoline/LPG and 25–32% for diesel; hybridisation raises WTW efficiency by an average of **41%** and cuts WTW energy by **29%**; the **diesel split hybrid** is the most efficient configuration and a BEV on the Indian grid is roughly equal to a gasoline car on WTW.\n\n**Q5.1 Compute WTW efficiency for these vehicles (📗 TTW values within the paper's ranges): gasoline car (WTT 80%, TTW 20%); diesel car (80%, 28%); BEV (WTT 20%, TTW 80%); hydrogen fuel-cell car (48%, 30%); diesel split hybrid (80%, 28% × 1.41).**\n\n| Vehicle | WTT | TTW | WTW = WTT × TTW | Source energy per km if 0.5 MJ/km is needed at the wheels |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Gasoline | 0.80 | 0.20 | **16.0%** | 0.5 ÷ 0.160 = 3.13 MJ |\n| Diesel | 0.80 | 0.28 | **22.4%** | 2.23 MJ |\n| BEV (Indian grid) | 0.20 | 0.80 | **16.0%** | 3.13 MJ |\n| Hydrogen FCEV | 0.48 | 0.30 | **14.4%** | 3.47 MJ |\n| Diesel split hybrid | 0.80 | 0.395 | **31.6%** | 1.58 MJ |\n\nThe ranking matches the paper: diesel hybrid best, BEV ≈ gasoline, hydrogen lowest.\n\n**Q5.2 Show how the electricity WTT of about 20% arises.**\nWTT is a chain of stage efficiencies: coal recovery and transport (~0.95) × weighted generation efficiency across the mix (~0.29, 📗) × (1 − 0.254 T&D losses = 0.746) = 0.95 × 0.29 × 0.746 ≈ **0.206 ≈ 20%**. Cutting T&D losses to 10% alone would raise WTT to 0.95 × 0.29 × 0.90 = 24.8%, a 20% relative gain. *Supply-chain lesson: in the energy sector the losses are upstream (generation and distribution), not at the point of use.*\n\n## Problem 6. Forecasting EV adoption with the logistic curve (Rietmann 2020) 📘 + 📗 ⭐⭐\nRietmann et al. model EV adoption with the logistic function I(t) = L ÷ [1 + (L ÷ I(0) − 1) · e^(−k·L·t)]. Their findings: EVs reach a 30% fleet share globally by about 2032 and 42.5% of the fleet by 2035, yet global passenger-car CO₂ still rises until about 2035; EV consumption 0.185 kWh/km; 17,000 km per year; ICE 2.45 kg CO₂ per litre.\n\nAssume (📗) a saturation share L = 100%, today's share I(0) = 2.5% and k·L = 0.35 per year.\n\n**Q6.1 Forecast the EV share after 5, 10 and 15 years.**\nI(t) = 1 ÷ [1 + 39 · e^(−0.35t)].\n- t = 5: e^(−1.75) = 0.174 → 1 ÷ (1 + 6.78) = **12.9%**.\n- t = 10: e^(−3.5) = 0.0302 → 1 ÷ (1 + 1.18) = **45.9%**.\n- t = 15: e^(−5.25) = 0.00525 → 1 ÷ (1 + 0.205) = **83.0%**.\nThe S-curve is slow at first, fastest around 50% and then saturates.\n\n**Q6.2 When does the share reach 30%?**\n0.30 = 1 ÷ (1 + 39e^(−0.35t)) → 39e^(−0.35t) = 2.333 → e^(−0.35t) = 0.0598 → t = ln(16.71) ÷ 0.35 = **8.0 years**.\n\n**Q6.3 Reproduce Rietmann's charging-energy figure: 439 million EVs, 17,000 km a year, 0.185 kWh per km.**\nEnergy = 439 × 10⁶ × 17,000 × 0.185 = 1.38 × 10¹⁵ Wh = **1,381 TWh per year**. That is new electricity demand of the size of a large country's whole grid, so power-sector supply chains (generation, transmission, charging infrastructure) must expand with the vehicle fleet.\n\n**Q6.4 For India, compare annual CO₂ per car: EV at 0.791 kg/kWh vs ICE at 0.135 kg/km, 20,800 km per year.**\nEV = 20,800 × 0.185 × 0.791 = **3.04 t per year**; ICE = 20,800 × 0.135 = **2.81 t per year**. Same conclusion as Problem 4: India must decarbonise the grid for EVs to help.\n\n## Problem 7. Tesla battery end-of-life economics (HBS case) 📘 + 📗 ⭐⭐⭐\nFrom the case: by 2025 up to **10,000** Tesla packs could need replacement, rising to **50,000 a year by 2030**; globally **1.2 million tonnes** of EV batteries will need disposal annually by 2030; only about **5%** of Li-ion batteries are recycled (95% reach municipal waste); second-life adaptation costs **$3,500 per battery for direct reuse, $6,700 when dismantled to module level, $8,000 at cell level**; recycling returns stay at about **$5,600 per battery after 2025**; recycling can recover about half of critical materials and cut up to **70% of GHG emissions**; replacing 5–30% of cells can restore a pack to 95% state of health.\n\n**Q7.1 What is the annual recycling value of Tesla's replacement packs in 2025 and 2030?**\n2025: 10,000 × $5,600 = **$56 million**. 2030: 50,000 × $5,600 = **$280 million a year**.\n\n**Q7.2 A used pack has 60 kWh nominal capacity and 70% state of health. Stationary-storage buyers pay $100 per usable kWh (📗). Compare direct reuse, module-level reuse and recycling.**\n- Second-life value = 60 × 0.70 × $100 = **$4,200**.\n- Direct reuse net = 4,200 − 3,500 = **+$700**, and the pack can still be recycled later (about $5,600 then), so the total value chain is about $6,300.\n- Module-level reuse net = 4,200 − 6,700 = **−$2,500** (only worth it if module grading lifts the value above $6,700).\n- Cell-level reuse net = 4,200 − 8,000 = **−$3,800**.\n- Immediate recycling = **+$5,600**.\n**Answer:** on these numbers immediate recycling ($5,600) beats direct reuse alone ($700) on cash, but **reuse first, recycle later** (≈ $6,300) maximises value and follows the waste hierarchy (EPA and EU directive: reduce → reuse → recycle → recover → dispose). Deeper dismantling only pays if second-life prices rise.\n\n**Q7.3 Which strategy (build, buy or partner) would you recommend for Tesla's recycling, and why?**\n**Partner** in the short term with specialists such as **Redwood Materials** (founded by Tesla's ex-CTO J.B. Straubel; about 95% recovery; valued at $3.78 billion) or **Li-Cycle** (5,000 t a year in Kingston, Ontario; >95% recovery; raised $1.6 billion). Volumes (10,000 packs in 2025) are too small for an efficient in-house plant, and recycling cost is highly sensitive to volume. Build in-house capability (for example at the Gigafactories) once volumes approach 50,000 packs a year, which also secures cobalt, nickel and lithium and reduces exposure to the DRC (≈65% of cobalt) and to ESG-rating criticism (FTSE scored Tesla zero on environment).\n\n## Problem 8. Energy-sector numericals: renewable PPA and avoided emissions 📗 ⭐⭐\nA cement plant signs a PPA for a 10 MW solar farm with a 19% capacity utilisation factor. Grid factor 0.71 kg CO₂ per kWh.\n\n**Q8.1 How much electricity does the solar farm generate and how much Scope 2 CO₂ does it avoid?**\nGeneration = 10 MW × 0.19 × 8,760 h = **16,644 MWh a year**. Avoided CO₂ = 16,644 × 0.71 = **11,817 t CO₂ a year** (market-based Scope 2).\n\n**Q8.2 If the plant uses 60,000 MWh a year, what share is renewable, and what remains?**\nRE share = 16,644 ÷ 60,000 = **27.7%**. Remaining grid Scope 2 = (60,000 − 16,644) × 0.71 = **30,783 t CO₂**. To reach Asian Paints' level (about 60% RE, Lecture 7) the plant would need about 36,000 MWh of RE, roughly **22 MW** of solar at the same CUF.\n\n**Q8.3 Map the energy supply chain stages the syllabus asks about, with one sustainability KPI each.**\n- **Supplies** (fuel extraction, critical minerals, equipment): methane-leak rate, % of minerals from audited sources.\n- **Production / conversion** (power plants, refineries): generation efficiency (coal plant ≈ 22% in the IISc paper), g CO₂ per kWh.\n- **Transmission and distribution**: T&D loss % (25.4% in the IISc paper).\n- **End use**: energy intensity per unit of output, % renewable electricity.\n- **Disposal**: battery and solar-panel recycling rate, decommissioning liabilities.\n\n---\n\n## Practice set 📗\n\n**Q9. A 1-litre PET bottle has a footprint of 0.08 kg CO₂e and is used once. A 1-litre steel bottle has 2.4 kg CO₂e and is used 500 times (washing adds 0.002 kg per use). Compare per litre delivered over 500 uses.**\nPET = 500 × 0.08 = **40 kg**. Steel = 2.4 + 500 × 0.002 = **3.4 kg**. Break-even uses: 2.4 ÷ (0.08 − 0.002) = **31 uses**.\n\n**Q10. A 1.5-tonne AC uses 1.5 kW for 1,500 hours a year; a 5-star model uses 1.0 kW and costs ₹12,000 more. Electricity ₹8 per kWh, grid 0.71 kg/kWh. Compute payback and annual CO₂ saving.**\nEnergy saving = 0.5 × 1,500 = 750 kWh → ₹6,000 a year → **payback 2 years**; CO₂ saving = 750 × 0.71 = **532.5 kg a year**.\n\n**Q11. A cradle-to-gate study of a T-shirt finds: cotton growing 2.1 kg CO₂e, spinning and weaving 1.8, dyeing 0.9, cutting and sewing 0.3. Use phase (50 washes at 0.04 kg per wash) and end of life (0.1) are excluded. What is the cradle-to-gate footprint, what is cradle-to-grave, and what is the biggest hotspot?**\nCradle-to-gate = 2.1 + 1.8 + 0.9 + 0.3 = **5.1 kg**. Cradle-to-grave = 5.1 + 50 × 0.04 + 0.1 = **7.2 kg**. The biggest single hotspot is **cotton growing (29% of cradle-to-grave)**; the use phase (2.0 kg, 28%) is almost as large, so a cradle-to-gate boundary misses a major lever (cold-water washing).\n\n**Q12. Characterise these emissions into CO₂e (GWP: CH₄ 28, N₂O 265): 1,000 kg CO₂, 20 kg CH₄, 2 kg N₂O.**\n1,000 + 20 × 28 + 2 × 265 = 1,000 + 560 + 530 = **2,090 kg CO₂e**.\n\n**Q13. Using WTT 85% for LPG and TTW 24%, compute WTW. If a hybrid improves WTW by 41%, what is the new WTW and the energy saving?**\nWTW = 0.85 × 0.24 = **20.4%**. Hybrid = 20.4 × 1.41 = **28.8%**. Energy at source falls by 1 − 20.4 ÷ 28.8 = **29%**, matching the IISc average.\n"}, {"slug": "ch01-sscm-business-performance", "label": "SSCM & Business Performance", "tag": "01", "group": "chapters", "title": "Defining SSCM & Its Impact on Business Performance", "md": "# Defining SSCM & Its Impact on Business Performance\nSyllabus topic 1 📘 (Lectures 1, 2 and 3). This is mainly **CO1**, which carries **low weightage** and appears as **3-mark short theory questions** in the end-sem paper. Expect \"define\", \"differentiate\", \"list with examples\" and \"why does it matter\" questions. Every answer below is written so that you can reproduce it in 5–8 lines. The strategy and stakeholder parts also feed CO6 (strategy) and the scorecard parts feed CO5 ([Performance Measurement Numericals](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md)).\n\n---\n\n## 1. From SCM to SSCM ⭐⭐⭐\n**Supply Chain Management (SCM)** 📘 is the *co-ordination of material, information and money flows from source to customer*: supplier (point of origin) → manufacturer → warehouse → distributor → retailer → customer (point of consumption). The course's one-liner: *\"SCM is why your Maggi, iPhone and Amazon order somehow reach you before your relatives do.\"*\n\n**Sustainability** 📘 (Brundtland Commission, 1987) is *meeting present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.*\n\n**Sustainable Supply Chain Management (SSCM)** is the management of material, information and capital flows, and of co-operation among companies along the supply chain, while taking goals from **all three dimensions of sustainable development (economic, environmental and social)** into account, derived from customer and stakeholder requirements (Seuring & Müller, 2008, the standard academic definition). The course frames it as **\"How to save the planet without killing the P&L.\"**\n\n### Old thinking vs new reality (Lecture 1) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Old thinking | New reality |\n|---|---|\n| Cheapest supplier wins | **Ethical supplier wins** |\n| Faster delivery | **Lower emissions** |\n| Maximum production | **Circular production** |\n| Waste is unavoidable | **Waste = design failure** |\n\nThe drivers of the change named on the slide: groundwater depletion, fast fashion, food waste, EV battery waste and coal-burning waste. *\"This is exactly why supply chains must change with the demanding times.\"*\n\n### Linear vs circular; cradle-to-grave vs cradle-to-cradle ⭐⭐⭐\n- **SCM is \"cradle to grave\"** 📘: take → make → use → dispose. Responsibility ends when the product is sold or discarded.\n- **SSCM is \"cradle to cradle\"** 📘: products and materials are designed to return to the cycle (reuse, remanufacture, recycle), so end-of-life becomes the start of a new life.\n- The course shows SCM moving **from \"Linear\" to \"Circular\"** by adding reverse logistics, recycling, returns and circular-economy loops to the forward chain.\n\n### Traditional SCM vs SSCM (Lecture 11 table) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Dimension | Traditional SCM | Sustainable SCM |\n|---|---|---|\n| Objective | Minimise cost, maximise efficiency | Balance economic, environmental and social value |\n| Time horizon | Short-term | Long-term |\n| Performance | Cost, speed, quality | Plus carbon, water, waste, human rights, resilience |\n| Supplier relations | Transactional, price-based | Collaborative, development-based |\n| Product flow | Linear | Circular (closed loop) |\n| Risk view | Operational disruptions | Climate, regulatory, social, reputational risks too |\n| Stakeholders | Shareholders and customers | All stakeholders, including communities and regulators |\n\n---\n\n## 2. The three pillars (Triple Bottom Line) ⭐⭐⭐\nThe **Triple Bottom Line** (John Elkington, 1994) says a firm should be measured on **People, Planet and Profit**, not only on profit. Lecture 1 gives the pillar content and Indian examples:\n\n| Pillar | What it covers (slide) | Indian examples (slide) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Environmental** (Planet) | Energy savings, water usage, packaging waste, fuel consumption | El Niño, Delhi AQI, Bengaluru floods |\n| **Social** (People) | Labour welfare, supplier ethics, safety, diversity | Workers, delivery boys, gig economy |\n| **Economic** (Profit) | Carbon emissions (cost), lower waste cost, ESG investments, brand value | Fuel imports, crop damages, water leaks |\n\n*\"Switch to sustainable SCM: nature is basically sending in compliance notices.\"* 📘\n\nThe SDGs: the slide's sequence **Denial → Panic → PowerPoint → Real action** describes how firms respond to sustainability, and states that *SSCM is central to achieving the SDG goals* (especially SDG 12 Responsible consumption and production, SDG 13 Climate action, SDG 8 Decent work, SDG 7 Clean energy, SDG 6 Clean water).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The sustainable supply chain framework (Lecture 1) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nEach stage of the chain gets \"+\" sustainable practices:\n\n| Stage | Sustainable additions |\n|---|---|\n| Procurement | Ethical sourcing, local sourcing, supplier audits |\n| Manufacture | Renewable energy, lean manufacturing, waste minimisation |\n| Logistics | EV fleets, route optimisation, multimodal transport |\n| Warehousing | Natural-lighting design, smart energy systems, rooftop solar |\n| Reverse logistics | Recycling, returns, circular economy |\n\n**Memory hook:** \"Procure ethically, Make lean & clean, Move smart, Store green, Bring it back.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. Four Indian case types from Lecture 1–2 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nThe course groups cases by the *type* of sustainability lever:\n\n| Case | Type | What it shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ITC e-Choupal (2000)** | Delivery-based | Direct farmer connectivity, fewer middlemen, price transparency, lower wastage, rural digital empowerment. *\"Before apps disrupted SCM, ITC disrupted mandis.\"* |\n| **HUL sustainable sourcing (2010)** | Delivery-based | Supporting small farmers, regenerative agriculture, resilient sourcing, lower wastage, climate-smart farming. |\n| **EV battery circular supply chain** (Tata Autocomp, Attero, Lohum) | Recovery-based | Lithium shortage → battery recycling, reverse logistics, circular economy; India's EV ecosystem is developing under the **EPR** framework (Battery Waste Management Rules). |\n| **Hindustan Zinc with GreenLine (2025)** | Reduction-based | LNG and EV trucks to decarbonise logistics (a ₹400 crore deal); trade-off between cost, emissions, infrastructure and scalability. |\n| **Mahindra Group carbon neutrality goal (2022)** | Reduction-based | Renewable energy, EV ecosystem, green logistics, circular economy (EPR), digitalisation. Leadership in sustainable mobility. |\n| **Flipkart e-commerce logistics (2022)** | Reduction-based | Recyclable packaging, last-mile EVs, route optimisation. |\n\n**Key takeaway** 📘: *\"Earlier supply chains competed on speed and cost but tomorrow they will compete on sustainability and resilience.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 5. SSCM strategy ⭐⭐⭐\n### Why strategy must change 📘\n*\"What happens if your supply chain is profitable today but becomes illegal, expensive or unacceptable tomorrow owing to carbon taxes, CBAM regulations, ESG reporting and customer sustainability audits?\"* Supply-chain strategies need to evolve with changing times.\n\n### Definition 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nA **sustainable supply chain strategy** is *a long-term plan that integrates economic, environmental and social objectives (the three pillars) while maintaining competitiveness and profitability.*\n\n### What it adds to traditional priorities 📘\nTraditional SC priorities are **cost, quality, speed and innovation**. Sustainability adds **carbon emissions, waste and labour practices**. Because *\"in most cases environmental impacts occur across the complete supply chain rather than within the company's own facilities\"*, strategy must cover all three emission scopes:\n- **Scope 1 — \"what you burn\"**: fuels and gas (direct).\n- **Scope 2 — \"what you buy\"**: electricity and heat.\n- **Scope 3 — \"what you influence\"**: all other upstream and downstream emissions.\n\n### The SSCM strategy hierarchy and the triple \"P\" 📘 ⭐⭐\nStrategy cascades **corporate strategy → business strategy → supply-chain strategy → operations and logistics strategy**, and at each level it must serve **People, Planet and Profit**. *\"Supply chain strategy must support overall business strategy.\"*\n\n### Why SSCM strategy is important 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Regulatory pressure**: BRSR (SEBI), EU CBAM, carbon markets (India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme), EPR rules, EU CSDDD due-diligence law.\n2. **Investor pressure**: ESG ratings drive cost of capital; ESG assets reached about $40.5 trillion in 2020 (Tesla case).\n3. **Customer pressure**: B2B customers audit suppliers' sustainability; consumers prefer responsible brands.\n4. **Operational benefits**: energy, waste and logistics savings; resilience.\n\n### Ingredients of SSCM strategy 📘 ⭐⭐\nBusiness **vision**, **stakeholder expectations**, **resources**, **capabilities**, **supply-chain design** and **sustainability objectives**. *\"Supply chain strategy must aid alignment and add value for business.\"*\n\n### Risks of ignoring sustainability 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Export barriers** (CBAM, EU deforestation regulation), **carbon taxes**, **supply disruptions** (climate events, water scarcity), **reputation damage**, **investor pressure** and, ultimately, **customer loss**.\n\n### Stakeholder mapping 📘 ⭐⭐\n*\"Successful supply chains don't just deliver products — they deliver value to stakeholders.\"*\n- **Internal**: employees (safe, fair work), management (performance), shareholders (returns with lower risk).\n- **External**: customers (sustainable, affordable products), suppliers (fair terms, long-term partnership), government and regulators (compliance, disclosure), communities (jobs, clean air and water), NGOs and media (transparency, human rights), investors and lenders (ESG performance).\nA common tool is the **power–interest grid**: manage closely (high power, high interest: regulators, key customers, investors), keep satisfied (high power, low interest), keep informed (low power, high interest: communities, NGOs), monitor (low, low).\n\n### The five-step strategy development process 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Step | What you do |\n|---|---|\n| 1. **Assess current state** | Carbon footprint, logistics network, supplier performance, waste generation, energy use |\n| 2. **Analyse stakeholders** | Who influences, who is impacted, what they expect |\n| 3. **Develop future scenarios** | Carbon pricing, supply disruptions, energy-cost increases, water scarcity |\n| 4. **Design strategy** | Green sourcing, renewable energy, circular economy, supplier development, network redesign |\n| 5. **Operationalise strategy** | Projects, KPIs, budgets, responsibilities |\n\n*\"Understand stakeholders → anticipate change → design the response → execute for impact.\"*\n\n### Sustainability Balanced Scorecard (Lecture 2) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nKaplan & Norton's Balanced Scorecard with sustainability KPIs in each perspective:\n\n| Financial | Customer | Internal process | Learning & growth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Energy savings | Sustainable product sales | Carbon emissions | Employee training |\n| Logistics cost reduction | Customer satisfaction | Water usage | Sustainability culture |\n| Waste-reduction savings | ESG ratings | Transport efficiency | Innovation projects |\n| ROI of sustainability projects | Supplier compliance | Supply-chain management | |\n\n*\"Balanced scorecards convert sustainability into action.\"*\n\n### Carbon management integration and future supply chains 📘\nCarbon accounting must cover **all stages** of the chain (sourcing, production, logistics, use, end-of-life). Future sustainable supply chains are **digital, transparent, circular, collaborative, low-carbon and resilient**: *\"Future-ready supply chains don't just survive disruption — they turn it into competitive advantage.\"*\n\n**Lecture 2 key takeaways** 📘: sustainability is a strategic issue, not a compliance issue; SC strategy must align with business strategy; stakeholder expectations shape future competitiveness; balanced scorecards convert sustainability into action; companies that adapt early gain long-term advantage.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Impact of SSCM on business performance ⭐⭐⭐\nThis is the syllabus's \"impact on business performance\" part. Structure your answer by the TBL, or by the levers below.\n\n### Lecture 3: \"Which company performs better and is sustainable?\" 📘\nCompany 1: higher sales, higher profits, higher pay scales, **higher debt finance**. Company 2: strong profits, **safe workplaces, lower emissions, ethical suppliers**. The second is better positioned because its performance is durable: it is less exposed to carbon costs, accidents, supplier scandals and financing risk. *\"Performance measurement is a core enabler, not an optional one.\"* and *\"If you cannot measure it, you cannot improve it.\"*\n\n### Traditional vs sustainable performance 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Traditional | Sustainable |\n|---|---|\n| Profit, revenue, cost | Profit **plus** environmental and social impact |\n| Short-term, financial | Long-term value creation |\n| Shareholders | All stakeholders |\n| Lagging financial KPIs | Leading and lagging KPIs across TBL |\n| Firm boundary | Whole value chain (Scope 3, supplier ESG) |\n\n### How SSCM improves business performance (with evidence) ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Cost reduction**: energy efficiency, less waste, route optimisation, packaging reduction. Example: Lecture 10's Company B cut energy 25%, CO₂/unit 30%, water/unit 20%, waste-to-landfill 60% → ~₹22 crore/year on illustrative numbers ([CO5 Problem 10](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md)).\n2. **Revenue growth and market access**: green products, eligibility for EU markets under CBAM, preferred-supplier status with customers that have Scope 3 targets (Walmart Project Gigaton, Apple).\n3. **Risk reduction and resilience**: diversified, audited suppliers; climate-adaptive sourcing (HUL climate-smart farming); fewer disruptions.\n4. **Lower cost of capital**: better ESG ratings, access to green bonds and sustainability-linked loans.\n5. **Brand and reputation**: customer loyalty, licence to operate.\n6. **Innovation**: new business models (product-as-a-service, battery-as-a-service, remanufacturing).\n7. **Talent**: employees prefer purpose-driven employers; safer workplaces raise productivity.\n8. **Compliance**: avoiding fines, export barriers and carbon taxes.\n\n### Performance management framework (Lecture 3) 📘 ⭐⭐\n**Inputs** (resources, investments) → **processes** (sustainable sourcing, production, logistics) → **outputs** (products, emissions, waste) → **outcomes** (impact on customers, communities, environment) → **business value** (profitability, growth, reputation). *\"The framework ensures overall sustainable performance.\"*\n\n### Performance dimensions Q + D + I + R + S (Lecture 3) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"High-performing supply chains integrate Q + D + I + R + S\"* across procurement → manufacturing → warehousing → transportation → distribution → customer:\n\n| Quality | Delivery | Innovation | Resilience | Sustainability |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Defect rate (PPM, %) | On-time delivery % | Number of new products | Supply disruptions | Carbon emissions |\n| Complaints / returns | Order-fill cycle time | R&D spend | Recovery time | Energy consumed |\n| Warranty claims | Lead time | Digital technology | Supplier diversity | Water consumed |\n| Six Sigma level | Delivery reliability | Patents | Inventory risk | Worker safety |\n| Supplier performance | Transport efficiency | Automation level | Business continuity | Ethical sourcing % |\n\n### SMART KPIs (Lecture 3) 📘\nSpecific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound. Slide examples: **economic** — cost per shipment, inventory days, % profitability; **environmental** — CO₂ per tonne-km, % material recycled, water intensity; **social** — accident frequency, % diversity, supplier audit score. *\"Measure smarter. Act faster. Perform better.\"*\n\n### Why measurement matters (Lecture 3) 📘\nPerformance measurement **drives accountability**, **supports better decision-making**, **builds shareholder trust**, **improves resilience** and **creates competitive advantage**.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Assignments from the lectures (likely short-answer prompts) 📘 ⭐⭐\n### Design a sustainable \"chai\" supply chain (Lecture 1–2)\n- **Tea sourcing**: Rainforest Alliance / Trustea-certified estates, fair wages for pluckers (the Lecture 5 tea-plantation S-LCA example warns that tea can be low-carbon yet socially unsustainable), direct sourcing from small growers.\n- **Milk**: local dairy co-operatives (Amul model) to cut transport and spoilage, cold-chain on solar, methane-reducing feed.\n- **Packaging**: kulhad (clay) or reusable steel cups, paper instead of plastic sachets, deposit-return for bulk packs.\n- **Transport**: consolidated deliveries, EV three-wheelers for last mile, local sourcing radius.\n- **Waste disposal**: compost tea leaves, collect cups for reuse, measure waste per cup.\n\n### A medium-size company exports to Europe (Lecture 2)\n- **Risks**: CBAM carbon cost on embedded emissions (steel, aluminium, cement, fertiliser, hydrogen, electricity), EU CSDDD due diligence, EU Deforestation Regulation, Digital Product Passport (from 2026–27 for batteries and textiles), customer audits, loss of orders.\n- **Stakeholders that matter most**: EU customers and importers, regulators (EU and Indian), lenders and investors, suppliers (who generate the Scope 3 data).\n- **KPIs**: embedded CO₂ per tonne of product, Scope 1 + 2 intensity, % renewable electricity, supplier ESG audit coverage, traceability %, OTIF.\n- **Strategy**: measure the product carbon footprint (ISO 14067), switch to RE (PPAs, rooftop solar), green-certify key suppliers, build traceability data, set SBTi-aligned targets, report through BRSR / GRI.\n\n### An apparel manufacturer exports to Europe (Lecture 3)\nDesign 5 KPIs each for environmental, social, financial, risk, innovation and customer dashboards (see [CO5 Problem 3](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md) for the model dashboard).\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers (3 marks each) ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Define SSCM.**\nSSCM is the management of material, information and money flows, and of co-operation among firms along the supply chain, that integrates economic, environmental and social goals derived from stakeholder requirements. It extends SCM from \"cradle to grave\" to \"cradle to cradle\": it designs out waste, uses renewable resources and recovers products through reverse logistics, while keeping the chain competitive and profitable (\"save the planet without killing the P&L\").\n\n**Q2. Differentiate linear and circular supply chains.**\nA linear chain follows take–make–use–dispose and ends responsibility at sale (cradle-to-grave). A circular chain keeps materials in use through reuse, repair, remanufacture and recycling, with reverse logistics returning products to production (cradle-to-cradle). Examples: Attero and Lohum recover battery metals; Renault's Refactory remanufactures vehicles.\n\n**Q3. What are the risks of ignoring sustainability in the supply chain?**\nExport barriers (CBAM), carbon taxes, supply disruptions from climate and resource scarcity, reputation damage, investor pressure (ESG ratings, cost of capital) and customer loss. A chain profitable today can become \"illegal, expensive or unacceptable tomorrow\".\n\n**Q4. List the five steps of SSCM strategy development.**\nAssess the current state (carbon footprint, network, supplier performance, waste, energy) → analyse stakeholders → develop future scenarios (carbon pricing, disruptions, energy cost, water scarcity) → design the strategy (green sourcing, RE, circularity, supplier development, network redesign) → operationalise it (projects, KPIs, budgets, responsibilities).\n\n**Q5. How does SSCM affect business performance?**\nIt lowers cost (energy, waste, logistics), opens markets and green-product revenue, reduces regulatory and disruption risk, improves ESG ratings and the cost of capital, strengthens brand and talent, and drives innovation in business models. Measured through TBL KPIs and a sustainability balanced scorecard.\n"}, {"slug": "ch02-lca-carbon-footprints", "label": "LCA & Carbon Footprints", "tag": "02", "group": "chapters", "title": "Environmental LCA, Carbon Footprints & Carbon Management", "md": "# Environmental LCA, Carbon Footprints & Carbon Management\nSyllabus topic 2 📘 (Lecture 4 LCA Fundamentals, Lecture 5 LCSA, and the deck \"Carbon management, carbon accounting and carbon trading\"). This chapter is the theory behind **CO3 (carbon accounting)**, one of the two **high-weightage** COs. The numericals are on [Carbon Accounting Numericals](p2-carbon-accounting-numericals.md) and [LCA & Energy Numericals](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md). Expect theory sub-parts such as \"explain the four phases of LCA\", \"carbon footprint vs LCA\", \"net zero vs carbon neutral\", \"what is MRV\" and \"explain Scope 3\".\n\n---\n\n## Part A — Life Cycle Assessment (Lecture 4)\n\n### 1. What LCA is and why managers care ⭐⭐⭐\n**ISO definition** 📘: *Life Cycle Assessment is a systematic method to evaluate the environmental impacts associated with every stage of a product's life* — raw-material extraction, manufacturing, distribution, use and end-of-life (the \"life cycle wheel\"). ISO 14040 sets the principles and framework; ISO 14044 sets the requirements and guidelines.\n\nLCA underpins **ESG, carbon accounting, product sustainability, eco-design, the circular economy, green procurement and sustainable SC decision-making** 📘. *\"Managers cannot improve what they don't measure.\"*\n\n**Learning outcomes the lecture lists** (useful as a structure for long answers): explain LCA; understand ISO 14040/14044; perform basic LCA thinking; identify environmental hotspots; compare products using life-cycle thinking; apply LCA in SC decisions; appreciate its limitations; relate LCA to carbon footprint, ESG and the circular economy.\n\n### 2. Evolution of LCA 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Period | Focus |\n|---|---|\n| 1970s | Energy analysis (oil crises) |\n| 1980s | Packaging comparison (e.g. bottles and cans) |\n| 1990s | ISO framework (ISO 14040 series, 1997–2000; revised 2006) |\n| 2000s | Corporate sustainability |\n| Today | Carbon accounting, ESG, Digital Product Passport, circular economy |\n\n### 3. System boundaries ⭐⭐⭐\n| Boundary | Covers | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cradle-to-gate** | Raw materials → factory gate | Supplier data, EPDs, B2B footprints, CBAM embedded emissions |\n| **Gate-to-gate** | One process or site only | Process improvement inside a plant |\n| **Cradle-to-grave** | Raw materials → use → disposal | Consumer products, EV vs ICE comparison |\n| **Cradle-to-cradle** | Cradle-to-grave + recycling back into new products | Circular-economy design |\n\n### 4. The four phases of LCA (ISO 14040/44) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"These steps are the backbone of LCA.\"* The phases are iterative: interpretation feeds back into every earlier phase.\n\n**Phase I — Goal and scope.** Answer five questions: (i) why are we conducting the study? (ii) who will use the results? (iii) what is the **functional unit**? (iv) what are the system boundaries? (v) what are the assumptions?\n- The **functional unit** is the quantified function the product delivers, and it is *\"the most misunderstood\"* 📘. Wrong: ❌ one plastic bottle, ❌ one shopping bag, ❌ one petrol barrel. Right: ✅ **delivering 1 litre of drinking water**, ✅ **carrying 20 kg of groceries per month**, ✅ **storing 159 litres of petrol**. Two products can only be compared when they deliver the same function ([worked example](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md)).\n\n**Phase II — Life Cycle Inventory (LCI).** Quantify every input (materials, energy, water) and output (emissions to air, water and soil; waste; products) for each process in the boundary.\n- **Primary data**: factory and supplier data, utility bills, meter readings.\n- **Secondary data**: government statistics, scientific literature, databases such as **ecoinvent**.\n- Issues: data quality, **allocation** of impacts across co-products (by mass, energy or economic value), cut-off rules.\n\n**Phase III — Life Cycle Impact Assessment (LCIA).** Classify the inventory into impact categories and characterise it with factors (for example GWP for climate change). Categories on the slide: **climate change, ozone depletion, smog (photochemical oxidation), water scarcity, land occupation, ecotoxicity, biodiversity**; others include acidification, eutrophication and resource depletion.\n\n**Phase IV — Interpretation.** Identify significant issues and hotspots, check completeness, run sensitivity analysis on key assumptions, draw conclusions and recommendations, and support decisions. Slide example: *\"EVs have higher manufacturing emissions but often lower lifetime emissions depending on the electricity mix.\"*\n\n### 5. Carbon footprint vs LCA 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| | Carbon footprint | LCA |\n|---|---|---|\n| Measures | CO₂e only (GWP) | Multiple impacts |\n| Scope | Climate | Whole environment |\n| Effort | Simpler, faster | Comprehensive, more detailed |\n| Standard | GHG Protocol, ISO 14067 (product), ISO 14064 (organisation) | ISO 14040/14044 |\n| Risk | Burden shifting (cut CO₂ but raise water use or toxicity) | Captures trade-offs |\n\nA carbon footprint is a single-indicator subset of LCA.\n\n### 6. Indian case studies (Lecture 4) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Company | Hotspots identified by LCA | Improvement actions |\n|---|---|---|\n| **UltraTech Cement** | Clinker production, kiln fuel, transportation | Waste-heat recovery, alternative fuels, fly-ash blending |\n| **Tata Steel** | Iron-ore mining, blast furnace, coal, transport | Hydrogen, scrap recycling, renewable electricity |\n| **Amul milk** | Farm emissions: feed, methane, cold chain, packaging, distribution | Efficient refrigeration, renewables, cattle-feed improvement |\n| **Parle Agro / Bisleri** | Resin (PET) production, transportation, packaging | Lightweight bottles, recycled PET, solar electricity, rail transport |\n\n*\"LCA reduces the impact substantially.\"* The general pattern: in heavy industry the hotspot is **process energy and raw materials**; in food it is the **farm stage (methane, feed)**; in packaged beverages it is the **packaging material**.\n\n### 7. LCA in supply-chain management 📘 ⭐⭐\nLCA supports supplier selection (compare suppliers' cradle-to-gate footprints), product and packaging design (eco-design), make-or-buy, mode and network choices, end-of-life strategy and claims substantiation. **\"LCA supports Scope 3 accounting\"** 📘 because Scope 3 categories (purchased goods, use of sold products, end-of-life) are life-cycle stages.\n\n### 8. Advantages and limitations 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Advantages | Limitations |\n|---|---|\n| Scientific | Data-intensive |\n| Transparent | Time-consuming |\n| Decision support | Expensive |\n| Identifies hotspots | Different assumptions give different answers |\n| Supports ESG | Regional variation (grid mix, farming practices) |\n| Supports eco-design | Allocation issues |\n| Supports carbon neutrality | Interpretation complexity |\n\n### 9. LCA software and trends 📘 ⭐\n| Software | Type | Company |\n|---|---|---|\n| **OpenLCA** | Free | GreenDelta, Germany |\n| **SimaPro** | Licence fee | PRé Sustainability, Netherlands |\n| **GaBi** | 45-day free trial, then licence | Sphera, USA |\n| **Umberto** | Licence fee | iPoint Systems, Germany |\n\n**Emerging trends**: AI-enabled LCA, Digital Product Passport (EU DPP), blockchain traceability, real-time LCA, supplier digital twins, automated Scope 3 reporting.\n\n**Key takeaways** 📘: LCA measures footprint across the entire life cycle; follows the ISO 14040 framework; supports sustainable product design, procurement and the circular economy; supports ESG; is an essential tool for operations managers.\n\n---\n\n## Part B — Life Cycle Sustainability Assessment (Lecture 5)\n\n### 10. LCSA = E-LCA + S-LCA + LCC ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 5 aligns with **UNEP/SETAC, ISO 14040/14044, ISO 15686-5** (life-cycle costing for buildings) and the **Klöpffer (2008)** LCSA framework: **LCSA = Environmental LCA + Social LCA + Life Cycle Costing.**\n\n*Traditional LCA* = environmental impacts only. *Modern LCA = LCSA* covers all three pillars, so that a product is **environmentally friendly + socially ethical + economically viable**.\n\n| Component | Question it answers | Example (Lecture 5) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **E-LCA** | \"What happens to the environment?\" | Manufacturing 1 laptop = **320 kg CO₂e + 18,000 L water + 850 MJ energy** |\n| **S-LCA** | \"What happens to people?\" | Indian tea plantation: E-LCA low carbon, but S-LCA shows poor labour conditions → *not fully sustainable* |\n| **LCC** | \"What does it really cost over its life?\" (TCO) | LED ₹250 / 25,000 h vs incandescent ₹40 / 1,000 h: LED has a higher purchase price but lower ownership cost, lower environmental impact and lower carbon footprint |\n\n### 11. E-LCA example: petrol car vs electric car 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Life stage | Petrol car | Electric car |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw materials | Medium | High (battery metals) |\n| Manufacturing | Medium | High |\n| Use phase | Very high emissions | Low emissions |\n| Maintenance | Medium | Low |\n| End-of-life | Recycling possible | Battery recycling critical |\n\n*If only manufacturing is studied, the petrol car appears greener; if the entire life cycle is studied, the electric car generally has much lower lifetime emissions.* The carbon deck adds that on the Indian grid, a typical ICE car is about 7 t (manufacturing) + 18 t (use) ≈ 25 t; an EV on the Indian grid about 12 t + 16 t ≈ 28 t; an EV on a renewable grid about 10 t + 3 t ≈ 13 t. **The EV benefit depends on grid cleanliness.**\n\n### 12. S-LCA impact categories (Lecture 5) 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Stakeholder group | Issues |\n|---|---|\n| **Workers** | Child labour, forced labour, occupational safety, equal wages, working hours, gender equality |\n| **Consumers** | Product safety, privacy, product quality |\n| **Society / community** | Local employment, community development, indigenous rights, access to resources |\n| **Governance (value chain)** | Ethical governance, corruption, human rights, fair competition |\n\n(UNEP 2020 S-LCA guidelines use the stakeholder groups workers, local community, value-chain actors, consumers, society and children.)\n\n### 13. Integrating the three: packaging choice 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Option | Environmental | Social | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Plastic | Poor | Average | Low |\n| Aluminium | Moderate | Moderate | High |\n| Paper | Good | Good | Moderate |\n\n- Decision on **cost only** → plastic.\n- Decision on **E-LCA** → paper.\n- Decision on **LCSA** → **paper from certified sustainable forests** (FSC/PEFC), because it balances environment, society and economics.\n\n**Final framework** 📘: sustainability → environmental (E-LCA) + social (S-LCA) + economic (LCC) → LCSA. *To gain competitive advantage, a sustainable business must optimise environmental performance + social responsibility + economic value.*\n\n---\n\n## Part C — Carbon management, carbon accounting and carbon trading\n\n### 14. Why carbon has become a business variable 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Greenhouse gases**: atmospheric gases such as CO₂, CH₄ and N₂O that trap heat and cause global warming.\n- **Global drivers**: climate change and net-zero commitments, investor ESG expectations, supply-chain decarbonisation, carbon border taxes.\n- **Indian context**: SEBI **BRSR** reporting (mandatory for the top 1,000 listed companies), export competitiveness (**CBAM**), energy transition (the deck cites renewable/non-fossil capacity at about 54% of India's total).\n- **Business reality**: **carbon = financial risk + opportunity.** *\"Carbon emissions are now economic variables, not environmental topics.\"*\n\n### 15. Carbon neutral vs net zero vs carbon zero vs climate positive 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Concept | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Carbon neutral** | Emissions balanced through offsets |\n| **Net zero** | Reduce emissions deeply (**90–95%**) and neutralise only the residual emissions (with removals) |\n| **Carbon zero** | Near-zero emissions across operations and value chain |\n| **Climate positive** | Removes more carbon than it emits |\n\n### 16. The net-zero hierarchy 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Measure** — Scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions; calculate CO₂e.\n2. **Reduce** — decarbonisation, energy efficiency, circularity, nature-based solutions.\n3. **RE transition** — shift from fossil fuels to solar, wind, hydro; EV fleets; efficient and renewable energy use.\n4. **Sustainable supply chain** — circularity, biodegradable packaging.\n5. **Offset unavoidable emissions** — afforestation, **CCUS** (carbon capture, utilisation and storage).\n\n**\"Magic mantra: reduction first, offset later.\"** 📘 The deck frames the path as *\"Carbon to competitive advantage: the roadmap to net zero 2050.\"*\n\n### 17. ESG and its metrics (carbon deck) 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Environmental | Social | Governance |\n|---|---|---|\n| GHG emissions (Scope 1, 2, 3); carbon intensity (tCO₂e per unit revenue or production) | Employee health & safety: LTIFR | Board independence: independent directors % |\n| Energy efficiency & renewable adoption: RE % | Diversity, equity & inclusion: gender-diversity ratio | Audit & risk oversight: ESG-linked executive pay |\n| Water withdrawal & discharge: water intensity | Human rights in supply chain; attrition rate | Anti-corruption policies; whistle-blower cases resolved |\n| Waste & circular economy: waste recycled % | Community engagement: CSR spending | Executive-compensation alignment; customer data protection |\n| Biodiversity impact; SBTi alignment; climate risk & resilience | Supply-chain audits | Compliance incidents |\n\n*\"ESG = business sustainability scorecard for future business.\"*\n\n### 18. Global standards and frameworks 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Body / standard | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **GHG Protocol** (WRI + WBCSD) | Corporate accounting standard; introduced the **Scope 1, 2, 3** classification |\n| **IPCC** | Scientific basis and emission factors; defines **GWPs** |\n| **ISO 14064** | Formal standard for GHG quantification and **verification** |\n| **UNFCCC** | Governs global reporting and national GHG inventories (Kyoto 1997, Paris 2015) |\n\n*These frameworks make emissions auditable like financial data: standardisation, auditability, comparable reporting.*\n\n**Reporting frameworks** 📘: **CDP** (climate change, water security, forests and deforestation, plastics and biodiversity); **GRI 305** (Scope 1, 2, 3; GHG intensity; reductions; ozone-depleting substances; NOx, SOx and other air emissions); **TCFD** (four pillars: governance, strategy, risk management, metrics and targets); **Integrated Reporting** (six capitals: financial, manufactured, intellectual, human, social and relationship, natural); **BRSR** (India: 9 principles, ~140 parameters; Section A general disclosure, Section B management and process, Section C principle-wise performance; P2 sustainable products and services, P6 environment).\n\n### 19. Scope 1, 2 and 3 (GHG Protocol) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Scope 1 — direct (\"what you burn\") | Scope 2 — energy indirect (\"what you buy\") | Scope 3 — value chain (\"what you influence\") |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fuel combustion (diesel in a plant generator) | Purchased grid electricity (e.g. MSEDCL power) | Purchased products and services from suppliers |\n| Company vehicles (petrol in company cars) | Purchased heating (hot water for buildings) | Fuel and energy not in Scope 1 & 2 (mining coal, refining petrol) |\n| Process emissions (chemical reactions in manufacturing, e.g. cement calcination) | Purchased cooling (energy for AC/refrigeration) | Product use and end-of-life treatment |\n| Fugitive emissions (refrigerant leaks) | Purchased gases (hydrogen for an industrial process) | Logistics of inbound materials and outbound products |\n\n- **Scope 3 is the most challenging and is often 70–90% (or more) of total emissions** 📘. In the course's automotive case Scope 1 + 2 are **under 5%** and Scope 3 **over 95%**; Tata Motors' BRSR shows \"use of sold products\" alone at about 96% of Scope 3.\n- *\"A company may reduce Scope 1 substantially while its total supply-chain footprint continues increasing → carbon accounting must follow the value chain\"* (Lecture 10).\n- **15 Scope 3 categories**: upstream 1 purchased goods & services, 2 capital goods, 3 fuel & energy-related, 4 upstream transport, 5 waste, 6 business travel, 7 employee commuting, 8 upstream leased assets; downstream 9 downstream transport, 10 processing of sold products, 11 use of sold products, 12 end-of-life of sold products, 13 downstream leased assets, 14 franchises, 15 investments.\n- **Location-based vs market-based Scope 2**: location-based uses the average grid factor; market-based uses the factor of contracts actually bought (green PPAs, RECs).\n\n### 20. Carbon accounting methodology (5 steps) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Identify emission sources** scope-wise (natural-gas furnace, diesel generators, purchased electricity, logistics).\n2. **Collect activity data** (litres of diesel, kWh of electricity, tonnes of steel purchased).\n3. **Apply emission factors**: **Emissions = Activity data × Emission factor** (e.g. 10,000 litres diesel × 2.68 kg CO₂/l = 26,800 kg CO₂; sources IPCC, DEFRA, national databases).\n4. **Convert to CO₂e** with GWP multipliers: CO₂e = emission × GWP.\n5. **Aggregate and report.**\n*\"If you cannot measure carbon, then you cannot manage it!\"*\n\n### 21. The Kyoto basket of GHGs and GWPs (100-year) 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Gas | Formula | GWP-100 | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Carbon dioxide | CO₂ | 1 | Rising sea levels, ocean acidification |\n| Methane | CH₄ | ~27–30 | Melting glaciers, crop damage |\n| Nitrous oxide | N₂O | ~273 | Ozone-layer thinning, soil and water pollution |\n| Hydrofluorocarbons | HFCs | 12–14,000 | Refrigerants |\n| Perfluorocarbons | PFCs | 7,000–12,000 | Very long lifetime (50,000+ years) |\n| Sulphur hexafluoride | SF₆ | ~25,200 | Switchgear; lifetime 3,200+ years |\n| Nitrogen trifluoride | NF₃ | ~17,000 | Electronics manufacturing; lifetime ~500–750 years |\n\n### 22. Financial accounting vs carbon accounting 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Attribute | Financial accounting | Carbon accounting |\n|---|---|---|\n| Measure | Currency | CO₂e |\n| Record | Ledger | Emission inventory |\n| Source | Cost centres | Emission sources |\n| Assessment | Audit | Verification |\n| Results | Profitability | Carbon intensity |\n\nEmerging concept: the **carbon ledger**; new roles: carbon accountants, ESG controllers.\n\n### 23. MRV — Measure, Report, Verify 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nMRV is *the foundation of carbon accounting and ESG reporting.*\n- **Measure**: collect activity and emission data (kWh, litres of diesel, m³ of natural gas, production units).\n- **Report**: disclose emissions (ESG reports, BRSR, CBAM filings, CDP).\n- **Verify**: independent validation (internal audit, third-party assurance, accredited agencies such as Bureau Veritas, TÜV, KBS).\nWithout MRV, carbon data lacks credibility, ESG disclosures become unreliable and export risk rises under CBAM. With MRV: trusted reporting, investor confidence, global competitiveness. *\"MRV converts sustainability claims into measurable, auditable and business-relevant data.\"*\n\n### 24. Carbon accounting software for India (carbon deck) 📘 ⭐\nSAMESG Lite (MSMEs, BRSR), Greenly (France, SMEs), IBM Envizi (mid-large enterprises), Sprih (Pune; BRSR/GRI/TCFD), Bharat Carbon (Pune; MSMEs), CleanCarbon (Noida; CBAM exporters). They help MSMEs measure emissions, simplify ESG reporting, meet BRSR compliance and compete globally.\n\n### 25. Carbon credits and carbon markets 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- A **carbon credit** = **1 tonne of CO₂e** avoided, reduced or removed, verified and issued by a registry.\n- Credits come from **avoided emissions** (solar, wind, biomass), **emission reductions** (methane capture) or **carbon removals** (afforestation, CCUS). Removal credits usually command higher prices.\n- Deck examples: a 50 kW solar farm generates about 50–55 credits a year (₹45–60 thousand at ₹850–1,100 per credit); a 50 kW wind farm 50–80 a year; 50 acres of afforestation 0.2–0.8 t CO₂ per acre a year at ₹800–3,000 per credit; a 50-tonne methane-capture plant about 1,250 credits a year (about $25–30 thousand at $20 per credit, ≈ ₹23–28 lakh); a 50 kW biomass plant 200–500 a year. ([Numericals](p2-carbon-accounting-numericals.md))\n- **Registries**: Verra (VCS), Gold Standard, American Carbon Registry, Climate Action Reserve.\n- **Compliance market** (mandatory: EU ETS, national schemes) vs **voluntary market** (corporate commitments, net-zero pledges).\n- **India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS)**, notified in 2023 under the Energy Conservation Act and operational from 2025–26, sets emission-intensity targets for energy-intensive sectors; firms that beat targets earn tradable credits. *\"From carbon compliance to carbon competitiveness.\"*\n\n### 26. Key takeaways of carbon management 📘\n- Carbon is becoming a business currency: you must measure and manage it.\n- Carbon accounting is the foundation: follow MRV and improve.\n- Net zero is a business transformation journey, not an environmental project.\n- Carbon credits create new value: environmental + financial.\n- Future competitiveness = **Carbon + Cost + Technology + ESG.**\n- New careers: carbon accounting, ESG reporting, sustainability analytics, carbon markets, climate-risk management.\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Explain the four phases of LCA with an example.**\n(1) Goal and scope: purpose, audience, functional unit (e.g. delivering 1 litre of drinking water), boundary (cradle-to-grave), assumptions. (2) Inventory: collect primary (plant, supplier, utility bills) and secondary (ecoinvent) data on inputs and outputs. (3) Impact assessment: convert flows into categories (climate change via GWP, water scarcity, ecotoxicity). (4) Interpretation: find hotspots (e.g. PET resin for Bisleri), run sensitivity, recommend (lightweight bottles, rPET, rail transport).\n\n**Q2. Distinguish carbon neutral and net zero.**\nCarbon neutral balances emissions with offsets, whatever the reduction. Net zero requires deep reductions (90–95% across Scopes 1–3, SBTi-aligned) and neutralises only residual emissions with permanent removals. \"Reduction first, offset later.\"\n\n**Q3. Why is Scope 3 the most important and the hardest?**\nIt is usually 70–90%+ of emissions (over 95% in the automotive case), but it sits outside the company's control: it depends on suppliers' data, customers' use and end-of-life, with 15 categories and heavy use of secondary data. Managing it needs supplier engagement, LCA and procurement levers.\n\n**Q4. What is MRV and why does it matter for CBAM?**\nMeasure, Report, Verify. CBAM requires exporters to declare verified embedded emissions; without MRV the importer must use punitive default values, so verified data protects competitiveness.\n"}, {"slug": "ch03-sustainable-procurement", "label": "Sustainable Procurement", "tag": "03", "group": "chapters", "title": "Sustainable Procurement & Its Impact on Business Performance", "md": "# Sustainable Procurement & Its Impact on Business Performance\nSyllabus topic 3 📘 (Lecture 6: \"Sustainable procurement, impact on business performance & supplier selection strategies\"). This is the theory behind **CO2 (weighted scoring of suppliers)**, a **medium-weightage** CO. The numericals (weighted scoring, VoltTech scorecard, TOPSIS, AHP, risk matrix, TCO) are on [Supplier Selection Numericals](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md). The lecture's formula: **\"Sustainable procurement + suitable supplier selection = sustained performance.\"**\n\n**Learning objectives (Lecture 6)** 📘: explain sustainable procurement; understand procurement's role in ESG and corporate strategy; measure procurement's contribution to business performance; develop supplier sustainability evaluation criteria; apply supplier-selection decision tools; evaluate suppliers with multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) methods; appreciate digital procurement trends.\n\n---\n\n## Part A — Sustainable procurement and business performance\n\n### 1. Definition ⭐⭐⭐\n**Sustainable procurement** is the process by which organisations buy goods, services and works in a way that achieves **value for money on a whole-life basis** while generating benefits for the organisation, society and the economy and minimising damage to the environment (UK Sustainable Procurement Task Force, 2006; ISO 20400:2017 \"Sustainable procurement — guidance\"). In the course's words: the focus changes from **best cost** to **best value (economic + environmental + social)**.\n\n### 2. Supplier A vs Supplier B — the opening debate 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Supplier A (traditional choice) | Supplier B (sustainable choice) |\n|---|---|\n| Lowest cost | 12% more expensive |\n| High emissions | Renewable energy |\n| Child-labour allegations | Zero human-rights issues |\n| | ISO 14001 certified |\n\n| Traditional procurement | Modern sustainable procurement |\n|---|---|\n| Cheapest supplier wins | Ethical supplier wins |\n| Faster delivery | Lower emissions |\n| Maximum production | Circular production |\n| Acceptable quality at inspection | **Quality at source** |\n\nA model answer chooses **Supplier B**: the 12% premium is the visible cost, but Supplier A carries hidden costs — reputational damage (Nike's labour scandals), legal exposure under due-diligence laws (EU CSDDD), carbon costs (CBAM, Scope 3 targets) and supply disruption if the supplier is sanctioned. Use a TCO or weighted model to show B's total value is higher ([P1 and P7](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md)).\n\n### 3. Why procurement matters 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"Procurement is not just about buying. It drives business value across the entire value chain.\"* Procurement contributes:\n1. **50–80% of manufacturing costs** — purchased goods and services typically account for 50–80% of total manufacturing cost.\n2. **A major share of Scope 3 emissions** — purchased goods and services usually contribute the highest share of Scope 3 (Category 1).\n3. **Supplier labour practices** — procurement influences working conditions, fair wages, health and safety across the supply base.\n4. **Product quality** — supplier capability, materials and processes directly shape product quality and reliability.\n5. **Brand reputation** — supplier misconduct or poor performance damages brand trust and stakeholder confidence.\nExamples on the slide: **Apple, Nike, Toyota, Unilever** depend heavily on supplier performance for quality, innovation, sustainability and brand trust. **\"Strategic procurement = resilient supply chain + sustainable growth.\"** Procurement connects raw-material suppliers, design, packaging suppliers, manufacturer, logistics, retailer and end customer.\n\n### 4. Why companies adopt sustainable procurement — six drivers with examples 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Driver | Example 1 | Example 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Customer expectations** | Apple: recycled and rare-earth materials | IKEA: wood from responsibly managed (FSC-certified) forests |\n| **ESG investing** | Unilever attracts ESG investors through regenerative agriculture | Microsoft secures investor confidence through supplier carbon-emission reporting |\n| **Regulations** | EU **CSDDD** (Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive) | EU **CBAM** for steel, cement, aluminium, fertilisers, hydrogen and electricity exporters |\n| **Climate change** | Nestlé works with farmers on regenerative agriculture to reduce emissions | Toyota encourages suppliers to raise energy efficiency and cut GHG |\n| **Supply-chain resilience** | Tata Motors localised ECU semiconductors after COVID-19 | Starbucks invests in climate-resilient coffee farming and supplier support |\n| **Brand reputation / risk reduction** | Patagonia audits suppliers and promotes Fair Trade Certified™ production; Nike strengthened supplier monitoring after labour-practice charges | Intel performs conflict-mineral due diligence; Walmart's Project Gigaton works with suppliers to cut emissions |\n\n### 5. Benefits to business performance 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Financial | Operational | Strategic |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reduced waste | Better supplier reliability | Brand trust |\n| Energy savings | Higher quality | Investor confidence |\n| Lower life-cycle cost | Innovation | (and multimodal transport efficiencies) |\n\n*\"Sustainable procurement drives profit, performance and long-term competitive advantage.\"*\n\n### 6. Cost vs value — from lowest price to lowest TCO 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Traditional | Sustainable |\n|---|---|\n| **Lowest purchase cost** — focus only on the upfront price | **Lowest Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)** — total cost across the life cycle |\n| Hidden costs later: higher maintenance, more energy consumption, more waste and disposal cost, shorter product life, environmental and compliance risks | Benefits: lower overall cost, higher efficiency and performance, reduced environmental impact, longer product life, better compliance and risk management |\n\n**Life-cycle cost components**: purchase price → maintenance → waste → energy → end-of-life. *\"Look beyond price. Focus on total value. Lowest TCO = higher value + sustainable growth.\"* ([TCO numerical](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md))\n\n### 7. Procurement and ESG 📘 ⭐⭐\nProcurement must track the same ESG metrics the company reports: environmental (Scope 1–3, carbon intensity, RE %, water intensity, waste recycled %, biodiversity, SBTi alignment, climate risk), social (LTIFR, diversity, human rights in the supply chain, attrition, CSR spending, customer data protection, supply-chain audits), governance (board independence, ESG-linked pay, anti-corruption, whistle-blower cases, compliance incidents). *\"ESG = business sustainability scorecard for future business.\"*\n\n### 8. Procurement KPIs — the business performance framework 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Environmental | Social | Operational | Financial |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Carbon footprint | Lost-time injury (LTIFR) | Supplier lead time (avg.) | ROI % |\n| RE usage % | Forced-labour violations (%) | OTIF % | Logistics costs % |\n| Water conservation | Training and capacity building (hrs) | Purchase-order cycle time | Cost savings % |\n| Waste recycled | Supplier diversity % | Supplier defect rate | Cost-avoidance variance |\n| Sustainable procurement % | Supplier audit pass rate (%) | Supplier fill rate | Purchase-price variance |\n| Climate-risk resilience index | | | |\n\n*\"What gets measured gets improved — sustainable procurement KPIs turn strategy into performance.\"*\n\n### 9. Case: Nestlé's sustainable procurement 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Practice | Business benefit |\n|---|---|\n| **Responsible sourcing** of cocoa, milk, coffee, palm oil, cereals | Reliable supply and lower compliance risk |\n| **Regenerative agriculture**: cover cropping, crop rotation, agroforestry, organic fertilisers | Improved soil health and climate resilience |\n| **Supplier training**: technical and climate-smart support, better planting materials, fiscal incentives | Higher productivity and better quality |\n| **Deforestation-free sourcing** of pulp, soy, coffee | Stronger brand reputation and regulatory compliance |\n| **Climate-smart procurement**: RE adoption, efficient irrigation, methane reduction in dairy | Lower Scope 3 emissions and reduced carbon risk |\n| **Digital traceability** from farm to factory | Greater transparency and consumer trust |\n| **Sustainable packaging**: reduced virgin plastic, recyclability | Lower environmental footprint, circular-economy benefits |\n\n### 10. Challenges in sustainable procurement 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"Moving towards sustainability is a journey, not a switch.\"*\n1. **Higher upfront cost** — sustainable materials, technologies and certifications come at a premium; longer paybacks; ROI not always immediate.\n2. **Supplier resistance** — change meets reluctance; concerns about cost, capability and complexity; lack of awareness or prioritisation.\n3. **Limited data** — no standardised ESG metrics; inconsistent or incomplete supplier data; hard to measure true impact.\n4. **Greenwashing** — exaggerated or misleading supplier claims; hard to verify real impact; risk to brand trust and credibility.\n5. **Poor visibility** — complex global chains; no end-to-end traceability; hidden risks in Tier-2/Tier-3.\n6. **Regulatory uncertainty** — evolving and inconsistent regulations; unclear compliance requirements; future risks and costs hard to predict.\n*Overcoming them requires collaboration, transparency, innovation and a long-term commitment to sustainable value.*\n\n**Key takeaway** 📘: *\"Sustainable procurement is now strategic, not administrative.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part B — Supplier selection strategies\n\n### 11. Old vs modern approach 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Old approach | Modern approach |\n|---|---|\n| Cost | **Value** |\n| Quality | **ESG** |\n| Delivery | **Innovation** |\n| Maximum output | **Risk** |\n\n### 12. Supplier selection criteria: \"ESG + RTI\" 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nThe lecture's scorecard has six families. **\"ESG + RTI = future supplier business evaluation scorecard\"** (Environmental, Social, Governance + Risk, Technology, Innovation).\n\n| Family | Key areas (measure) |\n|---|---|\n| **Environmental** | Carbon emissions (tCO₂e), renewable energy %, water management, waste management (solid / liquid / e-waste), circularity %, ISO 14001 certification |\n| **Social** | Health & safety practices, training (multi-skill matrix), diversity %, human-rights protection, community engagement, customer data protection |\n| **Governance** | Regulatory compliance (# cases), audit compliance (# NCs), transparency (# whistle-blower complaints), cybersecurity & data protection (# incidents), anti-bribery/corruption (% adherence) |\n| **Risk** | Business-risk analysis, financial stability (working capital / profitability), geographical location (exposure to natural hazards), supply continuity (# disruptions), speed of adaptation (% agility), cybersecurity (resilience index) |\n| **Technology** | Digitalisation (% capability), manufacturing technology (% automation), quality management (# QC tools), traceability systems (RFID, IoT), data-analytics capability (real-time data systems) |\n| **Innovation** | R&D capability (# patents), new-product development (# collaborations), continuous improvement (# kaizens), sustainable innovations (# cases) |\n\n### 13. Supplier scorecard (VoltTech example) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 6 shows a weighted scorecard for an EV-battery supplier (\"VoltTech\") with criteria scored and weighted, totalled out of 100 and banded (A excellent, B good, and so on). The worked version and the slide's banding inconsistency (86.5 sits in band B although the slide labels it \"A 86.2\") are on [P2](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md).\n\n### 14. Decision-making tools 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Qualitative**: expert judgement, supplier audits.\n**Quantitative**:\n| Method | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Weighted scoring** | Assign importance weights to criteria, score each supplier, total = Σ weight × score; choose the highest. Simple, transparent; weights are subjective. |\n| **AHP** (Analytic Hierarchy Process, Saaty) | Organises the decision as goal → criteria → alternatives; pairwise comparisons on a 1–9 scale give weights; consistency ratio CR < 0.10 checks logic. |\n| **TOPSIS** | Ranks alternatives by closeness to the ideal solution and distance from the worst (anti-ideal): normalise → weight → find ideal and anti-ideal → distances S⁺ and S⁻ → Cᵢ = S⁻ ÷ (S⁺ + S⁻). |\n| **DEA** (Data Envelopment Analysis) | Measures the relative efficiency of peer units (outputs ÷ inputs) and gives each supplier a DEA score; efficient suppliers form the frontier. |\n| **Linear Programming** | Finds the best outcome (minimum cost or maximum value) subject to linear constraints, e.g. order allocation across suppliers with capacity, carbon and quality limits. |\n\nWhen to use which: weighted scoring for quick, explainable shortlists; AHP when criteria weights are disputed; TOPSIS when you have many quantitative criteria; DEA for benchmarking many similar suppliers; LP for allocating volumes among already-approved suppliers.\n\n### 15. Supplier risk matrix — agri-food example 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nRisk score = **likelihood (1–5) × impact (1–5)**. Likelihood bands: rare (< 10%), unlikely (10–30%), possible (30–60%), most likely (60–80%), certain (> 80%). Impact: insignificant, minor, moderate, major, severe. Bands: **low 1–5, medium 6–14, high 15–25** (the slide prints 15–24).\n\n| Supplier | Risk | Key drivers | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | High | Drought risk, single-region sourcing | Weather monitoring, diversify sourcing |\n| B | Medium | Moderate leverage, seasonal cash flow | Financial monitoring, advance payments with controls |\n| C | Low | Strong fleet, good on-time performance | Continue KPI monitoring |\n\n### 16. Supplier segmentation and development 📘 ⭐⭐\nSegment suppliers (for example with the **Kraljic matrix**: profit impact × supply risk → strategic, leverage, bottleneck, non-critical items) and develop strategic ones through **training, joint innovation, technology transfer, audits, continuous improvement and long-term partnerships**. Development is cheaper than switching when the supplier is strategic or capacity is scarce.\n\n### 17. The way forward: digital procurement and emerging trends 📘 ⭐⭐\n**Digital procurement — \"technology, transparency, trust\"**: AI (smarter decisions, demand prediction, risk mitigation), supplier analytics, blockchain (secure, tamper-proof transactions), traceability (end-to-end visibility), IoT (connected sensors), real-time monitoring of orders, shipments and quality, **Digital Product Passport** (verified product ID, origin, materials, compliance, life cycle).\n\n**Emerging trends — building sustainable and resilient supply chains**: (1) carbon accounting, (2) Scope 3 reporting, (3) **CBAM readiness**, (4) supplier ESG ratings, (5) responsible sourcing, (6) circular procurement (recycled, reusable and renewable materials). *\"Sustainable choices today, a better future tomorrow.\"*\n\n### 18. Case studies 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Schneider Electric — Zero Carbon Project for suppliers**\n- Target: **50% reduction in the operational carbon footprint of its top 1,000 suppliers** (by 2025).\n- Works with key partners that account for **70% of its supply-chain emissions**.\n- Three-phase framework: **Analytics, Ambition, Action.**\n- *\"The road to net zero runs through the supply base.\"*\n\n**Walmart — Project Gigaton**\n- Goal: avoid **1 billion tonnes (a gigaton) of GHG** in its value chain by 2030.\n- **Six pillars**: energy, agriculture, waste, packaging, deforestation and product use.\n- Suppliers set self-directed, measurable goals on the **Walmart Sustainability Hub**.\n- Developed with **WWF** and **Environmental Defense Fund (EDF)**.\n- *\"Transforming global supply chains into powerful engines of climate action.\"*\n\n**Asian Paints — ESG-based sustainable procurement**\n- Carbon reduction: prioritising alternate grades, new chemistries and lower-carbon suppliers.\n- Bio-based goals: raise renewable or bio-based raw-material integration by at least 20%.\n- Formulation optimisation: redesign formulations to cut emissions.\n- *\"ESG-driven procurement begins with selecting the right suppliers.\"*\n\n**JSW Steel — ESG-based supplier assessment**\n- Digital self-assessment: suppliers complete structured scoring on **Synesgy**.\n- Action plans and capacity building: training for sustainability awareness.\n- ESG compliance embedded in core strategy and supplier selection.\n- *\"Digital ESG assessments strengthen supplier resilience and transparency.\"*\n\n### 19. Recap — the five-step logic 📘\n**Sustainable procurement → supplier sustainability → decision tools → business performance → competitive advantage.** *\"Procurement is no longer about buying products. It is about creating resilient, responsible and competitive value chains.\"*\n\n**Exercise (Lecture 6)**: for a laptop manufacturer, evaluate three suppliers on price, quality, carbon, safety and innovation and create your own scorecard → solved as [P6](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md).\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Why is procurement central to sustainability?**\nIt controls 50–80% of manufacturing cost and is usually the largest Scope 3 category, so most of a firm's footprint, labour risk and quality risk enters through purchasing. Choosing and developing suppliers on ESG + RTI criteria lowers lifecycle cost, carbon and reputational risk (Walmart Gigaton, Schneider Zero Carbon).\n\n**Q2. Distinguish lowest price and lowest TCO.**\nLowest price compares only purchase cost; lowest TCO adds maintenance, energy, waste, end-of-life and risk costs over the product's life. A dearer supplier with efficient, durable, compliant products often has a lower TCO.\n\n**Q3. Name five MCDM tools for supplier selection.**\nWeighted scoring, AHP, TOPSIS, DEA and linear programming (plus qualitative expert judgement and audits).\n\n**Q4. List the six challenges of sustainable procurement.**\nHigher upfront cost, supplier resistance, limited data, greenwashing, poor visibility and regulatory uncertainty.\n"}, {"slug": "ch04-sustainable-manufacturing", "label": "Sustainable Manufacturing", "tag": "04", "group": "chapters", "title": "Sustainable Manufacturing & Its Impact on Productivity", "md": "# Sustainable Manufacturing & Its Impact on Productivity\nSyllabus topic 4 📘 (Lecture 7: \"Sustainable manufacturing and its impact on productivity and organisational performance\"). It supports **CO4** (network and operations) and **CO5** (OEE, FPY and manufacturing KPIs; see [CO5 Problem 2](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md)). Theory questions here are usually \"define\", \"explain the principles with examples\", \"how does sustainability raise productivity\" and the **textile-factory exercise**.\n\n**Learning objectives** 📘: sustainable manufacturing principles; productivity improvement; resource efficiency; circular economy; business performance; industry best practices.\n\n---\n\n## Section A — Sustainable manufacturing\n\n### 1. The opening question: which company is more profitable after 10 years? 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Company A | Company B |\n|---|---|\n| Lowest cost of production | Adopts renewable energy |\n| High pollution | Efficient production |\n| High energy consumption | Drives a zero-waste initiative |\n\nCompany B, because its costs fall over time (energy, waste, compliance) while Company A's rise with energy prices, carbon taxes, pollution penalties and customer pressure.\n\n### 2. Traditional vs sustainable manufacturing 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Traditional manufacturing | Sustainable manufacturing |\n|---|---|\n| Maximise output, minimise costs | Balances the Triple Bottom Line (3Ps) |\n| High consumption of resources | Optimises resource usage |\n| Take–make–dispose (linear) | Adopts the circular economy |\n| Compliance-driven, less ESG focus | Innovation-driven, continuous improvement for sustainability |\n\nFocus change: **best cost → best value (economic + environmental + social).**\n\n### 3. Definition (UNEP) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Sustainable manufacturing** is *\"manufacturing products using economically sound processes that minimise environmental impacts while conserving energy and natural resources and ensuring employee, community and consumer safety.\"* (The US EPA uses almost the same wording.)\n\n### 4. The three pillars of sustainable manufacturing 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Environmental | Social | Economic |\n|---|---|---|\n| Carbon reduction, water conservation, renewable energy | Worker safety, ethical sourcing, community development | Productivity, profitability, innovation |\n| e.g. rooftop solar, recycling process water, recycling scrap | e.g. employee health & safety, fair wages and skill development, CSR | e.g. lean manufacturing, Industry 4.0, predictive maintenance |\n\n### 5. The sustainable manufacturing framework — \"Design · Produce · Use · Recover · Sustain\" 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Inputs** — sustainable raw materials, energy, water and other resources.\n2. **Manufacturing process** — efficient, clean, safe and value-added production.\n3. **Outputs** — quality products with minimal emissions and waste.\n4. **Recycling** — collecting and processing end-of-life materials to recover resources.\n5. **Reuse** — reusing components or materials in the same or new products.\n6. **Remanufacturing** — restoring used products or components to like-new condition.\n7. **Closed-loop system** — resources circulate continuously, creating value with zero waste.\n*\"Closed loop. Zero waste. Sustainable future.\"*\n\n### 6. Sustainable manufacturing principles with company examples 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Principle | What it means | Example 1 | Example 2 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Lean manufacturing** | Eliminate non-value-adding waste; flow and pull | Toyota: Toyota Production System (TPS), JIT | Danaher: Danaher Business System (DBS) to reduce waste and boost innovation |\n| **Six Sigma** | Reduce process variation (DMAIC; 3.4 defects per million) | Motorola developed Six Sigma to reduce variation and improve quality | GE Aviation: optimise processes, reduce defects, improve reliability |\n| **Kaizen** | Continuous, small, employee-led improvement | Toyota: all employees pursue continuous gains in quality, safety and efficiency | Canon: improves workflow, product quality and reduces waste |\n| **Circular economy** | Keep products and materials in use | Interface (carpets): recovers used carpet tiles, recycles materials, closed-loop remanufacturing | Michelin: recycled and renewable materials, retreads tyres |\n| **Cleaner production** | Prevent pollution at source rather than treat it at the end of the pipe | 3M: \"3P\" (Pollution Prevention Pays) programme to redesign products and processes | Asian Paints: water recycling, cleaner and energy-efficient technologies |\n| **Eco-design** | Design for low impact over the life cycle (energy, materials, disassembly) | Philips: energy-efficient, recyclable products, easy to dismantle at end-of-life | IKEA: modular furniture from renewable and recycled materials |\n| **Life-cycle thinking** | Consider impacts from cradle to grave (LCA) | Intel: uses LCA to reduce critical-material impacts | Tata Steel: applies LCA to improve resource efficiency and cut emissions |\n\n### 7. The seven manufacturing wastes (TIMWOOD) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nEach lean waste is also an environmental waste (\"Lean + Green\"):\n\n| Waste | Lean meaning | Environmental consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **T**ransport | Unnecessary movement of materials | Fuel, emissions, packaging, damage |\n| **I**nventory | Excess raw material, WIP, finished goods | Storage energy, obsolescence, spoilage, scrapping |\n| **M**otion | Unnecessary movement of people | Wasted energy, ergonomics and safety risks |\n| **W**aiting | Idle time for machines or people | Machines idling and consuming energy |\n| **O**ver-production | Producing more or earlier than needed | All the materials, energy and emissions of unneeded products |\n| **O**ver-processing | More work or precision than the customer needs | Extra energy, chemicals, materials |\n| **D**efects | Rework and scrap | Extra materials, energy, transport and landfill (\"quality failure is an environmental failure\", Lecture 10) |\n\nSome lists add an eighth waste: **S**kills (unused employee talent) → TIMWOODS.\n\n### 8. Sustainable manufacturing metrics 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Environmental | Social | Economic |\n|---|---|---|\n| Carbon intensity (tCO₂e per unit of revenue or production) | Employee health & safety: LTIFR | Productivity and operational efficiency: **OEE** |\n| Energy efficiency and renewable adoption: RE % | Diversity, equity & inclusion: gender-diversity ratio | Cost efficiency: cost per unit |\n| Water withdrawal and discharge: water footprint | Human rights and ethical practices: attrition rate | Resource efficiency: material yield %, scrap % |\n| Waste and circular economy: waste recycled % | Employee engagement and training: number of hours | Waste and loss reduction |\n| Biodiversity; SBTi alignment; GHG (Scope 1, 2, 3); climate risk | Customer data protection; supply-chain audits | Financial performance: operating margin %, ROI |\n\n---\n\n## Section B — Impact on productivity and organisational performance\n\n### 9. How sustainable manufacturing raises productivity 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nSix impacts on the slide: **lower defects, lower energy costs, reduced downtime, better equipment utilisation, better employee morale, higher customer trust.**\n\n### 10. The sustainable manufacturing productivity chain 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"Sustainable today, competitive tomorrow.\"*\n1. **Resource efficiency** — optimise the use of materials, energy, water and other resources.\n2. **Lower waste** — minimise waste, emissions and pollution at source.\n3. **Lower cost** — reduce consumption, disposal and rework costs across the value chain.\n4. **Higher productivity** — improve efficiency, reduce downtime and increase output.\n5. **Higher profitability** — better margins through efficiency, quality and innovation.\n6. **Competitive advantage** — stronger brand, happier customers and long-term success.\n*\"Efficiency drives profit. Sustainability secures the future.\"*\n\n### 11. Sustainable manufacturing KPIs 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Environmental | Social | Operational | Financial |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Energy intensity | Lost-time injury (LTIFR) | **OEE** | Manufacturing cost per unit |\n| GHG emissions | Forced-labour violations (%) | **First-pass yield (FPY)** | Energy cost per unit |\n| Water intensity | Training hours | Scrap / rejection rate | Cost savings % |\n| Waste generation rate | Attrition rate % | Manufacturing cycle time | ROI % |\n| Waste recycling rate | Employee satisfaction index | Overall productivity | Revenue from sustainable products |\n\nFormulas: OEE = Availability × Performance × Quality; FPY = good units first time ÷ units started; energy intensity = energy ÷ output. [Worked numbers](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md).\n\n### 12. The Toyota Way: Lean + JIT + Jidoka + Sustainability 📘 ⭐⭐\n- **JIT (just-in-time)**: produce only what is needed, when needed, in the quantity needed → less inventory, less over-production waste.\n- **Jidoka (automation with a human touch)**: stop the line at the first defect (andon) → defects are not passed on, so less rework and scrap.\n- **Heijunka** (levelled production), **kaizen**, **standard work**, **respect for people**.\n- Toyota's Bidadi plant (Karnataka) is the course's reference video for TPS in India; Toyota's Environmental Challenge 2050 extends TPS to zero CO₂ plants.\n\n### 13. Indian case studies (Lecture 7) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Company | Key attributes |\n|---|---|\n| **Tata Steel** | Circular economy (waste to energy); decarbonisation (alternate fuels, biochar trials); resource efficiency (high material utilisation) |\n| **Asian Paints** | Water stewardship (zero liquid discharge); renewable energy about **60% in FY25**; waste reduction (solvent recovery) |\n| **Dalmia Cement** | Low-carbon cement (**> 80%** of the mix is low carbon); waste as a resource (~40% recycled as input); renewable energy target **100% by 2030** |\n| **M&M Ltd.** | Renewable energy about **37% in FY25**; zero waste achieved in farm-equipment plants; smart manufacturing (digital twins, cobots, automation) |\n| **ITC Ltd.** | Triple-bottom-line integration of ESG; \"resource positive\" (carbon + water + solid waste); **50% RE** and sustainable packaging |\n| **Arvind Mills** | Water circularity (**100% recycled water**); recycled/regenerative fibres; cleaner processes (ozone washing, laser finishing) |\n\n### 14. Technology enablers — Industry 4.0 and the smart sustainable factory 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Industry 4.0 — \"smarter technologies, intelligent operations, sustainable future\"**\n1. **AI** — intelligent decisions, big-data analysis to optimise processes, quality and efficiency.\n2. **IoT** — connects machines, systems and sensors; real-time data; visibility and agile operations.\n3. **Robotics** — automate repetitive and hazardous tasks; precision, speed, consistency; safety.\n4. **Digital twins** — virtual replicas of assets and processes; simulate and optimise; reduce risk and speed innovation.\n5. **Predictive maintenance** — data and AI predict equipment failures; minimise downtime and cost; extend asset life.\n6. **Cloud manufacturing** — on-demand computing; collaboration across the value chain; scalable, cost-effective.\n\n**Smart sustainable factory — \"intelligent, connected, sustainable\"**: sensors (collect data from machines and environment) → real-time monitoring (operations, performance, energy use, emissions) → AI analytics (patterns, anomalies, improvement opportunities) → predictive maintenance → lower downtime → higher productivity.\n\n### 15. The way forward — future manufacturing: \"sustainable, intelligent, resilient\" 📘 ⭐⭐\n1. **Net-zero factory** — 100% renewable energy, energy efficiency and electrification, net-zero emissions across the value chain.\n2. **Circular economy** — design for durability; reuse, repair, remanufacture; zero waste to landfill.\n3. **Green hydrogen** — clean fuel for high-temperature processes; decarbonising hard-to-abate sectors; energy independence.\n4. **Industrial symbiosis** — collaboration across industries; by-product utilisation; shared resources (\"one industry's waste is another's resource\", e.g. Kalundborg, Denmark; fly ash from power plants into cement).\n5. **Carbon accounting** — measure, monitor and manage Scope 1, 2 and 3; data-driven climate action.\n6. **Digital Product Passport** — end-to-end product data transparency; traceability, compliance, circularity.\n7. **AI-driven manufacturing** — intelligent automation, real-time optimisation, predictive analytics.\n\n### 16. Key takeaways — Cleaner, Leaner, Smarter, Profitable, Resilient 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Cleaner**: carbon emissions, energy and water consumption, pollution and waste.\n- **Leaner**: process waste, defects, excess inventory, waiting time and unnecessary movement.\n- **Smarter**: AI, IoT, automation, digital twins and real-time data.\n- **Profitable**: reduces energy, material, waste, maintenance and operating costs.\n- **Resilient**: withstands resource shortages, energy-price volatility, supply disruptions, climate risks and changing regulations.\n*\"The factories that will dominate the future are not those that produce the most, but those that produce the smartest, cleanest and most efficiently.\"*\n\n---\n\n## The textile-factory exercise (Lecture 7) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Problem**: a textile factory has high energy use, high scrap and high water use. Suggest lean, green and digital solutions.\n\n| Problem | Lean solutions | Green solutions | Digital solutions |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **High energy use** | Eliminate idle running (waiting waste), SMED to cut changeover reheating, TPM to stop steam and air leaks | Rooftop solar and RE PPAs, heat recovery from dyeing effluent, efficient boilers on biomass, VFD motors | IoT energy meters per machine, AI to schedule energy-intensive batches in solar hours, digital twin of the boiler |\n| **High scrap** | Jidoka/poka-yoke at cutting, standard work, root-cause (5 Whys, fishbone), Six Sigma DMAIC on shade variation | Recycle offcuts into recycled yarn (Arvind's recycled fibres), design-to-reduce cut waste | AI vision inspection, CAD-based marker making to maximise fabric utilisation, SPC dashboards |\n| **High water use** | Right-first-time dyeing (fewer re-dyes), reduce over-processing (unnecessary washes) | Zero liquid discharge and 100% recycled water (Arvind), ozone washing and laser finishing (cut water and chemicals), rainwater harvesting | Smart water meters, leak detection, real-time effluent monitoring |\n\nKPIs to track: kWh per kg of fabric, scrap %, kL water per kg, FPY, OEE, % RE, % water recycled.\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Define sustainable manufacturing and give its pillars.**\nUNEP: making products with economically sound processes that minimise environmental impacts, conserve energy and natural resources and ensure employee, community and consumer safety. Pillars: environmental (carbon, water, RE), social (safety, ethical sourcing, community), economic (productivity, profitability, innovation).\n\n**Q2. How does sustainable manufacturing improve productivity?**\nThrough the productivity chain: resource efficiency → lower waste → lower cost → higher productivity → higher profitability → competitive advantage. Concretely: fewer defects, lower energy cost, less downtime, better equipment utilisation (OEE), better morale and higher customer trust.\n\n**Q3. Link lean and green.**\nAll seven lean wastes (TIMWOOD) consume materials and energy without adding value, so removing them cuts emissions and cost together; e.g. defects → rework energy and scrap, over-production → unneeded materials, transport → fuel.\n\n**Q4. Name four Industry 4.0 enablers and their sustainability benefit.**\nIoT (real-time energy and emissions monitoring), AI (optimise processes, reduce waste), digital twins (simulate before building, less trial-and-error), predictive maintenance (less downtime, longer asset life).\n"}, {"slug": "ch05-technology-quality-tools", "label": "Technology & Quality Tools", "tag": "05", "group": "chapters", "title": "Appropriate Technologies & Tools for Quality Improvement", "md": "# Appropriate Technologies & Tools for Quality Improvement\nSyllabus topic 5 📘. In the course this topic is taught in **Lecture 9** (\"Evaluating the trade-offs associated with technology adoption and sustainable supply-chain performance\") and **Lecture 10, Section B** (\"Quality management + sustainability\"). It is examined under **CO6** (low-weightage, 3-mark theory) and in the **20-mark online quiz** (CO5 + CO6, PPTs 9–11 only), so learn the lists and exact phrases. The scoring tools (TAS, Net Technology Value, the 2×2 matrix and the Navya exercise) are worked on [CO5 Problems 11–12](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md).\n\n**Lecture 9 learning objectives** 📘: (1) identify technologies that can improve SC sustainability; (2) distinguish technology benefits from sustainability outcomes; (3) identify economic, environmental and social trade-offs; (4) evaluate technology adoption using a multi-dimensional framework; (5) apply it to real SC decisions; (6) understand why the same technology can produce different sustainability outcomes in different supply chains.\n\n---\n\n## Part A — Technology and sustainability (Lecture 9)\n\n### 1. Technology ≠ sustainability 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*If a company installs AI, IoT, blockchain and robots in its supply chain, does that automatically make the supply chain sustainable?* **No.**\n\n| Technology **can reduce** | Technology **can increase** |\n|---|---|\n| Transport emissions | Energy consumption |\n| Inventory | Electronic waste |\n| Waste | Capital outlay |\n| Worker-safety incidents (and improve traceability) | Cybersecurity risks; the CO₂ footprint of digital infrastructure |\n\n**\"Technology adoption → operational performance → sustainability performance is *not* a guaranteed linear relationship.\"** 📘\n\n### 2. The fourth pillar: resilience 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nLecture 9 adds **Resilience** to the three pillars of sustainable network design:\n| Environmental (Planet) | Social (People) | Economic (Profits) | **Resilience** (4th pillar) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CO₂e, energy, water, pollution, circularity, consumption, waste | Safety, CSR, inclusion, diversity, human rights, data privacy | Cost, revenue, ROI, working capital, service level, productivity | **Visibility, agility, recovery, response, assurance** |\n\nLinked to the **Carter–Rogers (2008) framework**: SSCM sits at the intersection of environmental, social and economic performance, supported by four facets — **risk management, transparency, strategy and culture**. Short form on the slide: *environmentally friendly + socially ethical + economically viable.*\n\n### 3. The technology landscape — Supply Chain 4.0 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Pyramid, bottom to top (\"from digital foundations to intelligent, sustainable supply chains\")**:\n1. **ERP / digital integration** (foundation)\n2. **Automation / robotics / AGVs**\n3. **Cloud / platforms**\n4. **Blockchain / distributed ledger**\n5. **IoT / sensors / RFID**\n6. **Digital twins**\n7. **Advanced analytics / ML**\n8. **AI / GenAI** (top)\n\n**Industry 4.0 technologies applied to each SC process**:\n| Process | Technologies |\n|---|---|\n| Sourcing | AI, analytics, blockchain, supplier portals |\n| Production | IoT, robotics, digital twins, AI/ML, predictive maintenance |\n| Warehousing | IoT, automation, AGVs, analytics, WMS |\n| Transportation | GPS, IoT, route optimisation, real-time tracking |\n| Inventory | RFID, IoT, real-time visibility, inventory optimisation |\n| Demand planning | AI/ML, predictive analytics, forecasting tools |\n| Reverse logistics | IoT, tracking, return management, circular solutions |\n| Supplier management | Digital platforms, scorecards, collaboration, ESG monitoring |\nGoal: **smart, connected, transparent, sustainable, resilient.**\n\n### 4. From cost optimisation to sustainability optimisation 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| SC problem | Technology | Potential benefit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Excess inventory | AI forecasting | Less waste |\n| Empty trucks | AI route optimisation | Lower fuel / CO₂ |\n| Product recalls | Blockchain | Faster traceability |\n| Equipment failures | IoT + predictive maintenance | Less material / energy waste |\n| Warehouse labour | Robotics | Productivity / safety |\n| Food spoilage | IoT sensors | Less food waste |\n| Supplier ESG monitoring | Digital platforms | Better transparency |\n| Reverse logistics | RFID / IoT | Better recovery |\n| Circular economy | Digital product passports | Material traceability |\n| Scenario planning | Digital twins | Better network decisions |\n\n**NOTE: potential benefit ≠ realised benefit** 📘.\n\n### 5. The five hidden trade-offs 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| # | Trade-off | What goes wrong |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Efficiency vs environmental footprint** | Electricity consumption, equipment manufacture, e-waste, capital intensity |\n| 2 | **Traceability vs energy** | Blockchain architectures (especially proof-of-work) have a CO₂ footprint and high energy and resource needs |\n| 3 | **Visibility vs privacy** | Cybersecurity risks, surveillance concerns, data-ownership issues |\n| 4 | **Global optimisation vs local resilience** | A globally optimised, lean network is exposed to disruptions, geopolitical events, supplier failures, extreme weather |\n| 5 | **Digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion** | MNCs with advanced systems vs small suppliers who cannot afford them |\n\n**\"Technology can improve Tier-1 sustainability while worsening Tier-2 / Tier-3 inclusion.\"** 📘\n\n### 6. The technology–sustainability decision matrix 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n(The slide calls it a \"4 × 4 matrix\" but draws a 2 × 2 grid.)\n| | **Low business value** | **High business value** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **High sustainability impact** | **Reconsider** | **Prioritise** |\n| **Low sustainability impact** | **Avoid** | **Optimise** |\n\n### 7. Technology Adoption Score and Net Technology Value 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **TAS = 25% Economic + 25% Environmental + 20% Social + 15% Resilience + 15% Strategic fit**, each dimension scored **1–5**.\n- **Risk adjustment — Net Technology Value = Gross benefits − Adoption cost − Lifecycle sustainability cost − Risk cost.**\n- A 2026 study similarly lists **technology maturity, sustainability performance, emissions reduction and investment cost** among the key criteria for evaluating blockchain–IoT adoption.\n\n### 8. Case: Walmart–IBM blockchain (international) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Problem**: food-borne contamination needs rapid source identification; traditional traceability took *days*.\n- **Technology**: blockchain + IoT/data capture + QR-based identification; Walmart and IBM built a **Hyperledger Fabric**-based food-traceability system.\n- **Result**: the mango pilot cut provenance tracing from **7 days → 2.2 seconds**.\n- **Sustainability benefits**: economic (faster recalls, reduced damages, lower costs); environmental (less wastage); social (improved safety, consumer confidence); resilience (rapid contamination identification).\n- **Trade-offs**: integration cost, supplier capacity, data quantity and quality, interoperability, governance, scalability.\n\n### 9. Case: Asian Paints (Indian) 📘 ⭐⭐\nIntegrates **AI/ML, advanced analytics, IoT, digital twins, robotics and SC optimisation**. Technology helps reduce inventory, energy, water, operational inefficiency and manufacturing losses. Asian Paints reports its digital-manufacturing initiatives aim at **improving asset utilisation, reducing working capital, and reducing energy and water usage**.\n\n### 10. The Tech-Sustain framework — a 7-step guide 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Define the problem** — what SC problem are we solving?\n2. **Establish the baseline** — cost? CO₂? waste? service level? risk?\n3. **Identify technology alternatives** — AI / IoT / blockchain / automation / digital twin / platform.\n4. **Evaluate total lifecycle impact** — **capex + opex + energy + people + e-waste + cybersecurity.**\n5. **Evaluate the triple bottom line** — economic + environmental + social.\n6. **Stress-test unintended consequences** — ask *\"What could become worse because of this technology?\"*\n7. **Pilot → measure → scale** — do not jump directly from proof of concept to enterprise-wide implementation.\n*\"Smart technology. Sustainable impact. Measured value.\"* and **\"Don't digitise the problem — transform it sustainably.\"**\n\n### 11. Recommended case portfolio (Lecture 9) 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Case | Technology | Main trade-off |\n|---|---|---|\n| Walmart–IBM | Blockchain | Traceability vs cost/energy |\n| Thai tuna | AI + blockchain | Data value vs complexity |\n| Asian Paints | AI/ML + IoT + digital twin + robotics | Automation vs investment/energy |\n| Tata Motors | Digital supplier platforms + sustainability technologies | Decarbonisation vs supplier capability |\n| ITC e-Choupal | Digital platform | Technology sophistication vs inclusion |\n| Indian agri-food blockchain | Blockchain | Transparency vs adoption cost |\n\n### 12. Key takeaways (Lecture 9) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- Technology is an **enabler, not a sustainability strategy.**\n- **Optimising one KPI can damage another.**\n- Measure the **full technology life cycle**: manufacture → implementation → operation → maintenance → disposal.\n- Digital supply chains require **digital ecosystems** (suppliers must come along).\n- **The best technology is not the most advanced technology.**\n\n### 13. The Navya exercise (summary) 📘\nNavya Consumer Products (5 plants, 20 warehouses, 1,500 suppliers, 8,000 distributors, 50,000 outlets) considers PROJECT SUSTAIN_X (AI + IoT + blockchain): ₹80 crore capex, ₹12 crore a year opex; expected inventory −15%, transport −8%, waste −20%, forecast accuracy +15%. Recommended answer: **pilot**, because TAS is about 3.8 and NPV is positive only if about 87% or more of the benefits materialise; supplier inclusion is the main social risk. Full working: [CO5 Problem 11](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md).\n\n---\n\n## Part B — Quality management as a sustainability tool (Lecture 10, Section B)\n\n### 14. Why quality is a sustainability issue 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**\"Poor quality creates a costly chain\"**: defects → rework → extra materials + energy → extra transportation → extra waste → **higher carbon footprint**. *Quality at the source prevents waste, saves resources and reduces carbon.*\n**\"Quality failure is simultaneously an economic and environmental failure.\"** This connects **TQM + Lean + Six Sigma + Green Supply Chain.** ([Numerical](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md))\n\n### 15. The seven ISO quality-management principles and their sustainability link 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nISO identifies these as the foundation of ISO 9000/9001:\n1. **Customer focus**\n2. **Leadership**\n3. **Engagement of people**\n4. **Process approach** → *life-cycle thinking*\n5. **Improvement** → *carbon and waste reduction*\n6. **Evidence-based decision making** → *ESG data*\n7. **Relationship management** → *sustainable suppliers*\n*\"Quality today. Sustainability tomorrow.\"*\n\n### 16. Lean + Green + Quality: TIMWOOD 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nThe seven lean wastes: **T**ransportation, **I**nventory, **M**otion, **W**aiting, **O**verproduction, **O**verprocessing, **D**efects. Sustainability view: **each waste potentially creates cost + energy + material + carbon + time.** Reducing defects gives less rework → less material → less energy → less waste → **less CO₂**.\n\n### 17. Quality tools that serve sustainability ⭐⭐\nThe course names Six Sigma, TQM, Kaizen, Lean and ISO systems; standard tools you can cite:\n- **PDCA (Deming cycle)** — the logic of ISO 9001, 14001 and 45001 continual improvement.\n- **DMAIC (Six Sigma)** — Define, Measure, Analyse, Improve, Control; used for scrap, energy and yield projects.\n- **Seven QC tools** — check sheet, Pareto chart (which 20% of sources cause 80% of waste or emissions), cause-and-effect (fishbone), histogram, scatter diagram, control chart (SPC on energy per unit), stratification/flow chart.\n- **Poka-yoke** (mistake-proofing), **5S** (sort, set in order, shine, standardise, sustain; often extended with Safety), **TPM** (total productive maintenance; cuts breakdowns, leaks and energy waste), **value-stream mapping** (now \"environmental VSM\" with energy and water per step), **FMEA** (failure modes and effects, also for environmental aspects), **SMED** (quick changeover).\n- **Cost of quality** (prevention, appraisal, internal failure, external failure) extended to a \"carbon cost of poor quality\".\n\n### 18. Green quality management: ISO 14001 and the integrated management system 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **ISO 14001** provides a framework for an **Environmental Management System (EMS)** and continual improvement of environmental performance (Plan–Do–Check–Act: environmental policy, aspects and impacts, legal compliance, objectives, operational control, monitoring, internal audit, management review).\n- **The 2026 edition** emphasises environmental protection, business outcomes and integration with environmental priorities 📘.\n- **Integrated Management System (IMS)**: **ISO 9001 (quality) + ISO 14001 (environment) + ISO 45001 (occupational health & safety)**, all built on the common high-level structure (Annex SL), so one set of audits and reviews covers all three.\n- *\"ESG reporting: disclosure = integrated sustainable operations.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers (CO6 3-markers) ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. \"Technology does not automatically make a supply chain sustainable.\" Explain.**\nTechnology can cut transport emissions, inventory and waste, but it also adds energy use, e-waste, capital outlay, cyber risk and a digital-infrastructure footprint. The chain technology → operational performance → sustainability is not linear; benefits must be measured over the full technology life cycle, and potential benefit ≠ realised benefit.\n\n**Q2. List the five hidden trade-offs of technology adoption.**\nEfficiency vs environmental footprint; traceability vs energy (blockchain); visibility vs privacy; global optimisation vs local resilience; digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion (Tier-1 gains, Tier-2/3 excluded).\n\n**Q3. State the TAS formula and Net Technology Value.**\nTAS = 0.25 Economic + 0.25 Environmental + 0.20 Social + 0.15 Resilience + 0.15 Strategic fit (each 1–5). NTV = Gross benefits − Adoption cost − Lifecycle sustainability cost − Risk cost.\n\n**Q4. Why is quality a sustainability issue?**\nDefects cause rework, extra materials and energy, extra transport and waste, so they raise carbon. Quality at source prevents waste, saves resources and cuts CO₂: quality failure is both an economic and an environmental failure (TQM + Lean + Six Sigma + green SCM).\n\n**Q5. Explain the Tech-Sustain framework.**\nDefine the problem → establish a baseline (cost, CO₂, waste, service, risk) → identify alternatives → evaluate total lifecycle impact (capex, opex, energy, people, e-waste, cyber) → evaluate TBL → stress-test unintended consequences → pilot, measure, scale.\n"}, {"slug": "ch06-sustainable-logistics", "label": "Sustainable Logistics", "tag": "06", "group": "chapters", "title": "Sustainable Logistics & Its Impact on Business Performance", "md": "# Sustainable Logistics & Its Impact on Business Performance\nSyllabus topic 6 📘 (Lecture 8 Sections B and C: green logistics and reverse logistics; Lecture 10 FMCG distribution KPIs; Lecture 11 Strategy 4; Hindustan Zinc, Flipkart and DHL cases). It is the theory behind **CO4 (logistics cost and distance criteria)**, a **high-weightage** CO. Numericals: [Network & Logistics Numericals](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md). Network design itself is in [Designing the Sustainable Network](ch07-network-design.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Green logistics ⭐⭐⭐\n**Definition (Lecture 8)** 📘: *Green logistics means designing logistics activities to reduce the environmental impact while maintaining service and economic performance.*\n\nLogistics covers transport, warehousing, inventory, packaging, order processing and reverse flows. Freight transport is one of the hardest sectors to decarbonise: road dominates Indian freight (the IISc paper notes road accounts for about 87% of transport energy; transport is about 22% of India's energy use). Logistics also drives cost: in India logistics cost has been estimated at about 8–14% of GDP (the government's revised estimate is about 7.8–8.9%), against about 8% in developed economies, so greener logistics is usually also cheaper logistics.\n\n**Lecture 11 Strategy 4 — logistics decarbonisation** 📘: *\"The cleanest kilometre is the kilometre eliminated.\"* Order of levers: avoid (fewer km through network redesign and consolidation) → shift (to rail or water) → improve (efficient vehicles, load factor, driving) → switch fuel (CNG/LNG, EV, hydrogen, biofuels).\n\n### The impact of green logistics on business performance ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Cost**: fuel is 30–40% of road-freight cost, so fewer km, higher load factor and better fuel efficiency cut cost directly.\n- **Service**: route optimisation and consolidation can keep or improve OTIF when planned well.\n- **Risk and compliance**: lower exposure to fuel-price volatility, carbon pricing, city low-emission zones and customer Scope 3 audits.\n- **Revenue and brand**: customers with Scope 3 targets (Walmart, Apple, Unilever) prefer low-carbon logistics partners.\n- **Asset productivity**: better vehicle utilisation and fewer empty km raise revenue per truck.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The green transportation network ⭐⭐⭐\n**Principle** 📘: *use the lowest-impact transport mode compatible with service requirements.*\n\n**Typical hierarchy (emission intensity per tonne-km)**: **AIR > ROAD > RAIL > WATER.** *\"Emissions intensity increases towards air freight; actual performance depends on distance, load factor, vehicle and operating conditions.\"* 📘\n\n| Mode | Typical g CO₂e per tonne-km (indicative) | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Air | 500–800 | Speed, high-value and perishable goods | Highest emissions and cost |\n| Road (truck) | 60–150 | Door-to-door, flexible, fast for short haul | High emissions per t-km, congestion, safety |\n| Rail | 15–30 | Bulk, long haul, low emissions (India's Dedicated Freight Corridors) | Fixed routes, first/last mile by road |\n| Inland/coastal water | 10–30 | Very low cost and emissions for bulk | Slow, limited routes |\n| Deep-sea container | 5–20 | Lowest per t-km for long haul | Slow; bunker-fuel emissions; port congestion |\n\n### Vehicle and route decisions — the six network decisions 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Decision | Example from the slide |\n|---|---|\n| 1. **Which vehicle?** | Use a 7-tonne truck for regional deliveries |\n| 2. **Which route?** | Choose route A (shorter distance and less traffic) |\n| 3. **Which depot?** | Ship from the Delhi depot (not Mumbai or Bangalore) |\n| 4. **Which delivery frequency?** | Deliver 3 times a week (Mon, Wed, Fri) |\n| 5. **Which shipment size?** | Ship in 10-pallet batches |\n| 6. **Which customers should be consolidated?** | Consolidate customers A, B and C into one delivery |\n\nSupporting techniques: vehicle-routing optimisation (VRP), milk runs, backhauling to cut empty return km, load planning (weight and cube), eco-driving training, telematics, speed governance, tyre pressure, aerodynamic kits, and alternative fuels (CNG/LNG, EV, hydrogen, biodiesel, ethanol).\n\n### The FMCG distribution example (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n10,000 deliveries a month, average distance 180 km, diesel trucks, 62% average vehicle utilisation, 18% empty return km. **Five KPIs**: CO₂e per tonne-km, fuel efficiency (km per litre), vehicle utilisation %, empty km % and OTIF %. *\"This is where sustainability becomes a management decision rather than a reporting exercise.\"* ([Computed baseline and improvement](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md); [SMART targets](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md))\n\n### Indian logistics decarbonisation cases ⭐⭐\n- **Hindustan Zinc + GreenLine Mobility (2025)** 📘: LNG and EV trucks for mineral logistics (₹400 crore deal); reduction-based case with trade-offs between cost, emissions, infrastructure and scalability.\n- **Flipkart (2022)** 📘: recyclable packaging, last-mile EVs, route optimisation (Flipkart is part of the Climate Group's EV100).\n- **Mahindra** 📘: green logistics as part of the carbon-neutrality goal; Mahindra Logistics' green buildings.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Green warehousing ⭐⭐⭐\n**KPI: energy consumption per unit handled** 📘.\n\n| Initiative | Prominent example (slide) |\n|---|---|\n| Rooftop solar | Myntra, India |\n| LED lighting | IKEA, Sweden |\n| Smart HVAC | Prologis, Spain |\n| Natural lighting | Walmart, USA |\n| Automated material handling | Amazon, USA |\n| Energy monitoring | DHL, Germany |\n| Rainwater harvesting | CEAT Tyres, India |\n| EV charging | Flipkart, India |\n| Green building design | Mahindra Logistics, India |\n\nAlso: IGBC/LEED/GRIHA green-building certification, insulation and cool roofs, motion-sensor lighting, electric forklifts, waste segregation and recycling at DCs, and network-level choices (fewer, better-located DCs; cross-docking to avoid storage).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Green packaging ⭐⭐⭐\n| Traditional packaging | Sustainable packaging |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Large box | 1. **Right-sized** packaging |\n| 2. Plastic protection | 2. **Reusable** packaging |\n| 3. Pallet | 3. **Recycled** material |\n| 4. Stretch film | 4. **Returnable transport packaging** (crates, collapsible bins) |\n\n**Network implication** 📘: *\"Packaging is not simply a procurement decision. It affects **cube → vehicle utilisation → number of trips → fuel → CO₂**.\"* Right-sizing removes shipped air, so more units fit per truck and fewer trips are needed ([numerical](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md)). In India, **EPR for plastic packaging** (Plastic Waste Management Rules) makes brand owners responsible for collecting and recycling their packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Case: DHL green logistics — six levers 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"Delivering today, preserving tomorrow.\"*\n1. **Electric delivery vehicles** — EVs for last-mile deliveries to reduce emissions and air pollution.\n2. **Alternative fuels** — bio-CNG, HVO (hydrotreated vegetable oil) and other cleaner fuels.\n3. **Multimodal transport** — combining road, rail, air and sea to optimise routes, reduce transit time and lower emissions.\n4. **Sustainable warehousing** — energy-efficient buildings, renewable energy, smart systems, responsible resource management.\n5. **Shipment consolidation** — maximise load utilisation, reduce trips and lower emissions.\n6. **Packaging optimisation** — right-sized, recyclable and lightweight packaging.\n*\"Smarter logistics. Lower emissions. Greener future.\"* (DHL's Strategy 2030 targets net-zero logistics emissions by 2050 and more than 66% e-vehicles for last-mile pick-up and delivery by 2030.)\n\n---\n\n## 6. Reverse logistics ⭐⭐⭐\n**Definition (Lecture 8)** 📘: *Reverse logistics manages the movement of products/materials from the customer back toward the supply chain.* It is an important part of the sustainable supply-chain process. (Rogers & Tibben-Lembke: planning, implementing and controlling the efficient flow of materials and information from the point of consumption to the point of origin to recapture value or dispose properly.)\n\n**Why products come back**: commercial returns (e-commerce, wrong size), warranty and repair returns, end-of-use (leasing, trade-ins), end-of-life (scrap vehicles, batteries, e-waste), packaging returns, recalls.\n\n### Reverse logistics network: touch points and destinations 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nTouch points: **customer (used products) → collection point → regional consolidation centre → inspection / sorting.**\n\n| Condition after inspection | Destination |\n|---|---|\n| Good | **Resale** |\n| Repairable | **Repair** |\n| Recoverable | **Remanufacture** |\n| Material recovery only | **Recycling** |\n| No recovery value | **Responsible disposal** |\n\n**Principle: \"Preserve the highest possible value for the longest possible time.\"** 📘\n\n### Product recovery hierarchy (value retention, highest first) ⭐⭐⭐\nReuse/resale → repair → refurbish → remanufacture → recycle (material recovery) → energy recovery → disposal (landfill). The higher the option, the more of the original materials, energy and labour embedded in the product is preserved.\n\n### Why reverse logistics is difficult 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Forward logistics | Reverse logistics |\n|---|---|\n| Supplier → customer | Customer → supply chain |\n| Predictable quantity | **Uncertain quantity** |\n| Predictable destination | **Uncertain timing** |\n| Known product condition | **Uncertain condition** |\n\n**Seven challenges with examples (Lecture 8)** 📘:\n| Challenge | Example 1 | Example 2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Product-condition uncertainty** | Returned smartphone: unused, lightly used, cracked or water-damaged | Returned component: repair, remanufacture or totally damaged |\n| **Low return density** | Rural customers return a few units a month, so collection is unviable | Retailer collects only a few used batteries (geographic issues) |\n| **Collection costs** | E-commerce returns: hundreds of items in small trips raise cost | End-of-life vehicles in remote areas: high handling and transport cost |\n| **Sorting costs** | Electronic components must be separated into reuse / repair / recycle | Used packaging must be segregated by material, size, contamination |\n| **Information gaps** | Returned machines: history of age, usage, failure mode unavailable | Recycling batteries: inaccurate data on age, chemistry, state of charge |\n| **Lack of standardisation** | EV batteries: different manufacturers, designs, chemistries | Packaging: different suppliers, materials, dimensions |\n| **Consumer participation** | Customers keep old phones instead of returning them | E-waste thrown into household waste |\n\n### Designing collection networks: three configurations 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Configuration | Flow | When it fits |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Decentralised** | Customer → local collection centre → regional facility | High return volumes, bulky or low-value items, need for quick local sorting |\n| **B. Centralised** | Customer → collection → one central recovery facility | Low volumes, high-value items, specialised recovery technology, economies of scale |\n| **C. Hybrid** | Customer → local centre → regional hub → specialised recovery facility | Large countries like India with mixed density; most common for e-waste and batteries |\n\n**Decision factors** 📘: **volume + distance + product value + recovery technology + cost.**\n\n### Forward + reverse = closed-loop supply chain 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Forward (product flow)**: supplier → factory → DC → customer.\n- **Reverse (product-return flow)**: customer → collection → recovery → factory / secondary market.\n*\"This is the basic architecture of a closed-loop supply chain.\"*\n\n### Business value of reverse logistics ⭐⭐\nRecovered value (resale, parts, materials), lower virgin-material cost, EPR compliance (batteries, e-waste, plastic packaging, end-of-life vehicles), customer loyalty (easy returns, trade-in), data on product failures for design improvement, and lower Scope 3 (purchased goods, end-of-life). EV-battery recyclers such as **Attero** and **Lohum**, and Tata Autocomp's battery work, show India building this capability under the **Battery Waste Management Rules 2022** 📘.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Logistics KPIs (Lecture 8 dashboard + Lecture 10) ⭐⭐⭐\n| Environmental | Service | Circularity | Economic |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| kg CO₂e per unit | Order fill rate % | Return rate | Total logistics cost |\n| Transport emissions (g CO₂e per t-km) | Delivery time | Recovery rate | Cost per unit |\n| Energy per unit (warehouse) | Return turnaround time | Reuse rate | Inventory cost |\n| Waste to landfill | OTIF % | Remanufacture rate | Facility utilisation |\n| Renewable energy % | | Recycled content %; material circularity % | Vehicle utilisation, empty km % |\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Define green logistics and give four practices.**\nDesigning logistics activities to reduce environmental impact while maintaining service and economic performance. Practices: modal shift to rail/water, route optimisation and consolidation, EV/alternative-fuel fleets, green warehousing (solar, LED), right-sized returnable packaging.\n\n**Q2. Why is reverse logistics harder than forward logistics?**\nUncertain quantity, timing and condition; low return density; high collection and sorting cost; information gaps on product history; non-standard products; dependence on consumer participation.\n\n**Q3. Explain how packaging affects transport emissions.**\nPackaging determines cube; cube determines vehicle utilisation; utilisation determines the number of trips, and trips determine fuel and CO₂. Right-sizing and returnable packaging therefore cut emissions and cost together.\n\n**Q4. Compare centralised and decentralised collection networks.**\nCentralised sends all returns to one recovery facility (scale, specialised technology, suits low-volume high-value items, but longer transport). Decentralised sorts returns locally before regional facilities (shorter collection trips, suits high volumes and bulky items, but more facilities). Hybrid combines local centres, regional hubs and specialised recovery plants.\n"}, {"slug": "ch07-network-design", "label": "Designing the Sustainable Network", "tag": "07", "group": "chapters", "title": "Designing a Sustainable Supply Chain Network", "md": "# Designing a Sustainable Supply Chain Network\nSyllabus topic 7 📘 (Lecture 8: \"Designing a sustainable supply chain network model incorporating green logistics, reverse logistics and circular-economy principles\"). It is the theory behind **CO4 (SSCM distribution networks)**, a **high-weightage** CO. The network numericals (network A/B/C with λ break-evens, the 10,000-returns allocation, the consumer-electronics exercise, centre of gravity, load-distance, factor rating, number of DCs) are on [Network & Logistics Numericals](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md).\n\n**Learning objectives (Lecture 8)** 📘: (1) explain sustainable SC network design; (2) distinguish forward, reverse and closed-loop logistics; (3) integrate green logistics into network decisions; (4) apply circular-economy principles to network design; (5) identify appropriate facilities and material flows; (6) formulate a basic sustainable network-optimisation model; (7) evaluate economic, environmental and social trade-offs; (8) analyse real Indian and international cases.\n\n---\n\n## Section A — Why network design must change\n\n### 1. The traditional network ignores half the picture 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nThe traditional chain (supplier → manufacturer → warehouse → distributor → retailer → customer) often ignores **CO₂ emissions, resource depletion, packaging waste, product returns, end-of-life products, recycling opportunities and social impacts**. **Key message: \"The supply chain does not end at the customer.\"**\n\nThe sustainable network adds a **reverse flow**: customer → collection centre → inspection and sorting → repair / reuse / remanufacture / recycle → back to the manufacturer or a secondary market. *The sustainable supply chain moves from linear to closed loop.*\n\n### 2. Why network design matters 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Strategic network decisions determine**: number of factories, warehouse locations, distribution centres, collection centres, recycling facilities, remanufacturing plants, transport modes, supplier locations and customer allocation.\n- **A poor network creates**: longer distances → higher fuel consumption → higher CO₂ emissions → higher cost.\n- **A circular network creates**: recovery → reuse → lower virgin-material demand → lower waste → lower environmental impact (\"circularity creates a positive impact at every step\").\n\n**Network objective: Cost + Carbon + Resources + Resilience + Social value.** 📘\n\nBecause most cost and emissions are locked in when facilities and flows are chosen (a DC built in the wrong place adds truck-km every day for 20 years), network design is the most leveraged sustainability decision in logistics.\n\n### 3. The three pillars of sustainable network design 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Environmental (Planet) | Social (People) | Economic (Profits) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Which network produces the lowest environmental impact? | Which network creates acceptable social value? | Which network is of lowest cost? |\n\n*The network must be environmentally friendly + socially ethical + economically viable.* (Lecture 9 adds resilience as the fourth pillar.)\n\n### 4. From cost optimisation to sustainability optimisation 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Traditional | Sustainable |\n|---|---|\n| **Minimise** transportation cost + facility cost + inventory cost + procurement cost = **minimise total supply-chain cost** | **Minimise** cost + λ₁ CO₂ + λ₂ waste + λ₃ resource consumption |\n| | **While maintaining**: customer service (desired service levels), capacity (operate within available capacity), demand satisfaction (meet demand on time), regulatory compliance (laws and standards), recovery targets (return and recovery goals) |\n\nThe λ weights convert CO₂, waste and resources into the same units as cost (for example λ₁ = a carbon price in ₹ per tonne).\n\n---\n\n## Section D — Circular economy in the network\n\n### 5. Linear vs circular 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"Circularity seeks to retain products, components and materials in productive use and reduce waste.\"* UNEP emphasises **redesign, reuse, repair, remanufacturing and recycling** as the key circularity mechanisms. SCM is \"cradle to grave\"; SSCM is \"cradle to cradle\". (The Ellen MacArthur Foundation's three principles: eliminate waste and pollution, circulate products and materials at their highest value, regenerate nature.)\n\n### 6. Material loops — biological and technical cycles 📘 ⭐⭐\n- **Biological cycle**: biological resources → organic material → use → compost / regeneration → back to biological resources (food, cotton, wood, bio-packaging).\n- **Technical cycle**: product → use → repair → reuse → remanufacture → recycle → new product (metals, plastics, electronics).\n\n### 7. The R-strategy hierarchy (highest value first) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| # | Strategy | Meaning | Slide example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Refuse** | Don't accept products you don't need | Refuse plastic straws |\n| 2 | **Reduce** | Use fewer resources and less material | Buy products with minimal packaging |\n| 3 | **Reuse** | Use items again for the same purpose | Reusable water bottle |\n| 4 | **Repair** | Fix and extend the life of products | Repair a torn backpack |\n| 5 | **Refurbish** | Restore and update to good working condition | Refurbish an old sofa |\n| 6 | **Remanufacture** | Rebuild to like-new condition with warranty | Remanufactured car alternator |\n| 7 | **Repurpose** | Use items for a different purpose | Old boots turned into planters |\n| 8 | **Recycle** | Process materials to create new products | Recycle paper into new paper |\n\n**Each R-strategy requires different elements** 📘: facilities, transportation, skills, technology, inventory and information systems. **Key principle: \"Recycling is usually lower in the value-retention hierarchy than reuse, repair and remanufacturing.\"**\n\n### 8. Circular design changes the supply chain 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Products designed for**:\n1. **Disassembly** → easier recovery.\n2. **Modularity** → easier repair.\n3. **Durability** → longer life.\n4. **Standardised components** → easier remanufacturing.\n5. **Material identification** → easier recycling.\n*\"Sustainable network design begins at product design, not at the warehouse.\"*\n\n### 9. Product as a Service (PaaS) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Category | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Equipment leasing | CNH: harvester machine rental |\n| Industrial machinery as a service | SDLG: leasing construction equipment |\n| Battery-as-a-service (BaaS) | JSW MG Motor: Windsor EV (battery rented per km) |\n| Lighting-as-a-service | Philips / Signify solutions (pay for light, not bulbs) |\n| Furniture leasing | Furlenco: subscribe to furniture |\n\n**The manufacturer retains economic incentive + physical control + material value**, so it has a reason to design durable, repairable products and to take them back.\n\n### 10. Circular economy requires a different network — it becomes multidirectional 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nTraditional network: supplier → factory → DC → customer. Circular network: the same forward chain **plus** customer → collection → sorting → reuse / repair / remanufacture / recycle, feeding back into the factory, the DC or secondary markets.\n\n---\n\n## Section E — The sustainable network model\n\n### 11. The closed-loop sustainable network model and its nine nodes 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Node | Role |\n|---|---|\n| 1. **Suppliers** | Provide raw materials, components and parts; ensure quality and timely supply |\n| 2. **Manufacturing plants** | Convert inputs into finished goods; ensure quality, efficiency and compliance |\n| 3. **Distribution centres** | Store finished goods; manage inventory and outbound shipments |\n| 4. **Customers** | Purchase and use products; provide feedback and return products |\n| 5. **Collection centres** | Collect end-of-life or returned products; consolidate and transport |\n| 6. **Inspection / sorting centres** | Inspect returned items; sort by condition, type and material |\n| 7. **Repair / remanufacturing / recycling** | Repair usable items; remanufacture to like-new; recycle what can't be reused |\n| 8. **Recovered materials** | Recover metals, plastics and other materials; ensure quality and purity |\n| 9. **Manufacturing (again)** | Use recovered materials as inputs; close the loop and reduce virgin resource use |\n*\"This is a closed-loop sustainable supply chain network.\"*\n\n### 12. Network flows and decision levels 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Critical insight: a circular supply chain requires physical + information + financial integration.**\n\n| Level | Decisions | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Strategic** | Location of factories, DCs, collection centres and recycling facilities; capacity | *Strategic sets the network* |\n| **Tactical** | Which customers are served by which DC; which returns go to which recovery facility; which transport mode | *Tactical connects the network* |\n| **Operational** | Routing, collection frequency, shipment consolidation, inventory allocation | *Operational makes it perform* |\n\n### 13. Objective function — a simplified sustainable network 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Minimise: Total cost + Environmental cost + Social cost**\n- **Total cost** = facility cost + production cost + transportation cost + inventory cost + collection cost + recovery cost + disposal cost.\n- **Environmental** = CO₂ emissions + energy consumption + waste + resource consumption.\n- Example: Network A ₹100, CO₂ 100, recovery 20%; Network B ₹108, 75, 50%; Network C ₹115, 55, 70%. *\"The model searches for a Pareto-efficient solution.\"* Which is best? *\"There is no single answer without defining decision-maker priorities.\"* (Worked: A is best below λ = 0.32, B between 0.32 and 0.35, C above 0.35. See [P1](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md).)\n\n### 14. Basic mathematical model 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Decision variables**: xᵢⱼ = quantity transported from node i to node j; yⱼ = 1 if facility j is opened (binary); rᵢ = quantity recovered from returned products.\n- **Objective**: **Minimise Z = C_total + λ·E_CO₂ + μ·W_waste.**\n- **Subject to**: demand satisfaction; facility capacity; material balance; transport capacity; recovery capacity; collection constraints; recycling targets; non-negative flow.\n- **At every recovery facility: Returns = Reuse + Repair + Remanufacture + Recycling + Disposal.**\n- Example: 10,000 returned units → 2,000 reuse, 2,500 repair, 2,000 remanufacture, 3,000 material recycling, 500 disposal. *\"Where should each stream go to minimise cost and environmental impact?\"* ([worked](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md))\n\nThis is a **mixed-integer linear programme (MILP)**: binary yⱼ for open/close decisions and continuous xᵢⱼ and rᵢ for flows. Multi-objective versions are solved by weighting (as above), by ε-constraint (minimise cost subject to CO₂ ≤ cap) or by tracing the Pareto frontier.\n\n### 15. The integrated sustainable network — four questions 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Where should facilities be located?**\n2. **Which flows should move between facilities?**\n3. **Which transportation mode should be used?**\n4. **What should happen to returned products?**\nThese four decisions determine much of the network's **cost + carbon + circularity**. **Key message: green logistics overlays the entire network.**\n\n### 16. Facility-location methods you can apply (distance criteria) ⭐⭐⭐\nThe syllabus's \"distance criteria\" are answered with:\n- **Load-distance (tonne-km) method**: choose the candidate site that minimises Σ (load × distance). Tonne-km is a direct proxy for transport cost and CO₂.\n- **Centre of gravity**: Cx = Σ xᵢWᵢ ÷ ΣWᵢ, Cy = Σ yᵢWᵢ ÷ ΣWᵢ; gives a starting point, then test nearby feasible sites.\n- **Factor rating**: weighted scoring of qualitative and quantitative factors (land cost, labour, RE availability, rail access, flood risk, community impact).\n- **Break-even (locational cost-volume) analysis**: fixed + variable cost lines per site.\n- **Number of DCs trade-off**: more DCs → shorter outbound km (lower transport cost and CO₂) but higher facility and inventory cost (square-root law of inventory). The cost-optimal and carbon-optimal number of DCs often differ ([P7](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md)).\n- **MILP network models** (as above) for real decisions.\n\n### 17. Sustainable network KPI dashboard 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Environmental | Circularity | Economic | Service |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| kg CO₂e per unit | Return rate | Total logistics cost | Order fill rate % |\n| Transport emissions | Recovery rate | Cost per unit | Delivery time |\n| Energy per unit | Reuse rate | Inventory cost | Return turnaround time |\n| Waste to landfill | Remanufacture rate | Facility utilisation | |\n| Renewable energy % | Recycled content %; material circularity % | | |\n\n**Final design objective** 📘: *\"Create the lowest-cost network that delivers the required service while minimising environmental impact and maximising resource circularity.\"*\n\n**The final framework** 📘: *\"The sustainable supply chain is not a chain. It is a network of forward and reverse value loops.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 18. Case studies 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**International**\n- **IKEA (Sweden) — circular business model**: *\"Why they WANT you to return your old furniture.\"* IKEA's circular agenda extends product life through **repair, buy-back, resale and recycling** (Buy Back & Resell, spare parts, as-is sections), with a goal to be a circular business by 2030.\n- **Renault Refactory (Flins, France)**: a facility dedicated to the circular economy of mobility. Activities: **vehicle refurbishment, battery repair, parts remanufacturing, second-life applications, material recycling and industrial retrofitting.**\n\n**Indian**\n- **Mahindra CERO** (JV of Mahindra and MSTC Ltd., Government of India): India's first authorised **vehicle-recycling** facilities (end-of-life vehicles dismantled, materials recovered), relevant under India's Vehicle Scrappage Policy.\n- **Tata Motors Prolife**: **remanufacturing** as part of a circular network (used aggregates such as engines and gearboxes are reconditioned and sold with warranty). *\"An excellent example of remanufacturing rather than recycling by using used parts.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 19. The consumer-electronics network exercise (Lecture 8) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nA consumer-electronics company has 3 suppliers, 2 plants, 4 regional DCs, 10,000 customers and 20% expected returns. It is considering one centralised recycling centre, two regional collection centres and one remanufacturing facility.\n\n| Decision | Option A | Option B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Collection | Centralised | Regional |\n| Transport | Road | Rail + road |\n| Recovery | Recycle | Remanufacture |\n| Warehouse | Conventional | Solar |\n| Packaging | Single-use | Returnable |\n\n*Evaluate cost, CO₂, recovery and service. Question: should the company select the cheapest network?* **Answer: no** — Option B usually costs more in facilities but recovers far more value (remanufactured units sell at a premium over scrap), cuts CO₂ through rail and solar, and meets EPR targets; the best sustainable network is not necessarily the cheapest. Full numbers: [P10](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md).\n\n## 20. Key takeaways (Lecture 8) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- Green logistics reduces the environmental impact of movement.\n- Reverse logistics brings products and materials back.\n- The circular economy retains value and resources.\n- Network design determines where these activities occur.\n- Optimisation must balance **cost, carbon, circularity and service**.\n- **The best sustainable network is not necessarily the cheapest network.**\n- **Design the forward and reverse network together.**\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Write the objective function of a sustainable network model and explain its terms.**\nMinimise Z = C_total + λ·E_CO₂ + μ·W_waste, where C_total = facility + production + transport + inventory + collection + recovery + disposal cost, E_CO₂ is total emissions priced at λ, and W_waste is waste priced at μ. Decision variables xᵢⱼ (flows), yⱼ (open facility) and rᵢ (recovered quantity); constraints on demand, capacity, material balance, recovery and recycling targets.\n\n**Q2. Distinguish strategic, tactical and operational network decisions.**\nStrategic: where and how big facilities are (sets the network). Tactical: allocation of customers to DCs, returns to recovery sites, choice of modes (connects it). Operational: routing, collection frequency, consolidation, inventory allocation (makes it perform).\n\n**Q3. Why does circularity need a different network?**\nBecause flows become multidirectional: collection, inspection and sorting, repair, remanufacturing and recycling nodes must be added, each needing different facilities, transport, skills, technology, inventory and information systems, and the network must be designed together with the product (disassembly, modularity, standard parts).\n"}, {"slug": "ch08-performance-measurement", "label": "Measuring SSCM Performance", "tag": "08", "group": "chapters", "title": "Measuring Sustainable Supply Chain Performance", "md": "# Measuring Sustainable Supply Chain Performance\nSyllabus topic 8 📘 (Lecture 3 \"SSCM & performance monitoring\" and Lecture 10 \"SSCM performance measurement systems, QMS, reporting approaches and sectoral challenges\"). It is the theory behind **CO5 (effective performance measurement systems)**: medium weightage in the end-sem paper and half of the **20-mark online quiz**, which is drawn from PPTs 9, 10 and 11. Numericals: [Performance Measurement Numericals](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md). Quality management (Lecture 10, Section B) is covered in [Technology & Quality Tools](ch05-technology-quality-tools.md).\n\n**Lecture 10 learning objectives** 📘: (1) explain the dimensions of sustainable SC performance; (2) develop a sustainability performance measurement system; (3) select appropriate environmental, social, economic and operational KPIs; (4) explain the relationship between quality management, lean and sustainability; (5) distinguish major sustainability-reporting approaches; (6) interpret BRSR, GRI and ISSB/IFRS S1–S2 at a managerial level; (7) diagnose sustainability challenges across sectors; (8) apply these concepts to real companies.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What should a sustainable supply chain optimise? 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"Can a supply chain be green but economically unsustainable?\"* Yes — the slide's examples of one-sided optimisation:\n- Very low carbon but prohibitively expensive.\n- Low cost but poor labour conditions.\n- High recycling rate but poor product quality.\n- Local sourcing but higher emissions.\n- Fast delivery but excessive inventory or air freight.\n\n**Six performance dimensions and their typical question** 📘:\n| Dimension | Typical question |\n|---|---|\n| Economic | Is the supply chain profitable? |\n| Operational | Is it reliable and responsive? |\n| Environmental | What is its ecological footprint? |\n| Social | How does it affect workers and communities? |\n| Governance | Is it ethical, transparent and controlled? |\n| Resilience | Can it withstand disruption? |\n\n**Key message: \"Sustainability should not be treated as a separate department. It should become a performance dimension of the supply chain itself.\"** 📘\n\n## 2. What is supply-chain sustainability performance? 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Performance = actual outcome compared with the desired target.**\n\nA useful hierarchy: **Inputs** (energy, materials, labour, capital) → **Processes** (procurement, production, logistics, distribution) → **Outputs** (products, services, waste, emissions) → **Outcomes** (profitability, customer value, environmental impact, social impact, risk and resilience), with **continuous improvement** feeding back: identify material issues → measure baseline and KPIs → set targets → improve (implement actions) → verify (data quality and assurance) → report (disclose performance) → review (management decisions).\n\n**\"Do not confuse Activity → Output → Outcome → Impact.\"** 📘 Example: *\"100% of suppliers trained in ESG is an activity, not proof of sustainability improvement.\"* The proof is the outcome (supplier emissions down, violations down).\n\nLecture 3's version: **inputs → processes → outputs → outcomes → business value**, ensuring *overall sustainable performance*.\n\n## 3. Why measure sustainable performance? (Lecture 3) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Traditional vs sustainable performance**: traditional measures profit, revenue and cost for shareholders over the short term; sustainable performance adds environmental and social outcomes for all stakeholders over the long term.\n- Comparing two companies: one with higher sales, profits and pay but higher debt; the other with strong profits, safe workplaces, lower emissions and ethical suppliers — the second is the sustainable performer.\n- **\"If you cannot measure it, you cannot improve it.\"** **\"Performance measurement is a core enabler, not an optional one.\"**\n- Benefits: drives **accountability**, supports **better decision-making**, builds **shareholder trust**, improves **resilience**, creates **competitive advantage**.\n\n## 4. The KPI architecture (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"From KPIs to impact: measure, manage, improve.\"*\n\n| Level 1 — Economic | Level 2 — Operational | Level 3 — Environmental | Level 4 — Social | Level 5 — Governance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SC cost / sales | OTIF | Scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions | Lost-time injury rate | Supplier code compliance |\n| Total cost of ownership | Lead time | CO₂e per product | Employee turnover | Audit closure rate |\n| Procurement savings | Forecast accuracy | Energy per unit | Living-wage coverage | Ethics violations |\n| Inventory carrying cost | Perfect-order rate | Water per unit | Supplier labour violations | ESG data assurance |\n| Cost of poor quality | Inventory turnover | Waste per unit | Gender diversity | Traceability |\n| Return on SC assets | Supplier reliability | % recycled material | Human-rights assessments | |\n\n*\"Measure what matters. Manage consistently. Improve continuously.\"*\n\n### The KPI trap 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*\"More KPIs ≠ better performance management.\"* A company may measure **75 sustainability indicators, 40 supplier indicators and 30 logistics indicators**, *\"yet managers still cannot answer: are we becoming more sustainable?\"*\n\n**KPIs must be SMART** (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound), and **a KPI must answer four questions** 📘:\n1. **What happened?**\n2. **Why did it happen?**\n3. **Who owns it?**\n4. **What decision will change because of it?**\n\n### Leading vs lagging indicators 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Leading indicators | Lagging indicators |\n|---|---|\n| Supplier ESG assessments | Actual supplier incidents |\n| Employee training | Injury rate |\n| Renewable-energy contract | Actual Scope 2 emissions |\n| Eco-design projects | Product carbon footprint |\n| Preventive maintenance | Equipment failure |\n| Supplier development | Defect rate |\n| Risk assessments | Disruption losses |\n**\"Lagging KPIs tell you what happened; leading KPIs tell you whether you are likely to improve.\"** 📘\n\n### Adopt a Balanced Scorecard (Lecture 10 version) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Financial | Customer | Internal process | Learning & growth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Supply-chain cost | Sustainable product share | Carbon intensity | ESG skills |\n| ROIC | Customer complaints | Supplier quality | Innovation |\n| Cost avoidance | Green customer retention | Circularity | Employee engagement |\n(Lecture 2's Sustainability BSC is in [ch01](ch01-sscm-business-performance.md).)\n\n### SCOR meets sustainability 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| SCOR process | Sustainability KPI |\n|---|---|\n| **Plan** | Carbon-aware demand planning |\n| **Source** | Sustainable supplier score |\n| **Make** | Energy per unit |\n| **Deliver** | CO₂e per shipment |\n| **Return** | Recovery rate |\n| **Enable** | ESG data quality |\n*\"Sustainability should be embedded into supply-chain processes rather than measured only at the corporate level.\"* (ASCM's SCOR Digital Standard now adds an \"Orchestrate\" process and sustainability metrics, but the course uses the classic six.)\n\n### Supplier Sustainability Scorecard (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐\nDimensions: cost competitiveness, quality, delivery, environmental performance, labour & human rights, carbon reduction, governance, innovation (total 100). The question on the slide: *should a supplier scoring 95/100 on cost-quality-delivery but with serious labour violations be retained?* Answer: **no (or only under a strict corrective-action plan)** — a human-rights violation is a **knock-out criterion** that no weighted total can offset. *\"This is where sustainability becomes a management decision rather than a reporting exercise.\"* ([Worked](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md))\n\n## 5. Performance dimensions and SMART KPIs (Lecture 3) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q + D + I + R + S** across procurement → manufacturing → warehousing → transportation → distribution → customer:\n- **Quality** (meet or exceed expectations): defect rate (PPM, %), complaints/returns, warranty claims, Six Sigma level, supplier performance.\n- **Delivery** (right product, time, place, customer): on-time delivery %, order-fill cycle time, lead time, delivery reliability, transport efficiency.\n- **Innovation** (improve experience): number of new products, R&D spend, digital technology, patents, automation level.\n- **Resilience** (prepare for disruptions): supply disruptions, recovery time, supplier diversity, inventory risk, business continuity.\n- **Sustainability** (long-term ESG value): carbon emissions, energy consumed, water consumed, worker safety, ethical sourcing %.\n\nSMART KPI examples (Lecture 3): **economic** — cost per shipment, inventory days, % profitability; **environmental** — CO₂ per tonne-km, % material recycled, water intensity; **social** — accident frequency, % diversity, supplier audit score.\n\n## 6. The performance dashboard (Lecture 3) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nSix perspectives × four KPIs: **economic** (revenue growth, operating margin, cost per shipment, ROCE), **environmental** (CO₂ per t-km, energy intensity, water intensity, waste recycling rate), **social** (LTIFR, training hours, diversity, supplier audit score), **risk** (high-risk suppliers %, disruption incidents, risk assessments on time, risk-exposure score), **customer** (on-time delivery, perfect order, fill rate, NPS), **innovation** (new products, improvement projects, automation coverage, R&D % of revenue). *\"Balanced dashboard = performance data → strategic decisions.\"* *\"Track what matters. Take action. Drive sustainable performance across every dimension.\"* ([Reading and indexing it](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md))\n\n## 7. Benchmarking (Lecture 3) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n*Comparing an organisation's performance, processes and practices with the best-performing organisations or recognised standards to identify gaps and opportunities.*\n| Type | Compare on | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Industry leaders** | SC efficiency, sustainability initiatives, cost optimisation, innovation and digitalisation | Amazon (logistics), Toyota (lean), Unilever (sustainable sourcing) |\n| **Competitors** | Market share, product quality, cost competitiveness, ESG performance | Tata Motors vs M&M; Asian Paints vs Berger; HUL vs ITC |\n| **Global standards** | Certification and disclosure | ISO 9001 (quality), ISO 14001 (environment), ISO 45001 (OH&S), ISO 27001 (data security); BRSR, GRI, CDP, SBTi |\n| **Historical (internal)** | Own trend | Revenue growth, % profitability, carbon emissions, customer-satisfaction index, safety |\n*\"Benchmark not to imitate competition but to continuously outperform self.\"*\n\n## 8. Challenges in sustainability performance measurement (Lecture 3) ⭐⭐⭐\nLack of supplier transparency (Tier-2/3 data), multiple stakeholders with different expectations, **lack of standardised KPIs** (hard to compare), high cost and effort of data collection and reporting, **Scope 3 complexity**, data quality and verification, short-term financial pressure. **Enablers**: AI, IoT sensors, blockchain traceability, cloud platforms, big-data analytics and digital twins.\n\n**Emerging performance metrics** 📘: circularity, Scope 3 emissions, supplier ESG score, biodiversity, water risk, climate risk, **AI readiness**.\n\n**Indian case list (Lecture 3)** 📘: Tata Steel (integrated sustainability KPIs), ITC (water-positive and carbon-positive operations), HUL (sustainable sourcing and plastic circularity), Mahindra Group (ESG performance management), Asian Paints (digital SC performance), Amul (inclusive rural SC performance), Tata Motors (supplier sustainability scorecards), JSW Steel (carbon-intensity reduction), Infosys (ESG dashboards and reporting), DCM Shriram (resource efficiency in manufacturing).\n\n---\n\n## Section C — Sustainability reporting (Lecture 10)\n\n### 9. Why do companies report sustainability? 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nStakeholders demand information: **investors, customers, employees, regulators, banks, suppliers, communities**. Reporting serves **trust, risk management, capital allocation and accountability**. *\"But reporting creates a critical problem: what should be measured, and what should be disclosed?\"*\n\n### 10. The reporting landscape — \"different lenses, one goal: credible sustainability performance\" 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Framework | Focus / content |\n|---|---|\n| **CDP** (Carbon Disclosure Project) | Questionnaires on climate change, water security, forests and deforestation, plastics and biodiversity, supply-chain management |\n| **GRI 305** (Global Reporting Initiative) | Scope 1, 2, 3 emissions; GHG intensity; GHG reductions; ozone-depleting substances; NOx, SOx and other air emissions. GRI reports **impacts on the world** (impact materiality). |\n| **TCFD** | Four pillars: **governance** (climate risks and opportunities), **strategy** (actual and potential impacts), **risk management** (identify, assess, manage), **metrics and targets** |\n| **Integrated Reporting** (<IR>) | **Six capitals**: financial, manufactured, intellectual, human, social & relationship, natural; content elements: organisation overview, business model, governance, risks, performance |\n| **ISSB / IFRS S1–S2** | Global baseline for **investor-relevant** sustainability risks and opportunities; S1 general requirements, S2 climate (built on TCFD) |\n| **BRSR** (India, SEBI) | **9 principles** (NGRBC), ~140 parameters; **Section A** general disclosure, **Section B** management & process disclosure, **Section C** principle-wise performance disclosure; P2 sustainable products, P6 environment (emissions, energy, waste) |\n\n**Which question each answers (Lecture 10 takeaway)** 📘: **GRI → impacts; ISSB → investor-relevant sustainability risks/opportunities; BRSR → Indian regulatory ESG reporting.**\n\n### 11. BRSR Core — from disclosure to credibility 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- SEBI introduced **BRSR Core** as a subset of key performance indicators across **nine ESG attributes** (e.g. GHG footprint, water footprint, energy footprint, waste management, employee well-being and safety, gender diversity, inclusive development, fairness with customers, openness of business), requiring **reasonable assurance**.\n- The original 2023 glide path extended requirements progressively from the **top 150 listed entities in FY2023–24** toward the **top 1,000**.\n- Traditional reporting says *\"We are committed to sustainability\"*; BRSR Core says *\"Show me the data.\"*\n- Managerial implications: **data ownership, internal controls, measurement methodology, supplier data, audit/assessment readiness, evidence trails.**\n- *\"BRSR mandate: Don't just report sustainability. Prove it.\"*\n\n### 12. Company A vs Company B — report vs performance 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nCompany A: excellent report, beautiful graphics, ambitious targets, strong narrative. Company B: less attractive report but energy ↓25%, CO₂/unit ↓30%, water/unit ↓20%, supplier violations ↓40%, waste-to-landfill ↓60%. **\"The report is not the performance. Measure performance first, report performance later.\"** ([Value of B's gains](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md))\n\n### 13. Greenwashing vs greenhushing 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Greenwashing | Greenhushing |\n|---|---|\n| **Overstating sustainability performance without adequate evidence** | **Under-communicating genuine sustainability achievements** |\n| Examples: \"eco-friendly\", \"green\", \"sustainable\", \"carbon neutral\", \"100% eco-friendly\" with little evidence | Firms stay silent to avoid scrutiny, so stakeholders cannot reward real progress |\n**Good reporting includes** 📘: **claim, metric, boundary, methodology, baseline, target, evidence.** Key message: *credible sustainability communication.* (India's CCPA Guidelines for the Prevention and Regulation of Greenwashing, 2024, and the EU Green Claims rules now penalise vague claims.)\n\n---\n\n## Section D — Case studies (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Unilever (international)**: its 2025 annual report states it sourced **81% of key crops sustainably** (2030 target **95%**) and reported **more than 95% of purchase volumes of specified commodities as deforestation-free**. Possible scorecard: % sustainably sourced crops (sourcing transition), % traceable commodities (SC visibility), Scope 3 emissions (climate), farmer income (social impact), deforestation-free volume (nature), supplier compliance (governance), commodity supply risk (resilience). **Key lesson: agricultural sustainability is fundamentally a supply-chain problem.**\n\n**Mahindra & Mahindra (Indian)**: 2025–26 reporting shows a strong focus on supplier ESG management. The supplier code of conduct was signed by **95% of suppliers by purchase value**, while cumulative environmental due diligence since FY2024 covered **68.1% of suppliers by value**. **Key lesson: is supplier ESG assessment enough — or should supplier ESG performance affect purchasing decisions?** (Answer: it must feed into sourcing decisions, volume allocation and supplier development; signing a code is an activity, not an outcome.)\n\n---\n\n## Section E — Sectoral sustainability challenges 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n### 14. Sectoral sustainability matrix\n| Sector | Major sustainability hotspots |\n|---|---|\n| Automotive | Battery materials, Scope 3, circularity, supplier emissions |\n| FMCG | Packaging, agriculture, water, logistics |\n| Textiles | Water, chemicals, labour, energy |\n| Steel | Carbon, energy, raw materials |\n| Pharma | Water, chemicals, waste, compliance |\n| Electronics | Critical minerals, e-waste, labour |\n| Retail | Scope 3, packaging, food waste |\n| Aviation | Fuel, SAF, emissions, aircraft life cycle |\n| Construction | Cement, steel, waste, embodied carbon |\n| IT | Data centres, electricity, e-waste |\n| Oil & gas | GHG emissions, methane, energy use, biodiversity |\n| Hospitality | Food waste, water/plastics, procurement |\n| Healthcare | Energy, biomedical waste, chemicals |\n| Food | Agriculture, methane, water, food waste |\n\n### 15. Sector-specific KPIs\n| Sector | Key KPIs |\n|---|---|\n| Automotive | kg CO₂e per vehicle; % recycled material; battery traceability |\n| Steel | tCO₂e per tonne of steel; energy per tonne; scrap utilisation |\n| FMCG | grams of packaging per product; % recyclable packaging; CO₂e per product |\n| Logistics | g CO₂e per tonne-km; vehicle utilisation; empty kilometres |\n| Textile | litres of water per kg; chemical intensity; supplier labour compliance |\n| Oil & gas | tCO₂e per unit; methane intensity; energy intensity |\n| Healthcare | kg CO₂e per patient; waste per patient; % sustainable procurement |\n| Food | % food waste; GHG per kg; water use per kg |\n*\"Different hotspots. Different KPIs. One sustainability goal.\"* And: *\"A company may reduce Scope 1 substantially while its total supply-chain footprint continues increasing → carbon accounting must follow the value chain.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 16. The sustainable supply-chain performance loop 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Identify** — material sustainability issues.\n2. **Measure** — baseline + KPIs.\n3. **Target** — science, business or regulatory targets.\n4. **Improve** — projects + supplier engagement.\n5. **Verify** — data controls + assurance.\n6. **Report** — BRSR / GRI / ISSB / annual report.\n7. **Review** — management decisions.\n*\"Identify. Measure. Improve. Verify. Report. Repeat.\"*\n\n## 17. Five tests for a good SSCM measurement system 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Test | Question |\n|---|---|\n| **Material** | Does it measure what matters? |\n| **Balanced** | Economic + operational + environmental + social? |\n| **Actionable** | Can managers influence the KPI? |\n| **Comparable** | Across time, suppliers and competitors? |\n| **Credible** | Can the number be verified? |\n**\"If a KPI does not change a decision, question why it exists.\"**\n\n## 18. Key takeaways (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- Sustainability must become part of **core supply-chain performance management**.\n- A good measurement system balances **cost + quality + service + carbon + people + risk**.\n- Quality management and sustainability are interconnected: **waste + defects + resource efficiency + continuous improvement**.\n- Reporting frameworks answer different questions: **GRI — impacts; ISSB — investor-relevant risks and opportunities; BRSR — Indian regulatory ESG reporting**.\n- **Scope 3 makes supply-chain collaboration essential.**\n- **Sector determines the sustainability hotspot.**\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Distinguish leading and lagging indicators with two examples each.**\nLagging indicators report results after the fact (injury rate, actual Scope 2 emissions); leading indicators predict future results and can be acted on now (employee safety training, renewable-energy contracts). A good system pairs each lagging KPI with the leading KPIs that drive it.\n\n**Q2. What is the KPI trap and how do you avoid it?**\nTracking too many indicators (75 + 40 + 30) without being able to say whether the chain is becoming more sustainable. Avoid it with a small, balanced, SMART set where each KPI answers what happened, why, who owns it and what decision will change, and passes the five tests.\n\n**Q3. Compare GRI, TCFD/ISSB and BRSR.**\nGRI: impact reporting for all stakeholders (GRI 305 for emissions). TCFD/ISSB: investor-focused climate and sustainability risks and opportunities (governance, strategy, risk management, metrics and targets). BRSR: SEBI-mandated Indian disclosure on 9 principles (Sections A–C), with BRSR Core requiring assured KPIs.\n\n**Q4. Greenwashing vs greenhushing.**\nGreenwashing overstates performance without evidence (\"100% eco-friendly\"); greenhushing under-communicates real achievements. Credible reporting states the claim, metric, boundary, methodology, baseline, target and evidence.\n\n**Q5. List the seven steps of the SSCM performance loop.**\nIdentify → measure → target → improve → verify → report → review.\n"}, {"slug": "ch09-energy-sector", "label": "Energy-Sector Supply Chain", "tag": "09", "group": "chapters", "title": "Supply Chain in the Energy Sector: Supplies, Production, End Use, Disposal", "md": "# Supply Chain in the Energy Sector: Supplies, Production, End Use, Disposal\nSyllabus topic 9 📘. The course material for this topic is the set of readings in the zip: the two **IISc papers** on the life-cycle energy and GHG emissions of automotive fuels in India (Part 1 Tank-to-Wheel, Part 2 Well-to-Wheel), **Rietmann, Hügler & Lieven (2020)** on forecasting EV sales and worldwide CO₂, the **Tesla battery supply chain** case (William Davidson Institute, 2022), plus the energy content of Lectures 1, 2, 7 and 11 (Hindustan Zinc, critical minerals, green hydrogen) and the carbon deck. The syllabus asks you to follow energy through four stages: **supplies → production → end use → disposal**. Numericals (WTW efficiency, EV vs ICE, logistic EV forecast, battery economics, solar PPA): [LCA & Energy Numericals](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why the energy sector is a supply-chain problem ⭐⭐⭐\n- Energy is both a **product** (fuel, electricity, batteries) and an **input** to every other supply chain (Scope 1 fuels and Scope 2 electricity of every firm).\n- Energy supply chains are long, capital-intensive and global: crude oil, LNG, coal, critical minerals and solar modules cross many borders, so geopolitics, price volatility and human rights are built into them.\n- The energy transition moves the sustainability problem *upstream*: instead of burning fuel at the point of use, we now depend on **minerals** (lithium, nickel, cobalt, copper, graphite, rare earths) and on **clean electricity generation**. Lecture 11 calls this **the critical-minerals paradox**.\n- Every stage has its own hotspot: extraction (land, water, biodiversity, labour), conversion (efficiency, emissions), transmission (losses), end use (efficiency, behaviour) and disposal (hazardous waste, recovery).\n\n| Stage | Fossil-energy chain | Clean-energy chain | Key sustainability issues |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Supplies** | Oil & gas exploration, coal mining, crude imports | Lithium, nickel, cobalt, graphite, copper, rare earths, polysilicon | Methane leaks, biodiversity, water, conflict minerals, child labour, import dependence |\n| **Production / conversion** | Refining, gas processing, thermal power | Solar/wind generation, battery-cell manufacturing, green hydrogen | Conversion efficiency, CO₂ intensity, local pollution |\n| **Distribution** | Pipelines, tankers, rail, retail outlets | Transmission & distribution grids, charging networks | T&D losses, grid stability |\n| **End use** | ICE vehicles, boilers, kilns | EVs, heat pumps, electrified industry | Tank-to-Wheel efficiency, behaviour, rebound |\n| **Disposal / end-of-life** | Decommissioning wells and plants, fly ash | Battery recycling and second life, solar-panel and turbine-blade waste | Hazardous waste, recovery rates, EPR |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Supplies: fuels and critical minerals ⭐⭐⭐\n### Fossil supplies (IISc WTW paper) 📘\n- India imported **81.27%** of the crude it refined in 2010–11 (18.73% domestic); the overall weighted crude recovery efficiency was about 98.6% and refining efficiency differs by product.\n- Natural gas: about 23% imported (2010–11); recovery and processing efficiencies of about 94–97%.\n- **Well-to-Tank (WTT) efficiency** of petroleum fuels is **75–85%**; **LPG is highest at 85%**.\n- Methane (CH₄) leakage in gas supply chains is a major hidden GHG source (GWP ≈ 28–30).\n\n### The critical-minerals paradox (Lecture 11) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nThe green transition requires **more EVs + batteries and more solar + wind + grid**, which creates **high demand for lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, graphite and rare earths**, which in turn creates **water, biodiversity and emission impacts plus social and geopolitical risks**. The IEA's **2025 Critical Minerals Outlook**: **lithium demand rose almost 30% in 2024**, and **7% of global copper supply was exposed to flood or drought disruption risk in 2024**.\n\n### Battery-mineral hotspots (Tesla case) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Mineral | Supply concentration | Sustainability issue |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cobalt** (about 6% of NMC cell mass, the highest-cost input per tonne) | About **65%** from the **Democratic Republic of Congo** | Artisanal mines linked to child and forced labour and to conflict financing — *\"the blood diamond of batteries\"*; a 2019 lawsuit named Apple, Google, Microsoft and Tesla. Cobalt-free **LFP** chemistry is cheaper but has lower energy density and range. |\n| **Lithium** | **Lithium Triangle** (Chile, Bolivia, Argentina) and Australia | About **500,000 gallons of water per tonne** of lithium extracted from brine; linked indirectly to the 2019 Bolivian crisis |\n| **Nickel** | Indonesia, Philippines | In 2017 the Philippines closed **23 mines** on environmental grounds, causing panic because it supplied about **20%** of nickel |\n| **Cells** | **CATL** controlled about **30%** of the global EV-battery market | Concentration risk; Tesla relies on Panasonic, CATL and LG |\n\n### Geopolitics of energy supplies 📘 ⭐⭐\n- Lecture 11's polycrisis lists **critical-resource constraints** (lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, rare earths) and **geopolitical fragmentation** (trade restrictions, sanctions, protectionism, regionalisation).\n- **McKinsey (2026), \"Geopolitics and the geometry of global trade\"** (in the zip): US–China direct trade fell by about 30%; trade is re-routing through \"connector\" economies; AI-related goods drive about one-third of trade growth; China is becoming the *\"factory to the factories\"* (exporting intermediate goods and machinery). For energy supply chains this means diversification (\"China + 1\"), friend-shoring and strategic stockpiles.\n- Indian responses: production-linked incentives (PLI) for advanced-chemistry cells and solar modules, **KABIL** (Khanij Bidesh India Ltd) acquiring lithium assets abroad, the National Critical Mineral Mission (2025).\n\n**Sourcing strategies for energy supplies**: supplier due diligence and traceability (OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Responsible Minerals Initiative), chemistry substitution (LFP, sodium-ion), long-term offtake contracts, recycling as a \"secondary mine\", regional diversification.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Production and conversion ⭐⭐⭐\n### Electricity generation in India (IISc paper) 📘\n- **69.9%** of electricity came from coal (12.1% natural gas in the data used); typical coal-plant efficiency in the paper's calculation was about **22%** (from 0.72 kg CO₂/kWh and coal's calorific value of 22,732 kJ/kg).\n- **T&D losses of 25.4%** (since reduced to under 20% nationally).\n- Result: **electricity WTT efficiency ≈ 20%**, the lowest of all fuels — *the losses are upstream*.\n- Today non-fossil sources are about half of India's installed capacity (the carbon deck cites about 54%), but coal still supplies most of the *energy* generated, and the grid factor is about 0.71–0.79 kg CO₂/kWh.\n\n### Hydrogen\n- Hydrogen from natural-gas reforming has a production efficiency of about **70%**, giving WTT of only about **48%** (IISc), so fuel-cell vehicles were the least efficient on WTW.\n- **Green hydrogen** (electrolysis with renewable power) is the Lecture 7 \"future manufacturing\" lever for **high-temperature processes and hard-to-abate sectors** (steel, cement, fertiliser, refining). India's National Green Hydrogen Mission targets 5 Mt a year by 2030.\n\n### Renewable generation and corporate procurement ⭐⭐\nCompanies decarbonise Scope 2 through rooftop solar, open-access **PPAs** (power purchase agreements), green tariffs and RECs. Course examples: Asian Paints about 60% RE (FY25), ITC 50% RE, M&M 37% RE, Dalmia Cement targeting 100% RE by 2030, Apple's supply chain with **17.8 GW** of renewable electricity online (2024). ([PPA numerical](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md))\n\n### Battery manufacturing\nBattery cells are the most carbon-intensive part of an EV (the course's automotive case gives about 10 t CO₂e per EV including a 60 kWh battery vs about 6 t for an ICE car's materials). Gigafactories powered by renewable electricity (Tesla Gigafactory Nevada with Panasonic, planned for 500,000 packs a year) cut this embedded carbon.\n\n---\n\n## 4. End use: transport energy in India ⭐⭐⭐\n### Context (IISc papers) 📘\nTransport was India's second-largest energy-consuming sector (about **17.5%** of total energy in 2010–11 in Part 2; Part 1 puts transport at about **22% of energy and 8% of GHG emissions**), with **road transport about 87%** of transport's share and **124 of the 135 kg of per-capita transport CO₂** (2011). Passenger vehicles were about 13% of registered motor vehicles.\n\n### Tank-to-Wheel (TTW) — Part 1 📘 ⭐⭐\n- 28 vehicle configurations analysed (fuels × powertrains).\n- TTW efficiency: **gasoline/LPG 15–27%**, **diesel 25–32%**; hybrids gain through regenerative braking (about 19–23% of energy recovered).\n- **Split hybrids** reduce fuel use and CO₂ by about **20–40%**.\n\n### Well-to-Wheel (WTW) — Part 2 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **WTW = WTT × TTW.** For liquid fuels about 75–84% of WTW energy is used in the TTW stage, while for electricity about 83% of WTW energy is consumed upstream.\n- **Diesel split hybrid = most efficient** configuration overall.\n- **BEV on the Indian grid ≈ gasoline car** on WTW efficiency, because of coal power and T&D losses.\n- **Hydrogen fuel-cell vehicles = least efficient** (WTT about 48%).\n- **Hybridisation raises WTW efficiency by an average of 41% and cuts WTW energy by 29%.**\n- CO₂: 72–80% of WTW CO₂ from liquid fuels is emitted in the TTW stage; for BEVs almost all is at the power plant.\n\n### EV adoption and global CO₂ (Rietmann et al., 2020) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- Model: logistic diffusion I(t) = L ÷ [1 + (L/I(0) − 1)·e^(−k·L·t)], fitted country by country.\n- Findings: EVs reach about **30% of the fleet by 2032** and **42.5% by 2035**, yet **global passenger-car CO₂ still rises until about 2035** because the fleet grows and grids are still fossil-heavy.\n- India-specific: ICE 0.135 kg CO₂/km; grid **0.791 kg CO₂/kWh**; 20,800 km/yr; India's car CO₂ rises about **+129.5% (2018–2035)**. EVs only cut emissions as the grid decarbonises.\n- Charging energy for 439 million EVs (17,000 km/yr × 0.185 kWh/km) ≈ **1,381 TWh/yr** — the power-sector supply chain must expand with the fleet.\n\n### Logistics end use: Hindustan Zinc and GreenLine (Lecture 1–2) 📘\nHindustan Zinc's ₹400 crore deal with GreenLine Mobility to move ore and metal with **LNG and EV trucks** is the course's \"reduction-based\" energy case. Trade-offs: cost, emissions, refuelling/charging infrastructure, scalability.\n\n**Supply-chain lessons from end use**: compare fuels on WTW not TTW; pair electrification with renewable power; hybrids and efficiency are cheap early wins; demand-side measures (fewer km, higher load factor) matter as much as fuel switching.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Disposal: end-of-life energy assets ⭐⭐⭐\n### The EV-battery wave (Tesla case) 📘\n- By **2030, 1.2 million tonnes** of EV batteries a year will need disposal.\n- Only about **5%** of Li-ion batteries are recycled globally; **95%** reach municipal waste.\n- Tesla packs: up to **10,000** replacements by 2025 and **50,000 a year by 2030**.\n- Tesla's 2020 impact report claimed *\"none of our scrapped lithium-ion batteries go to landfills and 100 percent are recycled\"* — the case treats this as positive sentiment rather than a plan (a greenwashing risk).\n\n### End-of-life options (value-retention order)\n1. **Second life** (stationary storage): adaptation costs **$3,500** (direct reuse), **$6,700** (module level), **$8,000** (cell level); Tesla remanufactures packs at module level; replacing 5–30% of cells can restore 95% state of health.\n2. **Recycling**: economic return about **$5,600 per battery after 2025**.\n   - **Pyrometallurgy**: shred and smelt at high temperature; tolerant of mixed designs but energy-intensive; lithium is often lost.\n   - **Hydrometallurgy**: leach in acids; recovers lithium, cobalt, nickel and copper; chemical handling risks.\n   - **Direct recycling** (US DOE **ReCell** Center): keeps the cathode structure intact, minimal processing.\n   - **ReLiB** (UK): robotic disassembly and better separation.\n   - Best case: recover about half of critical materials and cut up to **70%** of GHG.\n3. **Disposal**: hazardous; the last resort.\n\n### Waste-management frameworks (Tesla case) 📘 ⭐⭐\n- **US EPA hierarchy** (most to least desirable): source reduction & reuse → recycling/composting → energy recovery → treatment/disposal.\n- **EU Waste Framework Directive** (five levels): prevention → preparing for reuse → recycling → recovery → disposal, with the **\"polluter pays\" principle** and **Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR)**. The EU Battery Regulation (2023) adds recycled-content targets and a **battery passport** (from 2027).\n- **India**: Battery Waste Management Rules 2022 (EPR for producers, recovery targets), E-Waste (Management) Rules 2022 (solar panels included), Vehicle Scrappage Policy (Mahindra CERO, Tata Re.Wi.Re).\n\n### Recycling partners (Tesla case) 📘\n- **Redwood Materials** (founded 2017 by Tesla's ex-CTO **JB Straubel**; Carson City, Nevada; about **95% recovery**; valued at **$3.78 billion**, not yet profitable).\n- **Li-Cycle** (founded 2016; Kingston, Ontario plant for **5,000 t/yr**; **>95% recovery** with no landfill; provides logistics; raised **$1.6 billion**).\n\n### Tesla's decision: build, buy or partner? 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Option | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Build** internal recycling | Control, material security, closed loop with Gigafactories, ESG credit | Capex, low early volumes, new competence |\n| **Buy** a start-up | Speed, technology, talent | Acquisition price, integration risk, immature technology |\n| **Partner** with Redwood / Li-Cycle | Low capex, scale through pooled volumes, flexibility | Less control; risk that the market cannot meet Tesla's needs in time |\n**Model answer**: partner now (volumes too small; recyclers need pooled scale), with equity or long-term offtake agreements that guarantee recovered cobalt, nickel and lithium back to Tesla; build in-house capability as packs reach 50,000 a year; design packs for disassembly. This improves ESG performance on both the \"E\" (recovery, lower Scope 3) and the \"S\" (less conflict-mineral demand).\n\n### ESG ratings divergence (Tesla case) 📘 ⭐⭐\nESG assets reached **$40.5 trillion** in 2020. Raters disagree: **MSCI** scored Tesla high on environment; **FTSE gave Tesla zero** on environment because its scoring looked at factories, not the emissions avoided by the cars; **Sustainalytics** was in between. Other frameworks in the case: SASB, TCFD, GRI. Lesson: **boundaries and methodology** (factory vs product life cycle) decide the score, the same lesson as LCA.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Energy-sector KPIs (Lecture 10) 📘 ⭐⭐\nOil & gas: **tCO₂e per unit, methane intensity, energy intensity**; hotspots: GHG emissions, methane, energy use, biodiversity. Power: g CO₂ per kWh, T&D loss %, plant load factor, % RE. Batteries/EV: kg CO₂e per kWh of cells, % recycled content, battery traceability, recovery rate. Corporate users: % RE electricity, energy intensity, Scope 2 (location- and market-based).\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. Explain the energy supply chain from supplies to disposal with one sustainability issue at each stage.**\nSupplies: fuel extraction and critical minerals (methane leaks; cobalt from the DRC with child labour; lithium brine using 500,000 gallons of water per tonne). Production: power and refining (coal plants at about 22% efficiency, WTT of electricity about 20%). Distribution: T&D losses of 25.4%. End use: vehicle efficiency (TTW 15–32%; EV benefit depends on the grid). Disposal: 1.2 Mt of batteries by 2030 with only 5% recycled; EPR and recycling needed.\n\n**Q2. What is the critical-minerals paradox?**\nDecarbonising energy needs EVs, batteries, solar, wind and grids, which multiply demand for lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, graphite and rare earths; their extraction brings water, biodiversity, emission, social and geopolitical risks (IEA 2025: lithium demand +30% in 2024; 7% of copper supply exposed to flood or drought).\n\n**Q3. Why can an EV be worse than a petrol car in India?**\nOn WTW, India's coal-heavy grid (about 0.79 kg CO₂/kWh, WTT about 20%) makes an EV's use-phase emissions about 0.146 kg/km vs 0.135 for an ICE car (Rietmann data), and battery manufacturing adds embedded carbon. The EV wins once the grid falls below about 0.63–0.73 kg/kWh or is charged with renewables.\n\n**Q4. What should Tesla do with end-of-life batteries?**\nFollow the hierarchy: remanufacture and second-life use first, then hydrometallurgical or direct recycling; partner with Redwood or Li-Cycle now and build in-house as volumes grow; design for disassembly; report recovery honestly to avoid greenwashing.\n"}, {"slug": "ch10-sscm-best-practices", "label": "SSCM Best Practices", "tag": "10", "group": "chapters", "title": "SSCM Best Practices & Future Strategies", "md": "# SSCM Best Practices & Future Strategies\nSyllabus topic 10 📘 (Lecture 11: \"Developing SSCM strategies — understanding contemporary challenges and future sustainability transitions\", plus the best-practice cases spread across Lectures 1–10). This is mainly **CO6** (low weightage, 3-mark theory questions) and is part of the **20-mark online quiz** (PPTs 9, 10, 11). Learn the lists, the frameworks and the one-line \"key messages\" — quiz MCQs are built from them.\n\n**Opening question (Lecture 11)** 📘: *\"If a supply chain is cheap, fast and reliable, but creates excessive carbon emissions, resource depletion or human-rights risks, is it actually a good supply chain?\"* **Sustainability is no longer an add-on to SCM; it is becoming a fundamental design constraint.**\n\n---\n\n## Part A — Why supply-chain strategy is changing\n\n### 1. Traditional vs sustainable SCM (Lecture 11) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Traditional SCM | Sustainable SCM |\n|---|---|\n| Lowest cost | **Life-cycle value** |\n| Linear flow | **Circular flow** |\n| Supplier price | **Supplier sustainability + resilience** |\n| Efficiency | **Efficiency + ecological limits** |\n| Scope 1/2 focus | **Full value chain / Scope 3** |\n| Waste = cost | **Waste = resource** |\n| Forecast demand | **Shape demand + circular business models** |\n| Global optimisation | **Resilient, diversified networks** |\n**\"Sustainable SCM does not mean sacrificing economic performance. It means changing the definition of performance.\"**\n\n### 2. The supply chain has become the sustainability battlefield 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nWhere sustainability impacts occur across the chain:\n| Stage | Impacts |\n|---|---|\n| Raw materials | Mining, agriculture, water, biodiversity, human rights |\n| Suppliers | Energy, labour, emissions, waste, occupational safety |\n| Manufacturing | Energy, materials, chemicals, water, waste |\n| Logistics | Transport, warehousing, packaging, fuel |\n| Customer | Product use, energy consumption, behaviour |\n| End-of-life | Reuse, repair, refurbishment, recycling, disposal |\n**Key question: \"Where in the value chain is the largest sustainability impact — and who has the power to change it?\"**\n\n### 3. The contemporary challenge: a \"polycrisis\" 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| # | Crisis | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Climate change** | Extreme weather, floods, droughts, heat; physical disruption of facilities and logistics |\n| 2 | **Geopolitical fragmentation** | Trade restrictions, sanctions, protectionism, regionalisation |\n| 3 | **Critical-resource constraints** | Lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, rare earths |\n| 4 | **Regulatory pressure** | Carbon reporting, due diligence, EPR, product traceability, digital product passports |\n| 5 | **Social risks** | Forced labour, unsafe working conditions, living wages, human rights |\n| 6 | **Technology disruption** | AI, automation, digital twins, blockchain, new manufacturing technologies |\n**Key message: \"The sustainable supply chain of 2030 must simultaneously be green and resilient\"** (World Economic Forum Global Risks Report 2026).\n\n### 4. The five big sustainability challenges 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Carbon** — decarbonise the entire value chain.\n2. **Resources** — do more with less material.\n3. **Circularity** — move from take–make–waste to closed loops.\n4. **People** — protect workers and communities.\n5. **Resilience** — survive disruption without shifting environmental/social costs elsewhere.\n**Strategic shift: Efficiency → Resilience → Sustainability → Regeneration.**\n\n### 5. Climate change changes supply-chain design 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nTraditional question: *what is the cheapest location?* New questions: What is the location's **climate risk**? Its **future water availability**? The **carbon intensity of its electricity**? Its exposure to **extreme weather**? How dependent is the network on a **single port**? Can **suppliers decarbonise**? What happens under a **1.5 °C / 2 °C / 3 °C scenario**?\n- **Mitigation**: reduce the supply chain's impact on the climate.\n- **Adaptation**: reduce the supply chain's vulnerability to climate change.\n- **Best strategy = both.**\n\n---\n\n## Part B — The strategic framework\n\n### 6. The sustainable supply-chain strategy architecture (7 steps) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Step | Action |\n|---|---|\n| 1. **Diagnose** | Map material, carbon, water, social and risk hotspots |\n| 2. **Prioritise** | Identify high-impact / high-risk activities |\n| 3. **Redesign** | Products, suppliers, processes, logistics, network |\n| 4. **Collaborate** | Suppliers, customers, government, competitors, communities |\n| 5. **Measure** | Carbon, circularity, cost, service, social performance |\n| 6. **Innovate** | Digitalisation, AI, clean technology, new business models |\n| 7. **Scale** | Embed sustainability into procurement, investment and governance |\nMemory hook: **\"D-P-R-C-M-I-S — Diagnose, Prioritise, Redesign, Collaborate, Measure, Innovate, Scale.\"**\n\n### 7. Five strategies 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Strategy 1 — Sustainable procurement**: a balanced KPI set:\n| Dimension | Example KPI |\n|---|---|\n| Economic | Total cost |\n| Environmental | kg CO₂e per unit |\n| Energy | Renewable-energy % |\n| Resources | Recycled content |\n| Water | m³ per unit |\n| Social | Safety incidents |\n| Human rights | High-risk suppliers assessed |\n| Resilience | Single-source dependency |\n| Transparency | Traceability % |\n**Key message: \"Do not outsource sustainability.\"** A company cannot claim a sustainable supply chain while its purchasing practices (price squeezes, last-minute orders) force suppliers toward unsustainable behaviour.\n\n**Strategy 2 — Sustainable product design**: instead of asking *\"How do we recycle this product?\"* ask *\"How should we design this product so that recycling, repair and reuse become easy?\"* Strategic concept: **Design → Supply chain → Use → Recovery**, not Design → Manufacture → Sell → Dispose.\n\n**Strategy 3 — Circular supply chains**: from the **linear model** (extract → make → use → dispose) through the **recycling model** (extract → make → use → recycle) to the **circular model** (reduce → reuse → repair → refurbish → remanufacture → recycle). A circular chain creates **new flows**: forward (supplier → manufacturer → customer) and reverse (customer → collection → sorting → repair/refurbish → remanufacture/recycle → supplier). **Key insight: \"Circularity is not simply a waste-management strategy. It is a supply-network redesign strategy.\"**\n\n**Strategy 4 — Sustainable logistics**: avoid the trap that **green transport ≠ sustainable logistics**; consider packaging, warehousing, inventory, delivery frequency, returns and empty miles. **Strategic principle: \"The cleanest kilometre is often the kilometre eliminated.\"**\n\n**Strategy 5 — Digital sustainable supply chains**:\n| Technology | Sustainability use |\n|---|---|\n| IoT | Real-time energy/material monitoring |\n| AI | Demand forecasting + routing + optimisation |\n| Digital twin | Simulate network alternatives |\n| Blockchain / distributed ledger | Traceability |\n| Digital Product Passport | Product-level sustainability information |\nExample: **the EU Digital Product Passport framework launched in July 2026** (the Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation; batteries first). **\"Future supply chain = physical supply chain + digital information layer.\"**\n\n### 8. Sustainability measurement — from metrics to decisions (Lecture 11) 📘 ⭐⭐\n| Economic | Environmental | Social | Resilience |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue, total cost, working capital, ROI, net profit, debt ratio | Scope 1–2–3 emissions, energy, water, waste, material | Safety, labour standards, human rights, community impact, diversity | Supplier density, recovery time, inventory buffers, geographic exposure |\n**The five tests for a good sustainability KPI: material, balanced, actionable, comparable, credible.**\n\n---\n\n## Part C — Future sustainability transitions\n\n### 9. The eight transitions — \"bigger than going green\" 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| # | From → To |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Fossil energy → **renewable / electrified energy** |\n| 2 | Virgin materials → **circular materials** |\n| 3 | Linear products → **circular products** |\n| 4 | Global concentration → **resilient diversification** |\n| 5 | Opaque supply chains → **traceable supply chains** |\n| 6 | Ownership → **access / service models** |\n| 7 | Reactive management → **predictive / AI-enabled management** |\n| 8 | Sustainability reporting → **sustainability embedded in decisions** |\n\n### 10. The critical-minerals paradox 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\nThe transition needs EVs, batteries, solar, wind and grids → high demand for lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, graphite and rare earths → water, biodiversity and emission impacts plus social and geopolitical risks. IEA 2025: **lithium demand +~30% in 2024; 7% of global copper supply exposed to flood or drought risk in 2024.** (Detail in [ch09](ch09-energy-sector.md).)\n\n### 11. From JIT to JIT + JIC + JIS 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **JIT** — just in time (lean, low inventory).\n- **JIC** — **just in case** (strategic buffers, dual sourcing for resilience).\n- **JIS** — **just in sequence** (parts delivered in assembly order; in the sustainability context it is also read as \"just sustainable\").\n- **Old model**: global → concentrated → lowest cost. **Emerging model**: **regional + diversified + digital + circular + low-carbon** (Americas, Europe/Africa, Asia-Pacific hubs).\n- **Network design variables**: cost + service + carbon + circularity + risk + social impact.\n- **New optimisation question: \"What is the lowest total life-cycle cost and risk — not merely the lowest purchase price?\"**\n\n---\n\n## Part D — Best-practice cases (the course's portfolio)\n\n### 12. International best practices 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Company | Practice | Result / lesson |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Apple** (Lecture 11) | Treats sustainability as a **value-chain transformation**: supplier decarbonisation (RE adoption, efficiency, carbon-neutral production target), materials (recycled cobalt, recycled rare earths, cleaner semiconductor manufacturing), product recovery (disassembly, recycling technologies such as the Daisy robot) | 2025: GHG **down >60% vs 2015**; supply chain had **17.8 GW** of RE online, avoiding about **21.8 Mt** of GHG in 2024. *\"The OEM becomes the orchestrator of supplier sustainability.\"* |\n| **Walmart** (Lecture 6) | **Project Gigaton**: 1 billion tonnes avoided by 2030; six pillars (energy, agriculture, waste, packaging, deforestation, product use); supplier goals on the Sustainability Hub; with WWF and EDF | Supplier-led Scope 3 reduction at scale |\n| **Schneider Electric** (Lecture 6) | **Zero Carbon Project**: cut the operational footprint of the top 1,000 suppliers by 50%; suppliers = 70% of its emissions; analytics → ambition → action | *\"The road to net zero runs through the supply base.\"* |\n| **Unilever** (Lecture 10) | 81% of key crops sustainably sourced (target 95% by 2030); >95% deforestation-free | *\"Agricultural sustainability is fundamentally a supply-chain problem.\"* |\n| **Nestlé** (Lecture 6) | Regenerative agriculture, supplier training, deforestation-free sourcing, digital traceability | Lower Scope 3, resilient supply |\n| **IKEA** (Lectures 6, 8) | FSC wood; circular agenda (repair, buy-back, resale, recycling) | Circular business model |\n| **Renault Refactory** (Lecture 8) | Refurbishment, battery repair, parts remanufacturing, second-life, recycling, retrofitting | Circular mobility facility |\n| **DHL** (Lecture 8) | EVs, alternative fuels, multimodal, green warehouses, consolidation, packaging optimisation | Green logistics at scale |\n| **Walmart–IBM** (Lecture 9) | Blockchain food traceability | Mango tracing 7 days → 2.2 seconds |\n| **Toyota** (Lectures 6, 7) | TPS, kaizen, supplier energy-efficiency programmes | Lean = green |\n| **Patagonia, Nike, Intel, Starbucks, Microsoft** (Lecture 6) | Fair Trade audits; supplier monitoring after labour charges; conflict-mineral due diligence; climate-resilient coffee; supplier carbon reporting | Risk reduction and brand trust |\n\n### 13. Indian best practices 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Company | Practice |\n|---|---|\n| **ITC e-Choupal** | Digital procurement direct from farmers; transparent prices; lower wastage; rural empowerment ([case](case-studies.md)) |\n| **ITC** | 50% RE; water-, carbon- and solid-waste-positive operations |\n| **HUL** | Sustainable sourcing from smallholders; regenerative and climate-smart farming; plastic circularity |\n| **Tata Motors** | **Re.Wi.Re** (\"Recycle with Respect\") registered vehicle-scrapping network; **Project Aalingana** linking decarbonisation + circular economy + nature; **Prolife** remanufacturing; supplier sustainability scorecards; localised ECU semiconductors after COVID |\n| **Mahindra** | Carbon-neutrality goal; 37% RE; zero-waste farm-equipment plants; **CERO** vehicle recycling (with MSTC); supplier code signed by 95% of suppliers by value; 68.1% covered by environmental due diligence |\n| **Tata Steel** | Waste to energy, alternate fuels and biochar, LCA-based resource efficiency, scrap recycling, hydrogen trials |\n| **Asian Paints** | ~60% RE, zero liquid discharge, solvent recovery, ESG procurement (lower-carbon grades, ≥20% bio-based), digital manufacturing |\n| **Dalmia Cement** | >80% low-carbon cement mix, waste as resource, 100% RE target by 2030 |\n| **Arvind** | 100% recycled water, recycled fibres, ozone washing and laser finishing |\n| **JSW Steel** | ESG supplier self-assessment on Synesgy; carbon-intensity reduction |\n| **UltraTech, Amul, Bisleri** | LCA hotspots → waste-heat recovery and fly ash; efficient refrigeration and cattle feed; lightweight rPET bottles and rail |\n| **Hindustan Zinc** | LNG and EV trucks with GreenLine |\n| **Flipkart, Myntra** | Last-mile EVs, recyclable packaging, rooftop solar, EV charging at DCs |\n| **EV-battery recyclers** (Attero, Lohum, Tata Autocomp) | Battery recovery under EPR |\n\n**Tata Motors circular lesson (Lecture 11)** 📘: Re.Wi.Re combines reverse logistics, circular economy, digital traceability, regulatory compliance, material recovery and supplier integration. *\"Where is the value created in this circular supply chain?\"* → scrap recovery, reduced virgin-material dependence, compliance, customer incentives (scrappage certificates), new business opportunities, reduced environmental impact.\n\n### 14. The sustainable supply-chain strategy matrix 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Strategic challenge | Immediate strategy | Future transition |\n|---|---|---|\n| Carbon | Renewable energy | Net-zero supply chains |\n| Materials | Efficiency | Circular materials |\n| Waste | Recycling | Zero-waste / circular systems |\n| Suppliers | ESG assessment | Supplier transformation |\n| Logistics | Route optimisation | Zero/low-carbon logistics |\n| Risk | Dual sourcing | Resilient regional networks |\n| Traceability | Supplier databases | Digital Product Passports |\n| Products | Eco-design | Circular products |\n| Agriculture | Sustainable sourcing | Regenerative supply |\n| People | Compliance | Living-wage / human-rights value chains |\n\n### 15. The sustainable supply-chain flywheel 📘 ⭐⭐\n**Measure → Prioritise → Redesign → Collaborate → Digitise → Circularise → Decarbonise → Regenerate → Measure again.** Each turn builds data, capability and savings that fund the next turn.\n\n### 16. The 7-step managerial roadmap 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n1. **Map** — map the entire supply network.\n2. **Measure** — carbon, water, material, social and risk hotspots.\n3. **Prioritise** — focus on material impacts rather than everything.\n4. **Redesign** — products, suppliers, logistics and network.\n5. **Collaborate** — work with suppliers, customers and ecosystem partners.\n6. **Digitise** — create traceability and real-time sustainability intelligence.\n7. **Transform** — move from incremental improvement to new circular/regenerative business models.\n*\"The future of SCM is not simply about being greener; it is about redesigning the supply chain so that economic value and sustainability reinforce each other.\"*\n\n### 17. Key takeaways (Lecture 11) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n- **Sustainability is a supply-chain design issue.**\n- **Scope 3 and supplier ecosystems matter more than the firm's own four walls.**\n- **Resilience and sustainability increasingly need to be designed together.**\n- **Circularity requires redesign of products, networks and business models — not just recycling.**\n- **The future sustainable supply chain will be digital, circular, low-carbon, resilient and increasingly regenerative.**\n\n### 18. Ten best practices to write in any long answer ⭐⭐⭐\n1. Measure the full footprint (Scopes 1–3, LCA) with MRV and assurance.\n2. Set science-based, time-bound targets and link them to executive pay.\n3. Make supplier ESG performance part of selection and volume allocation (not only codes of conduct).\n4. Develop suppliers (training, finance, technology transfer) instead of only auditing them.\n5. Switch to renewable electricity and electrify heat and transport where the grid allows.\n6. Design products for durability, repair, disassembly and recycled content.\n7. Redesign the network for fewer km, modal shift, consolidation and a reverse flow.\n8. Build traceability (blockchain, IoT, DPP) for high-risk materials.\n9. Use balanced scorecards with leading and lagging KPIs and the five tests.\n10. Collaborate across the industry (shared recycling, pre-competitive supplier programmes like Gigaton) and report credibly (BRSR Core, GRI, ISSB), avoiding greenwashing.\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam answers (CO6 3-markers) ⭐⭐⭐\n**Q1. What is the polycrisis facing supply chains?**\nSix simultaneous pressures: climate change (extreme weather), geopolitical fragmentation (sanctions, protectionism), critical-resource constraints (lithium, cobalt, rare earths), regulatory pressure (carbon reporting, due diligence, EPR, DPP), social risks (forced labour, living wages) and technology disruption (AI, automation). Supply chains of 2030 must be green and resilient at once.\n\n**Q2. Explain \"Do not outsource sustainability.\"**\nA firm's footprint and human-rights risks sit mostly with suppliers; if its own buying practices (extreme price pressure, short lead times) push suppliers to cut corners, it cannot claim a sustainable chain. Procurement must use balanced KPIs (cost, CO₂/unit, RE %, recycled content, safety, human rights, single-source dependency, traceability).\n\n**Q3. Distinguish mitigation and adaptation.**\nMitigation reduces the chain's impact on climate (RE, efficiency, modal shift); adaptation reduces its vulnerability to climate change (climate-risk siting, diversified suppliers, buffers). The best strategy does both.\n\n**Q4. What does \"JIT + JIC + JIS\" mean?**\nLean just-in-time flow combined with just-in-case buffers and dual sourcing for resilience and just-in-sequence delivery, in regional, diversified, digital, circular, low-carbon networks optimised for lowest life-cycle cost and risk, not lowest purchase price.\n\n**Q5. List the eight sustainability transitions.**\nFossil → renewable energy; virgin → circular materials; linear → circular products; global concentration → resilient diversification; opaque → traceable chains; ownership → access/service; reactive → predictive/AI management; reporting → sustainability embedded in decisions.\n"}, {"slug": "case-studies", "label": "Case Studies", "tag": "Case", "group": "practice", "title": "Case Studies — Course Cases with Exam Answers", "md": "# Case Studies — Course Cases with Exam Answers\nThe end-sem paper is based on the **complete syllabus including PPTs, reference books and case studies** 📘 (Lecture 11). This page condenses the long cases in the course zip — **ITC eChoupal** (HBS), **Tesla's battery supply chain** (WDI), the **TVS Motor and Tata Motors BRSR** reports and the **McKinsey 2026 trade** report — and the short slide cases (Walmart–IBM, Nestlé, Apple, Schneider, Walmart Gigaton, Unilever, M&M, IKEA, Renault, Tata Motors, DHL and others). Each case ends with likely exam questions and model answers. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers.\n\n---\n\n## 1. ITC eChoupal (HBS case 9-604-016, Upton & Fuller, 2003) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n### The problem\n- Agriculture was home to **72% of India's population and 60% of its workforce**.\n- ITC's International Business Division (IBD) had sales of only **₹450 crore** (1998) against ₹7,701 crore for the rest of ITC; soy was two-thirds of its agri exports. Target: **₹2,000 crore by 2005**.\n- Madhya Pradesh (\"soyabowl\") grew 4 of India's 5 million tonnes of soybean.\n- The **mandi** system was inefficient: farmers hauled crops 30–50 km, waited days (Bhopal mandi had 1,700 farmers a day), sold at auction to commission agents (CAs) who could buy low and sell to ITC at market price, crude beam scales cost about 0.5% of a lot, payments were delayed, and price discovery happened only after the crop was grown and carted.\n- Farmers **lost 60–70% of the potential value** of their crops; yields were a third to a quarter of global standards; risk aversion locked them in low-input, low-value farming.\n\n### The solution (May 1999)\n- A **computer kiosk in a lead farmer's home (the Sanchalak)**: PC, VSAT (in 75% of eChoupals) or phone line, **UPS and solar-battery backup**, dot-matrix printer. Set-up cost **₹1,70,000** per eChoupal plus ₹1,00,000 for people, training and software; payback expected within three years.\n- Website *soyachoupal.com* in Hindi: **localised weather** (70–80 km² pockets, via IMD), **best practices** (e.g. 18-inch seed spacing instead of 9 inches), crop information and soil testing, **market prices** (mandi prices, arrivals, Chicago Board of Trade outlook), Q&A forum, news, feedback.\n- **Sanchalak**: trusted literate farmer, takes a public oath, ITC logo on his house; aggregates sales and input orders; checks quality against control samples; earns **0.5% on soybean purchases** and **2–3% on inputs** (Jain of Dahod earned about ₹35,000 in a season).\n- **Samyojak**: former commission agents re-employed to set up eChoupals, run warehouse hubs, handle cash and logistics; commissions of 2–5% year-round (vs 1% as CA in peak months only).\n- **Hubs**: 5 processing units + 39 warehouses (44 points vs 51 big mandis); kiosk within 5 km and hub within 30 km of every farmer; **computerised weighbridge, cash on delivery, transport reimbursed**, shade, toilets, soil-testing lab, oil sold at the hub.\n- By 2003: **1,695 eChoupals covering 8,400 villages, 80% of MP's soybean and wheat areas**; procurement 50% mandi / 50% eChoupal (target 20/80).\n- Economics: ITC saved about **$5 per tonne** in freight and transaction costs; farmers earned about **$8 per tonne** more.\n- Policy: the state amended the **APMC Act** to allow purchases outside mandis, persuaded by the transparency of published prices.\n- Principles: **trust, transparency and freedom of choice** — no contract binds the farmer; information is free even if he sells at the mandi.\n- Scaling: **\"Roll Out, Fix It, Scale Up\"**; pilots in **coffee (Karnataka, Tradersnet e-market)**, **aqua (Andhra Pradesh, traceability premium)** and **wheat (UP, quality grading)**; future use as a rural **marketing and distribution channel** (fertilisers, agri-chemicals, seeds, insurance, credit). Chairman Deveshwar: *\"A commitment beyond the market.\"*\n\n### Sustainability lens (Lecture 1–2 \"delivery-based\" case) 📘\n| Pillar | Impact |\n|---|---|\n| Economic | Lower transaction and freight cost; better quality and less adulteration for ITC; higher farmer income; new rural sales channel |\n| Social | Empowered small farmers, price transparency, fair weighing and immediate payment, digital inclusion, entrepreneurship for Sanchalaks, intermediaries re-skilled rather than eliminated |\n| Environmental | Fewer wasted trips and less spoilage, best-practice inputs (right fertiliser via soil tests), solar-powered kiosks |\n\n**Q1. Why is eChoupal an SSCM case and not just an IT project?**\nIt redesigned the whole value chain (village → mandi → factory) to create value for farmers, intermediaries and ITC together: information flow (price discovery, weather, practices) was digitised while physical logistics was kept with existing intermediaries. It improved all three pillars and was built on trust and inclusion (Lecture 9 calls the trade-off \"technology sophistication vs inclusion\").\n\n**Q2. What roles did the Sanchalak and Samyojak play, and why keep the middlemen?**\nSanchalak: trusted village operator of the kiosk, aggregator and quality checker. Samyojak: ex-commission agent running hubs, cash and logistics. ITC kept the CAs' physical capability and local relationships but removed them from the information and price-setting flow, avoiding resistance and keeping costs low in weak infrastructure.\n\n**Q3. Are the eChoupal savings sustainable as markets become efficient?**\nLogistics savings shrink as mandis improve, so ITC built longer-term value through **traceability** (aqua premiums), **matching production to demand** (wheat quality grades) and an **electronic marketplace** (coffee Tradersnet), plus using the network as a rural distribution channel.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tesla's battery supply chain: a growing concern (WDI case, 2022) 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n**Situation**: Tesla had about 40% of EVs on the road and claimed *\"100 percent [of scrapped batteries] are recycled\"*, but global recycling capacity was in its infancy. By 2030 the world faces **1.2 Mt/yr** of end-of-life batteries; only **5%** of Li-ion batteries are recycled. Tesla expects **10,000** pack replacements by 2025 and **50,000/yr** by 2030. Upstream risks: cobalt (**65% from the DRC**, child labour, *\"blood diamond of batteries\"*), lithium (**Lithium Triangle**, **500,000 gallons of water per tonne**), nickel (Philippines closed **23 mines** in 2017; ~20% of supply), cell concentration (**CATL ~30%**). Second-life adaptation costs **$3,500 / $6,700 / $8,000** (pack / module / cell); recycling returns about **$5,600** per battery after 2025. Options: **build** in-house recycling, **buy** a start-up, or **partner** with **Redwood Materials** (JB Straubel; 95% recovery; $3.78B) or **Li-Cycle** (>95%; $1.6B raised). ESG raters disagree (**FTSE gave Tesla zero** on environment; MSCI high). Full analysis: [Energy Sector](ch09-energy-sector.md), numbers: [P7](p5-lca-energy-numericals.md).\n\n**Q1. Recommend build, buy or partner, with reasons.**\nPartner now with Redwood or Li-Cycle under long-term offtake/equity agreements (volumes too low and technology too immature to build; recyclers need pooled scale); design packs for disassembly; plan in-house or JV capacity at the Gigafactories as volumes approach 50,000/yr. This secures critical minerals, lowers Scope 3 and conflict-mineral exposure, and gives auditable recovery data for ESG ratings.\n\n**Q2. Is Tesla's \"100% recycled\" claim greenwashing?**\nAt risk of it: the claim lacks a stated boundary, methodology and evidence (Lecture 10's claim–metric–boundary–methodology–baseline–target–evidence test) while recycling capacity is small. Tesla should disclose recovery rates per material, volumes processed and partner audits.\n\n**Q3. Why do ESG ratings of Tesla differ so much?**\nDifferent boundaries and weights: FTSE looked at factory emissions (ignoring avoided tailpipe emissions), MSCI credited the product's clean-tech opportunity; Sustainalytics weighted risks such as labour and governance. Same lesson as LCA: boundaries decide results.\n\n---\n\n## 3. BRSR reports in the zip: TVS Motor and Tata Motors 📘 ⭐⭐\n- **TVS Motor (FY24)**: Scope 1 + 2 = **27,130.81 t** (−5.2% vs FY23); renewable electricity **93.2%** of electricity but only **48.8%** of total energy (fuels are fossil); Scope 3 = **2.27 crore t**, **99.88%** of the footprint, about 5.41 t per vehicle sold.\n- **Tata Motors CV business (FY25)**: Scope 1 + 2 = **1,75,161 t** (−20.8%); intensity 0.462 t per vehicle; Scope 3 = 14.49 crore t, of which **use of sold products = 96.3%**; Scope 3 ≈ 827 × Scope 1 + 2; assured by KPMG.\nFull calculations: [CO3 Problems 4–5](p2-carbon-accounting-numericals.md).\n\n**Q. What do these BRSR numbers tell an SCM manager?**\nThe plant is a tiny share of the footprint; the value chain (purchased parts and, above all, product use) dominates. Decarbonisation levers are therefore product efficiency and electrification, supplier engagement and end-of-life recovery, not only rooftop solar. Also, \"93% renewable\" can be true for electricity but misleading for total energy.\n\n---\n\n## 4. McKinsey Global Institute — Geopolitics and the geometry of global trade: 2026 update 📘 ⭐⭐\n- Trade in 2025 **did not retrench**: global goods trade grew about **6.5%**, faster than GDP; US imports and Chinese exports hit new highs.\n- **AI-related goods** (semiconductors, data-centre equipment) were about **one-third of global trade growth**, supplied by Taiwan, South Korea and Southeast Asia.\n- **China became a \"factory to the factories\"**, exporting industrial components and capital goods to emerging economies.\n- **US–China trade fell about 30%**; tariffs shifted **more than $165 billion** of trade away from the US–China corridor; the US replaced about two-thirds of the gap from other sellers; Chinese consumer-goods exporters cut prices about 8%.\n- ASEAN gained; the **EU faced a double squeeze** (more Chinese imports, higher US tariffs); **India gained ground in selected sectors** (e.g. electronics/smartphones).\n- Trade is **travelling shorter geopolitical distances** (reorienting towards aligned partners).\n- Message: durable shifts (AI, emerging-market growth, geopolitics) plus abrupt shocks → **long-term thinking coupled with agility**.\n\n**Q. How does this connect to SSCM strategy?**\nIt is the \"geopolitical fragmentation\" element of Lecture 11's polycrisis: supply chains must move from global-concentrated-lowest-cost to **regional, diversified, digital, circular and low-carbon** networks (JIT + JIC + JIS), with dual sourcing and scenario planning, while watching that regionalisation does not raise emissions or exclude small suppliers.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Short cases from the slides 📘 ⭐⭐⭐\n| Case | Lecture | Key facts | Lesson |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **HUL sustainable sourcing (2010)** | 1–2 | Smallholder support, regenerative and climate-smart farming, resilient sourcing, lower wastage | Sustainability integrated into sourcing |\n| **EV battery circular chain** (Tata Autocomp, Attero, Lohum) | 1–2 | Lithium shortage → recycling and reverse logistics under EPR | Recovery-based SSCM |\n| **Hindustan Zinc + GreenLine (2025)** | 1–2 | LNG and EV trucks, ₹400 crore | Reduction-based; cost vs emissions vs infrastructure |\n| **Mahindra carbon neutrality (2022)** | 2 | RE, EV ecosystem, green logistics, EPR, digitalisation | Strategic leadership in mobility |\n| **Flipkart (2022)** | 2 | Recyclable packaging, last-mile EVs, route optimisation | E-commerce decarbonisation |\n| **UltraTech, Tata Steel, Amul, Bisleri** | 4 | LCA hotspots and actions | LCA drives improvement |\n| **Nestlé** | 6 | Responsible sourcing, regenerative agriculture, supplier training, deforestation-free, digital traceability | Procurement creates resilience |\n| **Schneider Electric Zero Carbon** | 6 | −50% footprint for top 1,000 suppliers; 70% of emissions; analytics → ambition → action | Net zero runs through the supply base |\n| **Walmart Project Gigaton** | 6 | 1 Gt by 2030; six pillars; WWF + EDF; Sustainability Hub | Supplier-led Scope 3 cuts |\n| **Asian Paints / JSW Steel** | 6 | Lower-carbon grades, ≥20% bio-based; Synesgy supplier ESG self-assessment | ESG in supplier selection |\n| **Tata Steel, Asian Paints, Dalmia, M&M, ITC, Arvind** | 7 | RE shares, ZLD, low-carbon cement, zero waste, 100% recycled water | Sustainable manufacturing pays |\n| **DHL** | 8 | Six green-logistics levers | Network-wide decarbonisation |\n| **IKEA / Renault Refactory** | 8 | Buy-back, repair, resale; refurbishment, battery repair, remanufacturing | Circular business models |\n| **Mahindra CERO / Tata Motors Prolife** | 8 | Vehicle recycling; remanufacturing aggregates | Remanufacturing > recycling |\n| **Walmart–IBM blockchain** | 9 | Mango tracing 7 days → 2.2 s | Traceability vs cost/energy |\n| **Asian Paints digital** | 9 | AI/ML, IoT, digital twins, robotics | Automation vs investment/energy |\n| **Navya Consumer Products** | 9 | ₹80 cr capex, ₹12 cr/yr; benefits −15/−8/−20/+15% | Pilot before scaling ([worked](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md)) |\n| **Unilever** | 10 | 81% key crops sustainable (95% by 2030); >95% deforestation-free | Agri sustainability is an SC problem |\n| **M&M supplier ESG** | 10 | Code signed by 95% of suppliers by value; due diligence 68.1% | Assessment must change buying |\n| **Company A vs B** | 10 | Report vs real cuts (energy −25%, CO₂/unit −30% …) | The report is not the performance |\n| **Apple** | 11 | >60% below 2015; 17.8 GW supplier RE; 21.8 Mt avoided | OEM orchestrates supplier sustainability |\n| **Tata Motors Re.Wi.Re + Project Aalingana** | 11 | Vehicle scrapping network; decarbonisation + circularity + nature | Where is value created in circular chains? |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Assignment-style cases (answers in the chapters) 📘\n- **Design a sustainable chai supply chain** (Lectures 1–2) → [ch01 §7](ch01-sscm-business-performance.md).\n- **A medium-size company exports to Europe**: risks, stakeholders, KPIs, strategy (Lecture 2) → [ch01 §7](ch01-sscm-business-performance.md).\n- **Apparel exporter's KPI dashboards** (Lecture 3) → [CO5 Problem 3](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md).\n- **Laptop supplier scorecard** (Lecture 6) → [CO2 P6](p1-supplier-selection-numericals.md).\n- **Textile factory: lean, green, digital solutions** (Lecture 7) → [ch04](ch04-sustainable-manufacturing.md).\n- **Consumer-electronics network design** (Lecture 8) → [CO4 P10](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md).\n- **FMCG distribution: develop 5 KPIs** (Lecture 10) → [CO4 P3](p3-network-logistics-numericals.md), [CO5 P5](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md).\n- **Navya: adopt, pilot or reject?** (Lecture 9) → [CO5 P11](p4-performance-measurement-numericals.md).\n"}, {"slug": "exam-questions-qna", "label": "Exam Q&A Bank", "tag": "Q&A", "group": "practice", "title": "Exam Q&A Bank — Short and Long Theory Answers", "md": "# Exam Q&A Bank — Short and Long Theory Answers\nThe end-sem paper (40 marks) mixes numericals with **theory interpretation and application sub-questions**, and **CO1 and CO6 are asked as 3-mark short theory questions** 📘. This bank gives model answers sized for the marks. Numericals are on the five problem pages. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers.\n\n**How to write a 3-mark answer**: one-line definition → 3–4 points or a mini-table → one example (Indian if possible) → one-line conclusion. About 6–8 lines.\n\n---\n\n## CO1 — SSCM fundamentals and business performance (3 marks each)\n\n**Q1. Define Sustainable Supply Chain Management. How does it differ from traditional SCM?**\nSSCM manages material, information and money flows and co-operation across the chain while integrating economic, environmental and social goals from stakeholder requirements (Seuring & Müller 2008). Differences: objective (cost/efficiency → balanced TBL value), horizon (short → long term), flow (linear \"cradle to grave\" → circular \"cradle to cradle\"), suppliers (price-based → development-based), metrics (cost, speed, quality → plus carbon, water, waste, human rights, resilience), scope (own facilities → full value chain/Scope 3). Example: ITC eChoupal.\n\n**Q2. Explain the Triple Bottom Line with supply-chain examples.**\nElkington's TBL measures People, Planet and Profit. Environmental: energy, water, packaging waste, fuel (Delhi AQI, Bengaluru floods). Social: labour welfare, supplier ethics, safety, diversity (gig workers, delivery staff). Economic: carbon cost, waste cost, ESG investment, brand value (fuel imports, crop damage). A chain is sustainable only when all three are met together.\n\n**Q3. \"Old thinking vs new reality\" — explain four shifts.**\nCheapest supplier wins → ethical supplier wins; faster delivery → lower emissions; maximum production → circular production; waste is unavoidable → waste is a design failure. Drivers: groundwater depletion, fast fashion, food waste, EV-battery waste, coal waste.\n\n**Q4. Distinguish cradle-to-grave and cradle-to-cradle.**\nCradle-to-grave covers extraction to disposal, with responsibility ending at end-of-life (linear SCM). Cradle-to-cradle designs products so materials return into new products or safely to nature (circular SSCM), using reverse logistics, remanufacturing and recycling (e.g. Attero/Lohum battery recovery, Renault Refactory).\n\n**Q5. Explain the sustainable supply-chain framework stage by stage.**\nProcurement: ethical, local sourcing, supplier audits. Manufacturing: renewable energy, lean, waste minimisation. Logistics: EV fleets, route optimisation, multimodal. Warehousing: natural lighting, smart energy, rooftop solar. Reverse logistics: recycling, returns, circular economy. Together they move SCM from linear to circular.\n\n**Q6. Give four Indian SSCM cases and classify them.**\nITC eChoupal (delivery-based: farmer connectivity, price transparency); HUL sustainable sourcing (delivery-based: regenerative farming); EV-battery circular chain — Tata Autocomp, Attero, Lohum (recovery-based, EPR); Hindustan Zinc–GreenLine LNG/EV trucks and Mahindra carbon neutrality (reduction-based).\n\n**Q7. Define sustainable supply-chain strategy and explain why it matters.**\nA long-term plan integrating economic, environmental and social objectives while maintaining competitiveness and profitability. It matters because of regulation (BRSR, CBAM, CSDDD, carbon markets), investors (ESG ratings, cost of capital), customers (audits, green preference) and operational gains (energy, waste, logistics savings). A chain profitable today may be \"illegal, expensive or unacceptable tomorrow\".\n\n**Q8. What are the risks of ignoring sustainability?**\nExport barriers (CBAM, deforestation rules), carbon taxes, supply disruptions (climate, water), reputation damage (labour scandals), investor pressure (divestment, higher cost of capital) and customer loss.\n\n**Q9. Explain Scope 1, 2 and 3 in one line each with examples.**\nScope 1 \"what you burn\": diesel in DG sets, company vehicles, process and fugitive emissions. Scope 2 \"what you buy\": purchased electricity, heat, cooling. Scope 3 \"what you influence\": purchased goods, logistics, product use, end-of-life — usually 70–90%+ of the total.\n\n**Q10. List the five steps of strategy development.**\nAssess current state (carbon footprint, network, supplier performance, waste, energy) → analyse stakeholders (who influences, who is impacted, expectations) → develop scenarios (carbon pricing, disruptions, energy cost, water scarcity) → design strategy (green sourcing, RE, circularity, supplier development, network redesign) → operationalise (projects, KPIs, budgets, responsibilities).\n\n**Q11. Explain stakeholder mapping in SSCM.**\nIdentify internal (employees, management, shareholders) and external stakeholders (customers, suppliers, regulators, communities, NGOs, investors, lenders), their expectations and power/interest; manage high-power/high-interest groups closely. \"Successful supply chains don't just deliver products — they deliver value to stakeholders.\"\n\n**Q12. Draw/describe a Sustainability Balanced Scorecard.**\nFinancial: energy savings, logistics-cost reduction, waste-reduction savings, ROI of sustainability projects. Customer: sustainable product sales, satisfaction, ESG ratings, supplier compliance. Internal process: carbon emissions, water usage, transport efficiency, SC management. Learning & growth: employee training, sustainability culture, innovation projects.\n\n**Q13. How does SSCM improve business performance?**\nLower cost (energy, waste, fuel), higher revenue and market access (green products, EU eligibility), lower risk and better resilience, lower cost of capital (ESG ratings), stronger brand and talent, innovation in business models (PaaS, remanufacturing). Lecture 10's Company B (energy −25%, CO₂/unit −30%, waste −60%) outperforms Company A's glossy report.\n\n**Q14. Distinguish traditional and sustainable performance measurement.**\nTraditional: financial, lagging, short-term, shareholders, firm boundary. Sustainable: TBL plus governance and resilience, leading and lagging, long-term, all stakeholders, whole value chain. \"If you cannot measure it, you cannot improve it.\"\n\n**Q15. What are Q + D + I + R + S performance dimensions?**\nQuality (defect PPM, returns, warranty, Six Sigma level), Delivery (OTD, cycle time, lead time, reliability), Innovation (new products, R&D, patents, automation), Resilience (disruptions, recovery time, supplier diversity, continuity), Sustainability (carbon, energy, water, safety, ethical sourcing %).\n\n---\n\n## CO6 — Technology trade-offs and SSCM strategies (3 marks each)\n\n**Q16. \"Technology ≠ sustainability.\" Explain.**\nTechnology can cut transport emissions, inventory and waste and improve safety and traceability, but it can raise energy use, e-waste, capital outlay, cyber risk and digital-infrastructure CO₂. The link technology → operational performance → sustainability is not linear; potential ≠ realised benefit.\n\n**Q17. Explain the five hidden trade-offs of technology adoption.**\n(1) Efficiency vs environmental footprint (electricity, equipment manufacture, e-waste). (2) Traceability vs energy (blockchain). (3) Visibility vs privacy (cybersecurity, surveillance, data ownership). (4) Global optimisation vs local resilience. (5) Digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion — Tier-1 improves while Tier-2/3 are excluded.\n\n**Q18. Explain the technology–sustainability decision matrix.**\nBusiness value (low/high) × sustainability impact (low/high): low–low Avoid; low value–high impact Reconsider; high value–low impact Optimise; high–high Prioritise. Then score with TAS and NTV.\n\n**Q19. State TAS and Net Technology Value.**\nTAS = 25% Economic + 25% Environmental + 20% Social + 15% Resilience + 15% Strategic fit (1–5 each). NTV = Gross benefits − Adoption cost − Lifecycle sustainability cost − Risk cost.\n\n**Q20. What is resilience as the fourth pillar?**\nLecture 9 adds resilience — visibility, agility, recovery, response, assurance — to environmental, social and economic pillars (Carter–Rogers framework: environmentally friendly + socially ethical + economically viable, supported by risk management, transparency, strategy, culture).\n\n**Q21. Describe the Supply Chain 4.0 technology pyramid.**\nFrom the base: ERP/digital integration → automation/robotics/AGVs → cloud/platforms → blockchain → IoT/sensors/RFID → digital twins → advanced analytics/ML → AI/GenAI. Applied across sourcing, production, warehousing, transport, inventory, demand planning, reverse logistics and supplier management.\n\n**Q22. Explain the Walmart–IBM blockchain case.**\nFood-contamination tracing took days; Walmart and IBM built a Hyperledger Fabric blockchain with IoT data and QR codes; mango provenance fell from 7 days to 2.2 seconds. Benefits: faster recalls, less waste, safety, consumer trust. Trade-offs: integration cost, supplier capacity, data quality, interoperability, governance, scalability.\n\n**Q23. List the Tech-Sustain 7-step framework.**\nDefine the problem → establish the baseline (cost, CO₂, waste, service, risk) → identify alternatives → evaluate total lifecycle impact (capex + opex + energy + people + e-waste + cyber) → evaluate the TBL → stress-test unintended consequences → pilot → measure → scale. \"Don't digitise the problem — transform it sustainably.\"\n\n**Q24. Should Navya adopt, pilot or reject Project SUSTAIN_X? (short)**\nPilot. Gross benefits of about ₹46 cr/yr (assumed bases) against ₹12 cr opex, ₹80 cr capex and risk costs give a positive NPV and TAS ≈ 3.8, but NPV turns negative if less than ~87% of benefits arrive, and 1,500 suppliers/8,000 distributors raise inclusion risk. Pilot one plant and a supplier subset with stage-gates, then scale.\n\n**Q25. Explain the polycrisis facing supply chains.**\nClimate change, geopolitical fragmentation, critical-resource constraints, regulatory pressure, social risks and technology disruption — simultaneously. The 2030 chain must be green and resilient (WEF Global Risks Report 2026).\n\n**Q26. List the five big sustainability challenges and the strategic shift.**\nCarbon (decarbonise the value chain), resources (more with less), circularity (closed loops), people (protect workers and communities), resilience (survive disruption without shifting costs). Shift: efficiency → resilience → sustainability → regeneration.\n\n**Q27. Mitigation vs adaptation in supply chains.**\nMitigation reduces the chain's climate impact (RE, efficiency, modal shift, low-carbon materials). Adaptation reduces vulnerability to climate change (climate-risk site selection, water planning, multi-port and multi-supplier networks, buffers). Best strategy = both.\n\n**Q28. Explain the 7-step strategy architecture.**\nDiagnose hotspots → prioritise high-impact/high-risk activities → redesign products, suppliers, processes, logistics, network → collaborate with suppliers, customers, government, competitors, communities → measure carbon, circularity, cost, service, social → innovate (digital, AI, clean tech, new business models) → scale (embed in procurement, investment, governance).\n\n**Q29. \"Do not outsource sustainability.\" Explain.**\nMost impacts are in the supply base; a buyer whose price squeezes and erratic orders push suppliers into unsafe or polluting practices cannot claim a sustainable chain. Procurement KPIs must cover total cost, CO₂/unit, RE %, recycled content, water, safety, human-rights assessments, single-source dependency and traceability.\n\n**Q30. \"The cleanest kilometre is the kilometre eliminated.\" Explain.**\nGreen trucks are not the same as sustainable logistics; first remove km through network redesign, consolidation, better packaging cube, fewer empty miles, sensible delivery frequency and fewer returns, then shift modes and switch fuels.\n\n**Q31. List the eight sustainability transitions.**\nFossil → renewable/electrified energy; virgin → circular materials; linear → circular products; global concentration → resilient diversification; opaque → traceable chains; ownership → access/service models; reactive → predictive/AI management; reporting → sustainability embedded in decisions.\n\n**Q32. What is the critical-minerals paradox?**\nThe green transition (EVs, batteries, solar, wind, grids) multiplies demand for lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, graphite and rare earths, whose mining has water, biodiversity, emission, social and geopolitical impacts. IEA 2025: lithium demand +~30% in 2024; 7% of copper supply exposed to flood/drought.\n\n**Q33. Explain JIT + JIC + JIS.**\nThe old model (global, concentrated, lowest cost, pure JIT) gives way to regional, diversified, digital, circular, low-carbon networks that combine just-in-time efficiency with just-in-case buffers and dual sourcing and just-in-sequence delivery, optimising cost + service + carbon + circularity + risk + social impact for lowest life-cycle cost and risk.\n\n**Q34. What is the strategic lesson of Apple's supply-chain programme?**\nApple treats sustainability as a value-chain transformation (supplier RE, recycled cobalt and rare earths, cleaner semiconductors, disassembly), reaching >60% below 2015 with 17.8 GW of supplier RE and 21.8 Mt avoided in 2024: the OEM orchestrates supplier sustainability.\n\n**Q35. Explain the sustainable supply-chain flywheel and the 7-step roadmap.**\nFlywheel: measure → prioritise → redesign → collaborate → digitise → circularise → decarbonise → regenerate → measure again. Roadmap: map → measure → prioritise → redesign → collaborate → digitise → transform (incremental to circular/regenerative models).\n\n**Q36. Why is quality a sustainability issue? (Lecture 10, quiz-relevant)**\nDefects → rework → extra materials and energy → extra transport → extra waste → higher CO₂; quality at source prevents waste. Each TIMWOOD waste creates cost + energy + material + carbon + time. Link: TQM + Lean + Six Sigma + green SCM; ISO 9001 + 14001 + 45001 as an integrated system.\n\n---\n\n## Theory sub-questions for CO2–CO5 (interpretation and application)\n\n**Q37. (CO2) Why should weighted supplier scoring be followed by sensitivity analysis?**\nWeights are subjective; if a small change in weights reverses the ranking, the decision is fragile. Sensitivity shows the weight range in which a supplier stays best (e.g. Lecture 6's model: raising quality weight to 30% switches the winner from C to A), and supports negotiation and supplier development targets.\n\n**Q38. (CO2) Compare weighted scoring, AHP and TOPSIS.**\nWeighted scoring: simple, transparent; subjective weights; compensatory. AHP: derives weights from pairwise comparisons on a 1–9 scale with a consistency ratio (CR < 0.10); good when criteria importance is disputed. TOPSIS: ranks by closeness to the ideal and distance from the anti-ideal after vector normalisation; handles many quantitative criteria. All are compensatory, so add knock-out rules for human-rights or safety failures.\n\n**Q39. (CO2) Supplier A is 12% cheaper but has child-labour allegations. What do you do?**\nDo not select on price. Apply a knock-out/minimum-threshold rule for human rights, compute TCO including reputational and legal risk (CSDDD, customer audits), prefer Supplier B (RE, ISO 14001, zero human-rights issues), and if A is strategic, require a time-bound corrective plan with third-party audits before any award.\n\n**Q40. (CO3) What is MRV and why is it vital for CBAM exporters?**\nMeasure (activity data: kWh, litres, m³, production), Report (BRSR, CBAM filings, CDP), Verify (internal audit, third-party assurance by bodies like Bureau Veritas, TÜV). Verified embedded emissions avoid punitive default values under CBAM and build investor trust.\n\n**Q41. (CO3) Why can Scope 1 fall while the total footprint rises?**\nFirms outsource energy-intensive steps, buy more carbon-intensive inputs or sell more high-use-phase products; Scope 3 rises faster than Scope 1 falls. Hence \"carbon accounting must follow the value chain\".\n\n**Q42. (CO3) Interpret: a plant's Scope 1 + 2 is 1.1% of its total emissions (ICEV + EV case).**\nPlant energy projects alone cannot materially decarbonise the company; the levers are product use (efficiency, EV share, clean grid), purchased materials (green steel, aluminium, battery chemistry) and end-of-life. That is why Toyota and Tesla focus on supplier decarbonisation, RE, battery recycling and green logistics.\n\n**Q43. (CO3) Location-based vs market-based Scope 2?**\nLocation-based uses the average grid factor of where electricity is consumed; market-based uses the factor of contracts bought (PPAs, RECs, green tariffs). Report both; market-based shows the effect of procurement choices (e.g. Tata Motors' iREC-based Scope 2).\n\n**Q44. (CO4) Why is the cheapest network not necessarily the best sustainable network?**\nCost-minimal networks may have long distances, high CO₂, low recovery and fragile single sources. With a carbon price (λ) or recovery value (μ) in the objective, a dearer network (e.g. Network C, ₹115, 55 CO₂, 70% recovery) becomes optimal above λ ≈ 0.35. Decision-maker priorities and Pareto efficiency define \"best\".\n\n**Q45. (CO4) Interpret the result that the CO₂-optimal number of DCs is higher than the cost-optimal number.**\nMore DCs shorten outbound truck-km (less CO₂) but add facility and inventory cost; the gap shows the implicit carbon price needed (e.g. about ₹30,000/t in P7) to justify the extra DC. Managers can close it with green DCs, consolidation or rail on primary legs instead.\n\n**Q46. (CO4) Why is reverse logistics harder than forward, and how do you design the collection network?**\nUncertain quantity, timing and condition, low density, high collection/sorting cost, information gaps, non-standard products, consumer participation. Choose decentralised, centralised or hybrid collection by volume, distance, product value, recovery technology and cost; route each return to the highest-value option (resale → repair → remanufacture → recycle → dispose).\n\n**Q47. (CO4) Explain the centre-of-gravity method and its limitations.**\nCx = Σ xᵢWᵢ ÷ ΣWᵢ and Cy = Σ yᵢWᵢ ÷ ΣWᵢ give the weighted-average location minimising (approximately) ton-distance. Limitations: straight-line distance, ignores roads, land cost, terrain, labour, RE availability and social impact; the CoG point may be infeasible, so compare nearby feasible sites with load-distance and factor rating.\n\n**Q48. (CO5) What is the KPI trap and what makes a good measurement system?**\nToo many KPIs (75 sustainability, 40 supplier, 30 logistics) yet no answer to \"are we becoming more sustainable?\". A good system passes five tests — material, balanced, actionable, comparable, credible — and each KPI answers what happened, why, who owns it, what decision changes.\n\n**Q49. (CO5) Explain leading vs lagging KPIs with SCOR examples.**\nLeading: supplier ESG assessments, training, RE contracts, eco-design projects, preventive maintenance. Lagging: supplier incidents, injury rate, Scope 2, product footprint, failures. SCOR + sustainability: Plan carbon-aware planning, Source supplier score, Make energy/unit, Deliver CO₂e/shipment, Return recovery rate, Enable ESG data quality.\n\n**Q50. (CO5) A composite sustainability index is 100.3% \"on target\". Comment.**\nComposites hide failures: over-achievement on cost and carbon can mask safety and recycling misses. Report by perspective, cap achievement ratios, add knock-out thresholds for safety and human rights, and review whether targets are benchmark- or science-based.\n\n**Q51. (CO5) What does BRSR Core change for supply-chain managers?**\nIt moves from disclosure to assured KPIs across nine ESG attributes (top 150 entities from FY2023–24, rising towards 1,000), so managers need data ownership, internal controls, a measurement methodology, supplier data, audit readiness and evidence trails. \"Don't just report sustainability. Prove it.\"\n\n**Q52. (CO5) Activity vs outcome: \"100% of suppliers trained in ESG\" — is it proof of improvement?**\nNo — it is an activity (input/process). Proof is outcomes: supplier emissions per unit down, violations down, audit scores up. Measure the whole chain inputs → processes → outputs → outcomes → impact.\n\n---\n\n## Long-answer questions (8–10 marks)\n\n**Q53. Design a sustainable supply-chain strategy for an Indian auto-component exporter facing CBAM.**\nDiagnose: product carbon footprint (cradle-to-gate, ISO 14067), Scope 1–3 inventory with MRV; hotspots are steel/aluminium inputs and grid power. Prioritise: CBAM-covered products and top customers. Redesign: green steel/aluminium sourcing (scrap-based EAF), RE PPAs and rooftop solar, process efficiency (OEE, heat recovery), packaging and logistics consolidation, rail to port. Collaborate: supplier development and data sharing (Synesgy-style self-assessment), customer co-investment. Measure: kg CO₂e per part, RE %, supplier ESG coverage, OTIF, cost per unit; BSC with leading/lagging KPIs. Innovate: digital product passport-ready traceability, carbon-accounting software. Scale: embed in supplier selection (weighted ESG + RTI scorecard), capex approvals with an internal carbon price, governance. Report via BRSR Core and CBAM declarations; avoid greenwashing.\n\n**Q54. Explain LCA with its four phases and apply it to a PET water bottle.**\nGoal and scope: compare PET vs rPET bottles; functional unit \"delivering 1 litre of drinking water\"; cradle-to-grave. Inventory: resin, energy for blow moulding, filling, transport km, end-of-life split. Impact: GWP, fossil depletion, plastic leakage, water. Interpretation: hotspots are resin and transport (Bisleri), so lightweighting, rPET, solar power and rail cut impacts; sensitivity on recycling rate. Limits: data intensity, allocation, regional variation.\n\n**Q55. Discuss reverse logistics and the circular economy in network design with Indian examples.**\nDefinition, touch points and condition-based destinations; difficulties; collection configurations; closed-loop architecture; R-strategy hierarchy; circular design; PaaS; nine-node model; strategic/tactical/operational decisions; objective function with returns balance; KPIs (recovery, reuse, remanufacture rates). Examples: Tata Motors Prolife and Re.Wi.Re, Mahindra CERO, Attero/Lohum, Furlenco, JSW-MG BaaS, IKEA buy-back.\n\n**Q56. Critically evaluate technology adoption for sustainability using Lecture 9.**\nTechnology ≠ sustainability; SC 4.0 landscape and the problem–technology table; five hidden trade-offs; 2×2 matrix; TAS and NTV; Tech-Sustain seven steps; Walmart–IBM and Asian Paints cases; Navya recommendation (pilot); takeaways: enabler not strategy, one KPI can damage another, full life cycle, ecosystems, best ≠ most advanced.\n\n**Q57. Explain the role of sustainable procurement in business performance and supplier selection.**\n50–80% of cost and most Scope 3; drivers with examples (Apple, IKEA, Unilever, Microsoft, CSDDD, CBAM, Nestlé, Toyota, Tata Motors, Starbucks, Patagonia, Nike, Intel, Walmart); benefits; cost vs TCO; KPIs; ESG + RTI criteria; tools (weighted, AHP, TOPSIS, DEA, LP); risk matrix; segmentation and development; Schneider, Walmart Gigaton, Asian Paints, JSW; challenges.\n\n**Q58. Explain sustainability reporting frameworks and the risk of greenwashing.**\nWhy report; CDP, GRI 305, TCFD four pillars, Integrated Reporting six capitals, ISSB S1–S2, BRSR 9 principles and Sections A–C, BRSR Core; GRI = impacts, ISSB = investor risks, BRSR = Indian regulation; Company A vs B; greenwashing vs greenhushing; good reporting = claim, metric, boundary, methodology, baseline, target, evidence; MRV and assurance.\n"}, {"slug": "mcq-bank", "label": "MCQ Bank", "tag": "MCQ", "group": "practice", "title": "MCQ Bank — Quiz (OLT) and Quick Checks", "md": "# MCQ Bank — Quiz (OLT) and Quick Checks\n**The 20-mark online quiz is MCQ-based on CO5 and CO6, from PPTs 9, 10 and 11 only** 📘, so the first three sections (Lectures 9, 10, 11) are the largest. The later sections cover the rest of the syllabus for end-sem revision. Turn on **Self-test** (`T`) to hide answers. The **Quiz** tab also generates random questions from the glossary.\n\n---\n\n## Lecture 9 — Technology adoption and trade-offs (quiz) ⭐⭐⭐\n\n**Q1. According to Lecture 9, the relationship \"technology adoption → operational performance → sustainability performance\" is: (a) Always linear (b) Not guaranteed to be linear (c) Always negative (d) Irrelevant to SSCM**\n**Answer: (b) Not guaranteed to be linear** — potential benefit ≠ realised benefit.\n\n**Q2. Which of these can technology INCREASE in a supply chain? (a) Transport emissions only (b) Electronic waste and energy consumption (c) Inventory only (d) Traceability only**\n**Answer: (b) Electronic waste and energy consumption** — along with capital outlay, cyber risk and digital-infrastructure CO₂.\n\n**Q3. Lecture 9 adds which \"4th pillar\" to sustainable network design? (a) Governance (b) Innovation (c) Resilience (d) Technology**\n**Answer: (c) Resilience** — visibility, agility, recovery, response, assurance.\n\n**Q4. The framework \"environmentally friendly + socially ethical + economically viable\" is attributed to: (a) Kaplan–Norton (b) Carter–Rogers (c) Elkington (d) Klöpffer**\n**Answer: (b) Carter–Rogers**\n\n**Q5. At the top of the Supply Chain 4.0 pyramid is: (a) ERP (b) Cloud platforms (c) AI / GenAI (d) Blockchain**\n**Answer: (c) AI / GenAI** — ERP/digital integration is the base.\n\n**Q6. At the base of the Supply Chain 4.0 pyramid is: (a) IoT (b) ERP / digital integration (c) Digital twins (d) Robotics**\n**Answer: (b) ERP / digital integration**\n\n**Q7. In the problem–technology table, \"empty trucks\" are addressed by: (a) Blockchain (b) AI route optimisation (c) Digital product passports (d) Robotics**\n**Answer: (b) AI route optimisation** — lower fuel/CO₂.\n\n**Q8. \"Product recalls\" are addressed in the table by: (a) Blockchain — faster traceability (b) IoT — less food waste (c) Digital twins (d) AI forecasting**\n**Answer: (a) Blockchain — faster traceability**\n\n**Q9. \"Food spoilage\" is best tackled by: (a) RFID for returns (b) IoT sensors (c) ERP (d) Robotics**\n**Answer: (b) IoT sensors** — less food waste.\n\n**Q10. Digital twins in the table provide: (a) Material traceability (b) Scenario planning / better network decisions (c) Supplier ESG monitoring (d) Faster recalls**\n**Answer: (b) Scenario planning / better network decisions**\n\n**Q11. Which is NOT one of Lecture 9's five hidden trade-offs? (a) Efficiency vs environmental footprint (b) Traceability vs energy (c) Price vs quality (d) Digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion**\n**Answer: (c) Price vs quality** — the five are efficiency vs footprint, traceability vs energy, visibility vs privacy, global optimisation vs local resilience, digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion.\n\n**Q12. \"Technology can improve Tier-1 sustainability while worsening Tier-2/Tier-3 inclusion\" describes: (a) Visibility vs privacy (b) Digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion (c) Global optimisation vs local resilience (d) Traceability vs energy**\n**Answer: (b) Digital inclusion vs supplier exclusion**\n\n**Q13. Blockchain's CO₂ footprint and high energy needs illustrate the trade-off: (a) Traceability vs energy (b) Efficiency vs footprint (c) Visibility vs privacy (d) Inclusion vs exclusion**\n**Answer: (a) Traceability vs energy**\n\n**Q14. In the technology–sustainability matrix, high business value + low sustainability impact = (a) Avoid (b) Reconsider (c) Optimise (d) Prioritise**\n**Answer: (c) Optimise**\n\n**Q15. Low business value + high sustainability impact = (a) Avoid (b) Reconsider (c) Optimise (d) Prioritise**\n**Answer: (b) Reconsider**\n\n**Q16. The weight of the Social dimension in the Technology Adoption Score is: (a) 15% (b) 20% (c) 25% (d) 10%**\n**Answer: (b) 20%** — TAS = 25% Eco + 25% Env + 20% Soc + 15% Resilience + 15% Strategic fit.\n\n**Q17. TAS dimensions are each scored on a scale of: (a) 0–10 (b) 1–5 (c) 1–9 (d) 0–100**\n**Answer: (b) 1–5**\n\n**Q18. A technology scores Eco 4, Env 4, Soc 3, Res 4, Fit 4. Its TAS is: (a) 3.6 (b) 3.8 (c) 4.0 (d) 3.75**\n**Answer: (b) 3.8** — 1.0 + 1.0 + 0.6 + 0.6 + 0.6.\n\n**Q19. Net Technology Value = Gross benefits − Adoption cost − Lifecycle sustainability cost − ___: (a) Tax (b) Risk cost (c) Depreciation (d) Opportunity cost**\n**Answer: (b) Risk cost**\n\n**Q20. The Walmart–IBM mango pilot reduced provenance tracing from 7 days to: (a) 2.2 hours (b) 2.2 minutes (c) 2.2 seconds (d) 22 seconds**\n**Answer: (c) 2.2 seconds** — Hyperledger Fabric blockchain.\n\n**Q21. Walmart and IBM's traceability system was built on: (a) Ethereum (b) Hyperledger Fabric (c) Bitcoin (d) Corda**\n**Answer: (b) Hyperledger Fabric**\n\n**Q22. Asian Paints' digital-manufacturing initiatives aim at all EXCEPT: (a) Improving asset utilisation (b) Reducing working capital (c) Reducing energy and water use (d) Increasing finished-goods inventory**\n**Answer: (d) Increasing finished-goods inventory**\n\n**Q23. Step 4 of the Tech-Sustain framework is: (a) Define the problem (b) Evaluate total lifecycle impact (c) Pilot → measure → scale (d) Establish the baseline**\n**Answer: (b) Evaluate total lifecycle impact** — capex + opex + energy + people + e-waste + cybersecurity.\n\n**Q24. Step 6 of the Tech-Sustain framework asks: (a) \"What is our ROI?\" (b) \"What could become worse because of this technology?\" (c) \"Who owns the data?\" (d) \"Which vendor is cheapest?\"**\n**Answer: (b)** — stress-test unintended consequences.\n\n**Q25. The Tech-Sustain framework warns against jumping directly from: (a) Baseline to target (b) Proof of concept to enterprise-wide implementation (c) Pilot to measurement (d) Cost to value**\n**Answer: (b) Proof of concept to enterprise-wide implementation**\n\n**Q26. The slogan under the managerial decision framework is: (a) \"Measure first, report later\" (b) \"Don't digitise the problem — transform it sustainably\" (c) \"Reduction first, offset later\" (d) \"Do not outsource sustainability\"**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q27. In the case portfolio, ITC e-Choupal's main trade-off is: (a) Traceability vs cost (b) Technology sophistication vs inclusion (c) Automation vs investment (d) Data value vs complexity**\n**Answer: (b) Technology sophistication vs inclusion**\n\n**Q28. The Thai tuna case combines: (a) AI + blockchain (b) Robotics + IoT (c) Digital twin + ERP (d) Drones + RFID**\n**Answer: (a) AI + blockchain** — data value vs complexity.\n\n**Q29. Which is a Lecture 9 key takeaway? (a) The most advanced technology is always best (b) Technology is an enabler, not a sustainability strategy (c) Optimising one KPI improves all others (d) Measure only the operating phase**\n**Answer: (b)** — and \"the best technology is not the most advanced technology\".\n\n**Q30. Navya Consumer Products' Project SUSTAIN_X requires an initial investment of: (a) ₹12 crore (b) ₹80 crore (c) ₹46 crore (d) ₹120 crore**\n**Answer: (b) ₹80 crore** — plus ₹12 crore/yr operating cost.\n\n**Q31. Navya's expected product-waste reduction is: (a) −8% (b) −15% (c) −20% (d) +15%**\n**Answer: (c) −20%** — inventory −15%, transport −8%, forecast accuracy +15%.\n\n**Q32. Lecture 9 says to measure the full technology life cycle, which runs: (a) Design → sale (b) Manufacture → implementation → operation → maintenance → disposal (c) Purchase → use (d) Pilot → scale**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n---\n\n## Lecture 10 — Performance measurement, QMS, reporting, sectors (quiz) ⭐⭐⭐\n\n**Q33. In Lecture 10, \"performance\" is defined as: (a) Revenue minus cost (b) Actual outcome compared with the desired target (c) Number of KPIs tracked (d) Output per worker**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q34. \"100% of suppliers trained in ESG\" is an example of: (a) Impact (b) Outcome (c) Activity (d) Lagging indicator of emissions**\n**Answer: (c) Activity** — not proof of sustainability improvement.\n\n**Q35. The \"KPI trap\" refers to: (a) Too few KPIs (b) Tracking many KPIs yet being unable to say whether the chain is becoming more sustainable (c) KPIs without targets (d) Financial KPIs only**\n**Answer: (b)** — e.g. 75 sustainability, 40 supplier, 30 logistics indicators.\n\n**Q36. Which is NOT one of the four questions a KPI must answer? (a) What happened? (b) Why did it happen? (c) How much did it cost to measure? (d) What decision will change because of it?**\n**Answer: (c)** — the fourth is \"Who owns it?\".\n\n**Q37. Which is a LEADING indicator? (a) Injury rate (b) Actual Scope 2 emissions (c) Renewable-energy contract (d) Disruption losses**\n**Answer: (c) Renewable-energy contract**\n\n**Q38. \"Preventive maintenance\" is paired in Lecture 10 with which lagging indicator? (a) Defect rate (b) Equipment failure (c) Injury rate (d) Product carbon footprint**\n**Answer: (b) Equipment failure**\n\n**Q39. \"Supplier development\" is paired with which lagging indicator? (a) Defect rate (b) Disruption losses (c) Scope 2 (d) Injury rate**\n**Answer: (a) Defect rate**\n\n**Q40. \"Lagging KPIs tell you ___; leading KPIs tell you ___\": (a) Cost; quality (b) What happened; whether you are likely to improve (c) The past; the target (d) Inputs; outputs**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q41. In Lecture 10's Balanced Scorecard, \"circularity\" sits in the: (a) Financial (b) Customer (c) Internal process (d) Learning & growth perspective**\n**Answer: (c) Internal process** — with carbon intensity and supplier quality.\n\n**Q42. \"Green customer retention\" belongs to which BSC perspective? (a) Financial (b) Customer (c) Internal process (d) Learning & growth**\n**Answer: (b) Customer**\n\n**Q43. In SCOR + sustainability, the Deliver KPI is: (a) Energy/unit (b) CO₂e per shipment (c) Recovery rate (d) ESG data quality**\n**Answer: (b) CO₂e per shipment**\n\n**Q44. The SCOR \"Enable\" sustainability KPI is: (a) Carbon-aware demand planning (b) Sustainable supplier score (c) ESG data quality (d) Recovery rate**\n**Answer: (c) ESG data quality**\n\n**Q45. The SCOR \"Return\" sustainability KPI is: (a) Recovery rate (b) Return turnaround (c) Defect rate (d) Fill rate**\n**Answer: (a) Recovery rate**\n\n**Q46. In Lecture 10's KPI architecture, \"living-wage coverage\" is a: (a) Economic (b) Operational (c) Social (d) Governance KPI**\n**Answer: (c) Social**\n\n**Q47. \"Audit closure rate\" and \"traceability\" are: (a) Environmental (b) Governance (c) Operational (d) Economic KPIs**\n**Answer: (b) Governance**\n\n**Q48. \"Cost of poor quality\" is listed as a(n): (a) Economic (b) Social (c) Governance (d) Environmental KPI**\n**Answer: (a) Economic**\n\n**Q49. The five KPIs for the FMCG distribution exercise include all EXCEPT: (a) CO₂e/ton-km (b) Fuel efficiency (km/l) (c) Vehicle utilisation % (d) Revenue per employee**\n**Answer: (d)** — the five are CO₂e/t-km, FE km/l, vehicle utilisation %, empty km %, OTIF %.\n\n**Q50. In the FMCG scenario, average vehicle utilisation is: (a) 18% (b) 62% (c) 80% (d) 92%**\n**Answer: (b) 62%** — with 18% empty return km, 10,000 deliveries/month, 180 km average.\n\n**Q51. A supplier scoring 95/100 on cost-quality-delivery but with serious labour violations should be: (a) Retained — score is high (b) Not retained / knock-out, as sustainability becomes a management decision (c) Given more volume (d) Ignored**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q52. \"Quality failure is simultaneously an ___ and ___ failure\": (a) Social, legal (b) Economic, environmental (c) Financial, reputational (d) Technical, human**\n**Answer: (b) Economic, environmental**\n\n**Q53. The poor-quality chain on the slide runs: (a) Defects → rework → extra materials + energy → extra transport → extra waste → higher carbon (b) Defects → profit (c) Waste → defects → rework (d) Rework → defects → recycling**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q54. Lecture 10 links quality to sustainability through: (a) TQM + Lean + Six Sigma + Green supply chain (b) ERP + CRM (c) JIT + Kanban only (d) ISO 27001**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q55. How many ISO quality-management principles are there? (a) 5 (b) 7 (c) 8 (d) 9**\n**Answer: (b) 7** — customer focus, leadership, engagement of people, process approach, improvement, evidence-based decision making, relationship management.\n\n**Q56. The ISO principle \"evidence-based decision making\" connects to sustainability through: (a) Lifecycle thinking (b) ESG data (c) Sustainable suppliers (d) Carbon/waste reduction**\n**Answer: (b) ESG data**\n\n**Q57. \"Relationship management\" connects to: (a) Sustainable suppliers (b) ESG data (c) Lifecycle thinking (d) Customer focus**\n**Answer: (a) Sustainable suppliers**\n\n**Q58. TIMWOOD stands for Transport, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Overproduction, Overprocessing and: (a) Delay (b) Defects (c) Distribution (d) Design**\n**Answer: (b) Defects**\n\n**Q59. According to Lecture 10, each lean waste potentially creates: (a) Cost only (b) Cost + energy + material + carbon + time (c) Carbon only (d) Time only**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q60. ISO 14001 provides a framework for: (a) Quality management (b) Environmental management system (c) Occupational health & safety (d) Information security**\n**Answer: (b) Environmental management system** — the 2026 edition emphasises environmental protection, business outcomes and integration.\n\n**Q61. An integrated management system typically combines: (a) ISO 9001 + ISO 14001 + ISO 45001 (b) ISO 27001 + ISO 50001 (c) ISO 14064 + ISO 14067 (d) SA 8000 + ISO 26000**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q62. GRI 305 deals with: (a) Water (b) Emissions (c) Labour (d) Anti-corruption**\n**Answer: (b) Emissions** — Scope 1–3, intensity, reductions, ODS, NOx/SOx.\n\n**Q63. TCFD's four pillars are governance, strategy, risk management and: (a) Assurance (b) Metrics and targets (c) Materiality (d) Stakeholders**\n**Answer: (b) Metrics and targets**\n\n**Q64. Integrated Reporting uses how many capitals? (a) 3 (b) 4 (c) 6 (d) 9**\n**Answer: (c) 6** — financial, manufactured, intellectual, human, social & relationship, natural.\n\n**Q65. BRSR is organised around how many principles? (a) 6 (b) 7 (c) 9 (d) 17**\n**Answer: (c) 9** — with about 140 parameters; Sections A, B, C.\n\n**Q66. BRSR Section C covers: (a) General disclosures (b) Management & process disclosures (c) Principle-wise performance disclosure (d) Financial statements**\n**Answer: (c)**\n\n**Q67. Per Lecture 10's takeaway, which framework focuses on investor-relevant sustainability risks/opportunities? (a) GRI (b) ISSB (c) BRSR (d) CDP**\n**Answer: (b) ISSB** — GRI = impacts; BRSR = Indian regulatory ESG reporting.\n\n**Q68. BRSR Core's original glide path started with the top ___ listed entities in FY2023–24: (a) 100 (b) 150 (c) 500 (d) 1,000**\n**Answer: (b) 150** — progressively towards 1,000.\n\n**Q69. BRSR Core's message is: (a) \"We are committed to sustainability\" (b) \"Show me the data\" / \"Don't just report sustainability. Prove it\" (c) \"Report first\" (d) \"Offset first\"**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q70. Company B (energy −25%, CO₂/unit −30%, waste-to-landfill −60%) vs Company A (beautiful report) teaches: (a) Reports drive value (b) The report is not the performance — measure first, report later (c) Graphics matter most (d) Targets are enough**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q71. Unilever's 2025 report says it sourced what % of key crops sustainably? (a) 61% (b) 71% (c) 81% (d) 95%**\n**Answer: (c) 81%** — 2030 target 95%; >95% of specified commodities deforestation-free.\n\n**Q72. Mahindra's supplier code of conduct was signed by what share of suppliers by purchase value? (a) 68.1% (b) 81% (c) 90% (d) 95%**\n**Answer: (d) 95%** — environmental due diligence covered 68.1% by value.\n\n**Q73. In the sectoral matrix, the main hotspots for IT are: (a) Packaging, food waste (b) Data centres, electricity, e-waste (c) Methane, biodiversity (d) Water, chemicals, labour**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q74. A sector-specific KPI for textiles is: (a) tCO₂e per tonne of steel (b) Litres of water per kg (c) kg CO₂e per patient (d) Methane intensity**\n**Answer: (b) Litres of water per kg** — plus chemical intensity, supplier labour compliance.\n\n**Q75. \"Overstating sustainability performance without adequate evidence\" is: (a) Greenhushing (b) Greenwashing (c) Carbon leakage (d) Offsetting**\n**Answer: (b) Greenwashing**\n\n**Q76. \"Under-communicating genuine sustainability achievements\" is: (a) Greenwashing (b) Greenhushing (c) Greenwishing (d) Bluewashing**\n**Answer: (b) Greenhushing**\n\n**Q77. Good sustainability reporting includes claim, metric, boundary, methodology, baseline, target and: (a) Logo (b) Evidence (c) Slogan (d) Budget**\n**Answer: (b) Evidence**\n\n**Q78. Step 5 of the SSCM performance loop is: (a) Measure (b) Target (c) Verify — data controls + assurance (d) Review**\n**Answer: (c) Verify**\n\n**Q79. Which is NOT one of the five tests for good SSCM measurement? (a) Material (b) Balanced (c) Profitable (d) Credible**\n**Answer: (c) Profitable** — the five are material, balanced, actionable, comparable, credible.\n\n**Q80. \"Can managers influence the KPI?\" is the test of being: (a) Material (b) Actionable (c) Comparable (d) Credible**\n**Answer: (b) Actionable**\n\n**Q81. Lecture 10 closes with: (a) \"If a KPI does not change a decision, question why it exists\" (b) \"More KPIs are better\" (c) \"Report everything\" (d) \"Benchmark competitors\"**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q82. A good measurement system balances: (a) Cost + Quality + Service + Carbon + People + Risk (b) Cost only (c) Carbon only (d) Revenue + profit**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n---\n\n## Lecture 11 — Strategies, challenges and transitions (quiz) ⭐⭐⭐\n\n**Q83. Lecture 11 says sustainability is becoming a fundamental: (a) Marketing slogan (b) Design constraint (c) CSR activity (d) Cost centre**\n**Answer: (b) Design constraint**\n\n**Q84. In the traditional vs sustainable table, \"Waste = cost\" becomes: (a) Waste = profit (b) Waste = resource (c) Waste = risk (d) Waste = unavoidable**\n**Answer: (b) Waste = resource**\n\n**Q85. \"Sustainable SCM does not mean sacrificing economic performance. It means ___\": (a) Higher prices (b) Changing the definition of performance (c) Lower service (d) Outsourcing**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q86. The \"battlefield\" slide's key question is: (a) Who is the cheapest supplier? (b) Where in the value chain is the largest sustainability impact — and who has the power to change it? (c) Which mode is fastest? (d) How many KPIs?**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q87. Which is NOT one of the six polycrisis elements? (a) Climate change (b) Geopolitical fragmentation (c) Interest-rate cuts (d) Technology disruption**\n**Answer: (c)** — the six: climate, geopolitics, critical resources, regulation, social risks, technology.\n\n**Q88. The polycrisis key message (WEF Global Risks Report 2026) is that the 2030 supply chain must be: (a) Cheapest (b) Green and resilient simultaneously (c) Global and concentrated (d) Fully automated**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q89. The strategic shift in the five big challenges is: (a) Cost → speed → quality (b) Efficiency → resilience → sustainability → regeneration (c) Local → global (d) Compliance → reporting**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q90. \"Survive disruption without shifting environmental/social costs elsewhere\" defines which challenge? (a) Carbon (b) People (c) Resilience (d) Circularity**\n**Answer: (c) Resilience**\n\n**Q91. Reducing the supply chain's vulnerability to climate change is: (a) Mitigation (b) Adaptation (c) Offsetting (d) Insetting**\n**Answer: (b) Adaptation** — best strategy = both mitigation and adaptation.\n\n**Q92. Step 1 of the strategy architecture is: (a) Prioritise (b) Diagnose (c) Measure (d) Scale**\n**Answer: (b) Diagnose** — map material, carbon, water, social and risk hotspots.\n\n**Q93. \"Embed sustainability into procurement, investment and governance\" is step: (a) Collaborate (b) Innovate (c) Scale (d) Redesign**\n**Answer: (c) Scale** — step 7.\n\n**Q94. Strategy 1's key message is: (a) \"Reduction first, offset later\" (b) \"Do not outsource sustainability\" (c) \"Measure first\" (d) \"Design for disassembly\"**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q95. In Strategy 1's KPI table, \"single-source dependency\" measures: (a) Transparency (b) Resilience (c) Human rights (d) Resources**\n**Answer: (b) Resilience**\n\n**Q96. Strategy 2's strategic concept is: (a) Design → Manufacture → Sell → Dispose (b) Design → Supply chain → Use → Recovery (c) Make → Sell → Recycle (d) Source → Make → Deliver**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q97. Strategy 3's key insight: circularity is ___: (a) A waste-management strategy (b) A supply-network redesign strategy (c) A marketing tool (d) A recycling target**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q98. \"The cleanest kilometre is often the kilometre eliminated\" is the principle of: (a) Strategy 2 (b) Strategy 3 (c) Strategy 4 — sustainable logistics (d) Strategy 5**\n**Answer: (c)**\n\n**Q99. Lecture 11 warns that green transport is: (a) Equal to sustainable logistics (b) Not the same as sustainable logistics (c) Unnecessary (d) Always cheaper**\n**Answer: (b)** — consider packaging, warehousing, inventory, delivery frequency, returns, empty miles.\n\n**Q100. The EU Digital Product Passport framework example on the slide launched in: (a) July 2024 (b) January 2025 (c) July 2026 (d) 2030**\n**Answer: (c) July 2026**\n\n**Q101. In Strategy 5, digital twins are used to: (a) Track products (b) Simulate network alternatives (c) Monitor energy in real time (d) Forecast demand**\n**Answer: (b)** — IoT monitors energy/materials, AI forecasts and routes, blockchain traces, DPP gives product-level information.\n\n**Q102. \"Future supply chain = physical supply chain + ___\": (a) More warehouses (b) Digital information layer (c) More suppliers (d) Air freight**\n**Answer: (b) Digital information layer**\n\n**Q103. Which is one of the eight transitions? (a) Renewable → fossil (b) Ownership → access/service models (c) Traceable → opaque (d) Circular → linear**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q104. Transition 8 is: (a) Sustainability reporting → sustainability embedded in decisions (b) Reactive → predictive (c) Virgin → circular materials (d) Global → diversified**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q105. IEA's 2025 Critical Minerals Outlook: lithium demand in 2024 rose by almost: (a) 10% (b) 20% (c) 30% (d) 50%**\n**Answer: (c) 30%** — and 7% of copper supply was exposed to flood/drought.\n\n**Q106. The shift \"from JIT to ___\" in Lecture 11 is: (a) JIT + JIC + JIS (b) MRP (c) Kanban (d) VMI**\n**Answer: (a) JIT + JIC + JIS**\n\n**Q107. The emerging network model is: (a) Global → concentrated → lowest cost (b) Regional + diversified + digital + circular + low-carbon (c) Single-sourced (d) Offshore only**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q108. The new optimisation question asks for: (a) Lowest purchase price (b) Lowest total life-cycle cost and risk (c) Highest revenue (d) Fastest delivery**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q109. Apple reported GHG reductions of more than ___ vs its 2015 baseline: (a) 30% (b) 45% (c) 60% (d) 75%**\n**Answer: (c) 60%** — 17.8 GW of supplier RE; 21.8 Mt avoided in 2024.\n\n**Q110. Apple's strategic lesson is that: (a) The OEM becomes the orchestrator of supplier sustainability (b) Suppliers must act alone (c) Offsets are enough (d) Only own facilities matter**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q111. Tata Motors' \"Re.Wi.Re\" stands for: (a) Reuse With Renewables (b) Recycle With Respect (c) Re-Wire Electric (d) Return With Refund**\n**Answer: (b) Recycle With Respect** — end-of-life vehicle network.\n\n**Q112. Tata Motors' \"Project Aalingana\" links decarbonisation, circular economy and: (a) Digitalisation (b) Nature (c) Finance (d) Exports**\n**Answer: (b) Nature**\n\n**Q113. In the strategy matrix, the future transition for \"Risk\" is: (a) Dual sourcing (b) Resilient regional networks (c) Supplier databases (d) Compliance**\n**Answer: (b)** — dual sourcing is the immediate strategy.\n\n**Q114. The future transition for \"Traceability\" is: (a) Supplier databases (b) Digital Product Passports (c) Audits (d) ERP**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q115. The future transition for \"People\" is: (a) Compliance (b) Living-wage / human-rights value chains (c) Training (d) Automation**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q116. The first step of the 7-step managerial roadmap is: (a) Measure (b) Map (c) Prioritise (d) Digitise**\n**Answer: (b) Map** — map → measure → prioritise → redesign → collaborate → digitise → transform.\n\n**Q117. In the sustainable supply-chain flywheel, the step after \"Circularise\" is: (a) Digitise (b) Decarbonise (c) Regenerate (d) Measure again**\n**Answer: (b) Decarbonise** — then regenerate, then measure again.\n\n**Q118. Which is a Lecture 11 key takeaway? (a) The firm's four walls matter most (b) Scope 3 and supplier ecosystems matter more than the firm's own four walls (c) Resilience and sustainability are unrelated (d) Recycling alone achieves circularity**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q119. The future sustainable supply chain will be digital, circular, low-carbon, resilient and increasingly: (a) Global (b) Regenerative (c) Automated (d) Centralised**\n**Answer: (b) Regenerative**\n\n**Q120. The end-sem weightage puts which COs as HIGH? (a) CO1 and CO6 (b) CO2 and CO5 (c) CO3 and CO4 (d) CO5 and CO6**\n**Answer: (c) CO3 and CO4** — CO2 & CO5 medium; CO1 & CO6 low.\n\n---\n\n## Lectures 1–8 and readings (end-sem revision) ⭐⭐\n\n**Q121. The course's one-line description of SSCM is: (a) \"Cheapest wins\" (b) \"How to save the planet without killing the P&L\" (c) \"Green at any cost\" (d) \"Compliance first\"**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q122. \"SCM is cradle to grave; SSCM is ___\": (a) Gate to gate (b) Cradle to cradle (c) Cradle to gate (d) Grave to cradle**\n**Answer: (b) Cradle to cradle**\n\n**Q123. ITC e-Choupal and HUL sustainable sourcing are classified in Lecture 1 as: (a) Recovery-based (b) Reduction-based (c) Delivery-based (d) Offset-based**\n**Answer: (c) Delivery-based**\n\n**Q124. Scope 2 is \"what you ___\": (a) Burn (b) Buy (c) Influence (d) Sell**\n**Answer: (b) Buy** — electricity/heat.\n\n**Q125. Which ISO standards define LCA? (a) ISO 9001/9004 (b) ISO 14040/14044 (c) ISO 14064/14067 (d) ISO 45001**\n**Answer: (b) ISO 14040/14044**\n\n**Q126. \"Carrying 20 kg of groceries per month\" is an example of: (a) System boundary (b) Functional unit (c) Impact category (d) Allocation rule**\n**Answer: (b) Functional unit**\n\n**Q127. Ecoinvent is a source of: (a) Primary data (b) Secondary data (c) Impact categories (d) Software licences**\n**Answer: (b) Secondary data**\n\n**Q128. Which LCA software is free? (a) SimaPro (b) GaBi (c) OpenLCA (d) Umberto**\n**Answer: (c) OpenLCA** — GreenDelta, Germany.\n\n**Q129. LCSA = E-LCA + S-LCA + ___: (a) LCI (b) LCC (c) LCIA (d) EPD**\n**Answer: (b) LCC** — Klöpffer (2008).\n\n**Q130. Manufacturing one laptop (Lecture 5) = 320 kg CO₂e + ___ L water + 850 MJ: (a) 1,800 (b) 8,000 (c) 18,000 (d) 180,000**\n**Answer: (c) 18,000**\n\n**Q131. The Indian tea-plantation example shows a product can be: (a) Low carbon but socially unsustainable (b) High carbon but socially good (c) Cheap and green (d) Circular**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q132. In the packaging LCSA example the LCSA choice is: (a) Plastic (b) Aluminium (c) Paper from certified sustainable forests (d) Glass**\n**Answer: (c)**\n\n**Q133. Net zero requires deep emission reductions of about: (a) 30–40% (b) 50–60% (c) 90–95% (d) 100% with no removals**\n**Answer: (c) 90–95%** — residual emissions neutralised.\n\n**Q134. The net-zero \"magic mantra\" is: (a) Offset first (b) Reduction first, offset later (c) Report first (d) Buy credits**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q135. Diesel emission factor used in the carbon deck: (a) 1.9 kg CO₂/l (b) 2.31 kg CO₂/l (c) 2.68 kg CO₂/l (d) 3.0 kg CO₂/l**\n**Answer: (c) 2.68 kg CO₂/l** — 10,000 l → 26,800 kg.\n\n**Q136. MRV stands for: (a) Manage, Reduce, Verify (b) Measure, Report, Verify (c) Monitor, Review, Validate (d) Measure, Reduce, Value**\n**Answer: (b) Measure, Report, Verify**\n\n**Q137. The GWP of N₂O (100-year) is about: (a) 28 (b) 273 (c) 1,300 (d) 25,200**\n**Answer: (b) ~273** — CH₄ ~27–30, SF₆ ~25,200.\n\n**Q138. The Scope 1-2-3 classification was introduced by: (a) IPCC (b) GHG Protocol (WRI + WBCSD) (c) UNFCCC (d) SEBI**\n**Answer: (b) GHG Protocol**\n\n**Q139. Procurement typically accounts for what share of manufacturing cost (Lecture 6)? (a) 10–20% (b) 30–40% (c) 50–80% (d) 90–95%**\n**Answer: (c) 50–80%**\n\n**Q140. In Lecture 6's debate, Supplier B is: (a) Cheapest with child-labour allegations (b) 12% more expensive, RE-powered, ISO 14001, no human-rights issues (c) Same price (d) Uncertified**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q141. \"ESG + RTI\" — RTI stands for: (a) Right To Information (b) Risk, Technology, Innovation (c) Revenue, Tax, Investment (d) Reliability, Time, Inventory**\n**Answer: (b) Risk, Technology, Innovation**\n\n**Q142. A supplier risk score of 16 (likelihood 4 × impact 4) is: (a) Low (b) Medium (c) High (d) Negligible**\n**Answer: (c) High** — high 15–24/25, medium 6–14, low 1–5.\n\n**Q143. Schneider Electric's Zero Carbon Project targets a 50% footprint reduction for its top ___ suppliers: (a) 100 (b) 500 (c) 1,000 (d) 10,000**\n**Answer: (c) 1,000**\n\n**Q144. Walmart's Project Gigaton was developed with: (a) WWF and EDF (b) UNEP and IEA (c) CDP and GRI (d) SBTi and TCFD**\n**Answer: (a) WWF and EDF** — six pillars: energy, agriculture, waste, packaging, deforestation, product use.\n\n**Q145. JSW Steel's supplier ESG self-assessment platform is: (a) EcoVadis (b) Synesgy (c) Sedex (d) Ariba**\n**Answer: (b) Synesgy**\n\n**Q146. The UNEP definition of sustainable manufacturing includes all EXCEPT: (a) Economically sound processes (b) Minimise environmental impacts (c) Conserve energy and resources (d) Maximise output at any cost**\n**Answer: (d)**\n\n**Q147. 3M's \"3P\" programme stands for: (a) People, Planet, Profit (b) Pollution Prevention Pays (c) Plan, Produce, Protect (d) Product, Process, Packaging**\n**Answer: (b) Pollution Prevention Pays** — cleaner production.\n\n**Q148. Asian Paints' renewable-energy share in FY25 (Lecture 7) is about: (a) 37% (b) 50% (c) 60% (d) 100%**\n**Answer: (c) ~60%** — M&M 37%, ITC 50%, Dalmia target 100% by 2030.\n\n**Q149. Arvind Mills uses what share of recycled water? (a) 50% (b) 75% (c) 90% (d) 100%**\n**Answer: (d) 100%**\n\n**Q150. OEE = (a) Availability + Performance + Quality (b) Availability × Performance × Quality (c) Output ÷ input (d) Good units ÷ total units**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q151. Green logistics' typical emission-intensity hierarchy (highest first): (a) Water > Rail > Road > Air (b) Air > Road > Rail > Water (c) Road > Air > Rail > Water (d) Rail > Road > Air > Water**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q152. Lecture 8's KPI for green warehousing is: (a) Cost per pallet (b) Energy consumption per unit handled (c) Dock-to-stock time (d) Order accuracy**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q153. Packaging affects emissions via the chain: (a) Cube → vehicle utilisation → number of trips → fuel → CO₂ (b) Weight → price (c) Colour → brand (d) Label → recycling**\n**Answer: (a)**\n\n**Q154. Which is NOT one of DHL's six green levers? (a) Electric delivery vehicles (b) Alternative fuels (c) More air freight (d) Shipment consolidation**\n**Answer: (c)**\n\n**Q155. Reverse-logistics principle: (a) Dispose quickly (b) Preserve the highest possible value for the longest possible time (c) Recycle everything (d) Centralise all returns**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q156. A \"hybrid\" collection network flows: (a) Customer → one central facility (b) Customer → local centre → regional hub → specialised recovery facility (c) Customer → landfill (d) Customer → retailer only**\n**Answer: (b)**\n\n**Q157. In the R-strategy hierarchy, the highest-value strategy is: (a) Recycle (b) Refuse (c) Repurpose (d) Remanufacture**\n**Answer: (b) Refuse** — then reduce, reuse, repair, refurbish, remanufacture, repurpose, recycle.\n\n**Q158. \"Rebuild to like-new condition with warranty\" is: (a) Refurbish (b) Repair (c) Remanufacture (d) Repurpose**\n**Answer: (c) Remanufacture** — e.g. remanufactured car alternator.\n\n**Q159. JSW-MG's Windsor EV illustrates: (a) Lighting-as-a-service (b) Battery-as-a-service (c) Furniture leasing (d) Equipment rental**\n**Answer: (b) Battery-as-a-service**\n\n**Q160. In the network model, yⱼ = 1 means: (a) Flow from i to j (b) Facility j is opened (c) Quantity recovered (d) Demand at j**\n**Answer: (b) Facility j is opened**\n\n**Q161. At every recovery facility: Returns = Reuse + Repair + Remanufacture + Recycling + ___: (a) Resale (b) Disposal (c) Storage (d) Export**\n**Answer: (b) Disposal**\n\n**Q162. For Network A (₹100, 100 CO₂) vs B (₹108, 75 CO₂), the carbon price at which they tie is: (a) 0.25 (b) 0.32 (c) 0.35 (d) 0.50**\n**Answer: (b) 0.32** — 8 ÷ 25.\n\n**Q163. \"Strategic sets the network, tactical ___ it, operational makes it perform\": (a) Designs (b) Connects (c) Funds (d) Audits**\n**Answer: (b) Connects**\n\n**Q164. Mahindra CERO is a JV of Mahindra and: (a) NTPC (b) MSTC Ltd (Government of India) (c) BHEL (d) SAIL**\n**Answer: (b) MSTC Ltd** — vehicle recycling.\n\n**Q165. Electricity's WTT efficiency in India (IISc) is about: (a) 20% (b) 48% (c) 75% (d) 85%**\n**Answer: (a) 20%** — coal 69.9%, T&D losses 25.4%.\n\n**Q166. Per the IISc WTW study, the most efficient configuration is the: (a) BEV (b) Hydrogen FCEV (c) Diesel split hybrid (d) Gasoline ICE**\n**Answer: (c) Diesel split hybrid**\n\n**Q167. Rietmann et al. (2020) forecast EV adoption using a: (a) Linear trend (b) Logistic growth function (c) Moving average (d) Monte Carlo only**\n**Answer: (b) Logistic growth function**\n\n**Q168. In the Tesla case, about what share of cobalt comes from the DRC? (a) 25% (b) 45% (c) 65% (d) 85%**\n**Answer: (c) 65%**\n\n**Q169. Only about what share of Li-ion batteries were recycled globally (Tesla case)? (a) 5% (b) 15% (c) 30% (d) 50%**\n**Answer: (a) 5%**\n\n**Q170. ITC eChoupal's scaling approach was called: (a) \"Think Big, Start Small\" (b) \"Roll Out, Fix It, Scale Up\" (c) \"Pilot, Prove, Propagate\" (d) \"Plan, Do, Check, Act\"**\n**Answer: (b)**\n"}, {"slug": "glossary", "label": "Glossary A–Z", "tag": "A–Z", "group": "practice", "title": "Glossary — Sustainable Supply Chain Terms A–Z", "md": "# Glossary — Sustainable Supply Chain Terms A–Z\nExam-ready one- or two-line definitions. 📘 = term from your course PPTs, readings or cases; 📗 = standard textbook term added for completeness. Use the flashcards and quiz views to self-test.\n\n## 0–9\n- **3P programme** 📘: 3M's \"Pollution Prevention Pays\" programme to redesign products and processes so pollution is prevented at source.\n- **5 tests of a good KPI** 📘: Material, balanced, actionable, comparable, credible (Lectures 10 and 11).\n- **7 wastes (TIMWOOD)** 📘: Transport, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Over-production, Over-processing, Defects; each creates cost + energy + material + carbon + time.\n\n## A\n- **Activity → Output → Outcome → Impact** 📘: Lecture 10's warning not to confuse doing something (e.g. training suppliers) with proving improvement (lower supplier emissions).\n- **Adaptation** 📘: Reducing the supply chain's vulnerability to climate change (climate-risk siting, diversification, buffers).\n- **AHP (Analytic Hierarchy Process)** 📘: MCDM method that structures goal → criteria → alternatives and derives weights from pairwise comparisons on a 1–9 scale, with a consistency ratio below 0.10.\n- **Allocation** 📗: In LCA, dividing the impacts of a process among co-products by mass, energy or economic value.\n- **APMC Act** 📘: State law that confined agricultural trade to mandis; Madhya Pradesh amended it so ITC could buy soybeans outside the mandi through eChoupal.\n\n## B\n- **Balanced Scorecard (sustainability)** 📘: Kaplan–Norton's financial, customer, internal-process and learning-and-growth perspectives, each with sustainability KPIs.\n- **Battery-as-a-Service (BaaS)** 📘: Product-as-a-service model where the customer rents the battery (e.g. JSW-MG Windsor EV), so the maker keeps control of the battery's materials.\n- **Benchmarking** 📘: Comparing performance and practices with industry leaders, competitors, global standards or one's own history to find gaps; \"benchmark not to imitate but to outperform self\".\n- **Biological cycle** 📘: Circular-economy loop in which organic materials are used, composted and regenerated.\n- **BRSR** 📘: SEBI's Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report: 9 principles, about 140 parameters, Sections A (general), B (management & process), C (principle-wise performance).\n- **BRSR Core** 📘: Subset of assured BRSR KPIs across nine ESG attributes, phased from the top 150 listed companies in FY2023–24 towards 1,000; \"don't just report sustainability, prove it\".\n- **Burden shifting** 📗: Reducing one impact (e.g. CO₂) while increasing another (water, toxicity) or moving it to another life-cycle stage; LCA is designed to detect it.\n\n## C\n- **Carbon credit** 📘: Tradable certificate for one tonne of CO₂e avoided, reduced or removed, issued by a registry such as Verra or Gold Standard.\n- **Carbon footprint** 📘: Total GHG emissions of a product or organisation expressed in CO₂e; a single-indicator subset of LCA.\n- **Carbon intensity** 📘: Emissions per unit of output or revenue, e.g. tCO₂e per tonne or per ₹ crore.\n- **Carbon ledger** 📘: Emerging practice of recording emissions like financial transactions, managed by carbon accountants and ESG controllers.\n- **Carbon neutral** 📘: Emissions balanced through offsets, regardless of how much has been reduced.\n- **Carbon zero** 📘: Near-zero emissions across operations and value chain.\n- **Carter–Rogers framework** 📘: SSCM sits at the intersection of environmental, social and economic performance, supported by risk management, transparency, strategy and culture.\n- **CBAM** 📘: EU Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism charging importers for embedded emissions in steel, cement, aluminium, fertilisers, hydrogen and electricity.\n- **CCTS** 📘: India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, operational from 2025, setting intensity targets for energy-intensive sectors and allowing trading of credits.\n- **CCUS** 📘: Carbon capture, utilisation and storage, used to neutralise residual emissions.\n- **CDP** 📘: Carbon Disclosure Project; investor-backed questionnaires on climate change, water security, forests, plastics and supply chains.\n- **Centre of gravity method** 📗: Facility-location method giving Cx = Σ xᵢWᵢ ÷ ΣWᵢ and Cy = Σ yᵢWᵢ ÷ ΣWᵢ as a starting location.\n- **Circular economy** 📘: Keeping products, components and materials in productive use and reducing waste through redesign, reuse, repair, remanufacturing and recycling (UNEP).\n- **Cleaner production** 📘: Preventing pollution and waste at source rather than treating it at the end of the pipe (e.g. 3M, Asian Paints).\n- **Closed-loop supply chain** 📘: Forward flow (supplier → factory → DC → customer) combined with reverse flow (customer → collection → recovery → factory or secondary market).\n- **CO₂e** 📘: Carbon-dioxide equivalent; emission × global warming potential.\n- **Cradle to cradle** 📘: Design in which end-of-life materials become inputs for new products; the course's description of SSCM.\n- **Cradle to gate** 📘: LCA boundary from raw materials to the factory gate.\n- **Cradle to grave** 📘: LCA boundary from raw materials through use to disposal; the course's description of traditional SCM.\n- **Critical-minerals paradox** 📘: The green transition increases demand for lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, graphite and rare earths, whose mining brings water, biodiversity, social and geopolitical risks.\n- **CSDDD** 📘: EU Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive, requiring large firms to identify and address human-rights and environmental harms in their value chains.\n\n## D\n- **DEA (Data Envelopment Analysis)** 📘: Linear-programming technique measuring the relative efficiency of peer units (suppliers) as outputs over inputs.\n- **Decentralised collection** 📘: Reverse network where customers return to local collection centres that feed a regional facility.\n- **Delivery-based case** 📘: Lecture 1 category for cases where sustainability comes from a better delivery model (ITC eChoupal, HUL sourcing).\n- **Digital Product Passport (DPP)** 📘: Digital identity of a product with verified data on origin, materials, compliance and life cycle; EU framework launched July 2026.\n- **Digital twin** 📘: Virtual replica of an asset, process or network used to simulate and optimise before acting.\n- **DMAIC** 📗: Six Sigma improvement cycle: Define, Measure, Analyse, Improve, Control.\n\n## E\n- **E-LCA** 📘: Environmental life-cycle assessment; \"what happens to the environment?\"\n- **Eco-design** 📘: Designing products to minimise life-cycle impact, e.g. Philips' recyclable, easy-to-dismantle products and IKEA's modular furniture.\n- **Ecoinvent** 📘: Widely used secondary LCI database.\n- **Emission factor** 📘: Emissions per unit of activity, e.g. 2.68 kg CO₂ per litre of diesel or about 0.70 kg CO₂ per kWh of Indian grid power.\n- **Empty km %** 📘: Empty running km ÷ total km; a key green-logistics KPI (18% in the Lecture 10 FMCG case).\n- **EPR (Extended Producer Responsibility)** 📘: Policy making producers responsible for collecting and recycling their products and packaging at end of life.\n- **ESG** 📘: Environmental, Social and Governance; \"the business sustainability scorecard for future business\".\n- **ESG + RTI** 📘: Lecture 6's supplier evaluation scorecard: Environmental, Social, Governance plus Risk, Technology, Innovation.\n\n## F\n- **Factor rating** 📗: Location method scoring candidate sites on weighted qualitative and quantitative factors.\n- **Fill rate** 📗: Units (or lines) shipped from stock ÷ units ordered.\n- **First-pass yield (FPY)** 📘: Units made right first time ÷ units started.\n- **Functional unit** 📘: Quantified function delivered by a product, used as the basis for LCA comparison, e.g. \"delivering 1 litre of drinking water\"; \"the most misunderstood\" part of LCA.\n\n## G\n- **Gate to gate** 📘: LCA boundary covering a single process or site.\n- **GHG Protocol** 📘: WRI–WBCSD corporate accounting standard that introduced Scopes 1, 2 and 3.\n- **Global Warming Potential (GWP)** 📘: Warming effect of a gas relative to CO₂ over 100 years (CH₄ ~27–30, N₂O ~273, SF₆ ~25,200).\n- **Green hydrogen** 📘: Hydrogen made by electrolysis using renewable power; a lever for hard-to-abate, high-temperature processes.\n- **Green logistics** 📘: Designing logistics activities to reduce environmental impact while maintaining service and economic performance.\n- **Greenhushing** 📘: Under-communicating genuine sustainability achievements.\n- **Greenwashing** 📘: Overstating sustainability performance without adequate evidence (\"eco-friendly\", \"carbon neutral\").\n- **GRI 305** 📘: Global Reporting Initiative standard on emissions (Scopes 1–3, intensity, reductions, ODS, NOx/SOx).\n\n## H\n- **Hybrid collection** 📘: Reverse network of local centres, regional hubs and specialised recovery facilities.\n- **Hydrometallurgy** 📘: Battery recycling by acid leaching; recovers lithium, cobalt, nickel and copper.\n\n## I\n- **Industrial symbiosis** 📘: Collaboration in which one industry's waste or by-product becomes another's input.\n- **Industry 4.0** 📘: AI, IoT, robotics, digital twins, predictive maintenance and cloud manufacturing driving efficiency, sustainability and competitiveness.\n- **Integrated management system** 📘: Combined ISO 9001 (quality), ISO 14001 (environment) and ISO 45001 (occupational health & safety).\n- **Integrated Reporting** 📘: Reporting framework built on six capitals: financial, manufactured, intellectual, human, social & relationship, natural.\n- **ISO 14001** 📘: Standard for an environmental management system and continual improvement of environmental performance.\n- **ISO 14040/14044** 📘: International standards for LCA principles/framework and requirements/guidelines.\n- **ISO 14064** 📘: Standard for quantifying, reporting and verifying organisational GHG emissions.\n- **ISSB (IFRS S1–S2)** 📘: Global baseline for investor-relevant sustainability (S1) and climate (S2) disclosures.\n\n## J\n- **Jidoka** 📘: Toyota's \"automation with a human touch\": stop at the first defect so defects are not passed on.\n- **JIT + JIC + JIS** 📘: Lecture 11's future model combining just-in-time efficiency, just-in-case buffers and just-in-sequence delivery in resilient networks.\n\n## K\n- **Kaizen** 📘: Continuous, incremental improvement by all employees (Toyota, Canon).\n- **KPI trap** 📘: Tracking too many KPIs (e.g. 75 + 40 + 30) yet being unable to answer whether the chain is becoming more sustainable.\n- **Kraljic matrix** 📗: Supplier segmentation by profit impact and supply risk into strategic, leverage, bottleneck and non-critical items.\n\n## L\n- **Lagging indicator** 📘: KPI that tells you what happened (injury rate, actual Scope 2, defect rate).\n- **LCA (Life Cycle Assessment)** 📘: Systematic method to evaluate environmental impacts at every stage of a product's life (ISO).\n- **LCC (Life Cycle Costing)** 📘: Total cost of ownership over the life cycle rather than purchase price.\n- **LCI** 📘: Life Cycle Inventory; phase II of LCA quantifying inputs and outputs from primary and secondary data.\n- **LCIA** 📘: Life Cycle Impact Assessment; phase III converting inventory into impact categories.\n- **LCSA** 📘: Life Cycle Sustainability Assessment = E-LCA + S-LCA + LCC (Klöpffer 2008).\n- **Leading indicator** 📘: KPI that tells you whether you are likely to improve (supplier ESG assessments, training, RE contracts).\n- **Lean manufacturing** 📘: Eliminating non-value-adding waste through flow and pull (TPS, Danaher Business System).\n- **Load-distance method** 📗: Location method choosing the site with the lowest Σ (load × distance), a proxy for transport cost and CO₂.\n- **Location-based Scope 2** 📗: Scope 2 calculated with the average grid emission factor.\n- **LTIFR** 📘: Lost-time injury frequency rate = lost-time injuries × 1,000,000 ÷ person-hours worked.\n\n## M\n- **Market-based Scope 2** 📗: Scope 2 calculated with the factors of contracts bought (PPAs, RECs, green tariffs).\n- **Mitigation** 📘: Reducing the supply chain's impact on the climate.\n- **MRV** 📘: Measure, Report, Verify; the foundation of credible carbon accounting and ESG reporting.\n\n## N\n- **Net Technology Value** 📘: Gross benefits − adoption cost − lifecycle sustainability cost − risk cost (Lecture 9).\n- **Net zero** 📘: Reducing emissions deeply (about 90–95%) and neutralising only residual emissions with removals.\n- **Net-zero hierarchy** 📘: Measure → reduce → RE transition → sustainable supply chain → offset unavoidable emissions; \"reduction first, offset later\".\n\n## O\n- **OEE** 📘: Overall equipment effectiveness = availability × performance × quality.\n- **OTIF** 📘: On time in full; orders delivered both on time and complete ÷ total orders.\n\n## P\n- **Pareto-efficient network** 📘: A network for which no objective (cost, CO₂, recovery) can be improved without worsening another.\n- **Perfect order rate** 📘: Orders on time, in full, damage-free and with correct documents ÷ total orders.\n- **Polycrisis** 📘: Simultaneous climate, geopolitical, critical-resource, regulatory, social and technology pressures on supply chains.\n- **PPA (Power Purchase Agreement)** 📗: Long-term contract to buy electricity, often renewable, from a generator; cuts market-based Scope 2.\n- **Primary data** 📘: Data measured directly from the company or its suppliers (meters, bills, production records).\n- **Product as a Service (PaaS)** 📘: Selling use or performance instead of ownership (CNH harvester rental, Signify lighting, Furlenco furniture); the maker keeps material value.\n- **Product recovery hierarchy** 📗: Reuse → repair → refurbish → remanufacture → recycle → energy recovery → disposal.\n- **Pyrometallurgy** 📘: Battery recycling by shredding and smelting at high temperature; energy-intensive.\n\n## R\n- **R-strategy hierarchy** 📘: Refuse, reduce, reuse, repair, refurbish, remanufacture, repurpose, recycle (highest to lowest value).\n- **Recovery-based case** 📘: Lecture 1 category where sustainability comes from recovering value (EV-battery circular chain).\n- **Reduction-based case** 📘: Lecture 1 category where sustainability comes from cutting emissions or resources (Hindustan Zinc, Mahindra, Flipkart).\n- **Refurbish** 📘: Restore and update a product to good working condition (e.g. an old sofa).\n- **Regenerative agriculture** 📘: Farming that restores soil health (cover cropping, crop rotation, agroforestry, organic fertiliser), promoted by Nestlé, HUL and Unilever.\n- **Remanufacture** 📘: Rebuild a used product to like-new condition with a warranty (Tata Motors Prolife, remanufactured alternators).\n- **Repurpose** 📘: Use an item for a different purpose (old boots as planters).\n- **Resilience (4th pillar)** 📘: Visibility, agility, recovery, response and assurance; added to the TBL in Lecture 9.\n- **Reverse logistics** 📘: Managing the movement of products and materials from the customer back toward the supply chain.\n\n## S\n- **S-LCA** 📘: Social life-cycle assessment; \"what happens to people?\" across workers, consumers, society and governance.\n- **Samyojak** 📘: Former commission agent who, in ITC eChoupal, sets up kiosks, runs hubs and handles logistics and cash.\n- **Sanchalak** 📘: Trusted lead farmer who hosts and runs the eChoupal kiosk and aggregates village trade.\n- **SBTi** 📘: Science Based Targets initiative, which validates corporate emission-reduction targets against climate science.\n- **SCOR + sustainability** 📘: Plan (carbon-aware planning), Source (supplier score), Make (energy/unit), Deliver (CO₂e/shipment), Return (recovery rate), Enable (ESG data quality).\n- **Scope 1** 📘: Direct emissions from owned or controlled sources: \"what you burn\".\n- **Scope 2** 📘: Indirect emissions from purchased electricity, heat, cooling and steam: \"what you buy\".\n- **Scope 3** 📘: All other value-chain emissions in 15 categories: \"what you influence\"; usually 70–90%+ of the total.\n- **Secondary data** 📘: Data from databases, literature and government statistics (e.g. ecoinvent).\n- **Second-life battery** 📘: An EV battery repurposed for stationary storage; adaptation costs $3,500 (pack), $6,700 (module), $8,000 (cell) in the Tesla case.\n- **Six Sigma** 📘: Motorola's method to reduce process variation (3.4 defects per million opportunities).\n- **SMART KPI** 📘: Specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound.\n- **Supply Chain 4.0** 📘: Pyramid from ERP to automation, cloud, blockchain, IoT, digital twins, analytics/ML and AI/GenAI.\n- **Sustainable manufacturing** 📘: UNEP: making products with economically sound processes that minimise environmental impact, conserve energy and resources, and keep employees, communities and consumers safe.\n- **Sustainable procurement** 📘: Buying goods and services for whole-life value that benefits the organisation, society and economy while minimising environmental harm (ISO 20400).\n- **Sustainable supply chain strategy** 📘: A long-term plan integrating economic, environmental and social objectives while maintaining competitiveness and profitability.\n- **Sustainability (Brundtland)** 📘: Meeting present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs.\n- **SSCM** 📘: Sustainable supply chain management; integrating economic, environmental and social goals into material, information and money flows; \"saving the planet without killing the P&L\".\n\n## T\n- **TAS (Technology Adoption Score)** 📘: 25% economic + 25% environmental + 20% social + 15% resilience + 15% strategic fit, each scored 1–5.\n- **TCFD** 📘: Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures; four pillars: governance, strategy, risk management, metrics and targets.\n- **TCO (Total Cost of Ownership)** 📘: Purchase price plus maintenance, energy, waste and end-of-life costs; the basis of \"lowest TCO = higher value\".\n- **Technical cycle** 📘: Circular-economy loop for products: use → repair → reuse → remanufacture → recycle → new product.\n- **Tech-Sustain framework** 📘: Lecture 9's 7 steps: define problem, baseline, alternatives, lifecycle impact, TBL, unintended consequences, pilot → measure → scale.\n- **TOPSIS** 📘: MCDM method ranking options by closeness to the ideal solution and distance from the anti-ideal.\n- **Triple Bottom Line (TBL)** 📘: People, Planet, Profit (Elkington, 1994).\n- **TTW (Tank-to-Wheel)** 📘: Vehicle-level efficiency from fuel tank or battery to wheels (gasoline 15–27%, diesel 25–32%).\n\n## V\n- **Vehicle utilisation** 📘: Load carried ÷ vehicle capacity (by weight or cube); 62% in the Lecture 10 FMCG case.\n- **Verra (VCS)** 📘: Leading voluntary carbon-credit registry, alongside Gold Standard, American Carbon Registry and Climate Action Reserve.\n\n## W\n- **Waste hierarchy (EU)** 📘: Prevention → preparing for reuse → recycling → recovery → disposal, with \"polluter pays\" and EPR.\n- **Weighted scoring** 📘: Supplier-selection method: total = Σ weight × score; simple and transparent, weights subjective.\n- **WTT (Well-to-Tank)** 📘: Efficiency of producing and delivering fuel to the vehicle (petroleum fuels 75–85%, Indian electricity about 20%).\n- **WTW (Well-to-Wheel)** 📘: Full fuel-cycle efficiency = WTT × TTW; diesel split hybrid best in the IISc study.\n\n## Z\n- **Zero liquid discharge (ZLD)** 📘: Treating and reusing all wastewater so none leaves the plant (Asian Paints).\n- **Zero Carbon Project** 📘: Schneider Electric's programme to halve the operational footprint of its top 1,000 suppliers.\n"}];
